window.QUIZ_DATA = {"exams":[{"examNumber":1,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":1,"title":"SDL 1 — Cellular Mechanisms of Aging","questions":[{"id":"sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify the proposed causes of cellular aging","batch":1,"stem":"Which proposed cause of cellular aging results from progressive telomere attrition with each round of somatic cell division?","choices":{"A":"Loss of proteostasis","B":"Replicative senescence","C":"Dysregulated nutrient sensing","D":"Stem cell exhaustion","E":"Epigenetic alteration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Replicative senescence is the hallmark in which somatic cells lose telomeric DNA with each division and then exit the cell cycle into an irreversible nondividing state. Loss of proteostasis refers to failing chaperones, proteasomes, and autophagy. Dysregulated nutrient sensing is a separate hallmark from telomere-driven arrest. Stem cell exhaustion is a decline in tissue replenishment by stem cells. Epigenetic alteration refers to changes in DNA methylation and histone marks. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 1, proposed causes of cellular aging); Robbins Ch. 2 (Cellular aging). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal aging is associated with genetic changes such as telomere shortening and epigenetic changes such as DNA methylation (First Aid Pathology—Aging, normal aging, p. 225). COMLEX: aging hallmarks are interrelated, so one insult often feeds several of them (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — the nine hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify the proposed causes of cellular aging","batch":1,"stem":"Studies of cellular aging in yeast, worms, and mice indicate that the aging of cells is best described as which of the following?","choices":{"A":"A passive exhaustion of cell energy","B":"A random loss of nuclear chromosomes","C":"A gene-regulated loss of cell function","D":"A virus-driven decline in cell number","E":"An acquired loss of mitochondrial genes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cellular aging is a progressive decline in cell function and viability governed by evolutionarily conserved genes and pathways, not an unregulated process. The idea that cells simply run out of energy is the view the notes reject. Random loss of nuclear chromosomes is not the proposed basis of the aging program. Viral destruction of cells is an injury, not a gene-regulated program. Acquired loss of mitochondrial genes is likewise not the mechanism the notes propose. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 1); Robbins Ch. 2 (Cellular aging). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the free radicals that injure cells arise from radiation exposure, phase I drug metabolism, redox reactions, nitric oxide, transition metals, and the oxidative burst of white cells (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, free radical injury, p. 206). COMLEX: cellular aging combines intrinsic genetic programming with lifelong accumulated damage (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — the nine hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify the proposed causes of cellular aging","batch":1,"stem":"An investigator finds that the bone marrow of old mice regenerates poorly after irradiation and that their blood-forming precursors carry altered DNA methylation and histone marks. Which hallmark of cellular aging do these findings illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Stem cell exhaustion and epigenetic alteration","B":"Defective DNA repair and genomic instability","C":"Chaperone failure and protein aggregation","D":"Telomere attrition with replicative cell arrest","E":"Overactive insulin and mTOR signaling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Stem cell exhaustion with epigenetic alteration describes declining stem cell replenishment, such as hematopoietic stem cell decline, together with changes in DNA methylation and histone modification that compromise tissue renewal. Defective DNA repair concerns unrepaired genomic lesions, not methylation marks. Chaperone failure concerns misfolded proteins. Telomere attrition concerns shortened chromosome ends. Overactive nutrient-responsive signaling is a separate hallmark from stem cell decline. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 1). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: DNA methylation is the epigenetic change First Aid links to normal aging (First Aid Pathology—Aging, normal aging, p. 225). COMLEX: bone marrow mass falls and marrow fat rises with age (First Aid Pathology, Aging).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — the nine hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the concept of DNA damage","batch":1,"stem":"Which cellular process is the main endogenous source of the reactive by-products that damage DNA throughout life?","choices":{"A":"Anaerobic glycolysis within the cytosol","B":"Glycogen breakdown in hepatocytes","C":"Protein hydrolysis within the lysosomes","D":"Mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation","E":"Peptide chain synthesis on ribosomes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The main endogenous DNA-damaging by-products are reactive oxygen species generated during mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation. Anaerobic glycolysis in the cytosol does not use the electron transport chain. Glycogen breakdown in hepatocytes releases glucose without generating meaningful oxygen radicals. Lysosomal protein hydrolysis digests proteins rather than producing radicals. Ribosomal peptide synthesis builds proteins and is not a radical source. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 2, DNA damage); Robbins Ch. 2 (Free radicals; Cellular aging). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ionizing radiation injures DNA both directly and indirectly through free radicals, and its late complications include localized fibrosis and neoplasms such as leukemia and thyroid cancer (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, ionizing radiation toxicity, p. 207). COMLEX: mitochondrial dysfunction is the metabolic change First Aid links to normal aging (First Aid Pathology, Aging).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the concept of DNA damage","batch":1,"stem":"Which spontaneous chemical change converts cytosine residues in DNA to uracil and generates basal mutations?","choices":{"A":"Depurination","B":"Deamination","C":"Methylation","D":"Alkylation","E":"Acetylation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Spontaneous deamination removes the amino group of cytosine and turns it into uracil, a steady source of basal mutations. Depurination removes a purine base and leaves an abasic site. Methylation of cytosine is an epigenetic mark rather than a conversion to uracil. Alkylation adds alkyl groups, usually from chemical agents. Acetylation modifies histones rather than DNA bases. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 2). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: base excision repair, which starts with a glycosylase, handles spontaneous deamination damage (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, DNA repair, p. 37). COMLEX: repair fidelity is incomplete and repair efficiency declines with age, so lesions accumulate in nuclear and mitochondrial genomes (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the concept of DNA damage","batch":1,"stem":"Ataxia-telangiectasia and Bloom syndrome both reflect defective repair of which type of DNA lesion?","choices":{"A":"Thymine dimers","B":"Mismatched base pairs","C":"Oxidized guanine bases","D":"Interstrand cross-links","E":"Double-strand breaks"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ataxia-telangiectasia and Bloom syndrome involve genes that repair DNA double-strand breaks, the ATM kinase and the BLM helicase, and their loss produces genomic instability, early neurodegeneration, and early tissue senescence. Thymine dimers are removed by nucleotide excision repair. Mismatched base pairs are corrected by mismatch repair. Oxidized guanine is removed by base excision repair. Interstrand cross-links are handled by the Fanconi anemia pathway. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 2, progeroid syndromes). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nonhomologous end joining and homologous recombination repair double-strand breaks (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, DNA repair, p. 37). COMLEX: unrepaired lesions stall transcription and replication, arrest the cell cycle, or trigger intrinsic apoptosis (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the importance of telomeres and the changes which cause cellular senesence","batch":1,"stem":"Which hexameric nucleotide sequence is repeated at the ends of human chromosomes?","choices":{"A":"TATAAA","B":"CAGCAG","C":"TTAGGG","D":"AATAAA","E":"GCCGCC"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Human telomeres are noncoding TTAGGG repeats that cap chromosome ends and keep them from fraying, fusing, or being read as broken DNA. TATAAA is the TATA box of promoters. CAGCAG is the trinucleotide repeat expanded in Huntington disease. AATAAA is the polyadenylation signal of mRNA. GCCGCC is part of the Kozak consensus around the start codon. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 3, telomeres); Robbins Ch. 2 (Telomere attrition). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid remembers the telomeric repeat with the mnemonic Telomerase TAGs for Greatness and Glory (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, telomerase, p. 36). COMLEX: telomeric repeats are noncoding, so they serve as protective caps rather than carrying genes (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — cellular damage, aging, and organ dysfunction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the importance of telomeres and the changes which cause cellular senesence","batch":1,"stem":"The ribonucleoprotein that restores chromosome-end length synthesizes the new repeats by copying which template?","choices":{"A":"The opposite DNA strand","B":"A cytoplasmic mRNA","C":"A primase-made RNA primer","D":"A mitochondrial DNA copy","E":"An RNA within the enzyme"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Telomerase contains a reverse transcriptase catalytic subunit that uses its own intrinsic RNA as the template for new telomeric repeats. Copying the opposite DNA strand is what conventional polymerases do, and that leaves the extreme 3' end unreplicated. Cytoplasmic mRNA carries coding messages and is not a telomere template. A primase-made RNA primer starts lagging-strand synthesis rather than templating telomeric repeats. Mitochondrial DNA is a separate circular genome that does not template nuclear telomeres. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 3, telomerase activity); Robbins Ch. 2 (Telomere attrition). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: telomerase is a reverse transcriptase that adds TTAGGG to the 3' ends of chromosomes and is downregulated in aging (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, telomerase, p. 36). COMLEX: the 2009 Nobel Prize recognized how telomeres and telomerase protect chromosomes (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — cellular damage, aging, and organ dysfunction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the importance of telomeres and the changes which cause cellular senesence","batch":1,"stem":"In which type of normal human cell is telomerase activity highest?","choices":{"A":"Germ cells","B":"Hepatocytes","C":"Cardiac myocytes","D":"Dermal fibroblasts","E":"Cortical neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Telomerase is active in germ cells, present at low levels in stem and progenitor cells, and repressed or absent in most somatic tissues. Hepatocytes are somatic cells with repressed telomerase. Cardiac myocytes are somatic cells whose telomerase is likewise repressed. Dermal fibroblasts lack meaningful telomerase, so their telomeres shorten with each division. Cortical neurons are postmitotic somatic cells with repressed telomerase. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 3, expression patterns); Robbins Ch. 2 (Telomere attrition). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stem cells of rapidly regenerating tissues, such as skin, bone marrow, GI tract, and gonads, are the most susceptible to radiation injury (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, ionizing radiation toxicity, p. 207). COMLEX: telomere attrition limits tissue regenerative reserve and contributes to organ dysfunction (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — cellular damage, aging, and organ dysfunction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the role of tumor suppressor genes","batch":1,"stem":"The protein encoded at the CDKN2A locus enforces senescence by inhibiting which kinases?","choices":{"A":"CDK1 and CDK2","B":"PI3K and AKT","C":"CDK4 and CDK6","D":"JAK1 and JAK2","E":"MEK1 and ERK2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The CDKN2A product p16, also called INK4a, inhibits CDK4 and CDK6 so they do not phosphorylate the retinoblastoma protein. CDK1 and CDK2 act later in the cycle with cyclins E, A, and B. PI3K and AKT relay growth factor and insulin signals. JAK1 and JAK2 carry cytokine receptor signals. MEK1 and ERK2 relay mitogen signals in the MAP kinase cascade. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 4, CDKN2A locus). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cyclin-CDK complexes phosphorylate Rb to coordinate cell cycle progression (First Aid Biochemistry—Cellular, cell cycle, p. 44). COMLEX: by blocking CDK4 and CDK6, p16 shields cells from uncontrolled mitogenic stimuli and locks them into senescence (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — genetic versus epigenetic contributions to aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the role of tumor suppressor genes","batch":1,"stem":"Expression of which protein rises most tightly with chronological age across nearly all human and rodent tissues?","choices":{"A":"CDK inhibitor p16","B":"CDK inhibitor p27","C":"Cyclin D1 protein","D":"MDM2 ubiquitin ligase","E":"Telomerase subunit TERT"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Expression of the cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor p16, encoded at CDKN2A, correlates tightly with chronological age across virtually all human and rodent tissues. p27 is a separate CDK inhibitor not used as an age marker in the notes. Cyclin D1 activates CDK4 and CDK6. MDM2 degrades p53. TERT, the telomerase subunit, is repressed in most adult somatic tissues and does not track with age in this way. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 4, CDKN2A locus). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: both alleles of a tumor suppressor gene must be lost before disease appears, the Knudson two-hit hypothesis (First Aid Pathology—Neoplasia, tumor suppressor genes, p. 220). COMLEX: the same arrest drives aging by halting stem cell proliferation (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — genetic versus epigenetic contributions to aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the role of tumor suppressor genes","batch":1,"stem":"When DNA lesions in a cell are irreparable, sustained activation of the p53 pathway converts a temporary G1 arrest into which state?","choices":{"A":"Reversible quiescence","B":"Unlimited proliferation","C":"Terminal differentiation","D":"Cellular senescence","E":"Polyploid endoreplication"},"correct":"D","explanation":"If genomic lesions or telomere attrition are irreparable, sustained p53 activation stabilizes the G1 arrest into permanent cellular senescence, or induces apoptosis when stress is severe. Reversible quiescence is a resting state that cells leave when mitogens return. Unlimited proliferation marks immortalized tumor cells and is the opposite of a p53 effect. Terminal differentiation is a developmental program rather than a damage response. Polyploid endoreplication is DNA replication without division, which a G1 arrest prevents. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 4, the p53 pathway). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: germline TP53 mutation causes Li-Fraumeni syndrome (First Aid Pathology—Neoplasia, tumor suppressor genes, p. 220). COMLEX: critically uncapped telomeres feed into the same p53 pathway as other DNA lesions (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — genetic versus epigenetic contributions to aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the influence of protein homeostasis as it influences cellular aging","batch":1,"stem":"Which pathway sequesters worn-out organelles and protein aggregates in double-membrane vesicles for enzymatic digestion?","choices":{"A":"Autophagy","B":"Phagocytosis","C":"Pinocytosis","D":"Exocytosis","E":"Transcytosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autophagy wraps cytoplasmic proteins, misfolded aggregates, and worn-out organelles in double-membrane autophagosomes that fuse with lysosomes for digestion and recycling. Phagocytosis engulfs external particles. Pinocytosis takes up extracellular fluid. Exocytosis releases vesicle contents from the cell. Transcytosis moves cargo across a cell. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 5, proteostasis arms); Robbins Ch. 2 (Autophagy). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: atrophy shrinks tissue through autophagy, the programmed digestion of damaged or misfolded intracellular proteins, and through ubiquitin-proteasome breakdown of the cytoskeleton (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, cellular adaptations, p. 202). COMLEX: autophagosomes fuse with lysosomes so their contents can be degraded and recycled (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the influence of protein homeostasis as it influences cellular aging","batch":1,"stem":"Which molecules act as molecular chaperones that bind nascent polypeptides and prevent their inappropriate aggregation?","choices":{"A":"Ubiquitin ligases","B":"Executioner caspases","C":"Lysosomal cathepsins","D":"Matrix metalloproteinases","E":"Heat shock proteins"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Heat shock proteins are the molecular chaperones that bind nascent or partly unfolded chains, prevent aggregation, and assist folding, and their expression falls with aging. Ubiquitin ligases act on proteins already beyond rescue rather than assisting their folding. Executioner caspases cleave proteins during apoptosis. Cathepsins digest proteins inside lysosomes. Matrix metalloproteinases degrade extracellular matrix. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 5, molecular chaperones). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: heat shock proteins released from injured cells act as damage-associated molecular patterns, as do mitochondrial DNA and histones (First Aid Immunology—Lymphoid Structures, pathogen recognition, p. 97). COMLEX: defective proteostasis is listed among the best-described mechanisms of cellular aging (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the influence of protein homeostasis as it influences cellular aging","batch":1,"stem":"Misfolded proteins build up in the endoplasmic reticulum of an aging cell. Which change is part of the first adaptive response to this stress?","choices":{"A":"Increased global translation","B":"Increased chaperone synthesis","C":"Inhibited proteasome activity","D":"Blocked autophagosome formation","E":"Released mitochondrial cytochrome c"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ER stress first triggers the unfolded protein response, which increases chaperone synthesis and slows general translation. Increased global translation would add to the misfolded load. Inhibiting the proteasome would worsen clearance of misfolded proteins. Blocking autophagosome formation would also impair clearance rather than adapt to the stress. Cytochrome c release belongs to the mitochondrial apoptotic pathway that follows unresolved ER stress. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 5, ER stress and the unfolded protein response); Robbins Ch. 2 (ER stress). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: defects in the ubiquitin-proteasome system are implicated in neurodegenerative diseases (First Aid Biochemistry—Cellular, proteasome, p. 46). COMLEX: the unfolded protein response is protective until the misfolded load exceeds it (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Relate the concept of dysregulated nutrient sensing to it's influence of cellular aging","batch":1,"stem":"Activated SIRT6 promotes genomic stability by which action?","choices":{"A":"Inhibiting telomerase activity","B":"Increasing mTOR signaling","C":"Suppressing chaperone folding","D":"Activating DNA repair enzymes","E":"Increasing IGF-1 secretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Activated sirtuins, especially SIRT6, promote genomic stability by activating DNA repair enzymes through deacylation. Telomerase inhibition would shorten telomeres rather than stabilize the genome. Increased mTOR signaling accelerates senescence rather than protecting the genome. Increased IGF-1 secretion is a growth signal that sirtuins do not drive. Sirtuins promote chaperone-mediated folding rather than suppress it. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 6, the sirtuin pathway); Robbins Ch. 2 (Nutrient sensing). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mismatch repair removes mismatched nucleotides from the newly synthesized strand, mostly in S phase, and is defective in Lynch syndrome (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, DNA repair, p. 37). COMLEX: a constituent of red wine has been proposed to activate sirtuins, though human benefit is unproven (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 5 — IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Relate the concept of dysregulated nutrient sensing to it's influence of cellular aging","batch":1,"stem":"IGF-1, which signals cells that glucose and amino acids are abundant, is produced in response to which hormone?","choices":{"A":"Thyroid hormone","B":"Growth hormone","C":"Parathyroid hormone","D":"Antidiuretic hormone","E":"Luteinizing hormone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IGF-1 is produced in response to growth hormone and mimics insulin signaling to drive an anabolic state, cell growth, and replication. Thyroid hormone sets basal metabolic rate. Parathyroid hormone regulates calcium and phosphate. Antidiuretic hormone regulates water reabsorption. Luteinizing hormone drives gonadal steroid production. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 6, insulin/IGF-1/mTOR cascade). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: growth hormone acts on the liver to produce IGF-1 (First Aid Endocrine—Physiology, growth hormone, p. 333). COMLEX: high activity of the somatotrophic axis accelerates cellular senescence (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 5 — IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Relate the concept of dysregulated nutrient sensing to it's influence of cellular aging","batch":1,"stem":"Which cellular condition activates the sirtuin family of energy and stress sensors?","choices":{"A":"Rising NAD+ during fasting","B":"Rising ATP after a meal","C":"Rising insulin after a meal","D":"Rising IGF-1 with growth hormone","E":"Rising glucose during feeding"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sirtuins are NAD-dependent deacetylases activated when cellular energy is low and NAD+ rises, as during fasting or caloric restriction. Rising ATP after a meal signals energy abundance, which suppresses sirtuins. Rising insulin after a meal is a fed-state signal that favors growth pathways. Rising IGF-1 with growth hormone stimulates the IGF-1/mTOR axis rather than sirtuins. Rising glucose during feeding signals nutrient abundance and suppresses sirtuin programs. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 6, the sirtuin pathway). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the fasting state, glucagon signaling through cAMP shifts the liver toward gluconeogenesis and away from glycolysis (First Aid Biochemistry—Metabolism, regulation by fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, p. 74). COMLEX: caloric restriction without malnutrition prolongs lifespan and health span across eukaryotic species (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 5 — IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the contribution of inflammation to the aging process","batch":1,"stem":"Cells in irreversible growth arrest release cytokines, chemokines, and matrix-degrading proteases that inflame and remodel nearby tissue. What is this behavior called?","choices":{"A":"Epithelial-mesenchymal transition","B":"Integrated unfolded protein response","C":"Senescence-associated secretory phenotype","D":"Aerobic glycolytic Warburg phenotype","E":"Interferon-stimulated antiviral cell state"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The senescence-associated secretory phenotype is the mix of proinflammatory cytokines, chemokines, and matrix-degrading proteases that senescent cells actively release, which feeds inflammaging. Epithelial-mesenchymal transition is a change in cell identity during development, fibrosis, and invasion. The unfolded protein response handles ER stress. The Warburg phenotype is aerobic glycolysis in tumor cells. The interferon antiviral state is a response to viral nucleic acids. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 7, cellular senescence and SASP). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic inflammation is a prolonged mononuclear infiltrate of macrophages, lymphocytes, and plasma cells that destroys and repairs tissue at the same time, including angiogenesis and fibrosis (First Aid Pathology—Inflammation, chronic inflammation, p. 212). COMLEX: the matrix-degrading proteases in this secretory mix remodel the surrounding tissue as well as inflaming it (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — contributors to frailty","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the contribution of inflammation to the aging process","batch":1,"stem":"Defective clearance of damaged mitochondria in aging cells sustains proinflammatory gene transcription through which transcription factor?","choices":{"A":"HIF-1α","B":"SREBP-1","C":"PPAR-γ","D":"NF-κB","E":"FOXP3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Defective autophagy of damaged mitochondria that release ROS and inflammatory components, along with failed clearance of pathogens and debris, keeps NF-κB active and drives ongoing inflammatory transcription. HIF-1α responds to hypoxia. SREBP-1 regulates lipid synthesis. PPAR-γ regulates adipogenesis and insulin sensitivity. FOXP3 defines regulatory T cells. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 7, NF-κB pathway activation and autophagy defects). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Toll-like receptors that detect pathogen- or damage-associated molecular patterns activate NF-κB, which releases proinflammatory cytokines (First Aid Immunology—Lymphoid Structures, pathogen recognition, p. 97). COMLEX: aging immune systems clear pathogens and debris less effectively, which keeps inflammatory signaling switched on (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — contributors to frailty","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the contribution of inflammation to the aging process","batch":1,"stem":"Which molecule released from damaged or necrotic host cells acts as a damage-associated molecular pattern?","choices":{"A":"Lipopolysaccharide","B":"Bacterial flagellin","C":"Unmethylated CpG DNA","D":"Bacterial peptidoglycan","E":"Extracellular ATP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Extracellular ATP and nucleic acid breakdown products such as uric acid are damage-associated molecular patterns released from damaged or uncleared host cells. Lipopolysaccharide comes from gram-negative bacteria, so it is a pathogen-associated pattern. Bacterial flagellin is a microbial product rather than a host-derived signal. Unmethylated CpG DNA is a microbial nucleic acid motif. Bacterial peptidoglycan is a cell wall component of bacteria, not of damaged host cells. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 7, macromolecular debris and DAMPs). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pathogen-associated molecular patterns include LPS from gram-negative bacteria, bacterial flagellin, and viral nucleic acids (First Aid Immunology—Lymphoid Structures, pathogen recognition, p. 97). COMLEX: DAMPs act on macrophages and dendritic cells to keep cytokine secretion going (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — contributors to frailty","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize the effect of accumulation of damaged macromolecules in contributing to aging","batch":1,"stem":"The insoluble yellow-brown perinuclear wear-and-tear pigment of aging cells is composed of which material?","choices":{"A":"Iron stored within ferritin micelles","B":"Tyrosine-derived melanin polymers","C":"Oxidized lipid and protein polymers","D":"Unconjugated bilirubin crystals","E":"Calcium phosphate mineral deposits"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lipofuscin is made of cross-linked polymers of oxidized lipids and proteins derived from lipid peroxidation of subcellular membranes. Iron in ferritin micelles is hemosiderin. Tyrosine-derived polymers are melanin. Unconjugated bilirubin crystals form from heme breakdown. Calcium phosphate deposits are pathologic calcification. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 8, lipofuscin); Robbins Ch. 2 (Lipofuscin). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lipofuscin is the yellow-brown wear-and-tear pigment of normal aging (First Aid Pathology—Aging, lipofuscin, p. 225). COMLEX: lipofuscin itself is not injurious, but it marks past free radical injury (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — age-related eye changes and vision","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize the effect of accumulation of damaged macromolecules in contributing to aging","batch":1,"stem":"Free radicals that attack cellular proteins characteristically create which abnormal covalent links between protein chains?","choices":{"A":"Glycosidic bonds","B":"Disulfide bonds","C":"Phosphodiester bonds","D":"Thioester bonds","E":"Phosphoanhydride bonds"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Free radicals oxidize amino acid side chains and form abnormal covalent protein-protein cross-links such as disulfide bonds, which inactivate enzymes and promote misfolding. Glycosidic bonds link sugars. Phosphodiester bonds link nucleotides. Thioester bonds join acyl groups to thiols, as in acetyl-CoA. Phosphoanhydride bonds link the phosphates of ATP. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 8, oxidative modification of proteins); Robbins Ch. 2 (Free radicals). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: transition metals such as iron and copper generate free radicals through the Fenton reaction (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, free radical injury, p. 206). COMLEX: free radical injury also damages lipids and DNA (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — age-related eye changes and vision","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize the effect of accumulation of damaged macromolecules in contributing to aging","batch":1,"stem":"Advanced glycation end products form through which type of reaction?","choices":{"A":"Enzymatic glycosylation in the Golgi","B":"Lysyl oxidase cross-linking of collagen","C":"Ubiquitin ligase tagging of proteins","D":"Nonenzymatic binding of reducing sugars","E":"Transglutaminase cross-linking of fibrin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Advanced glycation end products form when reducing sugars react covalently and nonenzymatically with proteins and lipids, creating stable, irreversible cross-linked complexes. Golgi glycosylation is enzymatic and regulated. Lysyl oxidase forms normal collagen cross-links. Ubiquitin ligases add an enzymatic, regulated modification rather than a nonenzymatic cross-link. Transglutaminase, factor XIII, cross-links fibrin in clots. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 8, advanced glycation end products); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Glycation). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HbA1c, a glycated hemoglobin, reflects average blood glucose over the prior 3 months (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, diabetes mellitus, p. 350). COMLEX: glycation is one of the chemical reactions thought to accelerate aging (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — age-related eye changes and vision","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify the proposed causes of cellular aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man comes to the physician because of 10 months of progressive weakness of both hands and difficulty swallowing. His father died of a similar illness at age 70. Examination shows atrophy and fasciculations of the intrinsic hand muscles and tongue, hyperreflexia, and bilateral extensor plantar responses. Electromyography shows widespread active denervation in three limbs, and genetic testing identifies a pathogenic SOD1 variant. Which proposed cause of cellular aging is most directly illustrated by the injury to this patient's motor neurons?","choices":{"A":"Telomere attrition","B":"Loss of proteostasis","C":"Defective DNA repair","D":"Dysregulated nutrient sensing","E":"Stem cell exhaustion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Familial amyotrophic lateral sclerosis with a mutant SOD1 protein is one of the age-related degenerative disorders in which toxic, non-degradable protein aggregates injure cells, the picture of defective protein homeostasis. Telomere attrition limits division of mitotic cells and does not explain aggregate injury in postmitotic neurons. Defective DNA repair underlies the progeroid syndromes. Dysregulated nutrient sensing concerns growth signaling rather than aggregate injury. Stem cell exhaustion concerns failing tissue replenishment. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 1; Obj 5, impaired proteostasis in ALS); Robbins Ch. 2 (Cellular aging). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ALS combines upper and lower motor neuron signs, and familial cases can carry SOD1 mutations (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, p. 544). COMLEX: amyloid-beta, tau, and mutant SOD1 are among the aggregate-forming proteins named for proteostasis failure (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 7 — features of ALS","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify the proposed causes of cellular aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man comes to the physician because his rings and shoes no longer fit and he has had daily headaches for 2 years. He has hypertension and was diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus 6 months ago. Examination shows coarse facial features, a protruding jaw, widely spaced lower teeth, and large, doughy hands. Serum insulin-like growth factor 1 concentration is 780 ng/mL (reference range: 90–360 ng/mL), and MRI shows a 1.4-cm sellar mass. Chronic excess of the circulating mediator measured in this patient would be expected to accelerate which proposed cause of cellular aging?","choices":{"A":"Defective DNA damage repair","B":"Replicative telomere attrition","C":"Chaperone network failure","D":"Dysregulated nutrient sensing","E":"Sterile chronic inflammation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Excess growth hormone drives high IGF-1, and persistently high activity of the somatotrophic and IGF-1/mTOR axis is the nutrient-sensing hallmark that accelerates cellular senescence by keeping metabolic and synthetic workloads high. Defective DNA repair refers to unrepaired genomic lesions. Telomere attrition refers to end-replication loss with each division. Chaperone failure belongs to loss of proteostasis. Sterile chronic inflammation refers to inflammaging from senescent cells and debris. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 1; Obj 6, insulin/IGF-1/mTOR cascade). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acromegaly is diagnosed by an elevated serum IGF-1, because GH levels fluctuate through the day, and by failure of GH suppression after an oral glucose load (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, acromegaly, p. 343). COMLEX: attenuated IGF-1 signaling extends lifespan and healthy aging in animal models (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Classify the proposed causes of cellular aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 6 months of fatigue and reduced stamina on her daily walks. She has hypertension and knee osteoarthritis treated with amlodipine and acetaminophen. Her temperature is 36.8°C (98.2°F), and examination shows bony knee enlargement without warmth, effusion, or rash. Laboratory studies show a C-reactive protein of 14 mg/L (reference range: 0–10 mg/L), interleukin-6 of 9 pg/mL (reference range: 0–5 pg/mL), and normal TSH, and blood and urine cultures are negative. Which proposed cause of cellular aging do these laboratory findings most directly represent?","choices":{"A":"Defective DNA damage repair","B":"Replicative telomere attrition","C":"Chronic sterile inflammation","D":"Dysregulated nutrient sensing","E":"Chaperone network failure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A persistent, sterile, low-grade rise in circulating IL-6 and C-reactive protein in an older adult is inflammaging, the hallmark in which chronic proinflammatory cytokine secretion alters intercellular communication and damages tissue. Defective DNA damage repair is a separate hallmark that does not raise acute-phase markers. Replicative telomere attrition limits cell division without producing this cytokine profile. Dysregulated nutrient sensing concerns growth signaling rather than acute-phase proteins. Chaperone network failure produces misfolded proteins, not a raised CRP. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 1; Obj 7, inflammaging). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-6 causes fever and stimulates hepatic production of acute-phase proteins such as C-reactive protein (First Aid Immunology—Immune Responses, interleukin-6, p. 106; Pathology—Inflammation, acute-phase reactants, p. 209). COMLEX: inflammaging is driven by senescent cells, DAMPs, and NF-κB activation (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — resilience and homeostenosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the concept of DNA damage","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old man comes to the physician because of 4 months of fatigue and shortness of breath on exertion. He is a retired accountant, does not smoke or drink alcohol, and has never received chemotherapy or radiation. Examination shows conjunctival pallor and scattered petechiae on both shins. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin of 9.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume of 104 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), leukocyte count of 2,900/mm3 (reference range: 4,500–11,000/mm3), and platelet count of 88,000/mm3 (reference range: 150,000–400,000/mm3); vitamin B12 and folate levels are normal, and bone marrow biopsy shows a hypercellular marrow with dysplastic erythroid and myeloid precursors and 3% blasts. The rising incidence of this disorder with age is best explained by which process in hematopoietic stem cells?","choices":{"A":"Reactivation of telomerase activity","B":"Overexpression of heat shock proteins","C":"Increased sirtuin deacetylase activity","D":"Accumulation of somatic mutations","E":"Deposition of lipofuscin granules"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hematopoietic stem cells acquire about 14 new mutations per year because DNA repair is incomplete and declines with age, which explains why myelodysplasia, leukemias, and other malignancies predominate in older adults. Telomerase reactivation does not generate the new mutations that accumulate with age. More heat shock proteins would improve protein folding. Sirtuin activity promotes DNA repair. Lipofuscin marks oxidative wear and is not mutagenic. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 2, DNA damage). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: myelodysplastic syndromes are stem cell disorders with ineffective hematopoiesis, bone marrow blasts below 20%, and a higher frequency in older adults (First Aid Hematology and Oncology—Pathology, myelodysplastic syndromes, p. 436). COMLEX: DNA damage accumulates in both nuclear and mitochondrial genomes throughout life (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — the nine hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the concept of DNA damage","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman comes to the physician because of blurred vision in both eyes and hair graying that began in her early twenties. She was diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus at age 30, and her parents are first cousins. Examination shows short stature, thin, taut skin over the hands and feet, bilateral lens opacities, and shallow ulcers over both ankles. Fasting glucose is 168 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and fibroblasts cultured from a skin biopsy stop dividing after far fewer population doublings than age-matched control cells. A defect in which cellular function most likely underlies this patient's condition?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear lamin A processing","B":"Nucleotide excision repair","C":"DNA helicase unwinding","D":"DNA mismatch repair","E":"Lysosomal enzyme trafficking"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adult-onset premature aging with cataracts, early graying, diabetes, and scleroderma-like skin in a child of consanguineous parents fits Werner syndrome, caused by a defective DNA helicase needed for replication, unwinding, and repair, which leads to chromosomal instability. Abnormal lamin A processing causes Hutchinson-Gilford progeria, which begins in early childhood. Nucleotide excision repair defects cause xeroderma pigmentosum with photosensitivity and skin cancers. Mismatch repair defects cause Lynch syndrome. Lysosomal trafficking defects cause I-cell disease. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 2, progeroid syndromes); Robbins Ch. 2 (DNA damage). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: xeroderma pigmentosum reflects failed repair of UV-induced pyrimidine dimers (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, DNA repair, p. 37). COMLEX: progeroid syndromes show that defective DNA repair is enough to produce premature aging (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the concept of DNA damage","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of frequent falls and slurred speech that have worsened since he began walking. He has had pneumonia twice and recurrent sinus infections, and his older sister has a similar unsteady gait. Examination shows a wide-based gait, dysarthria, and dilated, tortuous vessels on the bulbar conjunctivae. Laboratory studies show a serum alpha-fetoprotein of 62 ng/mL (reference range: 0–10 ng/mL) and an undetectable serum IgA, and MRI shows cerebellar atrophy. The gene product that is defective in this patient normally functions as which type of enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Protein kinase","B":"DNA helicase","C":"DNA glycosylase","D":"Reverse transcriptase","E":"Lipid phosphatase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cerebellar ataxia, oculocutaneous telangiectasias, sinopulmonary infections, IgA deficiency, and a high alpha-fetoprotein indicate ataxia-telangiectasia, caused by mutation of ATM, a kinase that senses DNA double-strand breaks. A DNA helicase is defective in Werner and Bloom syndromes. DNA glycosylases start base excision repair. Reverse transcriptase activity belongs to telomerase. Lipid phosphatase activity belongs to PTEN. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 2, Bloom syndrome and ataxia-telangiectasia). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: patients with ataxia-telangiectasia are sensitive to radiation, so x-ray exposure is limited (First Aid Immunology—Immunodeficiencies, ataxia-telangiectasia, p. 115). COMLEX: failed double-strand break repair causes genomic instability, neurodegeneration, and early tissue senescence (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 6 — spinocerebellar degenerative diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the importance of telomeres and the changes which cause cellular senesence","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man comes to the physician because of 1 year of progressive shortness of breath on exertion and a dry cough. He has never smoked, and his mother died of bone marrow failure at age 58. His oxygen saturation is 91% on room air, and examination shows digital clubbing and fine bibasilar end-inspiratory crackles. Hemoglobin is 11.4 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL) with a mean corpuscular volume of 103 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL); CT of the chest shows subpleural reticulation with honeycombing, and genetic testing shows a loss-of-function variant in the gene for the catalytic subunit of telomerase. Critically shortened chromosome ends in this patient's alveolar epithelial cells most likely halt their division by triggering which response?","choices":{"A":"Unfolded protein response","B":"Heat shock protein response","C":"Type I interferon response","D":"Persistent DNA damage response","E":"Hypoxia-inducible factor response"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Once telomeres shorten past a critical threshold, the uncapped chromosome ends are read as broken double-stranded DNA, and the persistent DNA damage response forces the cell into irreversible G1 arrest. The unfolded protein response answers misfolded proteins in the ER. The heat shock response raises chaperone levels during proteotoxic stress. The type I interferon response answers viral nucleic acids. Hypoxia-inducible factor responds to low oxygen. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 3, induction of senescence; cancer vs premature aging); Robbins Ch. 2 (Telomere attrition). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is a progressive fibrotic lung disease associated with tobacco smoking, environmental pollutants, and genetic defects (First Aid Respiratory—Pathology, idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, p. 694). COMLEX: the arrest that follows telomere uncapping occurs in G1 and is irreversible (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — pulmonary conditions associated with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the importance of telomeres and the changes which cause cellular senesence","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a painless lump in her left breast that she noticed 2 months ago. She had menarche at age 11, has never been pregnant, and took combined estrogen-progestin therapy for 8 years after menopause. Examination shows a firm, fixed 2.5-cm mass in the upper outer quadrant of the left breast and a hard left axillary lymph node. Mammography shows a spiculated mass, core biopsy shows invasive ductal carcinoma, and the tumor cells keep dividing in culture well past the number of doublings at which her normal breast fibroblasts stop. Which change most likely allows these tumor cells to keep dividing?","choices":{"A":"Telomerase reactivation","B":"Increased p16 expression","C":"Increased p21 expression","D":"Hypophosphorylated RB protein","E":"Accelerated telomere shortening"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Immortalized malignant cells characteristically reactivate telomerase, which stabilizes telomere length and lets them divide indefinitely past the Hayflick limit. Increased p16 expression would inhibit cyclin-dependent kinases and enforce senescence. Increased p21 expression would likewise arrest the cell cycle. Hypophosphorylated RB is the active growth-suppressing form that blocks G1-to-S progression. Accelerated telomere shortening would hasten arrest. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 3, cancer vs premature aging); Robbins Ch. 2 (Telomere attrition). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: reactivation of telomerase to maintain and lengthen telomeres is a hallmark of cancer (First Aid Pathology—Neoplasia, hallmarks of cancer, p. 217). COMLEX: normal somatic cells have a finite replicative capacity and enter replicative senescence when it is spent (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — the nine hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the importance of telomeres and the changes which cause cellular senesence","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman comes to the physician because a shallow ulcer on her left shin has healed slowly over 3 months. She has hypertension treated with lisinopril and does not smoke. Examination shows a 2-cm clean, granulating ulcer surrounded by thin, atrophic skin, and the left dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are normal. Hemoglobin A1c is 5.4% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%), and dermal fibroblasts cultured from the wound edge stop dividing after fewer population doublings than fibroblasts from her 25-year-old granddaughter. The progressive loss of chromosome-end DNA in her fibroblasts with each division results from which property of DNA replication?","choices":{"A":"Exonuclease proofreading of new strands","B":"Mismatch repair of daughter strands","C":"Methylation of daughter-strand CpG sites","D":"Topoisomerase cleavage of the template","E":"Polymerase inability to copy 3' ends"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Conventional DNA polymerases do not fully duplicate the extreme 3' ends of linear chromosomes, so a small piece of telomeric DNA is lost with every division, the end-replication problem. Exonuclease proofreading removes misincorporated bases without shortening the chromosome. Mismatch repair corrects base pairing errors. CpG methylation is an epigenetic mark. Topoisomerase cleavage is resealed after relieving supercoils. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 3, end-replication problem); Robbins Ch. 2 (Telomere attrition). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: telomerase adds TTAGGG repeats to chromosome 3' ends to prevent loss of genetic material with each duplication (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, telomerase, p. 36). COMLEX: cells from older donors reach replicative senescence after fewer divisions than cells from young donors (general knowledge).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — factors in organ performance with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the role of tumor suppressor genes","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man comes to the physician because of 2 weeks of watery diarrhea and cramping during pelvic radiation therapy for rectal adenocarcinoma. He has taken no antibiotics recently, and his diet is unchanged. His temperature is 37.0°C (98.6°F), and abdominal examination shows mild diffuse tenderness without guarding. Serum potassium is 3.3 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and biopsy of nearby nontumorous rectal mucosa shows scattered shrunken, hypereosinophilic crypt cells with condensed, fragmented nuclei and little surrounding inflammation. In these crypt cells, the tumor suppressor protein activated by irreparable DNA damage most directly causes death through which event?","choices":{"A":"Activation of BAX and BAK","B":"Caspase-1 inflammasome activation","C":"Granzyme entry through perforin","D":"Ligation of TNF death receptors","E":"Lysosomal cathepsin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When DNA lesions are irreparable and stress is severe, sustained p53 activity upregulates pro-apoptotic BH3-only proteins that activate BAX and BAK, opening the intrinsic mitochondrial pathway of apoptosis seen as shrunken, eosinophilic cells with fragmented nuclei. Caspase-1 inflammasome activation produces pyroptosis. Granzyme entry through perforin is the cytotoxic T-cell route. TNF death receptors start the extrinsic pathway. Lysosomal cathepsin release is not the p53 death route. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 4, the p53 pathway); Robbins Ch. 2 (Apoptosis). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the intrinsic pathway, BAX and BAK form pores in the mitochondrial membrane that release cytochrome c and activate caspases (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, apoptosis, p. 204). COMLEX: radiation and cytotoxic anticancer drugs damage DNA, and irreparable damage triggers intrinsic apoptosis (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — iatrogenesis in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the role of tumor suppressor genes","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman comes to the physician because a mole on her back has enlarged and darkened over 3 months. Her mother and maternal uncle were treated for melanoma in their 40s. Examination shows a 9-mm asymmetric, irregularly bordered, variegated pigmented lesion and more than 50 atypical nevi on her trunk. Excisional biopsy shows a superficial spreading melanoma with a Breslow depth of 0.8 mm, and germline testing shows a loss-of-function variant at the CDKN2A locus. Loss of this gene product most directly allows which cell-cycle event to proceed unchecked?","choices":{"A":"G2-to-M phase transition","B":"Metaphase-to-anaphase transition","C":"G1-to-S phase transition","D":"Cytokinesis after telophase","E":"S-to-G2 phase transition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The CDKN2A product p16 keeps the retinoblastoma protein in its active, hypophosphorylated state by blocking CDK4 and CDK6, which prevents passage from G1 into S, so its loss lets mitogenic signals push cells into S phase. The G2-to-M transition is controlled by cyclin B and CDK1. The metaphase-to-anaphase step is controlled by the spindle checkpoint. Cytokinesis follows mitosis. Progression from S to G2 is not the step RB guards. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 4, CDKN2A locus). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CDKN2A encodes p16, which blocks G1, and is a tumor suppressor gene lost in many cancers such as melanoma, lung, and pancreatic cancer (First Aid Pathology—Neoplasia, tumor suppressor genes, p. 220). COMLEX: in aging tissue the same p16 brake removes stem cells from the proliferating pool (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — genetic versus epigenetic contributions to aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the role of tumor suppressor genes","batch":2,"stem":"A 77-year-old man comes to the physician because of fatigue 10 weeks after completing chemotherapy for diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. He has had no fever or bleeding and takes no other medications. Examination shows pallor and no lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), leukocyte count is 3,100/mm3 (reference range: 4,500–11,000/mm3), and platelet count is 96,000/mm3 (reference range: 150,000–400,000/mm3); bone marrow biopsy shows 5% cellularity (reference range for age: 15–35%) without lymphoma, and immunohistochemical staining of the biopsy shows strong expression of the cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor encoded at CDKN2A in CD34-positive precursor cells. Sustained activity of this tumor suppressor pathway most likely contributes to his poor marrow recovery through which effect?","choices":{"A":"Increased malignant transformation","B":"Reduced stem cell self-renewal","C":"Increased telomerase activity","D":"Accelerated protein aggregation","E":"Enhanced mTOR-driven growth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tumor suppressor pathways protect against cancer by arresting damaged cells, but their persistence drives aging by halting stem cell proliferation and depleting functional tissue reserve. Malignant transformation is what these pathways prevent. Increased telomerase activity is not a consequence of p16. Protein aggregation reflects proteostasis failure. mTOR-driven growth opposes the arrest that p16 enforces. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 4, role of tumor suppressor genes). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hematopoietic reserve falls with age, so older adults respond less vigorously to stressors such as blood loss (First Aid Pathology—Aging, hematopoietic, p. 225). COMLEX: senescence is actively enforced by tumor suppressor signaling rather than being passive decay (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — resilience and homeostenosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the influence of protein homeostasis as it influences cellular aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her daughter because of 2 years of progressive forgetfulness; she now repeats questions and has gotten lost driving to familiar places. She has hypertension, and her mother developed dementia in her 80s. Examination shows a Mini-Mental State Examination score of 19/30 with impaired delayed recall and no focal neurologic deficits. MRI shows bilateral hippocampal atrophy, and cerebrospinal fluid analysis shows a low amyloid-beta 42 concentration and an elevated phosphorylated tau concentration. An age-related decline in which cellular process most directly permits the protein deposits in her brain to accumulate?","choices":{"A":"Base excision DNA repair","B":"Telomere maintenance by telomerase","C":"Insulin/IGF-1 receptor signaling","D":"NF-κB cytokine transcription","E":"Autophagy and proteasome function"},"correct":"E","explanation":"With aging, chaperone expression drops and the ubiquitin-proteasome and autophagy-lysosome systems lose activity, so misfolded amyloid-beta and tau accumulate as toxic, non-degradable aggregates. Base excision repair fixes damaged DNA bases. Telomere maintenance concerns dividing cells, not neuronal protein clearance. Declining insulin/IGF-1 signaling is protective in aging models. NF-κB transcription drives inflammation rather than protein clearance. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 5, defective proteostasis). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alzheimer disease shows extracellular amyloid-beta plaques and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles of hyperphosphorylated tau (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, Alzheimer disease, p. 534). COMLEX: enhancing autophagy prolongs longevity in experimental models (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the influence of protein homeostasis as it influences cellular aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man comes to the physician because of 1 year of a right-hand tremor that is worse at rest and slowness when buttoning his shirts. He has had constipation and a reduced sense of smell for several years and takes no antipsychotic or antiemetic drugs. Examination shows a resting tremor of the right hand, cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia, and reduced arm swing on the right. Dopamine transporter SPECT shows reduced tracer uptake in the left putamen more than the right. Which misfolded protein most likely forms the aggregates that injure this patient's neurons?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-synuclein","B":"Amyloid-beta","C":"Phosphorylated tau","D":"Prion protein","E":"Huntingtin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Asymmetric resting tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia with reduced striatal dopamine transporter uptake indicate Parkinson disease, whose neurons accumulate alpha-synuclein aggregates when proteostasis fails. Amyloid-beta forms the plaques of Alzheimer disease. Phosphorylated tau forms neurofibrillary tangles. Misfolded prion protein accumulates in prion diseases. Huntingtin aggregates in Huntington disease. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 5, aggregates in degenerative disorders). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lewy bodies are intracellular eosinophilic inclusions of alpha-synuclein in the affected neurons of Parkinson disease (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, Parkinson disease, p. 534). COMLEX: the core motor features are resting tremor, cogwheel or lead-pipe rigidity, akinesia or bradykinesia, and postural instability (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, Parkinson disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — pathophysiology of Parkinson disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the influence of protein homeostasis as it influences cellular aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man comes to the physician because of 3 months of low back pain and fatigue. He has had no trauma and takes ibuprofen as needed. Examination shows pallor and point tenderness over the L2 vertebra. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin of 9.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), serum calcium of 11.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL), creatinine of 1.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and total protein of 9.2 g/dL (reference range: 6.0–7.8 g/dL); serum protein electrophoresis shows a monoclonal IgG spike, and bone marrow biopsy shows 40% clonal plasma cells. He begins treatment with a drug that inhibits the 26S proteasome. Which material most likely builds up in his malignant plasma cells as a result of this drug?","choices":{"A":"Iron-laden hemosiderin","B":"Polyubiquitinated proteins","C":"Cytoplasmic glycogen granules","D":"Neutral triglyceride droplets","E":"Lipofuscin residual bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the ubiquitin-proteasome system, misfolded or short-lived proteins carry ubiquitin chains that direct them to the 26S proteasome, so blocking the proteasome leaves ubiquitin-tagged proteins to accumulate and provoke ER stress. Hemosiderin is stored iron. Glycogen granules are stored glucose. Triglyceride droplets reflect lipid storage. Lipofuscin is an oxidized lipid-protein wear-and-tear pigment. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 5, ubiquitin-proteasome system). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: multiple myeloma presents with hypercalcemia, renal insufficiency, anemia, and lytic bone lesions (First Aid Hematology and Oncology—Pathology, multiple myeloma, p. 436). COMLEX: proteasome inhibitors such as bortezomib cause arrest at G2-M through accumulation of abnormal proteins, which leads to apoptosis (First Aid Hematology and Oncology—Pharmacology, bortezomib).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — secondary causes of osteoporosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Relate the concept of dysregulated nutrient sensing to it's influence of cellular aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man comes to the physician because of fatigue and a 15-kg weight gain over 5 years. He eats large portions of refined carbohydrates, drinks sugar-sweetened beverages daily, and does not exercise. His BMI is 38 kg/m2 and blood pressure is 142/90 mm Hg, and examination shows central obesity and acanthosis nigricans on the neck. Laboratory studies show a fasting glucose of 118 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), fasting insulin of 34 μU/mL (reference range: 2–20 μU/mL), triglycerides of 240 mg/dL (reference range: 50–150 mg/dL), and alanine aminotransferase of 58 U/L (reference range: 10–40 U/L). In this patient's cells, chronic nutrient excess most likely produces which change in longevity-related signaling?","choices":{"A":"Reduced mTOR activity","B":"Increased autophagic flux","C":"Reduced IGF-1 signaling","D":"Reduced sirtuin activity","E":"Increased NAD+ levels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Under persistent nutrient excess, sirtuin-mediated protective programs are suppressed while the IGF-1/mTOR axis stays overstimulated, which accelerates cellular aging. mTOR activity rises with nutrient excess. Autophagic flux falls rather than rises when mTOR is active. Insulin/IGF-1 pathway signaling stays high in nutrient excess, driven by his hyperinsulinemia. NAD+ rises with fasting, not overfeeding. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 6, dysregulated nutrient sensing). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acanthosis nigricans is a skin marker of insulin resistance (First Aid Dermatology—Pathology, acanthosis nigricans, p. 492). COMLEX: high mTOR activity promotes protein synthesis and cell growth while suppressing autophagy (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Relate the concept of dysregulated nutrient sensing to it's influence of cellular aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman comes to the physician for a routine visit 2 years after a kidney transplant for autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease. Six months ago, her immunosuppression was switched from a calcineurin inhibitor to a drug that binds FKBP12 and inhibits the mechanistic target of rapamycin. She feels well, and examination shows a well-healed graft scar and no edema. Serum creatinine is 1.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and fasting triglycerides are 210 mg/dL (reference range: 50–150 mg/dL). In animal models, drugs with this mechanism extend lifespan through which cellular effect?","choices":{"A":"Activation of telomerase","B":"Upregulation of autophagy","C":"Increased IGF-1 signaling","D":"Increased protein synthesis","E":"Inhibition of sirtuins"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pharmacologic mTOR inhibition by rapamycin slows cell growth and metabolism and upregulates protective autophagy, which extends lifespan and healthy aging in animal models. Telomerase activation is not the mechanism. IGF-1 signaling acts upstream of mTOR, and more of it accelerates senescence. Protein synthesis falls when mTOR is blocked. Sirtuin inhibition would remove protective programs. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 6, IGF-1/mTOR cascade); Robbins Ch. 2 (Nutrient sensing). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sirolimus binds FKBP and inhibits mTOR, and hyperlipidemia is a known adverse effect (First Aid Immunology—Immunosuppressants, sirolimus, p. 118). COMLEX: reduced insulin/IGF and TOR signaling are the best-understood longevity pathways (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Relate the concept of dysregulated nutrient sensing to it's influence of cellular aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with prediabetes returns for follow-up after 12 months in a supervised program that cut her caloric intake by 25% while keeping her diet nutritionally complete. She has hypertension treated with lisinopril. Her BMI has fallen from 31 to 26 kg/m2, and her blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Over the year, fasting glucose has fallen from 118 to 94 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and fasting insulin has fallen from 26 to 9 μU/mL (reference range: 2–20 μU/mL). Which additional cellular effect would the energy-sensing enzymes activated by this intervention most likely produce?","choices":{"A":"Increased apoptotic cell death","B":"Enhanced mTOR-driven growth","C":"Reduced chaperone-mediated folding","D":"Greater IGF-1 receptor signaling","E":"Upregulated antioxidant defenses"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Caloric restriction lowers cellular energy and raises NAD+, which activates sirtuins; besides increasing insulin sensitivity and glucose metabolism, they upregulate antioxidant defenses that counter reactive oxygen species. Sirtuins reduce apoptosis rather than increase it. mTOR-driven growth is a nutrient-excess signal that caloric restriction lowers. Sirtuins promote chaperone-mediated folding rather than reduce it. Greater IGF-1 receptor signaling reflects nutrient abundance, which caloric restriction reduces. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 6, the sirtuin pathway). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: besides scavenging enzymes and antioxidant vitamins, free radicals are cleared by spontaneous decay and by metal carrier proteins such as transferrin and ceruloplasmin (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, free radical injury, p. 206). COMLEX: lifestyle measures such as weight loss in prediabetes are a form of primary prevention (general knowledge).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the contribution of inflammation to the aging process","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old man is brought to the physician by his son because of 8 months of progressive weakness, slower walking, and difficulty rising from a chair. He has lost 5 kg unintentionally and has type 2 diabetes mellitus controlled with metformin. Examination shows reduced thigh and calf muscle bulk, a slow gait, and weak grip strength with normal reflexes and sensation. Serum albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL) and creatine kinase is 60 U/L (reference range: 25–90 U/L), and a research biopsy of his thigh muscle shows an accumulation of nondividing, beta-galactosidase-positive cells surrounded by macrophages and degraded matrix. Chronic exposure to factors released by the accumulated cells most likely contributes to his weakness through which effect on skeletal muscle?","choices":{"A":"Increased glycogen storage","B":"Denervation from demyelination","C":"Acetylcholine receptor blockade","D":"Accelerated proteolysis","E":"Increased fiber hypertrophy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Senescent cells, marked here by senescence-associated beta-galactosidase, release proinflammatory cytokines, chemokines, and proteases, and chronic exposure to these mediators creates a catabolic neurohormonal state that accelerates skeletal muscle protein breakdown, driving sarcopenia and frailty. Increased glycogen storage would not cause wasting. Demyelinating neuropathy would reduce reflexes and sensation. Acetylcholine receptor blockade causes fatigable weakness without cytokine elevation. Fiber hypertrophy would increase muscle bulk. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 7, systemic effects of inflammaging). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: skeletal muscle mass declines with age, a change called sarcopenia (First Aid Pathology—Aging, musculoskeletal, p. 225). COMLEX: inflammaging also accelerates vascular stiffening and promotes type 2 diabetes (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — contributors to frailty","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the contribution of inflammation to the aging process","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman is evaluated on the second day after surgical repair of a right femoral neck fracture sustained in a fall at home. She has osteoporosis treated with alendronate. Her temperature is 38.0°C (100.4°F) and pulse is 96/min; the incision is clean without erythema or drainage, and the lungs are clear. Laboratory studies show a leukocyte count of 12,800/mm3 (reference range: 4,500–11,000/mm3), C-reactive protein of 88 mg/L (reference range: 0–10 mg/L), and creatine kinase of 1,450 U/L (reference range: 25–90 U/L); urinalysis, chest x-ray, and blood cultures are negative. Molecules released from her injured tissue most likely trigger this response by binding which receptors on macrophages?","choices":{"A":"T-cell antigen receptors","B":"Fc gamma immunoglobulin receptors","C":"Pattern-recognition receptors","D":"B-cell antigen receptors","E":"IGF-1 tyrosine kinase receptors"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Damaged and necrotic cells release damage-associated molecular patterns such as extracellular ATP and uric acid, which engage pattern-recognition receptors on macrophages and dendritic cells and trigger sterile cytokine secretion. T-cell antigen receptors recognize specific peptide antigens presented on MHC. B-cell antigen receptors recognize specific antigens on B lymphocytes. Fc gamma receptors bind antibody-coated targets. IGF-1 receptors carry growth signals. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 7, macromolecular debris and DAMPs). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Toll-like receptors are pattern-recognition receptors of innate immunity (First Aid Immunology—Lymphoid Structures, innate immunity, p. 97). COMLEX: uncleared debris keeps this signal active in older adults, sustaining inflammaging (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — negative outcomes of falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the contribution of inflammation to the aging process","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man comes to the physician for a routine visit. He has no symptoms, does not smoke, walks daily, and takes a statin started 1 year ago for an LDL cholesterol of 168 mg/dL (reference range: 0–129 mg/dL). His BMI is 27 kg/m2 and blood pressure is 138/82 mm Hg, and examination shows no joint swelling or rash. Repeated laboratory studies over 6 months show a high-sensitivity C-reactive protein that stays between 5.8 and 6.4 mg/L (reference range: 0–3.0 mg/L), with a normal complete blood count and negative antinuclear antibody testing. Persistent elevation of this marker most likely reflects a state that promotes which change in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle hypertrophy","B":"Stronger vaccine responses","C":"Higher bone mineral density","D":"Reduced arterial stiffness","E":"Coronary atherosclerosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic exposure to inflammaging mediators such as IL-6, TNF-alpha, and C-reactive protein promotes atherosclerosis. The same catabolic state breaks down skeletal muscle rather than enlarging it. Vaccine responses weaken rather than strengthen with immune aging. Bone mineral density is not increased by chronic inflammation. It accelerates vascular stiffening, so arterial stiffness rises. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 7, systemic effects of inflammaging); First Aid Pathology—Aging, immune, p. 225. Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inflammation and endothelial injury are central steps in the formation of atherosclerotic plaque (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, atherosclerosis, p. 305). COMLEX: IL-6, TNF-alpha, and C-reactive protein are the circulating markers that define inflammaging (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — pathobiology and risk factors of ischemic heart disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize the effect of accumulation of damaged macromolecules in contributing to aging","batch":2,"stem":"An 88-year-old man dies of pneumonia after 3 years of progressive frailty and weight loss. He had hypertension and chronic kidney disease and had lived in a nursing home for 2 years. At autopsy, the heart weighs 230 g (reference range: 250–350 g), is dark brown on cut section, and has mild coronary atherosclerosis. Microscopy shows small cardiac myocytes containing yellow-brown, finely granular perinuclear pigment that does not stain with Prussian blue. Which property best explains why this pigment builds up in his cardiac myocytes?","choices":{"A":"Protection by heat shock chaperones","B":"Continuous ribosomal resynthesis","C":"Resistance to lysosomal hydrolases","D":"Shielding by ferritin iron cores","E":"Rapid proteasomal recycling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lipofuscin resists degradation by lysosomal hydrolases and is not exported from cells, so it persists in long-lived postmitotic cells such as cardiac myocytes, hepatocytes, and neurons. Heat shock chaperones fold proteins and do not protect pigment. Lipofuscin is not a ribosomal product. A negative Prussian blue stain shows the pigment is not iron-containing hemosiderin. Proteasomal recycling would clear it rather than let it build up. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 8, lipofuscin); Robbins Ch. 2 (Lipofuscin). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autopsies of older adults show lipofuscin in heart, colon, liver, kidney, and eye (First Aid Pathology—Aging, lipofuscin, p. 225). COMLEX: abundant lipofuscin with atrophy gives the heart a brown color called brown atrophy (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — structural changes of the aging heart","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize the effect of accumulation of damaged macromolecules in contributing to aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man comes to the physician because of 2 years of progressive shortness of breath on exertion and a morning cough. He has smoked 1.5 packs of cigarettes daily for 45 years. Examination shows a barrel-shaped chest, prolonged expiration, and diffuse wheezes. Spirometry shows an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.58 (reference range: 0.70–0.85) that does not improve after a bronchodilator, and serum alpha-1 antitrypsin is 160 mg/dL (reference range: 100–200 mg/dL). Free radicals in inhaled smoke most likely injure the membranes of his airway epithelial cells by which initial reaction?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of phospholipid ester bonds","B":"Oxidation of polyunsaturated fatty acids","C":"Glycation of membrane surface proteins","D":"Calpain cleavage of cytoskeletal spectrin","E":"Complement attack on membrane lipids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Reactive oxygen species attack the double bonds of polyunsaturated fatty acids in cell and organelle membranes, starting an autocatalytic chain of lipid peroxidation that destroys membrane fluidity and permeability and ends in necrosis or ferroptosis. Phospholipid hydrolysis is the work of calcium-activated phospholipases. Glycation is a nonenzymatic sugar reaction. Calpain cleavage of spectrin is calcium-driven protease damage. Complement forms membrane attack complexes rather than oxidizing lipids. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 8, lipid peroxidation by free radicals); Robbins Ch. 2 (Free radicals). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbon tetrachloride is converted by cytochrome P-450 into a free radical that causes fatty liver and centrilobular necrosis (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, free radical injury, p. 206). COMLEX: lung compliance rises and elastic recoil falls with normal aging (First Aid Pathology, Aging).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — pulmonary conditions associated with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize the effect of accumulation of damaged macromolecules in contributing to aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman comes for a follow-up visit for type 2 diabetes mellitus diagnosed 22 years ago and poorly controlled despite metformin and insulin. She reports gradually blurred vision and notes that her skin has become thin and less elastic. Her blood pressure is 168/72 mm Hg, and examination shows bilateral lens opacities and bounding peripheral pulses. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin A1c of 9.6% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%) and fasting glucose of 212 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and carotid-femoral pulse wave velocity is markedly increased. Which molecular change best explains how her chronic hyperglycemia has widened her pulse pressure?","choices":{"A":"Glycation cross-links in collagen","B":"Increased nitric oxide release","C":"Increased elastin fiber synthesis","D":"Lipofuscin in medial myocytes","E":"Telomerase loss in endothelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reducing sugars bind proteins nonenzymatically to form advanced glycation end products that accumulate in vascular walls and extracellular matrix and cross-link collagen, reducing compliance and stiffening large arteries, which widens pulse pressure. More nitric oxide would relax and dilate arteries. More elastin would increase compliance. Lipofuscin in medial cells is a marker of oxidative wear without effect on wall stiffness. Telomerase is already repressed in most adult endothelium. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 8, advanced glycation end products); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Glycation). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: arterial compliance falls and stiffness rises with normal aging (First Aid Pathology—Aging, cardiovascular, p. 225). COMLEX: AGEs also contribute to cataracts, skin aging, and neurodegeneration (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — central elastic arteries and systolic hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the concept of DNA damage","batch":3,"stem":"A 78-year-old man acquires new somatic mutations in sun-protected skin cells each year at a faster rate than he did at age 40. His exposures have not changed. Which change best explains the faster rate?","choices":{"A":"Less efficient DNA repair","B":"More telomerase activity","C":"More sirtuin activity","D":"More chaperone synthesis","E":"Fewer reactive oxygen species"},"correct":"A","explanation":"DNA repair fidelity is incomplete and repair efficiency declines with age, so lesions accumulate in nuclear and mitochondrial genomes over life. Telomerase is repressed in most somatic cells and does not create mutations. Sirtuins promote DNA repair. Chaperones fold proteins. Fewer reactive oxygen species would mean fewer lesions. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 2, DNA damage); Robbins Ch. 2 (DNA damage). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: radiotherapy harms tumor cells more than normal cells because tumor cells combine faulty DNA repair with rapid replication (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, ionizing radiation toxicity, p. 207). COMLEX: in model organisms, caloric restriction may switch on DNA repair and slow aging (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q02","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the importance of telomeres and the changes which cause cellular senesence","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman carries a telomerase gene variant, and the telomeres in her blood cells are critically short. Without their protective caps, her chromosome ends are prone to which abnormal event?","choices":{"A":"Centromere duplication","B":"Meiotic nondisjunction","C":"End-to-end fusion","D":"Histone hyperacetylation","E":"Ribosomal DNA loss"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Telomeres cap chromosome ends and keep them from fraying, fusing end to end, or being recognized as double-strand breaks, so uncapped ends are prone to fusion. Centromere duplication is not a consequence of losing telomere caps. Nondisjunction is a segregation error in meiosis. Histone hyperacetylation is an epigenetic change. Ribosomal DNA loss is unrelated to telomere capping. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 3, telomeres); Robbins Ch. 2 (Telomere attrition). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: telomerase, found only in eukaryotes, is an RNA-dependent DNA polymerase (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, telomerase, p. 36). COMLEX: engineered mice with shortened telomeres have reduced life spans that telomerase activation restores to normal (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — cellular damage, aging, and organ dysfunction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the role of tumor suppressor genes","batch":3,"stem":"After intense sun exposure, DNA-damaged keratinocytes of a 55-year-old woman activate a tumor suppressor protein that holds them in G1 by inducing the CDK inhibitor p21. This tumor suppressor protein belongs to which functional class?","choices":{"A":"Receptor tyrosine kinase","B":"GTP-binding protein","C":"Ligand-gated ion channel","D":"Transcription factor","E":"Cell adhesion molecule"},"correct":"D","explanation":"DNA damage sensors activate p53, a transcription factor that upregulates the CDK inhibitor p21 to arrest the cell at G1. Receptor tyrosine kinases transmit growth factor signals. GTP-binding proteins such as RAS relay mitogenic signals. Ligand-gated ion channels conduct ions. Cell adhesion molecules link cells to each other and to matrix. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 4, the p53 pathway). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: p53 induces p21, which inhibits CDKs and keeps Rb hypophosphorylated to block G1-to-S progression (First Aid Biochemistry—Cellular, cell cycle, p. 44). COMLEX: arrest at G1 gives the cell time for repair before replication (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — genetic versus epigenetic contributions to aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q04","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the influence of protein homeostasis as it influences cellular aging","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has neurons in which misfolded proteins are not tagged for destruction by the 26S proteasome. Which enzyme class normally adds this tag?","choices":{"A":"Protein kinases","B":"Ubiquitin ligases","C":"Histone acetylases","D":"DNA glycosylases","E":"Glycosyltransferases"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ubiquitin ligases covalently attach ubiquitin chains to misfolded or short-lived proteins and direct them to the 26S proteasome. Protein kinases add phosphate groups. Histone acetylases modify chromatin. DNA glycosylases remove damaged DNA bases. Glycosyltransferases add sugar residues to proteins and lipids. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 5, ubiquitin-proteasome system). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: posttranslational covalent alterations include phosphorylation, glycosylation, hydroxylation, methylation, acetylation, and ubiquitination (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, posttranslational modifications, p. 43). COMLEX: misfolded aggregates of tau, amyloid-beta, and alpha-synuclein cause direct cytotoxicity (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q05","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Identify the contribution of inflammation to the aging process","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has aging macrophages that fail to clear damaged mitochondria, which keeps a proinflammatory transcription factor active and his circulating cytokines raised for years. This chronic state most promotes which endocrine disease?","choices":{"A":"Type 1 diabetes mellitus","B":"Graves hyperthyroidism","C":"Primary adrenal insufficiency","D":"Primary hyperparathyroidism","E":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic exposure to inflammaging mediators induces a catabolic neurohormonal state and promotes type 2 diabetes. Type 1 diabetes results from autoimmune destruction of beta cells. Graves hyperthyroidism results from thyroid-stimulating autoantibodies. Primary adrenal insufficiency usually reflects autoimmune destruction of the adrenal cortex. Primary hyperparathyroidism usually results from a parathyroid adenoma. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 7, NF-κB activation with autophagy defects; systemic effects of inflammaging). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: type 2 diabetes reflects insulin resistance with progressive pancreatic β-cell failure and is associated with obesity (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, type 1 vs type 2 diabetes mellitus, p. 351). COMLEX: immunosenescence impairs responses to new antigens such as vaccines (First Aid Pathology, Aging).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — contributors to frailty","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q06","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize the effect of accumulation of damaged macromolecules in contributing to aging","batch":3,"stem":"At autopsy, cortical neurons from an 84-year-old woman contain golden-brown cytoplasmic granules that are negative on an iron stain. Electron microscopy places the granules inside membrane-bound vacuoles along with undigested lamellar debris. In which structures is this pigment stored?","choices":{"A":"Autophagic residual bodies","B":"Golgi secretory vesicles","C":"Rough endoplasmic reticulum","D":"Mitochondrial matrix granules","E":"Nuclear inclusion bodies"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lipofuscin, the wear-and-tear pigment of aging, resists lysosomal digestion and stays inside autophagic vacuoles as residual bodies in long-lived cells such as neurons, cardiac myocytes, and hepatocytes. Golgi secretory vesicles carry products for export. Rough endoplasmic reticulum synthesizes proteins. Mitochondrial matrix granules store calcium. Nuclear inclusions are not where this pigment collects. Sources: SDL 1 lecture notes (Obj 8, lipofuscin); Robbins Ch. 2 (Lipofuscin). Aligned with SDL 1 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lipofuscin may derive from lipid peroxidation of polyunsaturated lipids in subcellular membranes (First Aid Pathology—Aging, lipofuscin, p. 225). COMLEX: lipofuscin is a histologic marker of chronic oxidative stress and cellular aging (SDL 1 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — the nine hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":2,"title":"SDL 2 — Physiology of Aging: Cellular Mechanisms of Aging","questions":[{"id":"sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the concept of epigenetics as it relates to aging and contrast that to genetic contributions to aging","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following alters gene expression in aging cells without changing the underlying DNA nucleotide sequence?","choices":{"A":"Single-base substitution","B":"Frameshift deletion","C":"Chromatin remodeling","D":"Reciprocal translocation","E":"Trinucleotide repeat expansion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chromatin remodeling is one of the epigenetic mechanisms, along with other chemical changes to DNA and histones, that change how the cell reads a gene without altering its sequence. A single-base substitution changes one nucleotide. A frameshift deletion removes nucleotides and shifts the reading frame. A reciprocal translocation exchanges segments between chromosomes. A trinucleotide repeat expansion lengthens a repeated sequence. All four are genetic changes in the sequence itself. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 1, contrasting genetic and epigenetic mechanisms); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Epigenetics). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: heterochromatin is condensed and transcriptionally inactive, whereas euchromatin is less condensed and transcriptionally active (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, chromatin structure, p. 32). COMLEX: epigenetics overturned the older view that inherited DNA predetermines physical and emotional health outcomes (SDL 2 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — DNA damage and its sources","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the concept of epigenetics as it relates to aging and contrast that to genetic contributions to aging","batch":1,"stem":"Besides radiation and chemical toxins, which process generates the permanent gene mutations that somatic cells accumulate over a lifetime?","choices":{"A":"DNA replication errors","B":"X chromosome inactivation","C":"Genomic imprinting","D":"Noncoding RNA silencing","E":"Telomerase repression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gene mutations are lasting changes in the DNA sequence that arise from replication errors during somatic cell division or from physical and chemical mutagens. X chromosome inactivation silences genes epigenetically without changing the sequence. Genomic imprinting likewise silences one parental copy through methylation rather than mutation. Noncoding RNA silencing regulates expression without mutating DNA. Telomerase repression in most somatic cells lets telomeres shorten but does not itself create mutations. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 1, genetic factors); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (DNA is the master molecule of the cell). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in disorders of imprinting, one parental gene copy is silenced and the other copy is expressed, which produces parent-of-origin effects (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, disorders of imprinting, p. 56). COMLEX: not all mutations are harmful, and some, such as those shaping skin color and muscle mass, aided human adaptation (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — DNA damage and its sources","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the concept of epigenetics as it relates to aging and contrast that to genetic contributions to aging","batch":1,"stem":"Inherited genes influence a person's risk of age-related stroke, cancer, and kidney disease by setting which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Daily physical activity level","B":"Usual dietary protein intake","C":"Tobacco smoke exposure","D":"Baseline susceptibility","E":"Nightly sleep duration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Inherited genes establish a baseline susceptibility, or predisposition, to specific age-related conditions such as stroke, cancer, and kidney disease, and some inherited genes favor longevity. Daily physical activity is a lifestyle factor that acts on how genes are expressed. Usual dietary protein intake is a nutritional choice rather than an inherited trait. Tobacco smoke exposure is an environmental insult, not something set by genes. Nightly sleep duration is a behavior rather than an inherited baseline. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 1, genetic factors); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Genetic). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid defines normal aging as a gradual, time-dependent loss of organ function that leaves a person more susceptible to disease (First Aid Pathology—Aging, normal aging, p. 225). COMLEX: diet, exercise, social interaction, and good health habits can modify gene function and keep a person physiologically younger (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — DNA damage and its sources","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the epigenome and how it influences aging at the cellular level","batch":1,"stem":"Nerve, muscle, bone, and liver cells in one adult share essentially the same DNA blueprint. Which process does the epigenome direct to make these cell types different?","choices":{"A":"Somatic recombination","B":"Meiotic chromosome crossover","C":"Cellular differentiation","D":"Telomere lengthening","E":"Mitochondrial fission"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Trillions of cells in the body carry essentially identical DNA, and the epigenome instructs each cell whether to differentiate and function as nerve, muscle, bone, or liver tissue. Somatic recombination rearranges antigen receptor genes in lymphocytes. Meiotic crossover shuffles alleles in germ cells. Telomere lengthening by telomerase maintains chromosome ends. Mitochondrial fission divides mitochondria and does not set cell identity. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 2, cellular differentiation); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Epigenetics). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barr bodies are inactive X chromosomes that may be visible at the periphery of the nucleus (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, chromatin structure, p. 32). COMLEX: the epigenome sets a cell's growth and function as well as its type (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — the nine hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the epigenome and how it influences aging at the cellular level","batch":1,"stem":"The epigenome is the collection of molecular switches and chemical tags located where?","choices":{"A":"Within the DNA base sequence","B":"In the mitochondrial matrix","C":"Along the inner nuclear membrane","D":"On the outer surface of DNA","E":"Inside the ribosomal subunits"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The epigenome is the ensemble of molecular switches and chemical tags on the outer surface of a cell's DNA, which change how the DNA is read without changing it. A change within the base sequence would be a mutation. The mitochondrial matrix holds mitochondrial DNA and metabolic enzymes. The inner nuclear membrane borders the nucleus. Ribosomal subunits translate mRNA. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 2, definition of the epigenome); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Epigenetics). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mitochondria have their own circular DNA, which does not bind histones (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, chromatin structure, p. 32). COMLEX: daily choices such as exercise, diet, tobacco, and toxins act on these switches (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — the nine hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the epigenome and how it influences aging at the cellular level","batch":1,"stem":"Placing a mature somatic cell's nucleus into an oocyte whose own nucleus has been removed can reset the cell's aging clock. This finding shows that the aged state of the nucleus is carried by which feature?","choices":{"A":"Its epigenetic marks","B":"Its DNA sequence","C":"Its somatic mutations","D":"Its chromosome number","E":"Its mitochondrial DNA"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Somatic cell nuclear transfer resets the aging clock of a mature nucleus without changing its DNA, which shows that the aged state is a malleable epigenetic state rather than a fixed genetic one. The DNA sequence travels with the nucleus unchanged, so it is not what resets. Somatic mutations are carried over with the nucleus as well. Chromosome number is unchanged by the transfer. The nucleus carries no mitochondrial DNA, which resides in the cytoplasm. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 2, somatic cell nuclear transfer). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists DNA methylation, histone modification, and noncoding RNA as the primary mechanisms of epigenetic change (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, regulation of gene expression, p. 40). COMLEX: the reset was shown in vitro and is taken as proof that the epigenetic state is malleable (SDL 2 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — the nine hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the influences of genetics vs lifestyle in the process of aging","batch":1,"stem":"Approximately what share of age-related changes is attributed to genetics?","choices":{"A":"About 2%","B":"About 50%","C":"About 80%","D":"About 95%","E":"About 20%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"About 20% of age-related changes are controlled by genetics, whereas about 80% are governed by lifestyle, nutrition, and environment. A share of 2% is far below the reported genetic share. A share of 50% overstates the role of genes. A share of 80% is the portion attributed to lifestyle, nutrition, and environment rather than to genes. A share of 95% would make aging almost wholly genetic. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 3, the 80/20 rule of aging); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (DNA is the master molecule of the cell). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: with age, brain volume and cerebral blood flow decline, yet brain function is largely preserved despite mild cognitive decline (First Aid Pathology—Aging, nervous, p. 225). COMLEX: the new science of aging holds that people are not doomed by their genes (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the influences of genetics vs lifestyle in the process of aging","batch":1,"stem":"Cardiovascular diseases such as hypertension, heart attack, and stroke typically appear in older adults after how long a period of cumulative neglect?","choices":{"A":"15 to 25 years","B":"Under 6 months","C":"1 to 2 years","D":"3 to 4 years","E":"Over 60 years"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cardiovascular diseases are rarely caused by age by itself; they typically manifest after 15 to 25 years of cumulative neglect involving poor diet, physical inactivity, and environmental insults. Under 6 months is far too short for this cumulative process. A span of 1 to 2 years is also too short. A span of 3 to 4 years still falls well short of the reported range. Over 60 years would mean neglect beginning in childhood. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 3, chronic neglect versus inevitable senescence); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Cardiovascular function changes with advancing age). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: atherosclerosis most often involves the abdominal aorta, followed by the coronary, popliteal, and carotid arteries (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, atherosclerosis, p. 305). COMLEX: older adults die of chronic disease rather than of age itself, and in North America cardiovascular disease tops the list (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the influences of genetics vs lifestyle in the process of aging","batch":1,"stem":"Which daily dietary protein intake is recommended for older adults to prevent sarcopenia?","choices":{"A":"0.3 to 0.4 g/kg","B":"1.0 to 1.2 g/kg","C":"0.5 to 0.6 g/kg","D":"2.0 to 2.5 g/kg","E":"3.0 to 3.5 g/kg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Older adults are advised to eat 1.0 to 1.2 g/kg of protein per day, or up to 1.4 g/kg, to support muscle protein synthesis and prevent sarcopenia. An intake of 0.3 to 0.4 g/kg falls well below the recommendation and favors muscle loss. An intake of 0.5 to 0.6 g/kg is still below the recommended allowance. An intake of 2.0 to 2.5 g/kg exceeds the recommendation. An intake of 3.0 to 3.5 g/kg exceeds it even further. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 3, optimized nutrition); PROT-AGE Study Group 2013 (guideline support for 1.0 to 1.2 g/kg/day). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sarcopenia appears with osteopenia and joint cartilage thinning among the normal musculoskeletal changes of aging (First Aid Pathology—Aging, musculoskeletal, p. 225). COMLEX: men and women past 50 tend to fall short of the protein allowance (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe how cellular damage influences aging and organ dysfunction","batch":1,"stem":"Which term describes the progressive narrowing of physiologic reserve across multiple organ systems with aging?","choices":{"A":"Homeostasis","B":"Allostatic load","C":"Replicative senescence","D":"Homeostenosis","E":"Hormetic adaptation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Homeostenosis is the progressive narrowing of physiologic reserve that follows loss of parenchymal cells and extracellular remodeling. Homeostasis is the maintenance of a stable internal environment. Allostatic load is cumulative wear from repeated stress responses. Replicative senescence is the arrest of individual cells after a limited number of divisions. Hormetic adaptation is a beneficial response to a low dose of a stressor. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 4, organ dysfunction and homeostenosis). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aged bone marrow has less mass and more fat and mounts a less vigorous response to stressors such as blood loss (First Aid Pathology—Aging, hematopoietic, p. 225). COMLEX: homeostenosis results from loss of parenchymal cells together with extracellular remodeling (SDL 2 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — resilience and homeostenosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe how cellular damage influences aging and organ dysfunction","batch":1,"stem":"When accumulated macromolecular damage passes a critical mass, which cell fate releases damage-associated molecular patterns that provoke inflammation?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic apoptosis","B":"Necrotic cell lysis","C":"Replicative senescence","D":"Reversible quiescence","E":"Autophagic recycling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cells whose damage surpasses repair enter replicative senescence, undergo intrinsic apoptosis, or undergo necrotic lysis, and lysis releases proinflammatory damage-associated molecular patterns. Intrinsic apoptosis packages the cell into apoptotic bodies with an intact membrane and little inflammation. Replicative senescence stops division in a living cell. Reversible quiescence is a resting state. Autophagic recycling digests organelles within a surviving cell. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 4, cellular fate and attrition); First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, necrosis, p. 205; Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (damage reaching a critical mass). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: necrosis follows plasma membrane damage with leakage of intracellular components, enzymatic degradation, protein denaturation, and a local inflammatory reaction, unlike apoptosis (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, necrosis, p. 205). COMLEX: unrepaired damage to DNA, RNA, proteins, and membrane lipids builds up until it reaches this critical mass (SDL 2 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — resilience and homeostenosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe how cellular damage influences aging and organ dysfunction","batch":1,"stem":"Age-related sarcopenia shows preferential atrophy of which skeletal muscle fiber type?","choices":{"A":"Slow type 1 fibers","B":"Nuclear bag fibers","C":"Nuclear chain fibers","D":"Postural red fibers","E":"Fast type 2 fibers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sarcopenia reflects preferential atrophy of fast-twitch type 2 fibers, which compromises balance, raises fall risk, and drives frailty. Slow type 1 fibers, the red oxidative fibers that sustain posture, are relatively spared. Nuclear bag fibers are intrafusal spindle fibers that sense stretch rather than generate force. Nuclear chain fibers are also intrafusal spindle fibers. Postural red fibers are the slow oxidative fibers that are relatively spared. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 4, musculoskeletal system); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Clinical Focus 39.1, sarcopenia). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: type I fibers contract slowly, look red, and rely on oxidative phosphorylation, whereas type II fibers contract fast, look white, and rely on anaerobic glycolysis (First Aid Musculoskeletal—Anatomy and Physiology, types of skeletal muscle fibers, p. 460). COMLEX: muscle mass starts to decline around age 40, falls about 3% to 8% per decade, and can drop faster around age 60 (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — resilience and homeostenosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the proposed influence of IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in the aging process","batch":1,"stem":"Sirtuins, the cellular energy and stress sensors, belong to which enzyme class?","choices":{"A":"Protein kinases","B":"Protein deacetylases","C":"Protein phosphatases","D":"Ubiquitin protein ligases","E":"Adenylyl cyclases"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sirtuins, SIRT1 through SIRT7, are NAD-dependent protein deacetylases that remove acetyl groups from histones and key regulatory proteins. Protein kinases add phosphate groups. Protein phosphatases remove phosphate groups rather than acetyl groups. Ubiquitin ligases tag proteins for proteasomal degradation. Adenylyl cyclases make cAMP. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 5, sirtuins); Robbins Ch. 2 (Dysregulated nutrient sensing). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: niacin, vitamin B3, is a constituent of NAD+ and NADP+, which are used in redox reactions (First Aid Biochemistry—Nutrition, vitamin B3, p. 65). COMLEX: the sirtuins are spread across different parts of the cell, and each has its own role in adapting to food scarcity and DNA damage (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 6 — dysregulated nutrient sensing in cellular aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the proposed influence of IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in the aging process","batch":1,"stem":"Chronically elevated signaling through the somatotrophic IGF-1 axis accelerates cellular aging partly by generating an excess of which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Telomerase activity","B":"Heat shock proteins","C":"Oxygen free radicals","D":"Antioxidant scavenger enzymes","E":"Autophagic vesicles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"High activity of the IGF-1 axis imposes heavy synthetic and metabolic workloads that generate excess reactive oxygen species and cellular damage, which accelerates cellular aging. Telomerase activity is low in most somatic cells and does not cause damage. Heat shock proteins are protective chaperones. Antioxidant scavenger enzymes neutralize free radicals and so oppose this damage. Autophagic vesicles clear damaged organelles, which also opposes aging. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 5, insulin/IGF-1 signaling pathway). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: free radicals are eliminated by scavenging enzymes such as catalase, superoxide dismutase, and glutathione peroxidase and by antioxidants such as vitamins A, C, and E (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, free radical injury, p. 206). COMLEX: antioxidant defenses normally neutralize reactive oxygen species quickly, but radiation, pollutants, and smoking can overwhelm them (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 6 — dysregulated nutrient sensing in cellular aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the proposed influence of IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in the aging process","batch":1,"stem":"Which effect on programmed cell death do activated sirtuins produce?","choices":{"A":"More caspase activation","B":"More cytochrome c release","C":"Greater BAX pore formation","D":"Faster apoptotic body formation","E":"Less inappropriate apoptosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Activated sirtuins deacetylate key regulatory proteins and reduce inappropriate apoptosis, while also stimulating chaperone-mediated folding and countering oxygen free radicals. More caspase activation would increase programmed cell death, the opposite of the sirtuin effect. More cytochrome c release would trigger the mitochondrial apoptotic pathway. Greater BAX pore formation would also promote apoptosis. Faster apoptotic body formation reflects more cell death, not less. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 5, sirtuins); Robbins Ch. 2 (Sirtuins). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in apoptosis, caspases drive cell shrinkage, chromatin condensation, membrane blebbing, and apoptotic bodies, while the cell membrane typically stays intact without significant inflammation (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, apoptosis, p. 204). COMLEX: much remains to be learned before sirtuin-activating pills are available to increase longevity (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 6 — dysregulated nutrient sensing in cellular aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the concept of epigenetics as it relates to aging and contrast that to genetic contributions to aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a painless lump in her left breast that she noticed 3 weeks ago. At age 24, she received radiation therapy to the chest for Hodgkin lymphoma, and she has no family history of cancer. Examination shows a 2-cm firm, fixed mass in the upper inner quadrant of the left breast, within the old radiation field. Biopsy shows invasive ductal carcinoma, and sequencing shows a TP53 point mutation in the tumor cells that is absent from her blood leukocytes. Compared with an epigenetic alteration, the change found in her tumor is best described as which of the following?","choices":{"A":"A reversible promoter mark","B":"An altered base sequence","C":"A modified histone tail","D":"A remodeled chromatin region","E":"A silenced X chromosome"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Radiation is a physical mutagen that causes gene mutations, lasting alterations in the DNA nucleotide sequence, and this mutation is somatic because her leukocytes lack it. Epigenetic alterations, by contrast, change how a sequence is read without changing it. A reversible promoter mark is epigenetic and leaves the base sequence intact. A modified histone tail changes chromatin, not the sequence. A remodeled chromatin region alters access to DNA without mutating it. A silenced X chromosome reflects epigenetic inactivation rather than a sequence change. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 1, genetic versus epigenetic factors); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (DNA is the master molecule of the cell). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: free radical injury damages cells through membrane lipid peroxidation, protein modification, and DNA breakage and can be initiated by radiation exposure such as cancer therapy (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, free radical injury, p. 206). COMLEX: dysfunctional cells normally self-destruct by apoptosis, and cells become malignant when mutations override that response (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — DNA damage and mutagens","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the concept of epigenetics as it relates to aging and contrast that to genetic contributions to aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with prediabetes and a BMI of 29 kg/m2 begins walking 45 minutes a day and practicing daily stress reduction. In a research study, his blood cells are sampled before and after 1 year. The second sample shows changed chemical marks on the DNA and on the histones at several inflammatory genes, and expression of those genes has fallen. Sequencing shows no change in any of the genes. Physiologically, where is this kind of gene regulation said to operate?","choices":{"A":"Above the level of the gene","B":"Within the coding exons","C":"In the promoter base sequence","D":"Inside the intron sequence","E":"In the mitochondrial genome"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Epigenetics describes changes in gene expression caused by activating and deactivating genes without altering the DNA nucleotide sequence, regulation said to operate above the level of the gene; DNA methylation and histone modifications respond to daily behaviors such as exercise and to stressors. A change within the coding exons would alter the sequence itself, which was unchanged. A change in the promoter base sequence would also be a sequence change. A change inside the intron sequence would likewise alter the DNA sequence. The mitochondrial genome is separate from these nuclear inflammatory genes. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 1, definition of epigenetics). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: skeletal muscle hypertrophy adds sarcomeres in parallel, whereas atrophy removes myofibrils through the ubiquitin-proteasome system (First Aid Musculoskeletal—Anatomy and Physiology, skeletal muscle adaptations, p. 460). COMLEX: regular daily exercise of at least 30 minutes and resistance training preserve muscle mass and counter the progression toward frailty (SDL 2 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — domains of management in disease prevention","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the concept of epigenetics as it relates to aging and contrast that to genetic contributions to aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman with a 40-pack-year smoking history undergoes bronchoscopy for a 1.5-cm central mass arising from a mainstem bronchus. Biopsy of the mass shows squamous cell carcinoma. Both the tumor and normal-appearing bronchial epithelium 4 cm away show epigenetic silencing of the same tumor suppressor gene, with absent protein on immunostaining of the biopsies and a normal coding sequence on DNA sequencing. Her serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL). Which property of this change best explains its presence across a wide field of bronchial cells?","choices":{"A":"Spread to neighbors through gap junctions","B":"Change in the gene's base sequence","C":"Removal by excision repair enzymes","D":"Copying to daughter cells at division","E":"Transfer between cells in exosomes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Epigenetic changes are heritable through cell division, so a silencing mark is copied to daughter cells at each division, and smoke-exposed cells and their progeny carry it across the field without any change in DNA sequence. Gap junctions pass ions and small molecules, not chromatin marks. The coding sequence was normal, so a base-sequence change is excluded. Excision repair removes damaged bases rather than spreading a mark. Exosome transfer between cells does not install heritable silencing at a gene. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 1, definition of epigenetics); First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, regulation of gene expression, p. 40. Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma of the lung is a central hilar mass linked to cigarettes that can cause hypercalcemia by producing PTHrP (First Aid Respiratory—Pathology, lung cancer, p. 703). COMLEX: tobacco and other toxins are among the daily exposures that modify gene expression above the level of the gene (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — pulmonary conditions associated with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the epigenome and how it influences aging at the cellular level","batch":2,"stem":"Two 74-year-old monozygotic twin brothers who were raised apart enroll in an aging study. One smoked for 40 years, eats mostly processed food, and has type 2 diabetes mellitus, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and a myocardial infarction at age 66; his hemoglobin A1c is 8.4% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). The other never smoked, exercises daily, and takes no medications; his hemoglobin A1c is 5.3% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). Genotyping confirms identical inherited DNA sequences. Which comparison of their blood cells would most likely show the greatest difference?","choices":{"A":"Coding gene sequences","B":"HLA class I haplotypes","C":"Chromosome karyotypes","D":"Mitochondrial DNA sequences","E":"DNA methylation patterns"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Monozygotic twins share one genetic blueprint, but the epigenome stays plastic and responds to lifestyle and environment, so decades of different exposures drive divergent DNA methylation and chromatin marks that track their divergent health. Coding gene sequences are inherited and identical in monozygotic twins. HLA class I haplotypes are inherited and shared as well. Chromosome karyotypes are identical in monozygotic twins. Mitochondrial DNA comes from the same mother and is shared as well. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 2, epigenetic alterations and the cellular aging clock); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Epigenetics). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: monozygotic twins arise from one fertilized egg that splits in early pregnancy, and the timing of the split sets chorionicity and amnionicity (First Aid Reproductive—Embryology, twinning, p. 635). COMLEX: epigenetic alteration is recognized as a major hallmark of aging (SDL 2 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — the nine hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the epigenome and how it influences aging at the cellular level","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman in a longitudinal aging study has given blood samples every 10 years since age 39. She has hypertension and osteoarthritis and takes amlodipine. Her leukocyte count is 6,200/mm3 (reference range: 4,500–11,000/mm3). Comparison of her current leukocytes with her banked sample from age 39 shows the same DNA sequence, but loss of methyl marks across large genomic regions, new marks at many promoters, and loosened chromatin at loci that were silent. Which consequence of this drift most likely contributes to her cellular aging?","choices":{"A":"Gain of whole chromosomes","B":"Expanded trinucleotide repeats","C":"Uniparental chromosome disomy","D":"Weakened DNA repair pathways","E":"Duplicated gene segments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Progressive drift in DNA methylation and chromatin structure, the epigenetic-alteration hallmark of aging, destabilizes normal gene expression and impairs cellular repair pathways. Gain of whole chromosomes changes chromosome number, a genetic rather than epigenetic change. Expanded trinucleotide repeats alter the DNA sequence, which was unchanged. Uniparental chromosome disomy is an inheritance error rather than a drift in marks. Duplicated gene segments are structural genetic changes. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 2, epigenetic alterations and the cellular aging clock). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: DNA methylation alters how a DNA segment is expressed, reversibly and without changing its sequence, and it plays a part in aging, carcinogenesis, genomic imprinting, and X chromosome inactivation (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, DNA methylation, p. 32). COMLEX: unlike lasting genomic mutations, the epigenome remains plastic and responsive to lifestyle and environment (SDL 2 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — proposed causes of cellular aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the epigenome and how it influences aging at the cellular level","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man enrolls in a study of muscle aging. His gait speed is 0.7 m/s (reference range for age: above 0.8 m/s), and his grip strength is 22 kg (reference range for age: 27 kg or more). A vastus lateralis biopsy shows smaller muscle fibers than those of healthy 30-year-old volunteers. The coding sequences of his contractile and mitochondrial genes are normal, but the chemical marks on the DNA and histones at those genes differ from the volunteers' marks. These shifted marks most likely shrink his fibers by changing which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Number of genes inherited","B":"Length of the telomeres","C":"Type and amount of protein made","D":"Sequence of the coding exons","E":"Count of chromosomes per nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The epigenome directly regulates the type and quantity of proteins a cell produces, which in turn governs its growth, shape, and specialized work; drift in these marks with age destabilizes expression of contractile and mitochondrial proteins and shrinks fibers. The number of genes he inherited is unchanged by epigenetic marks. The sequence of his coding exons is unchanged as well. Telomere length concerns chromosome ends in dividing cells, not the marks at these genes. Chromosome number is not altered by epigenetic marks. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 2, regulation of protein synthesis); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Epigenetics; Clinical Focus 39.1, sarcopenia). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: methylation of CpG islands in a gene promoter typically silences transcription, and dysregulated DNA methylation is implicated in fragile X syndrome (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, DNA methylation, p. 32). COMLEX: testosterone and IGF-1 promote muscle growth, and both decline with advancing age (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — factors leading to sarcopenia with aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the influences of genetics vs lifestyle in the process of aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man comes for a follow-up visit. For 2 years he has been the only caregiver for his wife with advanced dementia; he sleeps about 4 hours a night and describes constant worry. A food diary shows the same low-fat diet he has eaten for 10 years, and his BMI is stable at 24 kg/m2. Two years ago his serum LDL cholesterol was within the reference range; it is now 158 mg/dL (reference range: <130 mg/dL). TSH is 2.1 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL), fasting glucose is 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and urinalysis shows no protein. Which factor most likely accounts for the rise in his LDL cholesterol?","choices":{"A":"Chronic emotional stress","B":"Dietary saturated fat","C":"Primary hypothyroidism","D":"Nephrotic syndrome","E":"Familial hypercholesterolemia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chronic stress can raise serum LDL cholesterol more than eating fatty foods and is now considered a major risk factor for stroke; his diet and weight are unchanged while 2 years of caregiver stress coincide with the rise. His diet has stayed low in fat for 10 years. A normal TSH excludes hypothyroidism. Urine without protein argues against nephrotic syndrome. Familial hypercholesterolemia raises LDL from birth, but his LDL was normal 2 years ago. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 3, stress reduction and health habits); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Physiological Changes and Aging). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dyslipidemia with high LDL and low HDL is a modifiable atherosclerosis risk factor, along with hypertension, tobacco smoking, and diabetes (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, atherosclerosis, p. 305). COMLEX: until the 1960s, physicians generally did not connect stress with stroke, hypertension, or heart attack (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — risk factors for ischemic heart disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the influences of genetics vs lifestyle in the process of aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman comes to the physician after a wrist fracture from a fall on level ground. She has smoked a pack of cigarettes daily for 45 years, has been sedentary since retiring, eats mostly processed food, and drinks 2 cups of coffee daily. Her mother lived independently to age 94 without fractures. Dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry shows a femoral neck T-score of −2.8 (reference range: −1.0 or higher), and her gait speed is 0.6 m/s (reference range for age: above 0.8 m/s). Serum calcium is 9.3 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL), and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 34 ng/mL (reference range: 30–100 ng/mL). Which factor accounts for the largest share of her musculoskeletal decline?","choices":{"A":"Her inherited gene variants","B":"Her daily coffee intake","C":"Her vitamin D status","D":"Her dietary calcium intake","E":"Her inactivity and smoking"},"correct":"E","explanation":"About 80% of bone and muscle degeneration, including demineralization, osteoporosis, and sarcopenia, is driven by a sedentary lifestyle, smoking, an unhealthy diet, and chronic stress, while chronological aging and intrinsic genetics account for about 20%. Her mother's fracture-free longevity argues against a strong inherited predisposition. Two cups of coffee a day is not among the lifestyle drivers of bone and muscle degeneration. Her vitamin D status is normal. Her dietary calcium intake is adequate. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 3, musculoskeletal evidence); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Musculoskeletal degeneration). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteoporosis is most commonly due to increased bone resorption related to decreased estrogen, old age, and cigarette smoking (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Skin, and Connective Tissue—Pathology, osteoporosis, p. 467). COMLEX: exercise and a healthy lifestyle can reverse bone loss, osteoporosis, and frailty (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — factors contributing to bone loss and fractures","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the influences of genetics vs lifestyle in the process of aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man comes for a wellness visit. He weighs 70 kg and eats toast and coffee for breakfast, soup for lunch, and a large steak dinner, for a total of about 75 g of protein daily. Over 2 years his grip strength has fallen from 31 kg to 25 kg (reference range for age: 27 kg or more), and he has trouble rising from a chair. Serum albumin is 3.9 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Which change to his protein intake is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Most protein at dinner","B":"Halving the daily total","C":"About 30 g at each meal","D":"Doubling the daily total","E":"Skipping protein at dinner"},"correct":"C","explanation":"He eats about 1.1 g/kg/day, within the recommended 1.0 to 1.2 g/kg/day, but most of it comes at dinner; the notes advise spacing protein at about 30 g per meal, the per-meal amount that supports muscle protein synthesis. Keeping most protein at dinner continues the pattern linked to his decline. Halving the daily total would fall below the recommendation. Doubling the daily total would exceed the 1.4 g/kg upper target without fixing distribution. Skipping protein at dinner would lower his total. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 3, optimized nutrition); PROT-AGE Study Group 2013 (per-meal protein threshold). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: causes of tissue atrophy include disuse, denervation, poor nutrition, and loss of blood supply or hormonal stimulation (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, cellular adaptations, p. 202). COMLEX: skinless poultry, lean beef, trout, salmon, tofu, lentils, beans, and quinoa are good protein sources (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — risk factors for poor nutritional status in older adults","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe how cellular damage influences aging and organ dysfunction","batch":2,"stem":"An 86-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of 1 day of confusion and poor oral intake. At a routine visit 2 months ago, her serum creatinine was 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL), and her resting pulse and blood pressure were normal. Today, her temperature is 38.1°C (100.6°F), pulse is 104/min, and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. Urinalysis shows pyuria and nitrites, and serum creatinine is 2.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL). Which feature of her aging organs best explains why her resting values were normal before this illness?","choices":{"A":"Redundant organ capacity","B":"Higher renal blood flow","C":"Greater arterial compliance","D":"Larger lean body mass","E":"Increased nephron number"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because organs have built-in redundancy, older adults often keep normal resting values despite losing parenchymal cells, but homeostenosis leaves too little reserve to compensate when an infection adds stress, as her acute kidney injury and hypotension show. Renal blood flow falls with age. Arterial compliance decreases as arteries stiffen. Lean body mass declines with sarcopenia. Nephron number falls with age, which lowers GFR. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 4, organ dysfunction and homeostenosis). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: renal aging brings a lower GFR from fewer nephrons, lower renal blood flow, and reduced hormonal function (First Aid Pathology—Aging, renal, p. 225). COMLEX: chronic disease is more drastic than normal aging because compensatory mechanisms are reduced or lost (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — atypical symptoms of infection in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe how cellular damage influences aging and organ dysfunction","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman who has never smoked has pulmonary function testing before elective hip replacement. She walks 1 mile daily without dyspnea and has no cough. Lung examination shows clear breath sounds. Testing shows a total lung capacity of 98% of predicted (reference range: 80–120% of predicted), residual volume of 138% of predicted (reference range: 75–120% of predicted), and FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.72 (reference range for age: 0.70 or higher). Arterial blood gas analysis on room air shows a Pao2 of 78 mm Hg (reference range for age: 75–100 mm Hg). Which change most likely accounts for her increased residual volume?","choices":{"A":"Fibrosis of alveolar walls","B":"Surfactant deficiency","C":"Bronchial smooth muscle spasm","D":"Loss of lung elastic recoil","E":"Stronger expiratory muscles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"With age the lung parenchyma loses elasticity and the chest wall stiffens, so air is trapped and residual volume and functional residual capacity rise while total lung capacity stays normal; gas exchange becomes less efficient and hypoxemia is more likely. Alveolar wall fibrosis would stiffen the lungs and lower lung volumes. Surfactant deficiency causes alveolar collapse in neonates. Bronchospasm would lower the FEV1/FVC ratio, which is normal. Expiratory muscles weaken rather than strengthen with age. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 4, respiratory system); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Aging and Respiratory Changes). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aging increases lung compliance through lost elastic recoil, stiffens the chest wall, weakens respiratory muscles, and raises residual volume while total lung capacity is unchanged (First Aid Pathology—Aging, respiratory, p. 225). COMLEX: older adults also have a blunted ventilatory response to hypoxia and hypercapnia, a less vigorous cough, and slower mucociliary clearance (First Aid Pathology—Aging, respiratory).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — respiratory changes from youth to old age","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe how cellular damage influences aging and organ dysfunction","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old man asks about his skin, which has become deeply wrinkled and sagging over 10 years. He eats fried or grilled meat at most meals and does not have diabetes mellitus; his hemoglobin A1c is 5.4% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). Skin autofluorescence, a noninvasive marker of damaged long-lived dermal proteins, is well above the reference range for age. Slit-lamp examination shows early lens opacities in both eyes. Accumulation of the material causing these changes has also been linked to which neurodegenerative disorder?","choices":{"A":"Multiple sclerosis","B":"Parkinson disease","C":"Myasthenia gravis","D":"Huntington disease","E":"Narcolepsy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Frying and grilling foods generate advanced glycation end products, which cross-link proteins and drive skin wrinkling, arterial stiffening, and cataracts, and their accumulation has been linked to Parkinson disease, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, and Alzheimer disease. Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune demyelinating disease. Myasthenia gravis results from antibodies against acetylcholine receptors. Huntington disease results from an inherited CAG repeat expansion. Narcolepsy reflects loss of hypocretin-producing neurons. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 4, advanced glycation end products); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Glycation). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acquired cataract risk rises with age, smoking, heavy alcohol use, intense sunlight, long-term glucocorticoid therapy, and diabetes mellitus (First Aid Neurology—Ophthalmology, cataract, p. 550). COMLEX: the body has protective mechanisms against these compounds that an unhealthy diet can overwhelm (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — pathophysiology of Parkinson disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the proposed influence of IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in the aging process","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with lymphangioleiomyomatosis comes for follow-up 1 year after starting an oral drug that binds FKBP12 and inhibits a nutrient-sensing kinase. Her FEV1 has stayed stable since treatment began, after a steady decline in the 2 years before. She has had two episodes of mouth ulcers. Her platelet count is 138,000/mm3 (reference range: 150,000–400,000/mm3), and her trough drug level is 7 ng/mL (reference range for therapy: 5–15 ng/mL). In model organisms, drugs with this mechanism extend lifespan partly by reducing which process that the target kinase normally promotes?","choices":{"A":"Autophagic organelle clearance","B":"Proteasomal protein breakdown","C":"Protein translation and growth","D":"Repair of DNA strand breaks","E":"Mitochondrial fatty acid oxidation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sirolimus, also called rapamycin, inhibits mTOR, the downstream kinase of the nutrient-sensing cascade that promotes protein translation and cell growth while suppressing autophagy; blocking it extends lifespan in model organisms. Autophagic clearance is suppressed by mTOR, so inhibition increases it. Proteasomal protein breakdown is a protective process that mTOR does not drive. Repair of DNA strand breaks is likewise not an mTOR output. Fatty acid oxidation is favored by fasting signals rather than by mTOR. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 5, mTOR pathway); Robbins Ch. 2 (Dysregulated nutrient sensing). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sirolimus blocks T-cell activation and B-cell differentiation by preventing the response to IL-2 and is used for kidney transplant rejection prophylaxis (First Aid Immunology—Immunosuppressants, sirolimus, p. 118). COMLEX: in model organisms, mTOR inhibition reduces age-associated pathology as well as extending lifespan (SDL 2 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis in cellular aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the proposed influence of IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in the aging process","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman of short stature comes for a routine visit. Her height is 122 cm (4 ft), and she has a prominent forehead, a small jaw, and truncal obesity. Her parents are first cousins, and two siblings are also short. Serum insulin-like growth factor 1 is 18 ng/mL (reference range: 80–280 ng/mL), and fasting insulin is 3 μU/mL (reference range: 2–20 μU/mL). Genetic testing shows biallelic loss-of-function variants in a gene required for normal IGF-1 production. In animal models, lifelong low activity of the signaling pathway affected in this patient has which effect on cells?","choices":{"A":"Faster telomere attrition","B":"Lower basal metabolic demand","C":"More oxygen free radicals","D":"More protein synthesis and growth","E":"Lower sirtuin activity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Her inherited defect blocks IGF-1 production. Attenuated IGF-1 signaling lowers basal metabolic demand and cell growth rates, which reduces cellular injury and extends longevity in animal models. Telomere attrition is not accelerated by low IGF-1 signaling. Lower IGF-1 activity means a lighter metabolic workload and fewer free radicals. IGF-1 signaling drives protein synthesis and cell growth, so both fall. Sirtuins are favored by low-nutrient states rather than suppressed. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 5, insulin/IGF-1 signaling pathway); Robbins Ch. 2 (IGF-1 signaling pathway). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: growth hormone is secreted in pulses under GHRH control, and its effects on linear growth and muscle mass are carried out by liver-derived IGF-1 (First Aid Endocrine—Physiology, growth hormone, p. 333). COMLEX: people with this kind of inherited defect have been reported to have low rates of cancer and diabetes (general knowledge).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — life expectancy, life span, and health span","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the proposed influence of IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in the aging process","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man comes for a checkup after 6 months of daily injections of recombinant insulin-like growth factor 1 that he buys from an anti-aging clinic. He reports episodes of sweating and shakiness before meals. His fasting glucose is 62 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). Because the injected factor mimics insulin signaling, it tells his cells that which condition is present?","choices":{"A":"Cellular starvation","B":"Oxygen deprivation","C":"Low energy charge","D":"Genotoxic stress","E":"Abundant nutrients"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IGF-1 mimics insulin signaling to inform cells that glucose and amino acids are abundant, which switches cells into a building state of growth and replication; sustained high signaling through this axis accelerates cellular aging through heavy synthetic and metabolic workloads. His low glucose reflects the insulin-like action of the injections. Cellular starvation is the opposite of what this signal conveys. Oxygen deprivation is sensed by other pathways. A low energy charge is also the opposite of the abundance signal. Genotoxic stress is sensed by DNA damage pathways rather than by IGF-1. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 5, insulin/IGF-1 signaling pathway). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: growth hormone secretion falls with aging, obesity, hyperglycemia, and somatostatin (First Aid Endocrine—Physiology, growth hormone, p. 333). COMLEX: hormone therapy with testosterone or growth hormone for sarcopenia often has severe side effects, and no drug is FDA approved for sarcopenia (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the concept of epigenetics as it relates to aging and contrast that to genetic contributions to aging","batch":3,"stem":"A 59-year-old man quits smoking. Over the next 10 years, serial biopsies of his bronchial lining show that smoking-related changes in how the cells' genes are read move back toward the never-smoker pattern, but smoking-induced mutations in the same cells persist. Which property best explains the difference?","choices":{"A":"Faster turnover of basal cells","B":"Telomerase activity in the lining","C":"Germline origin of the mutations","D":"Reversibility of epigenetic marks","E":"Selective repair of the mutations"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Epigenetic modifications are reversible and alter how the cell reads DNA without changing the sequence, so marks set by smoking can fade after exposure stops, whereas gene mutations are lasting changes in the nucleotide sequence. Both changes sit in the same bronchial cells, so tissue type does not explain the difference. Basal cell turnover would carry mutations forward to new cells rather than erase them. Telomerase does not erase gene-regulatory marks. Smoking-induced mutations are somatic, not germline. Selective repair would remove the mutations, but they persist. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 1, contrasting genetic and epigenetic mechanisms). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: histone methylation usually silences transcription in a reversible way, although depending on where the methyl groups sit it can also switch transcription on (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, histone methylation, p. 32). COMLEX: environmental exposures, lifestyle, diet, and emotional stress all shape how genes are expressed over time (SDL 2 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q02","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the influences of genetics vs lifestyle in the process of aging","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with osteopenia starts resistance training 3 days a week to slow her bone loss. Her serum calcium is 9.5 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL). Besides preserving muscle mass, this training most directly helps her bones by which effect?","choices":{"A":"Raising serum calcium","B":"Raising vitamin D synthesis","C":"Stimulating bone remodeling","D":"Lowering estrogen levels","E":"Raising parathyroid hormone output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Regular exercise and resistance training preserve muscle mass, stimulate bone remodeling, keep joints mobile, and counter progression toward frailty, and resistance training also strengthens bones, ligaments, and tendons. Training does not act by raising serum calcium, which is normal. Vitamin D synthesis depends on sunlight on the skin. Lower estrogen speeds bone loss. A sustained rise in parathyroid hormone increases bone resorption. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 3, physical activity); Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39 (Clinical Focus 39.1, sarcopenia). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteoporosis is diagnosed from DEXA bone mineral density measured at the hip, femoral neck, and lumbar spine (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Skin, and Connective Tissue—Pathology, osteoporosis, p. 467). COMLEX: strength training with weights, resistance bands, or exercise machines is the best exercise for improving muscle mass (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 7 — nonpharmacologic treatment of osteoporosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe how cellular damage influences aging and organ dysfunction","batch":3,"stem":"A healthy 82-year-old man has an echocardiogram before surgery. His blood pressure is 156/70 mm Hg. His large arteries have stiffened with age. Which cardiac change most likely accompanies this arterial stiffening?","choices":{"A":"Thicker left ventricular wall","B":"Dilated right ventricle","C":"Larger left ventricular cavity","D":"Thinner interventricular septum","E":"Thinner mitral valve leaflets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Collagen cross-linking and loss of elastin stiffen arteries, raise systolic pressure, and increase afterload, and the left ventricular wall thickens, which blunts maximal exercise tolerance. Right ventricular dilation is not a feature of normal aging. The left ventricular cavity becomes smaller, not larger, with age. The septum thickens and can become sigmoid shaped. Mitral and aortic valves thicken and calcify. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 4, cardiovascular system); First Aid Pathology—Aging, cardiovascular, p. 225. Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiovascular aging brings lower arterial compliance, a larger aortic diameter, a smaller left ventricular cavity with a sigmoid-shaped septum, aortic and mitral valve calcification, and a lower maximum heart rate (First Aid Pathology—Aging, cardiovascular, p. 225). COMLEX: the collagen cross-linking that rises with age comes largely from nonenzymatic glycation, unlike the normal lysyl oxidase cross-links formed during collagen synthesis (SDL 2 notes; First Aid Biochemistry—Cellular, collagen synthesis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — central elastic arteries and systolic hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe how cellular damage influences aging and organ dysfunction","batch":3,"stem":"A healthy 81-year-old woman who never smoked has pulmonary function testing before surgery. Her residual volume is above the value predicted for a young adult, and her total lung capacity is normal. Besides residual volume, which quantity is most likely increased by normal aging of her lungs and chest wall?","choices":{"A":"Total lung capacity","B":"Forced vital capacity","C":"Inspiratory capacity","D":"Tidal volume","E":"Dead-space volume"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Loss of lung elasticity and chest wall compliance increases dead-space air and elevates residual volume while gas exchange becomes less efficient. Total lung capacity is unchanged with age, as in her test. Forced vital capacity falls as residual volume takes up more of the unchanged total. Inspiratory capacity falls as functional residual capacity rises. Tidal volume is maintained at rest. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 4, respiratory system); First Aid Pathology—Aging, respiratory, p. 225. Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the A-a gradient and ventilation-perfusion mismatch both increase with age (First Aid Pathology—Aging, respiratory, p. 225). COMLEX: aging lungs raise the risk of pneumonia and low blood oxygen, and altered breathing patterns can progress to sleep apnea (Rhoades & Bell Ch. 39).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — evaluating pulmonary symptoms in older patients","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the proposed influence of IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in the aging process","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with prediabetes enrolls in a trial of an investigational drug that activates her sirtuins. Which metabolic change would these activated enzymes most likely produce?","choices":{"A":"Greater insulin sensitivity","B":"Greater hepatic gluconeogenesis","C":"Higher glucagon secretion","D":"Higher cortisol secretion","E":"Lower glucose uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Activated sirtuins enhance insulin sensitivity and glucose metabolism, which is why they are studied as targets for diabetes. Greater hepatic gluconeogenesis would raise blood glucose. Higher glucagon secretion would also raise glucose. Higher cortisol secretion opposes insulin action rather than enhancing it. Lower glucose uptake is the opposite of the sirtuin effect. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 5, sirtuins); Robbins Ch. 2 (Sirtuins). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: growth hormone increases insulin resistance and is diabetogenic (First Aid Endocrine—Physiology, growth hormone, p. 333). COMLEX: better insulin action is one reason sirtuins are being studied as targets in diabetes (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 6 — dysregulated nutrient sensing in cellular aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the proposed influence of IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in the aging process","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man follows a supervised diet with 25% fewer calories than his usual intake, without malnutrition, for 2 years. Which change in his cellular signaling is proposed to explain how this kind of diet prolongs life span across eukaryotic organisms?","choices":{"A":"More mTOR activity","B":"Less anabolic signaling","C":"Higher IGF-1 signaling","D":"More insulin secretion","E":"Faster cell replication"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Caloric restriction without malnutrition consistently prolongs life span and health span across eukaryotic organisms by downregulating anabolic pathways and upregulating cellular stress defenses. More mTOR activity belongs to the nutrient-rich anabolic state. Higher IGF-1 signaling is also an anabolic signal that caloric restriction lowers. More insulin secretion reflects feeding rather than restriction. Faster cell replication is a building-state response, the opposite of caloric restriction. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 5, caloric restriction paradigm). Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the fed state, insulin drives triglyceride and glycogen storage together with protein synthesis (First Aid Biochemistry—Metabolism, fasting and starvation, p. 89). COMLEX: the long-term safety and efficacy of such antiaging interventions in humans are not fully understood (Robbins Ch. 2).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — the nine hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the concept of epigenetics as it relates to aging and contrast that to genetic contributions to aging","batch":5,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with a sedentary lifestyle enrolls in a 6-month resistance training study. Her BMI is 31 kg/m2, and her fasting glucose is 104 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). A baseline vastus lateralis biopsy shows low acetylation of the histones bound to the promoter of a mitochondrial biogenesis gene and low transcription of that gene. After training, a repeat biopsy shows higher histone acetylation at the promoter and a 3-fold rise in transcription, and sequencing shows no change in the gene. Before training, the low histone acetylation most likely reduced transcription of this gene through which effect?","choices":{"A":"Tighter coiling of the DNA","B":"Mutation of the promoter sequence","C":"Loss of telomeric repeat DNA","D":"Faster decay of the mRNA","E":"Blocked splicing of the transcript"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histone acetylation removes the positive charge of histones and loosens DNA coiling so the gene can be transcribed; with less acetylation, the DNA coils tightly around the histones and transcription falls. Training reversed the mark without changing the gene, the picture of a reversible epigenetic change driven by lifestyle. The promoter sequence was unchanged on sequencing. Telomere loss affects chromosome ends, not a single promoter. Faster mRNA decay acts after transcription and would not lower transcription itself. Blocked splicing also acts after transcription. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 1, epigenetic factors); First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, histone acetylation, p. 32. Textbook enrichment (FA) — detail not in SDL 2 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetylation is not limited to histones; acetylating the thyroid hormone receptor, for example, can change thyroid hormone synthesis (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, histone acetylation, p. 32). COMLEX: First Aid ties histone deacetylation to the altered gene expression seen in Huntington disease (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, histone deacetylation).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — preserving musculoskeletal structure and function with aging","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"FA"},{"id":"sdl02_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the proposed influence of IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in the aging process","batch":5,"stem":"A 66-year-old man enrolls in a trial of a monoclonal antibody that blocks the receptor for insulin-like growth factor 1, tested as a way to slow age-related cellular damage. He has no chronic illness, and his fasting glucose is 96 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). The receptor targeted by this antibody belongs to which class?","choices":{"A":"Nonreceptor tyrosine kinase","B":"Intracellular steroid receptor","C":"Ligand gated ion channel","D":"Receptor guanylyl cyclase","E":"Receptor tyrosine kinase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IGF-1 binds a receptor tyrosine kinase, mimics insulin signaling, and acts through the downstream kinase mTOR to drive anabolic growth; sustained high signaling accelerates cellular aging by imposing heavy synthetic and metabolic workloads. A nonreceptor tyrosine kinase acting through the JAK/STAT pathway is used by the growth hormone receptor, not by IGF-1. Intracellular receptors bind steroid and thyroid hormones. Ligand-gated ion channels mediate fast synaptic signals. Receptor guanylyl cyclases bind the natriuretic peptides. Sources: SDL 2 lecture notes (Obj 5, insulin/IGF-1 signaling pathway); First Aid Endocrine—Physiology, signaling pathways of endocrine hormones, p. 341. Textbook enrichment (FA) — detail not in SDL 2 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 2 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid places the growth hormone receptor in the nonreceptor tyrosine kinase group, which signals through JAK/STAT (First Aid Endocrine—Physiology, signaling pathways of endocrine hormones, p. 341). COMLEX: IGF-1 pushes cells toward an anabolic state of growth and replication (SDL 2 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 6 — dysregulated nutrient sensing in cellular aging","isHighYield":false,"enrichmentSource":"FA"}]},{"sdlNumber":3,"title":"SDL 3 — Clinical Implications of the Aging Process","questions":[{"id":"sdl03_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the diffence of life expectance, life span, and health span","batch":1,"stem":"Which term denotes the biological upper limit on how many years a member of a species can live?","choices":{"A":"Life expectancy","B":"Median survival time","C":"Maximum life span","D":"Mortality rate","E":"Survival rate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Life span is the biological limit to how many years a member of a species can survive. Life expectancy is a population average rather than a biological limit. Median survival time describes a cohort with a given disease. Mortality rate describes the share of a population dying over a period. Survival rate describes the share still alive after a period, not a species limit. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 1, life span); Kane Ch. 1 (Introduction). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a Kaplan-Meier curve plots the probability of survival against time for time-to-event outcomes, most often death (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Kaplan-Meier curve, p. 259). COMLEX: good geriatric care can lengthen average survival, and future genetic advances may eventually move the species limit as well (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — goals of chronic disease management in the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the diffence of life expectance, life span, and health span","batch":1,"stem":"Life expectancy is a statistical average calculated from which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Species maximum age","B":"Telomere length at birth","C":"Number of chronic diseases","D":"Parents' ages at death","E":"Birth year or current age"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Life expectancy is a demographic metric for the average time a person is expected to live and is calculated from year of birth or current age. The species maximum age defines life span, not the average. Telomere length at birth is a biological marker, not an input to the calculation. The number of chronic diseases describes one person's health rather than the demographic statistic. Parents' ages at death are family history, not the basis of the calculation. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 1, life expectancy); Kane Ch. 1 (Introduction). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the population pyramid comparison, a growing population has high birth and mortality rates with short life expectancy, while a stable or shrinking one has low rates with long life expectancy (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, demographic transition, p. 259). COMLEX: the concept of old age has changed as life expectancy increased, although the biology has not (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — goals of chronic disease management in the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the diffence of life expectance, life span, and health span","batch":1,"stem":"Geriatric care aims to delay the onset of disability so that chronic illness and dependency are confined to a shorter period just before death. What is this goal called?","choices":{"A":"Survival curve shift","B":"Epidemiological transition","C":"Expansion of morbidity","D":"Compression of morbidity","E":"Demographic transition"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Compression of morbidity is the narrowing of chronic disability and functional dependency into a shorter span between a later age of onset of disability and death. A survival curve shift describes a change in survival at each age without regard to disability. The epidemiological transition describes a population shift in the leading causes of death. The demographic transition describes falling birth and mortality rates as a country develops. Expansion of morbidity is the opposite pattern, in which longer life brings more years of disability. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 1, compression of morbidity); Kane Ch. 1 (Fries, 1980). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tertiary prevention treats symptomatic disease to prevent its progression and the morbidity that follows (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Healthcare Delivery, disease prevention, p. 275). COMLEX: the idea that disability can decrease as morbidity is compressed was introduced by Fries in 1980 (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — goals of chronic disease management in the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify and discuss the principal factors influencing the functional performance of an organ with aging","batch":1,"stem":"How well an aging organ functions in an older person depends on two principal factors. One is the organ's rate of deterioration. What is the other?","choices":{"A":"Level of performance needed","B":"Number of remaining stem cells","C":"Age when decline began","D":"Organ size at maturity","E":"Resting laboratory values"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An aging organ's performance depends on its rate of deterioration and on the level of performance needed, the demand required for daily activities or to compensate for homeostatic stress. The number of remaining stem cells is not one of the two principal factors. The age when decline began does not set current performance. Organ size at maturity is likewise not a principal factor. Resting laboratory values are usually normal in older adults and do not capture performance under demand. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 2, two principal determinants of organ performance); Kane Ch. 1 (Changes associated with normal aging). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: with normal aging the ventilatory response to hypoxia and hypercapnia is blunted, the cough is less vigorous, and mucociliary clearance is slower (First Aid Pathology—Aging, respiratory, p. 225). COMLEX: clinicians should avoid both dismissing treatable pathology as part of old age and treating natural aging changes as diseases (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — resilience and homeostenosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify and discuss the principal factors influencing the functional performance of an organ with aging","batch":1,"stem":"Cross-sectional studies comparing younger and older groups suggested that most organ systems lose function at about what rate beginning around age 30?","choices":{"A":"About 0.1% per year","B":"About 1% per year","C":"About 5% per year","D":"About 10% per year","E":"About 20% per year"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cross-sectional comparisons suggested a loss of about 1% a year from around age 30, but longitudinal data show that decline in healthy people is much less dramatic. A rate of 0.1% a year is far below the cross-sectional estimate. A rate of 5% a year overstates it. A rate of 10% a year overstates it further. A rate of 20% a year would exhaust organ function within a few decades. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 2, rate of deterioration); Kane Ch. 1 (Changes associated with normal aging). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a cross-sectional study assesses the frequency of disease and risk factors in the present, measures prevalence, and does not establish causality (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, cross-sectional study, p. 256). COMLEX: there is no universal marker of aging, and what constitutes normal aging is still imprecisely known (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — resilience and homeostenosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify and discuss the principal factors influencing the functional performance of an organ with aging","batch":1,"stem":"Gradual cell loss in an aging organ does not become clinically significant until which of the following occurs?","choices":{"A":"Resting labs first change","B":"Function loss begins","C":"Organ weight falls by half","D":"Loss crosses a threshold","E":"Stem cells stop dividing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gradual decrements do not become functionally significant until the loss is fairly extensive and crosses a critical threshold, so clinicians are advised to think in terms of thresholds. Resting laboratory values usually stay normal even after substantial loss. Function loss may begin in early adulthood without symptoms. No fixed fraction of organ weight defines the threshold. Stem cell arrest is a cellular hallmark, not the clinical threshold. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 2, organ redundancy and thresholds); Kane Ch. 1 (Changes associated with normal aging). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal aging lowers lower esophageal sphincter tone, gastric mucosal protection, and colonic motility (First Aid Pathology—Aging, gastrointestinal, p. 225). COMLEX: many homeostatic losses begin in early adulthood but stay silent until the loss is fairly extensive (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — resilience and homeostenosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the nine hallmarks of aging","batch":1,"stem":"Which hallmark of aging arises from spontaneous mutagenesis and failing DNA repair systems, so that unrepaired lesions cause copying errors?","choices":{"A":"Loss of proteostasis","B":"Cellular senescence","C":"Genomic instability","D":"Epigenetic alteration","E":"Deregulated nutrient sensing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Genomic instability is the continuous accumulation of DNA lesions from endogenous stressors such as reactive oxygen species and from physical and chemical mutagens, together with age-related defects in DNA repair; unrepaired lesions cause replication and transcription errors and cell death. Loss of proteostasis concerns protein folding and clearance. Cellular senescence is irreversible cell cycle arrest. Epigenetic alteration changes gene expression without changing the sequence. Deregulated nutrient sensing concerns the somatotrophic axis, mTOR, and sirtuins. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 3, genomic instability); Kane Ch. 1 (Hallmarks of aging, Table 1-2). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in base excision repair, a glycosylase removes the altered base, AP endonuclease and AP lyase cut the backbone, DNA polymerase-β fills the gap, and DNA ligase seals it (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, DNA repair, p. 37). COMLEX: the nine hallmarks used in geriatrics come from the 2013 framework of Lopez-Otin and colleagues (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — proposed causes of cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the nine hallmarks of aging","batch":1,"stem":"In the hematopoietic system, the hallmark of stem cell exhaustion increases the risk of which disorder?","choices":{"A":"Polycythemia","B":"Myelodysplasia","C":"Thrombocytosis","D":"Leukocytosis","E":"Multiple myeloma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells decline in number and proliferative capacity with age, which heightens the risk of myelodysplasia. Polycythemia is an overproduction of red cells, the opposite of a depleted stem cell pool. Thrombocytosis is an overproduction of platelets. Leukocytosis is a raised white cell count rather than marrow failure. Multiple myeloma is a plasma cell neoplasm rather than a consequence of stem cell exhaustion. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 3, stem cell exhaustion); Kane Ch. 1 (Table 1-2). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilobed neutrophil nuclei, the pseudo-Pelger-Huët anomaly, point to a myelodysplastic syndrome or to drugs such as immunosuppressants (First Aid Hematology and Oncology—Pathology, myelodysplastic syndromes, p. 436). COMLEX: the stem and progenitor pool shrinks in number as well as in proliferative capacity (SDL 3 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — proposed causes of cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the nine hallmarks of aging","batch":1,"stem":"Inflammaging, an inflammatory state that damages several organ systems at once, defines which hallmark of aging?","choices":{"A":"Altered intercellular communication","B":"Exhaustion of tissue stem cell pools","C":"Deregulated nutrient sensing","D":"Impaired protein quality control","E":"Accumulated genomic instability"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Altered intercellular communication is the disruption of neurohormonal and endocrine signaling by a chronic, sterile, low-grade systemic inflammatory state called inflammaging, which affects muscle, bone, arteries, and brain at the same time. Stem cell exhaustion concerns depletion of progenitor pools. Deregulated nutrient sensing concerns growth hormone, insulin/IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuin signaling. Impaired protein quality control is loss of proteostasis. Genomic instability is the accumulation of unrepaired DNA lesions. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 3, altered intercellular communication); Kane Ch. 1 (Hallmarks of aging). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimuli for chronic inflammation include persistent infections, type IV hypersensitivity, autoimmune diseases, and prolonged exposure to toxic agents or foreign material (First Aid Pathology—Inflammation, chronic inflammation, p. 212). COMLEX: intercellular communication normally runs through the endocrine system and neurohormonal signaling (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — proposed causes of cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the diffence of life expectance, life span, and health span","batch":2,"stem":"A 92-year-old woman with treated hypertension comes for a routine visit. She tells her physician that her mother died of a diarrheal illness at age 34 in 1941 and that many children in her village did not reach adulthood. Since 1955 she has lived in a city with treated water, routine vaccination, and a steady food supply. Her serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). National data show that average age at death rose from 58 to 79 years over her lifetime, while the oldest verified ages changed little. Which factor best explains the rise in this population measure?","choices":{"A":"Sanitation and nutrition","B":"A longer species life span","C":"Lower radiation exposure","D":"Fewer inherited mutations","E":"Later onset of menopause"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The population measure is life expectancy, the average time a person is expected to live, which rose over the past century with advances in sanitation and nutrition. Life span, the biological limit for the species, has changed little, as the stable oldest ages show. Lower radiation exposure has not changed enough to explain a population-wide gain. Fewer inherited mutations would not accumulate within a century. A later onset of menopause would not explain longer survival in men and women alike. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 1, life expectancy). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: as a country develops, its birth and death rates fall by different amounts, which reshapes the age makeup of its population (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, demographic transition, p. 259). COMLEX: Kane cautions that life expectancy and life span are different measures that should be kept distinct (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the diffence of life expectance, life span, and health span","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man with osteoarthritis of both knees who walks with a cane joins a community program of resistance training, nutrition counseling, and fall prevention. In the program's 10-year follow-up, participants and matched controls died at the same average age of 86 years. Participants first needed help with bathing or dressing at an average age of 84 years, compared with 80 years in controls. They also reported fewer days of chronic pain each year. Which measure did the program most likely extend?","choices":{"A":"Life expectancy","B":"Maximum life span","C":"Health span","D":"Mortality rate","E":"Survival time"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Health span counts the years a person lives without functional limitations, chronic illness, or chronic pain; the program delayed dependency by 4 years and reduced pain without changing age at death, which also compresses morbidity into a shorter span before death. Life expectancy was unchanged because both groups died at the same average age. Survival time was likewise unchanged. Maximum life span is a species limit that this program did not test. Mortality rate is the number of deaths in a population over a period. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 1, health span); Kane Ch. 1 (compression of morbidity). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: regular weight-bearing exercise and adequate calcium and vitamin D intake throughout adulthood are the listed prophylaxis for osteoporosis (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Skin, and Connective Tissue—Pathology, osteoporosis, p. 467). COMLEX: moderate eating and drinking, staying physically active, and keeping socially engaged are viewed as keys to aging well (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the diffence of life expectance, life span, and health span","batch":2,"stem":"A 104-year-old woman dies at home. Her death certificate records 104 years and 2 months between her birth and her death. The longest verified human life is about 122 years. When the term life span is applied to this individual woman rather than to the species, it denotes which measure?","choices":{"A":"Species maximum age","B":"Life expectancy at birth","C":"Years free of disability","D":"Functional age","E":"Chronological age"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At the individual level, life span denotes chronological age, the total measured time between birth and death, here 104 years and 2 months; at the species level it is the biological upper limit, about 122 years for humans. The species maximum age is that upper limit, not her own span. Life expectancy at birth is a statistical average for her birth cohort. Years free of disability measure health span. Functional age describes capacity, not elapsed time. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 1, life span). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid labels intrinsic aging of the skin as chronological aging, marked by a fall in the biosynthetic capacity of dermal fibroblasts (First Aid Pathology—Aging, skin, p. 225). COMLEX: Kane describes life span as a biological ceiling on the years a species can expect to survive (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — comorbidity and risk of disability and mortality","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify and discuss the principal factors influencing the functional performance of an organ with aging","batch":2,"stem":"A healthy 82-year-old woman who walks daily asks whether her kidneys and lungs will decline the way her older sister's did. Her physician describes a longitudinal study that followed 600 adults without chronic disease, hypertension, or diabetes from age 60 to 90 and repeated kidney, lung, and muscle strength measurements every 3 years in the same people. Which pattern of organ decline did such longitudinal data show in healthy participants?","choices":{"A":"Uniform yearly loss","B":"Equal organ decline","C":"Early rapid decline","D":"Marked variability","E":"Late abrupt collapse"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Longitudinal data, which follow the same healthy people over time, show that organ deterioration is much less dramatic than cross-sectional comparisons suggested and shows marked individual variability, so her sister's course does not predict hers. A uniform yearly loss is the cross-sectional estimate that longitudinal data did not confirm. Organs do not decline at equal rates within a person. An early rapid decline is not what longitudinal data show in healthy people. A late abrupt collapse does not describe healthy aging either. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 2, rate of deterioration). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that aging lowers renal mass and renal blood flow, which is one reason renally cleared drugs need closer dosing in older adults (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, age-related changes in pharmacokinetics, p. 246). COMLEX: cross-sectional comparisons of young and old groups exaggerated the expected rate of organ decline (SDL 3 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — age-related changes in absorption, distribution, metabolism, and elimination","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify and discuss the principal factors influencing the functional performance of an organ with aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old man comes for a wellness visit. He has no symptoms, and his BMI is 25 kg/m2. His fasting plasma glucose is 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL), and his hemoglobin A1c is 5.6% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). Two hours after a 75-g oral glucose load, his plasma glucose is 176 mg/dL (reference range: <140 mg/dL). His resting pulse is 68/min, and his serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL). Which other finding in this patient would most likely reflect the same feature of aging physiology?","choices":{"A":"Absent fever during pneumonia","B":"High fasting glucose at rest","C":"Raised resting serum creatinine","D":"Low resting hemoglobin","E":"Rapid resting heart rate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Older adults typically keep normal resting values but adapt poorly to external stress, so a normal fasting glucose can coexist with failure to clear a glucose load; the same blunted response appears as a reduced febrile response to systemic infection and poor blood pressure regulation on standing. A high fasting glucose at rest is an abnormal resting value, not the pattern of normal aging. A raised resting serum creatinine points to kidney disease rather than normal aging. A low resting hemoglobin is anemia, which needs its own explanation. A rapid resting heart rate is also an abnormal resting finding. By ADA criteria, a 2-hour value of 176 mg/dL also falls in the prediabetes range (impaired glucose tolerance, 140–199 mg/dL); Kane and the SDL 3 notes use this pattern as the example of a blunted response to a glucose load. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 2, homeostenosis and adaptation to external stress); Kane Ch. 1 (Changes associated with normal aging). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrogens such as LPS make macrophages release IL-1 and TNF, which increase COX activity and PGE2 in the anterior hypothalamus and reset the temperature set point upward (First Aid Pathology—Inflammation, systemic manifestations of the acute-phase reaction, p. 209). COMLEX: this failure to respond to stress explains why many diseases present atypically in older adults, for example with confusion rather than fever or pain (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — atypical presentations in the aging population","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify and discuss the principal factors influencing the functional performance of an organ with aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man comes for follow-up of his kidney function. From age 60 to 70, his estimated GFR was stable between 74 and 78 mL/min/1.73 m2. At age 70 he developed hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus, and both have been poorly controlled since. Today his blood pressure is 162/94 mm Hg, his hemoglobin A1c is 8.7% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%), his estimated GFR is 41 mL/min/1.73 m2 (reference range: 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 or higher), and his urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio is 240 mg/g (reference range: <30 mg/g). Which explanation best accounts for the decline in his kidney function over the last 8 years?","choices":{"A":"Prior acute kidney injury","B":"Chronic disease progression","C":"Normal senescence with age","D":"Inherited nephron deficit","E":"Lower tubular creatinine secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Longitudinal data show that healthy organs decline far less than cross-sectional studies suggested, and in the kidney some people keep stable function for years; a steep fall that begins with poorly controlled hypertension and diabetes, together with albuminuria, reflects chronic disease accelerating loss rather than normal senescence. No acute kidney injury is described, and his decline has been steady since the two diagnoses. Normal senescence is gradual and varies widely between people. His function was stable for a decade, which argues against an inherited nephron deficit. Less tubular secretion of creatinine raises serum creatinine without changing true filtration, and it does not explain albuminuria or a decline that began with hypertension and diabetes. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 2, normal senescence versus chronic disease); Kane Ch. 1 (Changes associated with normal aging). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetic glomerulonephropathy is the most common cause of ESRD in the United States, is detected by albuminuria on a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio, and is slowed by renoprotective ACE inhibitors and ARBs (First Aid Renal—Pathology, diabetic glomerulonephropathy, p. 616). COMLEX: the earlier theory of gradual renal loss with age must be reassessed as reflecting disease rather than aging (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — effects of hypertension on other organ systems","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the nine hallmarks of aging","batch":2,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman with no chronic illness donates a small skin biopsy to an aging study. Fibroblasts cultured from her skin are compared with fibroblasts from healthy 25-year-old donors grown under the same conditions. Her cells show reduced electron transport chain complex activity and lower ATP synthesis per cell. Her cells show no protein aggregates, and they keep dividing in culture. Which hallmark of aging do these findings most directly represent?","choices":{"A":"Loss of protein homeostasis","B":"Stem cell exhaustion","C":"Mitochondrial dysfunction","D":"Cellular senescence","E":"Deregulated nutrient sensing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mitochondrial dysfunction is the structural and functional decay of mitochondria with less efficient electron transport, reduced ATP synthesis, and more leakage of reactive oxygen species; this bioenergetic decline impairs cell function. Loss of protein homeostasis would show misfolded protein aggregates, which are absent. Stem cell exhaustion concerns depleted progenitor pools. Cellular senescence is irreversible cell cycle arrest, but her cells keep dividing. Deregulated nutrient sensing concerns growth hormone, IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuin signaling. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 3, mitochondrial dysfunction); Kane Ch. 1 (Hallmarks of aging). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: electrons from NADH enter the electron transport chain at complex I and those from FADH2 at complex II, and the resulting proton gradient drives ATP synthesis (First Aid Biochemistry—Metabolism, electron transport chain and oxidative phosphorylation, p. 76). COMLEX: failing mitochondria can speed up the aging process in mammals (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — cellular damage, aging, and organ dysfunction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the nine hallmarks of aging","batch":2,"stem":"Two 101-year-old women live in the same assisted-living facility. The first walks without an aid, manages her own medications, and has a Montreal Cognitive Assessment score of 26 (reference range for age: 22 or higher). The second has heart failure, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and dementia and needs help with every daily activity. In a research study, the chromosome-end repeat regions of the second woman's leukocytes are markedly shorter than those of the first woman. The difference between these women most directly reflects which hallmark of aging?","choices":{"A":"Genomic instability","B":"Epigenetic alteration","C":"Cellular senescence","D":"Telomere attrition","E":"Stem cell exhaustion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Telomere attrition is the progressive shortening of the noncoding TTAGGG repeats at chromosome ends with each division, because DNA polymerases do not fully copy linear chromosome ends and telomerase is repressed in most somatic cells. Genomic instability refers to unrepaired lesions throughout the genome. Epigenetic alteration changes gene expression without shortening DNA. Cellular senescence is the arrest that critical shortening can trigger, not the shortening itself. Stem cell exhaustion is depletion of progenitor pools. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 3, telomere attrition); Kane Ch. 1 (Hallmarks of aging). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: telomerase is upregulated in progenitor cells and often in cancer and is downregulated in aging and progeria (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, telomerase, p. 36). COMLEX: because cancers often depend on telomerase, drugs that block it are being explored as cancer treatments (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — telomeres and cellular senescence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the nine hallmarks of aging","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man and his identical twin brother enroll in an aging study. Whole-genome sequencing of their blood monocytes shows identical DNA sequences, and their CpG methylation profiles are also similar. The brother who smoked for 40 years has COPD, with a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 52% of predicted (reference range: 80% of predicted or higher). His monocytes show markedly different expression of inflammatory genes, and chromatin immunoprecipitation shows altered acetylation and methylation of the proteins around which DNA is wound at those genes. Which mechanism of this hallmark of aging most directly explains the difference in gene expression?","choices":{"A":"Somatic point mutations","B":"Histone modifications","C":"Telomere shortening","D":"Mitochondrial DNA deletions","E":"Chromosomal translocations"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Epigenetic alterations are age-associated changes in gene expression and transcriptional control without changes in the underlying DNA sequence, through altered DNA methylation, histone modifications, and chromatin remodeling. With identical sequences and similar DNA methylation, the altered acetylation and methylation of histone proteins at his inflammatory genes point to histone modification. Somatic point mutations are sequence changes, which sequencing excluded. Telomere shortening is a loss of chromosome-end repeats rather than a change in how genes are read. Mitochondrial DNA deletions are structural DNA changes. Chromosomal translocations are also structural changes that sequencing would show. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 3, epigenetic alterations); Kane Ch. 1 (Hallmarks of aging). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetylating histones removes their positive charge, which loosens DNA coiling and increases transcription, whereas histone methylation usually silences genes (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, chromatin structure, p. 32). COMLEX: Kane lists DNA methylation, histone changes, and chromatin remodeling as the epigenetic alterations that contribute to aging (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — the epigenome and aging at the cellular level","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the diffence of life expectance, life span, and health span","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old physician who emigrated from a low-income country notes that people born in his birth year in that country have an average expected survival 20 years shorter than people born that year in his new country. The oldest verified ages in the two countries are similar. What does this comparison illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Differing species limits","B":"Genetic drift by region","C":"Regional selection bias","D":"Differing birth cohorts","E":"Varying life expectancy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Life expectancy is a statistical average of expected survival, and it varies markedly by region and population, as this comparison of people born in the same year shows. The similar oldest ages show that the biological limit for the species does not differ between the countries. Genetic drift does not account for a 20-year gap in average survival. Selection bias is a study design error, and these are national population averages. Both groups share the same birth year, so cohort differences do not apply. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 1, life expectancy). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mortality rate counts the deaths in a population over a set period, usually given per 1000 people per year (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, quantifying risk, p. 259). COMLEX: average expected survival differs widely from one region and population to another (SDL 3 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — challenges in preventive care in older adults","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the diffence of life expectance, life span, and health span","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman asks her physician how long she might live. Life expectancy can be expressed as a proportion of which value?","choices":{"A":"Her parents' ages at death","B":"Possible maximum age","C":"National retirement age","D":"Her biological age","E":"Average age at diagnosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"As defined in Kane and the SDL 3 notes, life expectancy can be calculated from year of birth or current age, or expressed relative to the possible maximum age a person could reach. Her parents' ages at death are family history rather than the reference value. The national retirement age is a policy figure. Her biological age reflects her own health state, not the reference for a demographic calculation. An average age at diagnosis describes a disease rather than survival. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 1, life expectancy). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on a Kaplan-Meier curve, survival probability equals 1 minus the event probability, and survival differences are tested with the log rank test or Cox regression (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, Kaplan-Meier curve, p. 259). COMLEX: life expectancy is a demographic metric that describes populations rather than a biological limit for any one person (SDL 3 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify and discuss the principal factors influencing the functional performance of an organ with aging","batch":3,"stem":"A healthy 80-year-old man has a normal resting pulse and a normal resting cardiac output on echocardiography. Which measurement is most likely reduced compared with a healthy 30-year-old?","choices":{"A":"Peak cardiac output","B":"Resting cardiac output","C":"Resting heart rate","D":"Resting stroke volume","E":"Supine blood pressure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aging blunts the ability to raise heart rate and cardiac output during exercise, even though resting pulse and cardiac output stay normal, so the deficit appears under stress. Resting cardiac output is typically normal in older adults. Resting heart rate is also typically normal. Resting stroke volume is maintained at rest. Supine blood pressure is a resting value and is not lowered by normal aging. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 2, adaptation to external stress); Kane Ch. 1 (Changes associated with normal aging). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: maximum heart rate decreases with normal aging (First Aid Pathology—Aging, cardiovascular, p. 225). COMLEX: in an older adult, a normal pulse and cardiac output at rest can hide an inadequate rise in both during exercise (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — beta-adrenergic responsiveness of the aging heart","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify and discuss the principal factors influencing the functional performance of an organ with aging","batch":3,"stem":"A healthy 68-year-old man asks when his organs will start losing function. Longitudinal studies that followed healthy people over time found that meaningful decline usually begins at which point?","choices":{"A":"Around age 60","B":"In the early 40s","C":"Well after age 70","D":"Around age 50","E":"In late adolescence"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cross-sectional comparisons suggested a 1% yearly loss from about age 30, but longitudinal data show that deterioration in healthy people is much less dramatic, begins well after age 70, and varies widely between individuals. Decline around age 60 comes earlier than longitudinal data show. Decline in the early 40s reflects the cross-sectional estimate rather than longitudinal data. Decline around age 50 is also too early. Decline in late adolescence is not supported by any data in healthy people. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 2, rate of deterioration); Kane Ch. 1 (Changes associated with normal aging). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a prospective cohort study follows groups with and without a risk factor forward in time and compares disease incidence (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, observational studies, p. 256). COMLEX: older adults in the coming century may show less osteoporosis because of lifelong calcium and vitamin D intake, regular activity, and early treatment (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — changes in muscle and bone with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the nine hallmarks of aging","batch":3,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman with no chronic illness takes part in a research bone marrow study. Compared with marrow from young adults, her marrow contains fewer hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells, and those cells divide less when stimulated in culture. Which common blood finding does this hallmark of aging most directly produce in older adults?","choices":{"A":"Higher platelet count","B":"Higher neutrophil count","C":"Higher red cell mass","D":"Higher rate of anemia","E":"Higher lymphocyte count"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stem cell exhaustion, the progressive loss of proliferative capacity and number of tissue stem and progenitor cells, shows up in the hematopoietic system as an increased incidence of anemia. A higher platelet count would require more marrow output, the opposite of an exhausted stem cell pool. A higher neutrophil count would also need greater marrow output. A higher red cell mass is the opposite of the anemia that stem cell exhaustion causes. A higher lymphocyte count would likewise require more output. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 3, stem cell exhaustion). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aplastic anemia results when hematopoietic stem cells fail or are destroyed, for example after radiation, viral infection, or in Fanconi anemia (First Aid Hematology and Oncology—Pathology, aplastic anemia, p. 427). COMLEX: Kane uses the decline of hematopoietic stem cells with age as the model example of stem cell exhaustion (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — resilience and homeostenosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the nine hallmarks of aging","batch":3,"stem":"An 82-year-old woman's skin fibroblasts are heat-stressed in a research study. Compared with young cells, hers accumulate clumps of unfolded proteins, although proteasome and autophagy activity are similar. Which failure most directly explains the clumps?","choices":{"A":"Failed chaperone refolding","B":"Faster telomere shortening","C":"Excess mTOR inhibition","D":"Greater NAD+ availability","E":"More DNA methylation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of proteostasis is impaired stability, folding, and degradation of intracellular proteins: under stress, molecular chaperones fail to refold denatured polypeptides, and the ubiquitin-proteasome and autophagy-lysosome pathways decline, so unfolded and aggregated proteins accumulate and damage the cell. With clearance activity similar to young cells here, the failure lies in refolding. Faster telomere shortening limits cell division rather than protein refolding. Excess mTOR inhibition promotes autophagy rather than impairing protein handling. Greater NAD+ availability supports sirtuin activity and does not explain failed refolding. More DNA methylation changes gene expression rather than refolding proteins. Sources: SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 3, loss of proteostasis); Kane Ch. 1 (Hallmarks of aging). Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chaperone proteins help other proteins fold and stay folded, and heat shock proteins such as HSP60 rise with heat, acidic pH, or hypoxia to prevent misfolding (First Aid Biochemistry—Molecular, chaperone protein, p. 43). COMLEX: when unfolded proteins are neither refolded nor degraded, they pile up and injure the cell (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis in cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the diffence of life expectance, life span, and health span","batch":5,"stem":"Two 76-year-old women are seen in the same clinic on the same day. The first walks 2 miles daily, takes no medications, and has a gait speed of 1.2 m/s (reference range for age: >0.8 m/s). The second has heart failure with an ejection fraction of 25%, oxygen-dependent COPD, and stage 4 chronic kidney disease, with a serum creatinine of 3.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL). A validated mortality index places the second woman's 4-year risk of death at 64%, compared with 4% for the first woman. The difference in their aging is best captured by which measure?","choices":{"A":"Median survival age","B":"Maximum life span","C":"Birth cohort year","D":"Population life expectancy","E":"Gerontological age"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gerontological age is calculated from a person's risk of dying, the force of mortality, so two people born in the same year can differ widely in biological age according to their health. Median survival age describes a group rather than one person's health state. Their birth cohort year is the same, so it does not separate them. Maximum life span is a species limit. Population life expectancy is an average for a group. Sources: Kane Ch. 1 (chronological versus gerontological aging); SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 1). Textbook enrichment (Kane) — detail not in SDL 3 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: relative risk divides the risk of an outcome in an exposed group by the risk in an unexposed group and is typically used in cohort studies (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, quantifying risk, p. 258). COMLEX: part of a person's risk of dying can be changed by care, and part can only be forecast (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — comorbidity and risk of disability and mortality","isHighYield":false,"enrichmentSource":"Kane"},{"id":"sdl03_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the diffence of life expectance, life span, and health span","batch":5,"stem":"A 70-year-old man enrolls in a trial testing whether metformin, by targeting a fundamental mechanism of aging, can delay several age-related diseases at once. Which concept is the trial testing?","choices":{"A":"Rate-of-living theory","B":"Disposable soma theory","C":"Geroscience hypothesis","D":"Hayflick replication limit","E":"Free radical theory"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The geroscience hypothesis holds that targeting fundamental aging mechanisms may lower the likelihood of multiple aging processes at once, and drugs such as metformin, acarbose, angiotensin receptor blockers, and rapamycin are being studied for this. The rate-of-living theory tries to explain why aging occurs rather than naming a treatment strategy. The disposable soma theory is likewise an evolutionary explanation of aging. The free radical theory explains how damage accumulates rather than proposing a strategy. The Hayflick replication limit describes finite division of cultured cells. Sources: Kane Ch. 1 (Introduction); SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 1, health span). Textbook enrichment (Kane) — detail not in SDL 3 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metformin decreases hepatic gluconeogenesis, increases insulin sensitivity, and can cause lactic acidosis in patients with renal insufficiency (First Aid Endocrine—Pharmacology, metformin, p. 359). COMLEX: the goal of such strategies is to extend health span, the years free of functional limitation (SDL 3 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — IGF-1, mTOR, and sirtuins in aging","isHighYield":false,"enrichmentSource":"Kane"},{"id":"sdl03_q22","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the diffence of life expectance, life span, and health span","batch":5,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman asks why some friends her age are disabled while others are fully active. Her geriatrician explains that, compared with 40 years ago, today's adults over 75 include both more people living with disabilities and more people who are healthy and fully independent. What is this pattern called?","choices":{"A":"Cohort effect","B":"Bimodal shift","C":"Survivor bias","D":"Survival rectangularization","E":"Selection effect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Kane describes today's older adults as reflecting a bimodal shift, with both more people living with disabilities and more older people in good health. A cohort effect is any difference due to the era in which a group was born. Survivor bias is a study design distortion. A selection effect is also a sampling distortion rather than a population pattern. Rectangularization of the survival curve describes more people living close to the maximum age and dying over a narrower age range, not a split into two health groups. Sources: Kane Ch. 1 (Introduction); SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 1, life expectancy and health span). Textbook enrichment (Kane) — detail not in SDL 3 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: extrinsic aging, or photoaging, comes from UVA degradation of dermal collagen and elastin, with the breakdown products collecting in the dermis as solar elastosis (First Aid Pathology—Aging, skin, p. 225). COMLEX: clinicians increasingly recognize that aging proceeds differently, and at different speeds, from one person to the next (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — challenges in preventive care in older adults","isHighYield":false,"enrichmentSource":"Kane"},{"id":"sdl03_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Classify and discuss the principal factors influencing the functional performance of an organ with aging","batch":5,"stem":"An 86-year-old woman is evaluated before starting a renally cleared antibiotic. She weighs 44 kg and has lost muscle mass over several years. Her serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL), and her blood urea nitrogen is 18 mg/dL (reference range: 7–20 mg/dL). A timed 24-hour urine collection shows a creatinine clearance of 38 mL/min (reference range: 88–128 mL/min). Which factor best explains why her serum creatinine is normal despite reduced filtration?","choices":{"A":"Higher renal blood flow","B":"Greater glomerular filtration","C":"Faster hepatic creatinine clearance","D":"Lower creatinine production","E":"Greater muscle mass"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glomerular filtration and renal blood flow both fall with age, but serum creatinine often stays normal because lean muscle mass, and with it creatinine production, falls at the same time; serum creatinine is therefore a poor guide to renal function in older adults, and a clearance estimate such as the Cockcroft-Gault formula is preferred for dosing. Renal blood flow decreases rather than increases with age. Glomerular filtration also falls, as her measured clearance shows. The liver does not clear creatinine (general knowledge). Her muscle mass has fallen, not risen. Sources: Kane Ch. 1 (Changes associated with normal aging; the mechanism is Kane-only); SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 2, normal serum creatinine despite reduced GFR is the phenomenon); First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, age-related changes in pharmacokinetics, p. 246. Textbook enrichment (Kane) — detail not in SDL 3 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: because the proximal tubule secretes some creatinine, creatinine clearance runs slightly above the true GFR it is used to estimate (First Aid Renal—Physiology, clearance, p. 600). COMLEX: falling renal mass and blood flow lower GFR and renal clearance in older adults (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, age-related changes in pharmacokinetics, p. 246), so Kane recommends estimating creatinine clearance from serum creatinine with the Cockcroft-Gault formula (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — age-related changes in absorption, distribution, metabolism, and elimination","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Kane"},{"id":"sdl03_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the nine hallmarks of aging","batch":5,"stem":"A 70-year-old man donates skin fibroblasts to a laboratory study. As the cultured cells approach replicative senescence, they are transfected with the gene for the catalytic subunit of the enzyme that adds DNA bases to chromosome ends. Compared with untreated cells from the same culture, which outcome is expected?","choices":{"A":"More cell divisions","B":"Immediate growth arrest","C":"Faster end shortening","D":"Earlier apoptotic death","E":"Loss of DNA methylation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Kane reports that transfecting the catalytic component of telomerase into cells at or near senescence extends their telomeres and their replicative life span, so the cells undergo more divisions and take on a youthful pattern of gene expression. Immediate growth arrest is the opposite of the expected effect. Earlier apoptotic death is also the opposite of what telomere extension produces. Telomerase lengthens chromosome ends rather than shortening them faster. Loss of DNA methylation is an epigenetic change unrelated to telomerase. Sources: Kane Ch. 1 (Hallmarks of aging); SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 3, telomere attrition). Textbook enrichment (Kane) — detail not in SDL 3 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cancer cells gain limitless replicative potential because telomerase maintenance prevents the chromosome shortening that leads to cell aging (First Aid Pathology—Neoplasia, hallmarks of cancer, p. 217). COMLEX: whether telomere shortening drives senescence and aging inside the living body is still an open question (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — telomeres and cellular senescence","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Kane"},{"id":"sdl03_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the nine hallmarks of aging","batch":5,"stem":"A 99-year-old woman enrolled in a centenarian study asks what her leukocyte telomere length can tell her. In studies of the oldest old, telomere length has not shown a relationship with which outcome?","choices":{"A":"Physical function","B":"Overall health status","C":"Daily functioning","D":"Healthy aging","E":"Risk of death"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Studies of the oldest old have not demonstrated a relationship between telomere length and mortality, but telomere length may track function and health instead: healthy centenarians had significantly longer telomeres than unhealthy centenarians, and a population cohort aged 70 to 79 showed similar findings. Physical function is one of the outcomes telomere length may track. Overall health status is another. Daily functioning also differed with telomere length in these cohorts. Healthy aging is the broader outcome linked to longer telomeres. Sources: Kane Ch. 1 (Hallmarks of aging, telomere attrition; Table 1-2); SDL 3 lecture notes (Obj 3, telomere attrition). Textbook enrichment (Kane) — detail not in SDL 3 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 3 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists telomere shortening as a genetic change tied to normal aging, alongside DNA methylation and mitochondrial dysfunction (First Aid Pathology—Aging, normal aging, p. 225). COMLEX: Kane suggests telomere length may reflect function and health more than exceptional longevity (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — telomeres and cellular senescence","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Kane"}]},{"sdlNumber":4,"title":"SDL 4 — Health Promotion and Disease Prevention","questions":[{"id":"sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review the difference and examples of primary prevention, secondary prevention, and tertiary prevention of disease","batch":1,"stem":"Giving the seasonal influenza vaccine to a 70-year-old man with no chronic illness is an example of which level of prevention?","choices":{"A":"Secondary prevention","B":"Tertiary prevention","C":"Quaternary prevention","D":"Primordial prevention","E":"Primary prevention"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Primary prevention wards off a disease or delays its onset; immunizations against influenza, pneumococcus, tetanus and diphtheria, and herpes zoster are the classic examples. Secondary prevention detects disease early or slows disease that is already present. Tertiary prevention optimizes function in established chronic disease through rehabilitation and proactive management. Quaternary prevention removes unnecessary interventions. Primordial prevention targets the development of risk factors in whole populations. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 1, primary prevention); Kane Ch. 5 (General Principles). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the disease prevention table pairs each level with a target group, from asymptomatic people screened for presymptomatic disease to symptomatic patients whose overtreatment is stopped to avoid iatrogenic harm (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Healthcare Delivery, disease prevention, p. 275). COMLEX: in older adults, a single primary measure such as a vaccine can also shorten illness and lower the need for a hospital stay (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — goals and challenges of chronic disease management in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review the difference and examples of primary prevention, secondary prevention, and tertiary prevention of disease","batch":1,"stem":"Tertiary prevention in older adults is often described by what alternative name?","choices":{"A":"Primordial prevention","B":"Catastrophe prevention","C":"Lifestyle risk reduction","D":"Risk factor screening","E":"Quaternary prevention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tertiary prevention optimizes function, rehabilitation, and chronic disease management after a condition is established, and in geriatrics it is described as catastrophe prevention; it is the core of proactive chronic care. Primordial prevention targets the emergence of risk factors in whole populations. Lifestyle risk reduction belongs to primary prevention, before a condition is established. Risk factor screening is an earlier level of prevention as well. Quaternary prevention withdraws unnecessary interventions to avoid overtreatment. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 1, tertiary prevention); Kane Ch. 5 (General Principles). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetes damages small vessels, producing retinopathy, neuropathy, and nephropathy, and large vessels, producing coronary, cerebrovascular, and peripheral vascular disease (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, diabetes mellitus, p. 350). COMLEX: Kane calls tertiary prevention the central part of good geriatric care because it works to keep disease from turning into disability (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — goals and challenges of chronic disease management in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review the difference and examples of primary prevention, secondary prevention, and tertiary prevention of disease","batch":1,"stem":"Stopping smoking in a patient who already has peripheral arterial insufficiency, to prevent gangrene and amputation, illustrates which aim of secondary prevention in older adults?","choices":{"A":"Eradicating infectious agents","B":"Modifying the home layout","C":"Rehabilitating after a stroke","D":"Slowing established disease","E":"Vaccinating healthy adults"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secondary prevention has two meanings: screening for asymptomatic or early disease, and using preventive techniques in people who already have a disease to delay its progression and its morbidity, such as stopping smoking in a patient with peripheral arterial insufficiency to prevent gangrene and amputation. First Aid (p. 275) files the prevention of progression in established, symptomatic disease under tertiary prevention, so a Step 1 question may label this example tertiary; the course follows Kane and the SDL 4 notes. Vaccinating healthy adults is primary prevention. Modifying the home layout is also primary prevention, acting before an injury. Rehabilitating after a stroke is tertiary prevention. Eradicating infectious agents is a public health goal rather than a level of individual prevention. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 1, secondary prevention); Kane Ch. 5 (General Principles). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: modifiable risk factors for atherosclerosis are hypertension, tobacco smoking, dyslipidemia, and diabetes, and the disease can cause claudication in the legs (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, atherosclerosis, p. 305). COMLEX: for older adults, the second meaning of secondary prevention may matter more than screening, because it delays morbidity in people who already carry the disease (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — goals and challenges of chronic disease management in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the challenges in preventive care in older adults","batch":1,"stem":"Many preventive interventions, such as cancer screening and intensive glycemic control, show a population benefit only after a lag of roughly how long?","choices":{"A":"Under 3 months","B":"About 1 year","C":"30 to 40 years","D":"Over 50 years","E":"5 to 15 years"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The lag time to benefit for many preventive measures, including cancer screening and intensive glycemic control, is roughly 5 to 15 years (SDL 4 notes), whereas the harms of the test or treatment occur immediately; prevention plans for older adults should therefore weigh near-term function and quality of life. Under 3 months is far too short for these measures to pay off. About 1 year is still too short. A lag of 30 to 40 years exceeds the time frame described. Over 50 years is far beyond it. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 2, lag time to benefit); Kane Ch. 5 (General Principles, Table 5-1). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lead-time bias arises when earlier detection is read as longer survival even though the disease course is unchanged, as with breast cancer found early by mammography (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, bias and study errors, p. 262). COMLEX: Kane advises that prevention decisions for older adults weigh both immediate and future benefit, with emphasis on morbidity and quality of life over mortality (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — aging and multiple comorbid illnesses","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the challenges in preventive care in older adults","batch":1,"stem":"Because older adults have a high baseline risk of conditions such as influenza complications and osteoporotic fracture, an effective preventive intervention in this group typically yields what?","choices":{"A":"Greater absolute benefit","B":"Little benefit of any kind","C":"A higher false-negative rate","D":"Shorter lag time to benefit","E":"Lower drug toxicity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The benefit of prevention depends on how common the problem is and how effective the intervention is; because baseline risk is high in older adults, effective measures such as influenza immunization and osteoporosis treatment produce higher absolute risk reductions. Ageism may lead clinicians to assume there is little benefit, but the evidence does not support that view. A high baseline risk does not raise a test's false-negative rate. It does not shorten the lag time to benefit either. Drug toxicity is higher, not lower, in older adults. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 2, high baseline risk); Kane Ch. 5 (General Principles). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absolute risk reduction is the difference, rather than the proportion, in risk between the control and treated groups, and its reciprocal gives the number needed to treat (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, quantifying risk, p. 258). COMLEX: Kane notes that preventing bone loss gives better value for money in old age than in youth, since falls and fractures are already frequent at baseline (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — aging and multiple comorbid illnesses","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the challenges in preventive care in older adults","batch":1,"stem":"Evidence for many preventive interventions is less certain in geriatric patients because clinical trials historically excluded which group?","choices":{"A":"Adults aged 50 to 64","B":"Adults under 40","C":"Adults over 75 or 80","D":"Current smokers","E":"Adults aged 65 to 74"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Many landmark prevention trials excluded adults over 75 or 80 and those with multiple chronic illnesses, so clinicians must use individualized judgment when applying the results to frail adults and those over 90. Adults aged 50 to 64 were well represented in these trials. Adults under 40 were not the excluded group. Current smokers were not excluded on that basis. Adults aged 65 to 74 were also commonly enrolled. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 2, underrepresentation in research, including the 75 or 80 cutoff); Kane Ch. 5 (Effectiveness of Prevention in Older People). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: selection bias occurs when subjects are sampled or assigned nonrandomly, leaving a study group unlike the population of interest (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, bias and study errors, p. 262). COMLEX: because the oldest and sickest patients were left out of most trials, prevention guidance for a frail 90-year-old rests on clinical judgment more than on trial data (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — aging and multiple comorbid illnesses","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the different domains of mangement in prevention of disease in a geriatric population","batch":1,"stem":"In preventive care for older adults, the focus shifts from preventing single-organ disease toward preventing what?","choices":{"A":"Rare genetic diseases","B":"Childhood infections","C":"Acute surgical emergencies","D":"Geriatric syndromes","E":"Occupational injuries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Prevention in older adults shifts from organ-specific disease toward preventing geriatric syndromes such as falls, delirium, incontinence, immobility, and malnutrition, while preserving function and independence. Rare genetic diseases are not a main target of geriatric prevention. Childhood infections belong to pediatric prevention. Acute surgical emergencies are treated rather than prevented in this framework. Occupational injuries concern working-age adults. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 3, shift in focus); Kane Ch. 5 (General Principles). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: delirium is an acute, fluctuating change in attention and arousal that is usually secondary to an identifiable illness or drug and is typically reversible (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, delirium, p. 575). COMLEX: Kane points out that among older adults, preventing falls, dizziness, and functional decline matters more than targeting any one disease (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the different domains of mangement in prevention of disease in a geriatric population","batch":1,"stem":"Within the medication domain of geriatric prevention, which tool helps the clinician identify potentially inappropriate drugs to deprescribe?","choices":{"A":"Braden Scale","B":"Beers Criteria","C":"Morse Fall Scale","D":"Katz ADL Index","E":"Glasgow Coma Scale"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The medication domain includes brown bag reviews of every drug the patient takes, deprescribing, and screening for potentially inappropriate medications with the Beers Criteria and STOPP/START. The Braden Scale estimates pressure injury risk. The Morse Fall Scale estimates fall risk. The Katz ADL Index measures basic activities of daily living. The Glasgow Coma Scale grades level of consciousness. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 3, medication domain); Kane Ch. 5 (Special Risks in Long-Term Care Facilities). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: muscarinic antagonists such as atropine cause hyperthermia, dry flushed skin, cycloplegia, constipation, and disorientation, and in older adults they can provoke acute angle-closure glaucoma or urinary retention (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, atropine, p. 240). COMLEX: Kane pairs explicit screening lists, the Beers Criteria and STOPP/START, with the habit of stopping any drug that is no longer needed (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the different domains of mangement in prevention of disease in a geriatric population","batch":1,"stem":"In the Wisconsin Star Framework, delivering age-appropriate immunizations and monitoring chronic diseases proactively belong to which domain of geriatric prevention?","choices":{"A":"Medical domain","B":"Medication domain","C":"Environmental domain","D":"Social and functional domain","E":"Lifestyle domain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The medical domain covers finding remediable acute and chronic conditions, monitoring chronic diseases proactively, and giving age-appropriate immunizations. The medication domain centers on reviewing and deprescribing drugs. The environmental domain concerns how well the home fits the person. The social and functional domain addresses daily function and isolation. The lifestyle domain covers exercise, diet, and similar behaviors. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 3, medical domain); Kane Ch. 5 (Immunizations). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the PPSV23 pneumococcal vaccine uses capsular polysaccharide, a thymus-independent antigen that is not presented by MHC to T cells, so it is weakly immunogenic (First Aid Immunology—Immune Responses, thymus-independent antigens, p. 103). COMLEX: Kane reports that only about 20% of older adults had received the herpes zoster vaccine, far below the national goal of 90% (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify how iatrogenesis affects the geriatric adult","batch":1,"stem":"The risk of an adverse drug reaction rises to nearly 100% when an older adult takes 10 medications. Approximately what is the risk with a single medication?","choices":{"A":"About 1%","B":"About 30%","C":"About 10%","D":"About 50%","E":"About 70%"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Polypharmacy is the strongest driver of adverse drug reactions: the risk is about 10% with one medication and climbs to nearly 100% with 10 (SDL 4 notes). Kane estimates that about 15% of older adults are at risk for medication-related adverse events. A 1% risk underestimates the single-drug figure. A 30% risk lies between the single-drug and 10-drug figures. A 50% risk also lies between them. A 70% risk approaches the 10-drug figure rather than the single-drug one. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 4, polypharmacy) for the 10% and 100% figures; Kane Ch. 5 (Iatrogenesis) for the 15% figure. Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: two drugs can act additively, synergistically, or by potentiation, in which a drug lacking therapeutic action of its own strengthens the action of another (First Aid Pharmacology—Pharmacokinetics and Pharmacodynamics, drug effect modifications, p. 234). COMLEX: Kane advises prescribing a new drug only when it is needed and starting it at a low dose that is raised slowly (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — risk for adverse drug reactions in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify how iatrogenesis affects the geriatric adult","batch":1,"stem":"Roughly what share of medication-related adverse events in older adults is considered preventable?","choices":{"A":"At least half","B":"Fewer than 5%","C":"About one tenth","D":"About one fifth","E":"Under one quarter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"About 15% of older adults are at risk for a medication-related adverse event (Kane), and at least half of these events are preventable. Fewer than 5% far understates how many could be avoided. About one tenth also understates the preventable share. About one fifth is still well below it. Under one quarter likewise understates what medication review and careful prescribing can prevent. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 4, polypharmacy); Kane Ch. 5 (Iatrogenesis). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a near miss is an unplanned event that causes no harm but could have, such as a pharmacist catching a drug interaction and canceling the order (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Healthcare Delivery, types of medical errors, p. 277). COMLEX: Kane identifies iatrogenic disease as possibly the most preventable problem in the care of older people (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — risk for adverse drug reactions in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify how iatrogenesis affects the geriatric adult","batch":1,"stem":"What term describes the serial development of multiple medical complications set off by a single, seemingly innocuous first event in an older patient?","choices":{"A":"Diagnostic momentum","B":"Cascade iatrogenesis","C":"Defensive medicine","D":"Medication nonadherence","E":"Therapeutic inertia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cascade iatrogenesis is the serial development of multiple medical complications triggered by a seemingly innocuous first event; for example, postoperative opioids can lead to oversedation, hypoventilation, atelectasis, intubation, ventilator-associated pneumonia, sepsis, and death. Diagnostic momentum is the carrying forward of an early diagnosis without reassessment. Defensive medicine is testing done mainly to avoid liability. Medication nonadherence is failure to take medications as prescribed. Therapeutic inertia is failure to intensify treatment when indicated. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 4, cascade iatrogenesis); Kane Ch. 5 (Special Risks of Hospitals). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: opioid intoxication stimulates μ receptors, producing pinpoint pupils with respiratory and CNS depression, and an overdose is reversed with naloxone (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, psychoactive drug intoxication and withdrawal, p. 588). COMLEX: Kane urges clinicians to talk through the possible harms and gains of each test and treatment with older patients before going ahead (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — risk for adverse drug reactions in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review the difference and examples of primary prevention, secondary prevention, and tertiary prevention of disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman with osteoarthritis of both knees lives alone in a two-story house. A home visit finds loose rugs and stacks of magazines in the hallway, a low armchair that she struggles to rise from, and no handholds in the bathroom. She has not fallen. The team removes the clutter, raises the chair and bed to a suitable height, and installs grab bars beside the toilet and tub. These changes prevent falls through which approach?","choices":{"A":"Greater host resistance","B":"Earlier disease detection","C":"Slower disease progression","D":"Safer surroundings","E":"Restored lost function"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Primary prevention lowers risk either by making the person more resistant or by making the environment less harmful; clearing clutter, adjusting chair and bed heights, and installing grab bars are home safety adaptations that act on the environment before any fall has occurred. Greater host resistance is the route of vaccination and exercise. Earlier disease detection is one meaning of secondary prevention. Slower disease progression in established disease is the other meaning of secondary prevention. Restoring lost function after an injury is tertiary prevention, and she has not yet fallen. USPSTF: exercise for community-dwelling adults ≥65 at increased fall risk (B); multifactorial programs including home modification are individualized (C). Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 1, primary prevention, home safety); Kane Ch. 5 (General Principles). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteoporosis can present with vertebral compression fractures or with fractures of the femoral neck or distal radius (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Skin, and Connective Tissue—Pathology, osteoporosis, p. 467). COMLEX: seat belts, clutter removal, and grab bars count as primary prevention because they make the setting less dangerous rather than changing the patient (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — interventions for patients with fall risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review the difference and examples of primary prevention, secondary prevention, and tertiary prevention of disease","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old man has heart failure, type 2 diabetes, and mild cognitive impairment and lives by himself. His primary care team, which includes a nurse, a pharmacist, and a social worker, performs a comprehensive geriatric assessment every year and checks on him by phone between visits. His hemoglobin A1c is 7.4% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). What is the main purpose of this proactive, team-based tertiary care?","choices":{"A":"Screen for occult cancers","B":"Lengthen maximum life span","C":"Replace primary care visits","D":"Reduce drug costs","E":"Preserve independent living"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tertiary prevention in older adults centers on optimizing function and managing established chronic disease so that it does not progress to disability; regular comprehensive geriatric assessment within proactive, multidisciplinary primary care catches early changes and helps the patient keep living on his own. Screening for occult cancer is secondary prevention. Maximum life span is a species limit that care does not change. The team supports rather than replaces primary care visits. Lowering drug costs is not the main aim of the assessment. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 1, tertiary prevention); Kane Ch. 5 (General Principles). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: every patient with diabetes needs teaching on diet, exercise, glucose monitoring, and managing complications, and metformin is the first-line oral agent for type 2 diabetes (First Aid Endocrine—Pharmacology, diabetes mellitus therapy, p. 358). COMLEX: a comprehensive effort that addresses the physical, psychological, and environmental contributors to dependency is what makes tertiary prevention effective in older adults (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — goals and challenges of chronic disease management in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review the difference and examples of primary prevention, secondary prevention, and tertiary prevention of disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 77-year-old woman with well-controlled hypertension comes for her annual wellness visit and has no complaints. Her blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. Which planned intervention at this visit is an example of secondary prevention?","choices":{"A":"Zoster vaccination","B":"Hearing assessment","C":"Bathroom grab bars","D":"Post-stroke therapy","E":"Diabetic footwear"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Secondary prevention includes early detection of conditions that are present but not yet causing complaints, and depression, vision, hearing, oral health, skin lesions, and blood pressure are listed in the course notes as targeted screens. USPSTF grades depression (B) and BP (A), but rates hearing, vision, oral health and skin screening in asymptomatic older adults as I (insufficient evidence). Zoster vaccination is primary prevention because it acts before disease exists. Bathroom grab bars are also primary prevention, acting before an injury occurs. Physical therapy after a stroke is tertiary prevention, which limits complications of established disease. Protective diabetic footwear is likewise tertiary prevention. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 1, secondary prevention examples); Kane Ch. 5 (General Principles, Table 5-2). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: high sensitivity makes a test useful for ruling disease out when the result is negative, which suits screening (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, evaluation of diagnostic tests, p. 260). COMLEX: in geriatric screening the goal is to find problems that will matter during the patient's remaining years, not indolent conditions that would stay harmless (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — sensory system changes with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the challenges in preventive care in older adults","batch":2,"stem":"An 88-year-old woman with advanced dementia, oxygen-dependent COPD, and heart failure lives in a nursing home and needs help with all basic activities of daily living. Her prognosis is estimated at under 2 years. She has never had a colonoscopy, and her daughter asks whether one should be scheduled now. Her hemoglobin is 12.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL), and her serum albumin is 3.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Which factor most strongly argues against screening?","choices":{"A":"Limited life expectancy","B":"Low baseline cancer risk","C":"Poor test sensitivity at 88","D":"Absence of family history","E":"Recent normal blood counts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cancer screening yields its benefit only after years, while the harms of bowel preparation, sedation, and perforation occur at once, so a patient with an expected survival under 2 years is unlikely to live long enough to benefit. Baseline cancer risk rises, rather than falls, with age. Poor test sensitivity at 88 is not the deciding factor. Absence of a family history does not outweigh the mismatch between survival and the time to benefit. Recent normal blood counts do not change that mismatch either. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 2, lag time to benefit; Obj 1, colonoscopy example); Kane Ch. 5 (Cancer Screening and Older Adults). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: average-risk adults begin colorectal cancer screening at age 45 with colonoscopy, and flexible sigmoidoscopy, fecal immunochemical testing, and CT colonography are alternatives (First Aid Gastrointestinal—Pathology, colorectal cancer, p. 395). COMLEX: screening a 90-year-old woman for breast cancer is Kane's example of looking for disease unlikely to become a problem in her lifetime (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — life expectancy, life span, and health span","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the challenges in preventive care in older adults","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old woman's blood pressure was 168/86 mm Hg, and her physician started amlodipine, lisinopril, and chlorthalidone at once to reach a systolic target below 120 mm Hg. Three weeks later she falls while getting out of bed. Her blood pressure is 108/60 mm Hg supine and 84/50 mm Hg after 3 minutes of standing. Her serum sodium is 133 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L), and her serum creatinine is 1.3 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL). Which feature of preventive care in older adults best explains this outcome?","choices":{"A":"Higher baseline disease risk","B":"Narrowed therapeutic window","C":"Longer lag time to benefit","D":"Lower adherence to therapy","E":"Higher cancer incidence"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Older adults have a narrowed therapeutic window because of altered pharmacokinetics, reduced renal excretion, and diminished homeostatic resilience, so a dose that is safe in a younger adult can cause harm; overzealous blood pressure lowering produces orthostatic hypotension and falls, just as prophylactic anticoagulation can cause fatal bleeding. Higher baseline risk increases the potential benefit of prevention rather than the harm. A longer lag time to benefit delays benefit but does not explain an immediate adverse effect. She took her drugs as prescribed, so adherence is not the issue. Higher cancer incidence is unrelated to her fall. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 2, narrowed therapeutic window); Kane Ch. 5 (Table 5-1; Iatrogenesis). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the therapeutic index is the median toxic dose divided by the median effective dose, and drugs with a low index such as warfarin, digoxin, and lithium usually need monitoring (First Aid Pharmacology—Pharmacokinetics and Pharmacodynamics, therapeutic index, p. 233). COMLEX: falls caused by aggressive antihypertensive treatment show how a preventive drug can do immediate harm in an older adult (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 6 — medication classes associated with fall risk","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the challenges in preventive care in older adults","batch":2,"stem":"An 86-year-old man has heart failure with an ejection fraction of 20%, stage 4 chronic kidney disease, and COPD. Two years ago he began a bisphosphonate after a vertebral compression fracture. He has had no further fractures but dies of progressive heart failure. His last serum creatinine was 3.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL). Which challenge of geriatric prevention does his course illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Lag time to benefit","B":"Narrow therapeutic window","C":"Trial underrepresentation","D":"Lead-time bias","E":"Competing risks"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Multimorbidity creates competing risks: even when a preventive treatment lowers the risk of one outcome, such as fracture, the patient may die of another condition first, which limits the net benefit of preventing a single disease. A lag time to benefit would mean the drug had not yet worked, but he had no further fractures. A narrow therapeutic window describes drug harm, which did not occur. Trial underrepresentation concerns the evidence base rather than his outcome. Lead-time bias concerns survival statistics after screening. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 2, competing risks); Kane Ch. 5 (General Principles, Table 5-1). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bisphosphonates are pyrophosphate analogs that bind hydroxyapatite and promote osteoclast apoptosis, and their adverse effects include esophagitis, osteonecrosis of the jaw, and atypical femoral stress fractures (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Skin, and Connective Tissue—Pharmacology, bisphosphonates, p. 496). COMLEX: when several serious illnesses coexist, the benefit of preventing one disease is diluted because another illness is likely to cause death first (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — comorbidity and risk of disability and mortality","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the different domains of mangement in prevention of disease in a geriatric population","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old widower with macular degeneration has lost 4 kg over 3 months. He no longer drives, cannot carry groceries, and often skips lunch and dinner because cooking is difficult. His children live in another state, and he rarely leaves the house. His serum albumin is 3.2 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL), and his hemoglobin is 13.0 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Which intervention best addresses the domain of prevention most responsible for his decline?","choices":{"A":"Meals on Wheels referral","B":"Brown bag medication review","C":"Hearing aid fitting","D":"Colonoscopy screening","E":"Bone density scan"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The social and functional domain addresses deficits in activities of daily living and instrumental activities such as shopping and cooking, social isolation, and caregiver resources, and it links patients with community services such as Meals on Wheels; his weight loss stems from inability to shop and cook while living alone. A brown bag review belongs to the medication domain, and he has no drug problem described. A hearing aid fitting targets a sensory loss he does not have; his deficit is visual. Colonoscopy screening is disease screening that does not address why he is not eating. A bone density scan is also a screen that leaves his nutrition problem untouched. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 3, social and functional domain). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: kwashiorkor is protein malnutrition with skin lesions, edema from low plasma oncotic pressure, and a fatty liver (First Aid Biochemistry—Nutrition, protein-energy malnutrition, p. 69). COMLEX: the Wisconsin Star Framework places instrumental activities, isolation, and community services in the social and functional domain (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — risk factors for poor nutritional status in older adults","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the different domains of mangement in prevention of disease in a geriatric population","batch":2,"stem":"An 86-year-old woman is in a rehabilitation unit after treatment for pneumonia. She could walk with a cane before admission. Each time she sits up at the edge of the bed, a bed-exit sensor sounds and staff help her lie back down, so she now spends most of the day in bed. Which change in her care setting best follows the environmental domain of prevention?","choices":{"A":"Adding a lap restraint","B":"Starting a sedative","C":"Removing the bed alarm","D":"Inserting a urinary catheter","E":"Adding bed side rails"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The environmental domain identifies hazards and removes counterproductive institutional restrictions, such as bed alarms that discourage mobility, while ensuring safe seating and bed heights; removing the alarm lets her get up and walk, which protects against deconditioning. A lap restraint restricts movement further and adds its own harms. Starting a sedative raises the risk of falls and delirium. Inserting a urinary catheter tethers her and invites infection. Adding bed side rails restricts movement and can worsen falls. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 3, environmental domain); Kane Ch. 5 (Preventing Disability, bed alarms and deconditioning). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pressure injuries form at weight-bearing sites such as the sacrum, ischium, and heel when unrelieved pressure persists, as during immobility (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Skin, and Connective Tissue—Dermatology, cutaneous ulcers, p. 491). COMLEX: function-focused care, which builds functional tasks into every care interaction, helps maintain function and physical activity in older patients (Kane Ch. 5, Special Risks in Long-Term Care Facilities).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — negative outcomes of impaired mobility and falls","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the different domains of mangement in prevention of disease in a geriatric population","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman with low income has diabetes, heart failure, and depression and has had several urgent care visits in the past year. She enrolls in a program in which a nurse practitioner and a social worker visit her at home, carry out a geriatric assessment, and develop a care plan with a geriatrics team. Her hemoglobin A1c is 8.1% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). Compared with usual care, this type of interprofessional model has been shown to produce which outcome?","choices":{"A":"Longer maximum life span","B":"Lower vaccination rates","C":"More emergency room visits","D":"Fewer hospital admissions","E":"Higher overall costs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Interprofessional care brings together primary care clinicians, geriatricians, nurses, pharmacists, physical and occupational therapists, and social workers; the GRACE program, in which a nurse practitioner and social worker team visits low-income older adults at home, reduces hospital admissions, emergency department visits, and costs and improves self-rated health. Maximum life span is a species limit. Lower vaccination rates are not a result of team care. Emergency room visits fell rather than rose. Overall costs also fell rather than rose. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 3, interprofessional team, GRACE). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: palliative care is delivered by an interdisciplinary team, for example physicians, nurses, and social workers, in hospitals, outpatient clinics, and patients' homes (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Healthcare Delivery, palliative care, p. 276). COMLEX: team models such as GRACE pair home-based assessment with a geriatrics team plan to keep low-income older adults out of acute care (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl04_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify how iatrogenesis affects the geriatric adult","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman is brought in because she has been confused and drowsy for 3 days. Five days ago she began over-the-counter cimetidine for heartburn. Her other medications, unchanged for years, are levothyroxine, amlodipine, calcium carbonate, and acetaminophen as needed. She is afebrile, and a urinalysis is normal. Her serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L), and her serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL). Which property of cimetidine most likely explains her confusion?","choices":{"A":"P-450 enzyme inhibition","B":"Blood-brain barrier entry","C":"Antiandrogenic activity","D":"Reduced creatinine secretion","E":"Gastric acid suppression"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The SDL 4 notes list new confusion from anticholinergic drugs and H2 blockers such as cimetidine among the adverse drug events of older adults. Cimetidine crosses the blood-brain barrier, where it can cause confusion, dizziness, and headache, and a drug started just before the change is the leading suspect. Its inhibition of cytochrome P-450 causes interactions with other drugs, none of which here act on the brain. Its antiandrogenic effect causes gynecomastia and impotence, and its reduction of renal creatinine secretion raises the serum creatinine without harming the kidney. Acid suppression is its intended effect. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 4, adverse drug events); First Aid Gastrointestinal—Pharmacology, H2-blockers, p. 406. Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 blockers such as famotidine and cimetidine reversibly block histamine H2 receptors on parietal cells, lowering acid secretion, and are used for peptic ulcer disease, gastritis, and mild reflux (First Aid Gastrointestinal—Pharmacology, H2-blockers, p. 406). COMLEX: in an older adult with new confusion, a recently started drug, including an over-the-counter one, is a leading suspect (general knowledge).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — risk for adverse drug reactions in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify how iatrogenesis affects the geriatric adult","batch":2,"stem":"An 81-year-old man has been in the hospital for 3 weeks after a stroke with right hemiparesis and has been immobile for most of that time. He now has a stage 2 sacral pressure injury. His serum calcium has risen since admission and is now 11.2 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL), and his parathyroid hormone is 9 pg/mL (reference range: 10–65 pg/mL). Which process most likely accounts for his hypercalcemia?","choices":{"A":"Increased bone resorption","B":"Tumor PTHrP secretion","C":"Increased calcitriol synthesis","D":"Increased renal calcium reabsorption","E":"Increased intestinal absorption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Prolonged immobility removes the mechanical loading that maintains the skeleton, and resorption outpaces formation; the released calcium raises the serum level and suppresses parathyroid hormone. The SDL 4 notes list bone demineralization with hypercalcemia among the musculoskeletal effects of bed rest, and Kane's list of bed-rest complications pairs bone resorption with hypercalcemia. A tumor secreting PTHrP would also suppress PTH, but his calcium rose steadily during immobilization and nothing in his course suggests cancer. Increased calcitriol synthesis is not expected, because suppressed PTH lowers renal calcitriol production and nothing suggests granulomatous disease or lymphoma (general knowledge). Increased renal calcium reabsorption is unlikely when PTH is suppressed (general knowledge). Increased intestinal absorption also falls rather than rises once calcitriol drops (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 4, effects of bed rest); Kane Ch. 5 (Special Risks of Hospitals, Table 5-11). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parathyroid hormone raises serum calcium by increasing bone resorption, increasing calcium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule, and activating 1α-hydroxylase to make calcitriol, while lowering phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule (First Aid Endocrine—Physiology, parathyroid hormone, p. 336). COMLEX: Kane warns that the bed is a dangerous place for an older patient and that confinement to it should be avoided whenever possible (Kane Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — factors contributing to bone loss and fractures","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify how iatrogenesis affects the geriatric adult","batch":2,"stem":"An 88-year-old woman moved into a long-term care facility 6 months ago after a hip fracture. At admission she could dress herself and feed herself slowly. To save time, aides have since dressed, bathed, and fed her every day. She now says she is too frail to do these things and needs help with all of them, and her thigh muscles have visibly wasted. Her TSH is 1.8 μU/mL (reference range: 0.4–4.0 μU/mL), and her Mini-Cog score is unchanged from admission. Which process best explains her decline?","choices":{"A":"Hypothyroid myopathy","B":"Normal cognitive aging","C":"Learned dependency","D":"Medication side effects","E":"Progressive dementia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Learned dependency occurs when staff perform activities of daily living for residents for the sake of efficiency; residents come to see themselves as fragile, and the result is disuse atrophy, loss of self-efficacy, permanent dependence, and a higher risk of falls. Her TSH is normal, so hypothyroid myopathy does not explain the wasting. Normal cognitive aging does not explain a new loss of self-care, since her cognition is stable. No new drug was started, so medication side effects are unlikely. Progressive dementia is excluded by her stable cognition. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 4, learned dependency in long-term care); Kane Ch. 5 (Special Risks in Long-Term Care Facilities). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: skeletal muscle that is not used undergoes atrophy, a loss of tissue mass from a decrease in cell size and/or number (First Aid Pathology—Cellular Injury, atrophy, p. 202). COMLEX: well-meant care that does tasks for residents instead of with them can create the very dependence it was meant to ease (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — contributing factors to frailty in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Review the difference and examples of primary prevention, secondary prevention, and tertiary prevention of disease","batch":3,"stem":"A healthy 74-year-old man who walks 3 miles a day asks whether he should have a screening colonoscopy. The course notes cite colonoscopy, individualized to health and expected survival, as an example of secondary prevention for people under age 75 to 79 who have at least what remaining life expectancy?","choices":{"A":"2 years","B":"5 years","C":"20 years","D":"30 years","E":"10 years"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The SDL 4 notes give colonoscopy for people under age 75 to 79 with at least a 10-year life expectancy as an example of secondary prevention through early detection, adjusted for function and expected survival; a healthy, active 74-year-old meets that bar. USPSTF screens 45–75 (A 50–75, B 45–49) and selectively 76–85 (C) based on health, prior screening and preference; the notes' 10-year rule fits that. A 2-year threshold is too short for the benefit of screening to appear. A 5-year threshold is also too short. A 20-year threshold would exclude most older adults who stand to gain. A 30-year threshold would exclude nearly all of them. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 1, secondary prevention examples); Kane Ch. 5 (Cancer Screening and Older Adults). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron deficiency anemia in men, especially over age 50, and in postmenopausal women raises suspicion for colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal—Pathology, colorectal cancer, p. 395). COMLEX: for older adults, the decision to screen for colorectal cancer rests on function and expected survival more than on age alone (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — life expectancy, life span, and health span","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the challenges in preventive care in older adults","batch":3,"stem":"A 91-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes lives with her son, who has forbidden all desserts and candy. She says meals have become joyless. Her hemoglobin A1c is 7.6% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). What is the best advice?","choices":{"A":"Occasional sweets","B":"Strict sugar-free diet","C":"A second daily insulin","D":"Tighter A1c target","E":"Daily glucose logs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Prevention in older adults involves quality-of-life trade-offs: withholding sweets from a 90-year-old with diabetes erodes her enjoyment of life for little meaningful gain at her age. A strict sugar-free diet adds burden for little gain at her age. A second daily insulin raises the risk of hypoglycemia. A tighter A1c target also raises that risk. Daily glucose logs add effort without addressing what matters to her. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 2, adherence and quality-of-life trade-offs); Kane Ch. 5 (Challenges to Preventive Care Among Older Adults). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a hemoglobin A1c of 6.5% or higher meets the diagnostic cutoff for diabetes and reflects average blood glucose over the previous 3 months (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, diabetes mellitus, p. 350). COMLEX: asking an older adult to give up a source of daily pleasure is a real cost that must be set against any distant gain (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — goals and challenges of chronic disease management in the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the challenges in preventive care in older adults","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old man has a screening test that comes back abnormal. He undergoes further imaging and an invasive biopsy, which shows no disease, but he spends weeks worrying. Which burden of prevention do these events illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Lead-time bias","B":"Length-time bias","C":"Selection bias","D":"Indirect costs","E":"Recall bias"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Prevention carries direct and indirect costs; indirect burdens include anxiety, diagnostic workups triggered by false-positive results, invasive confirmatory biopsies, and lifestyle restrictions. Lead-time bias distorts survival statistics after screening. Length-time bias also concerns screening statistics rather than burdens on the patient. Selection bias concerns how subjects enter a study. Recall bias concerns inaccurate memory of exposures. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 2, direct and indirect costs); Kane Ch. 5 (General Principles, Table 5-1). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a test with high specificity yields few false positives, so a positive result helps rule disease in (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Epidemiology and Biostatistics, evaluation of diagnostic tests, p. 260). COMLEX: worry, extra testing, and biopsies after a false alarm count against screening even when no disease is found (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — shared decision making with the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the different domains of mangement in prevention of disease in a geriatric population","batch":3,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman with heart failure lives alone in the house she has owned for 50 years. Her children want her to move to assisted living, but she says that staying in her own home matters more to her than anything else. Her medications were recently reviewed, and the home has been made safe. What should the team do next?","choices":{"A":"Insist on assisted living","B":"Discuss her priorities","C":"Order a brain MRI","D":"Start vitamin E","E":"Repeat echocardiography"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The psychological and values domain of prevention explores the patient's beliefs, clarifies her priorities and health preferences, and respects her autonomy about where she lives, along with treating mood disorders such as depression. Her medication and environmental domains are already addressed, so the next step is to discuss what matters most to her and build the plan around it. Insisting on assisted living overrides her autonomy. A brain MRI addresses no identified need. Starting vitamin E has no stated indication. A repeat echocardiogram is not prompted by anything in her course. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 3, psychological and values domain). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a physician judges capacity for a specific healthcare decision, using four components: understanding, appreciation, reasoning, and expressing a choice (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, decision-making capacity, p. 267). COMLEX: respecting an older adult's choice about where to live is part of preventive care, not a departure from it (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — shared decision making with the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify how iatrogenesis affects the geriatric adult","batch":3,"stem":"An 87-year-old nursing home resident has no fever or urinary complaints. A routine urine culture grows E. coli, and she is given ciprofloxacin. A week later she has watery diarrhea, and a stool test for C. difficile toxin is positive. Which decision began this harm?","choices":{"A":"Treating bacteriuria","B":"Stopping her laxative","C":"Encouraging oral fluids","D":"Starting a probiotic","E":"Checking a stool toxin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"C. difficile colitis after antibiotics given for asymptomatic bacteriuria is a listed example of iatrogenesis, an unintended harm from testing or treatment that did not serve the patient's goals. Stopping her laxative did not cause colitis. Encouraging oral fluids does not cause colitis either. A probiotic was not the trigger. The stool toxin test confirmed the diagnosis rather than causing it. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 4, iatrogenesis examples); Kane Ch. 5 (Iatrogenesis). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: C. difficile makes toxins A and B that damage enterocytes, and infection most often follows antibiotics such as clindamycin, cephalosporins, and fluoroquinolones (First Aid Microbiology—Clinical Bacteriology, Clostridioides difficile, p. 136). COMLEX: an unneeded antibiotic in an older adult can set off a serious complication with no offsetting benefit (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 6 — host factors in infection and appropriate antimicrobial use","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify how iatrogenesis affects the geriatric adult","batch":3,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman is admitted to a windowless room for cellulitis. Her eyeglasses and hearing devices were left at home. On the second night she becomes disoriented and agitated. Which change would most likely have helped prevent this?","choices":{"A":"Wrist restraints at night","B":"A bedtime benzodiazepine","C":"Restoring sensory aids","D":"Dimmed daytime lighting","E":"Frequent night vital signs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hospitals expose older adults to an unfamiliar environment with poor visual cues and sensory deprivation, which can trigger delirium; returning her glasses and hearing devices restores orientation. Wrist restraints at night raise the risk of delirium. A bedtime benzodiazepine is a recognized delirium trigger. Dimmed daytime lighting removes the visual cues she needs. Frequent night vital signs break up sleep and add to the risk. Sources: SDL 4 lecture notes (Obj 4, hospital hazards and delirium); Kane Ch. 5 (Special Risks of Hospitals). Aligned with SDL 4 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: delirium is managed by treating its cause and by orientation aids such as a nearby clock or calendar, while avoiding restraints and drugs such as benzodiazepines and opioids (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, delirium, p. 575). COMLEX: an unfamiliar hospital room that strips away visual and hearing cues can tip a vulnerable older patient into delirium (SDL 4 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — sensory system changes with aging","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":5,"title":"SDL 5 — Resilience and Homeostenosis","questions":[{"id":"sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concepts of resilience and homeostenosis","batch":1,"stem":"Homeostenosis typically remains clinically silent under which circumstance?","choices":{"A":"During a fever","B":"After surgery","C":"While at rest","D":"In a heat wave","E":"On standing up"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Homeostenosis is the gradual, time-dependent loss of physiological reserve. Because organs carry built-in redundancy, it stays clinically silent at rest, and resting laboratory values often look deceptively normal. During a fever, the stress of infection unmasks the narrowed margin. After surgery, the demands of recovery expose it as well. In a heat wave, the need to shed heat reveals the lost reserve. On standing up, the postural challenge unmasks it rather than hiding it. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 1, homeostenosis clinical features); Kane Ch. 1 (Resilience and Homeostenosis). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: when arterial pressure falls, reduced stretch lowers baroreceptor firing, which raises sympathetic outflow and lowers parasympathetic outflow to restore heart rate, contractility, and blood pressure (First Aid Cardiovascular—Physiology, baroreceptors and chemoreceptors, p. 299). COMLEX: Kane describes homeostenosis as a diminished capacity to respond to a range of homeostatic stressors (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — contributing factors to frailty in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concepts of resilience and homeostenosis","batch":1,"stem":"Resilience in an older adult is defined by the rapidity and completeness with which the person returns to what after an acute stressor such as surgery or infection?","choices":{"A":"Baseline function","B":"Peak adult fitness","C":"Prior body weight","D":"Normal resting labs","E":"Youthful organ reserve"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Resilience describes how quickly, how completely, and how dynamically a person recovers after an acute stressor such as surgery, infection, trauma, or acute illness and returns to baseline function. Peak adult fitness is not the benchmark for recovery. Prior body weight can return even when function has not. Normal resting labs can look normal before function recovers. Youthful organ reserve is not what an older adult is expected to regain. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 1, resilience); Kane Ch. 1 (Resilience and Homeostenosis). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: because aging alters how drugs pass through the body, standard doses can produce higher plasma concentrations, and older patients often need reduced doses to prevent toxicity (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, age-related changes in pharmacokinetics, p. 246). COMLEX: a resilient older adult also shows a stable response to social and behavioral stressors, not only to medical ones (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — contributing factors to frailty in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concepts of resilience and homeostenosis","batch":1,"stem":"In the suspension-bridge analogy for resilience in older adults, a trivial added weight causes an irreversible collapse only after which change has occurred?","choices":{"A":"Failure of one main cable","B":"A single heavy load","C":"Loss of the roadway","D":"Many damaged components","E":"A strong gust of wind"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the bridge analogy, the structure stays standing because many interdependent parts share the load and recover from stress; when several of those parts are damaged at the same time, even a trivial weight can bring the bridge down. Likewise, an older adult with several compromised systems can decline sharply after a minor illness. Failure of one main cable is a single insult that an intact structure can absorb. A single heavy load is likewise absorbed when the parts are sound. Loss of the roadway misreads the analogy, since the roadway carries the load rather than being a damaged part. A strong gust of wind is another single insult that a sound bridge withstands. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 1, structural analogy); Kane Ch. 1 (Resilience and Homeostenosis). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aging acts most on adaptive immunity, with fewer naive B and T cells, preserved memory cells, and weaker responses to new antigens such as pathogens and vaccines (First Aid Pathology—Aging, immune, p. 225). COMLEX: in an older adult with compromised resilience, an otherwise minor medical event can set off systemic decline (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — contributing factors to frailty in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the relationship between aging and multiple comorbid illnesses","batch":1,"stem":"Although chronic illnesses can begin at younger ages, which factor is the single strongest predictor of chronic disease and multimorbidity?","choices":{"A":"Advancing age","B":"Family history","C":"Tobacco smoking","D":"Low income","E":"Physical inactivity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Advancing chronological age is the single strongest predictor of developing chronic disease and multimorbidity. Family history raises the risk of particular diseases rather than of accumulated conditions. Tobacco smoking raises the risk of specific diseases but predicts multimorbidity less strongly than age. Low income is a weaker predictor. Physical inactivity also predicts accumulation less strongly than age. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 2, advancing age as the primary predictor); Kane Ch. 1 (The Link Between Aging and Multimorbidity). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: age, male sex, postmenopausal status, and family history are the non-modifiable risk factors for atherosclerosis (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, atherosclerosis, p. 305). COMLEX: because chronic conditions accumulate with age, an older patient's visit usually involves several diseases at once (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — age and geriatric multimorbidity in ischemic heart disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the relationship between aging and multiple comorbid illnesses","batch":1,"stem":"About 35% of US adults aged 65 to 79 report two or more chronic diseases. Approximately what share of adults aged 80 and older report two or more?","choices":{"A":"About 10%","B":"About 35%","C":"About 50%","D":"About 95%","E":"About 70%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The share of US adults reporting two or more chronic diseases roughly doubles from about 35% at ages 65 to 79 to about 70% at age 80 and older. A figure of 10% is far too low. A figure of 35% is the rate for the 65-to-79 group. A figure of 50% understates the rise. A figure of 95% overstates it. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 2, epidemiology of multimorbidity); Kane Ch. 1 (The Link Between Aging and Multimorbidity). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: age is a predisposing factor for osteoarthritis, along with female sex, obesity, and joint trauma (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Skin, and Connective Tissue—Pathology, osteoarthritis vs rheumatoid arthritis, p. 472). COMLEX: multimorbidity, rather than a single disease, is the usual situation in patients older than 80 (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — age and geriatric multimorbidity in ischemic heart disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the relationship between aging and multiple comorbid illnesses","batch":1,"stem":"Among Medicare beneficiaries, two-thirds have two or more chronic conditions. Roughly what fraction have four or more?","choices":{"A":"One-tenth","B":"One-fifth","C":"One-third","D":"Three-quarters","E":"Two-thirds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Two-thirds of Medicare beneficiaries carry at least two chronic conditions, and one-third carry four or more. One-tenth understates the figure. One-fifth also understates it. Three-quarters overstates it. Two-thirds is the figure for two or more conditions. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 2, epidemiology of multimorbidity); Kane Ch. 1 (The Link Between Aging and Multimorbidity). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medicare Part D covers prescription drugs, a large expense for beneficiaries who carry several chronic conditions (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Healthcare Delivery, Medicare and Medicaid, p. 276). COMLEX: Kane links comorbidity with high health-care use and spending (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — age and geriatric multimorbidity in ischemic heart disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the 'Geriatric Syndromes'","batch":1,"stem":"In geriatric syndromes, the presenting complaint often gives little clue to which feature of the illness?","choices":{"A":"The severity","B":"The etiology","C":"The duration","D":"The body site","E":"The urgency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Geriatric syndromes are common, multifaceted conditions that do not fit single-organ disease categories, and the presenting complaint often says little about the cause; a single presentation, such as immobility, can stem from physical, emotional, or environmental problems. The complaint still conveys the severity of the problem. It also conveys the duration. The body site is usually evident from the complaint. The urgency can likewise be judged from it. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 3, definition and core concept); Kane Ch. 1 (Geriatric Syndromes). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal pressure hydrocephalus produces the triad of urinary incontinence, gait apraxia, and cognitive dysfunction, so one cause can underlie several presenting problems (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, hydrocephalus, p. 536). COMLEX: Kane advises exploring the underlying causes of a presenting problem with the patient so that the right interventions follow (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — multifactorial contributors to fall risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the 'Geriatric Syndromes'","batch":1,"stem":"Among the classic \"I's\" of geriatric syndromes, what does inanition refer to?","choices":{"A":"Inattention","B":"Social withdrawal","C":"Malnutrition","D":"Inactivity","E":"Incontinence"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inanition is malnutrition, seen as unintentional weight loss and loss of lean muscle mass. Inattention is a feature of delirium within intellectual impairment. Social withdrawal falls under isolation. Inactivity relates to immobility. Incontinence is its own \"I.\" Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 3, the I's of geriatric syndromes); Kane Ch. 1 (Geriatric Syndromes). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: marasmus is calorie-deficient malnutrition without edema that produces muscle wasting (First Aid Biochemistry—Nutrition, protein-energy malnutrition, p. 69). COMLEX: Kane's list of I's runs from immobility and instability through iatrogenesis, insomnia, immune deficiency, and impotence (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — multifactorial contributors to fall risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the 'Geriatric Syndromes'","batch":1,"stem":"An adult child asks whether new urinary leakage in an older parent should simply be accepted. How is urinary incontinence in an older adult best regarded?","choices":{"A":"An expected part of aging","B":"A sign of dementia","C":"An untreatable condition","D":"Common but abnormal","E":"A rare finding after 80"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Urinary incontinence is common in older adults but is not a normal part of aging, so new leakage deserves evaluation rather than acceptance. Calling it an expected part of aging wrongly dismisses it. It is not a specific sign of dementia. Kane notes that geriatric syndromes usually respond to nonpharmacological and medical treatment, so it is not untreatable. It is frequent rather than rare after 80. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 3, incontinence); Kane Ch. 1 (Geriatric Syndromes). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stress incontinence comes from outlet incompetence and overflow incontinence from incomplete emptying, while detrusor overactivity causes leakage with a sudden urge to void (First Aid Renal—Pathology, urinary incontinence, p. 618). COMLEX: Kane stresses that geriatric syndromes are common in older adults yet are not part of normal aging (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — multifactorial contributors to fall risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the association between comorbidity and risk for disability and mortality","batch":1,"stem":"Comorbidity reflects the aggregation of established disease damage. Frailty, by contrast, reflects the aggregate of which change across several organ systems?","choices":{"A":"Subclinical system losses","B":"A single severe disease","C":"Diagnosed organ damage","D":"Age-related cognitive change","E":"Recent weight gain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Comorbidity is the sum of established clinical disease, whereas frailty is the sum of subclinical losses of physiological reserve across several organ systems; each independently predicts functional disability and institutionalization. A single severe disease describes neither concept. Diagnosed organ damage describes comorbidity. Age-related cognitive change is not the definition of frailty. Recent weight gain is not part of the definition either. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 4, independent drivers of adverse outcomes); Kane Ch. 1 (The Link Between Aging and Multimorbidity). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aging lowers hepatic mass and blood flow, which reduces first-pass metabolism and hepatic drug clearance (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, age-related changes in pharmacokinetics, p. 246). COMLEX: comorbidity and frailty each predict disability on their own, so a patient can be frail with few diagnoses (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — goals and challenges of chronic disease management in the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the association between comorbidity and risk for disability and mortality","batch":1,"stem":"One month after hospital discharge, older patients with high multimorbidity and frailty have disability rates about how many times higher than robust peers?","choices":{"A":"Two times","B":"Three times","C":"Twenty times","D":"Fifty times","E":"Seven times"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Once hospitalized, older patients with high multimorbidity and frailty have disability rates 1 month after discharge that are about seven times those of robust peers. Two times understates the difference. Three times also understates it. Twenty times overstates it. Fifty times overstates it further. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 4, risk for disability). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: delirium is the commonest form of altered mental status among inpatients, above all in the ICU or after long hospital stays (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, delirium, p. 575). COMLEX: frail older adults with many chronic diseases leave the hospital with far more new disability than robust peers do (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — goals and challenges of chronic disease management in the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the association between comorbidity and risk for disability and mortality","batch":1,"stem":"Multimorbidity in older adults is directly linked to higher rates of which health care outcome?","choices":{"A":"Elective surgery","B":"30-day readmissions","C":"Preventive screening","D":"Outpatient vaccination","E":"Routine dental care"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The SDL 5 notes link multimorbidity to more emergency department visits, longer hospital stays, more unplanned 30-day readmissions, and higher health care spending. Elective surgery is not a utilization outcome tied to multimorbidity. Preventive screening is not one of the listed outcomes. Outpatient vaccination is routine care rather than a marker of multimorbidity. Routine dental care is not among the listed outcomes. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 4, health care utilization); Kane Ch. 1 (The Link Between Aging and Multimorbidity, utilization and expenditures). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: under a bundled payment, a health care organization receives one set amount for a service, whatever it ends up costing, and divides it among all the providers and facilities involved (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Healthcare Delivery, healthcare payment models, p. 276). COMLEX: unplanned returns to the hospital within 30 days are one of the utilization outcomes the notes tie to multimorbidity (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — goals and challenges of chronic disease management in the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the potential for atypical presentations of common clinical problems in the aging population","batch":1,"stem":"Typical signs of disease such as fever, leukocytosis, and severe localized pain are produced by what?","choices":{"A":"The pathogen itself","B":"Direct toxin effects","C":"Antibiotic therapy","D":"Tissue regeneration","E":"The host response"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Classic signs of illness are not produced directly by the pathogen or injury; they are the body's physiological and inflammatory response to the insult. Because homeostenosis and immunosenescence blunt that response, older adults often fail to mount typical signs. The pathogen itself causes damage, but the fever, white cell rise, and pain come from the host. Direct toxin effects also injure tissue without producing these signs themselves. Antibiotic therapy does not generate the signs. Tissue regeneration follows the illness rather than producing its signs. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 5, pathophysiological basis of atypical presentations); Kane Ch. 1 (Atypical Presentation of Common Clinical Problems). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leukocytosis is a systemic sign of the acute-phase reaction, and the predominant white cell depends on the cause, such as neutrophils in bacterial infection (First Aid Pathology—Inflammation, systemic manifestations of the acute-phase reaction, p. 209). COMLEX: Kane notes that decreased responsiveness in older people leads to atypical presentations of disease (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — how infection symptoms differ in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the potential for atypical presentations of common clinical problems in the aging population","batch":1,"stem":"Standard fever definitions often miss infection in older adults. Which rise in temperature above a person's baseline is a more sensitive indicator of fever?","choices":{"A":"0.4°F","B":"1.0°F","C":"4.5°F","D":"2.4°F","E":"6.0°F"},"correct":"D","explanation":"More sensitive fever criteria for older adults include a temperature of 100°F or greater, a rise of 2.4°F over baseline, or an oral temperature above 99°F. A rise of 0.4°F falls within normal variation. A rise of 1.0°F is also within normal variation. A rise of 4.5°F would miss many infections. A rise of 6.0°F would miss even more. Guideline note: the IDSA guideline for long-term care facilities uses a rise of more than 2°F (1.1°C) over baseline, or repeated oral readings above 99°F; the 2.4°F figure follows the SDL 5 notes. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 5, infection); Kane Ch. 1 (Table 1-1, age-related changes and their implications); IDSA 2008 fever guideline for long-term care facilities. Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-1 causes fever and acute inflammation and activates endothelium to express adhesion molecules (First Aid Immunology—Immune Responses, important cytokines, p. 106). COMLEX: Kane's table of aging changes lists a lower baseline temperature, so an infection may not push the reading into the usual fever range (Kane Ch. 1, Table 1-1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — how infection symptoms differ in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the potential for atypical presentations of common clinical problems in the aging population","batch":1,"stem":"Approximately what fraction of infected older adults lack localized physical findings of infection?","choices":{"A":"About one-tenth","B":"About one-half","C":"About one-quarter","D":"About three-quarters","E":"About nine-tenths"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About half of infected older adults have no localized physical findings, and nearly half have no fever or classic laboratory markers of inflammation. About one-tenth understates the problem. About one-quarter also understates it. About three-quarters overstates it. About nine-tenths overstates it further. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 5, infection). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystitis classically presents with dysuria, frequency, urgency, and suprapubic pain, the localizing complaints that an older adult with infection may lack (First Aid Microbiology—Systems, urinary tract infections, p. 178). COMLEX: an examination without localizing signs does not exclude infection in an older adult (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — how infection symptoms differ in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concepts of resilience and homeostenosis","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman lives alone in a third-floor apartment without air conditioning. At a checkup 2 months earlier, her examination and basic laboratory results were normal. During a 4-day heat wave, her daughter finds her lethargic and hot to the touch. Her temperature is 40.3°C (104.5°F), pulse 108/min, and blood pressure 102/60 mm Hg, and her skin is dry. Her only medication is amlodipine. A urinalysis is normal, and a chest radiograph is clear. Which feature of her aging physiology best explains her temperature?","choices":{"A":"Hypothalamic set-point elevation","B":"Occult bacterial infection","C":"Excess thyroid hormone","D":"Anticholinergic drug effect","E":"Narrow thermoregulatory margin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Homeostenosis is silent at rest, which is why her checkup was normal, but a stressor exposes the narrowed compensatory margin; an elevated body temperature in response to high ambient heat is a listed example. Her dry skin and the timing with the heat wave point to failure to shed heat rather than fever. An elevated hypothalamic set point is the mechanism of fever, which is driven by cytokines during infection, and the focused search for a urinary or pulmonary source is negative. Nothing suggests excess thyroid hormone. Amlodipine is not an anticholinergic drug. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 1, homeostenosis clinical features); Kane Ch. 1 (Resilience and Homeostenosis). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: fever is driven by cytokine activation and usually stays below 40°C (104°F), whereas heat stroke reflects an inability to dissipate heat, usually exceeds 40°C, and can cause confusion, rhabdomyolysis, and acute kidney injury (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, fever vs heat stroke, p. 530). COMLEX: Kane gives a raised body temperature in hot surroundings as a clinical example of homeostenosis (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — factors influencing organ performance with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concepts of resilience and homeostenosis","batch":2,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman has a fasting plasma glucose of 94 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL) and a hemoglobin A1c of 5.4% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%) at her annual visit. Two hours after a large holiday meal, a home glucose check reads 182 mg/dL (reference range: <140 mg/dL). She has no symptoms. Which statement best describes her glucose regulation?","choices":{"A":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus","B":"Glucometer reading error","C":"Impaired response to a load","D":"Excess insulin secretion","E":"Primary adrenal insufficiency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because of organ redundancy, homeostenosis leaves resting values such as fasting glucose and A1c looking deceptively normal; an acute challenge exposes the narrowed margin, and inability to clear a glucose load is a listed example. Her fasting glucose and A1c are below the diabetes cutoffs of 126 mg/dL and 6.5%, and a post-meal value of 182 mg/dL is abnormal but below 200 mg/dL, so she does not meet criteria for type 2 diabetes. Nothing suggests a glucometer reading error. Excess insulin secretion would push glucose down, not up. Primary adrenal insufficiency would also lower glucose. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 1, homeostenosis clinical features); First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, diabetes mellitus, p. 350. Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetes is diagnosed by an A1c of 6.5% or higher, a fasting plasma glucose of 126 mg/dL or higher, or a 2-hour oral glucose tolerance test value of 200 mg/dL or higher (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, diabetes mellitus, p. 350). COMLEX: a normal resting value in an older adult does not show how much reserve remains for a challenge (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — cellular damage, aging, and organ dysfunction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concepts of resilience and homeostenosis","batch":2,"stem":"A healthy 76-year-old man takes part in an exercise physiology study. His resting plasma norepinephrine level is higher than that of the young adult volunteers. During graded exercise his catecholamine levels climb further than theirs, yet his peak heart rate rises much less. He takes no medications. Which feature of homeostenosis does his limited rise in heart rate illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Blunted end-organ response","B":"Increased negative feedback","C":"Lower basal sympathetic tone","D":"Expanded functional reserve","E":"Enhanced receptor density"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Homeostenosis features reduced reserve, increased baseline activity, blunted end-organ responsiveness, and a breakdown of negative feedback. His high resting norepinephrine reflects increased baseline activity, and the small heart rate rise despite even higher catecholamine levels shows that the target organ responds poorly. Negative feedback breaks down rather than strengthens with age. His basal sympathetic tone is high rather than low. His limited response argues against expanded functional reserve. It also argues against enhanced receptor density. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 1, physiological hallmarks); Kane Ch. 1 (Resilience and Homeostenosis). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: β1 receptors are Gs-coupled and raise heart rate, contractility, renin release, and lipolysis (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, G-protein–linked second messengers, p. 237). COMLEX: Kane lists enhanced basal activity and poor end-organ responsiveness among the features of homeostatic dysregulation (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — beta-adrenergic responsiveness of the aging heart","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the relationship between aging and multiple comorbid illnesses","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has smoked a pack of cigarettes daily for 50 years and has had hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus for 20 years. He had a myocardial infarction at age 66 and a left hemispheric ischemic stroke at age 70. His serum creatinine is 1.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL), and his estimated GFR is 44 mL/min/1.73 m² (reference range: 60 mL/min/1.73 m² or higher). What best explains why these three organ diseases developed together?","choices":{"A":"Chance co-occurrence with age","B":"Adverse effects of his drugs","C":"A single genetic mutation","D":"Shared vascular risk factors","E":"Recurrent cardiac emboli"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Multimorbidity often develops because separate organ systems share common risk factors and vascular causes; hypertension, smoking, and diabetes together drive coronary artery disease, stroke, and chronic kidney disease. His three diseases follow decades of exposure to all three risk factors, so their clustering is not chance. No adverse effect of his drugs would explain disease in the heart, brain, and kidneys together. A single genetic mutation does not fit decades of shared exposures. Recurrent cardiac emboli do not cause chronic kidney disease of this kind. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 2, shared pathophysiological mechanisms); Kane Ch. 1 (The Link Between Aging and Multimorbidity). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertension predisposes to coronary artery disease, left ventricular hypertrophy, heart failure, atrial fibrillation, aortic dissection, stroke, chronic kidney disease, and retinopathy (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, hypertension, p. 304). COMLEX: Kane points to smoking, hypertension, and diabetes as risk factors shared by coronary disease, stroke, and chronic kidney disease (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — effects of hypertension on other organ systems","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the relationship between aging and multiple comorbid illnesses","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman has type 2 diabetes mellitus, coronary artery disease, chronic kidney disease, and progressive loss of muscle mass with a slowing gait. Her C-reactive protein is 6.2 mg/L (reference range: <3.0 mg/L). Elevated levels of which catabolic cytokines are thought to form a shared biological pathway linking her conditions with aging?","choices":{"A":"IL-2 and IFN-γ","B":"IL-6 and TNF-α","C":"IL-4 and IL-13","D":"IL-10 and TGF-β","E":"IL-12 and IL-23"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Shared biological pathways, including chronic inflammation, elevated catabolic cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF-α, insulin resistance, and cellular senescence, provide a common foundation for many comorbid conditions and for aging itself; her raised CRP fits this low-grade inflammatory state. IL-2 and IFN-γ are Th1 products that activate macrophages and cytotoxic T cells (general knowledge). IL-4 and IL-13 are Th2 cytokines (general knowledge). IL-10 and TGF-β dampen inflammation (general knowledge). IL-12 and IL-23 steer T-cell differentiation (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 2, shared biological pathways); First Aid Immunology—Immune Responses, cytokines, p. 106. Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF-α activates endothelium, recruits white cells, causes vascular leak, and drives cachexia in malignancy (First Aid Immunology—Immune Responses, cytokines, p. 106). COMLEX: chronic inflammation, insulin resistance, and cellular senescence link several chronic diseases to the aging process itself (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 7 — inflammation in the aging process","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the relationship between aging and multiple comorbid illnesses","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, stage 3 chronic kidney disease, and knee osteoarthritis. Her heart failure has been stable for a year on metoprolol succinate, her only medication. Two weeks ago she began over-the-counter ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily for knee pain. She now has a 3-kg weight gain, ankle edema, and worse shortness of breath. Which type of interaction best describes the worsening of her heart failure?","choices":{"A":"Drug-drug interaction","B":"Disease-disease interaction","C":"Drug-disease interaction","D":"Prescribing cascade","E":"Medication nonadherence"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Multimorbidity narrows the therapeutic window and raises the risk of disease–disease, drug–drug, and drug–disease interactions. Ibuprofen, taken for her osteoarthritis, promotes salt and water retention and has worsened another of her conditions, her heart failure, which is a drug–disease interaction. The harm comes from the drug acting on her heart failure, not from an effect on or by metoprolol, so a drug–drug interaction does not explain it. A disease–disease interaction is unlikely because her conditions were stable together until the new drug was started. A prescribing cascade would mean a new drug given to treat a side effect. Medication nonadherence does not apply, since she took the drug as directed. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 2, clinical and therapeutic complexity). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs reversibly inhibit COX-1 and COX-2, and because prostaglandins dilate the afferent arteriole, they can cause renal ischemia as well as interstitial nephritis and gastric ulcers (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Skin, and Connective Tissue—Pharmacology, NSAIDs, p. 496). COMLEX: with several chronic diseases, a drug that helps one condition can harm another, so each new drug is checked against every diagnosis (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — risk for adverse drug reactions in the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the 'Geriatric Syndromes'","batch":2,"stem":"An 85-year-old woman has had urge incontinence for a year, with sudden urges and leakage several times a night. Last night, hurrying to the bathroom in the dark, she tripped and fell, bruising her hip. Radiographs show no fracture, and her urinalysis is normal. She has mild knee osteoarthritis. Which intervention would most directly lower her risk of another fall?","choices":{"A":"A nightly sedative hypnotic","B":"Treatment of her urgency","C":"Raised bed side rails","D":"Hip protector garments","E":"Vitamin B12 injections"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Geriatric syndromes share risk factors, and one syndrome raises the likelihood of another; urinary incontinence that sends a person rushing to the bathroom is the classic route to a fall. Treating the urgency that drives her night-time dashes targets what sent her rushing in the dark. A nightly sedative hypnotic raises fall risk. Raised bed side rails add a barrier she may climb over. Hip protector garments reduce fracture rather than falls. Nothing suggests vitamin B12 deficiency, so injections would not help. Guideline note: the 2023 AGS Beers Criteria advise avoiding strongly anticholinergic bladder drugs such as oxybutynin in older adults with a history of falls, and current overactive bladder guidance prefers behavioral measures and a β3 agonist first. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 3, shared etiological pathways); AGS Beers Criteria 2023. Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: urgency incontinence is treated with muscarinic antagonists such as oxybutynin, which block M3 receptors, or with β3 agonists such as mirabegron, which relax the detrusor (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, micturition control, p. 236). COMLEX: when one geriatric syndrome appears, look for the next one it may set off, such as a fall after incontinence, and in an older adult who has fallen a β3 agonist is preferred over oxybutynin (SDL 5 notes; AGS Beers Criteria 2023).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — prevalence and impact of urinary incontinence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the 'Geriatric Syndromes'","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old man has had two falls in 6 months, new urinary incontinence, and an episode of delirium during a recent hospital stay. Examination shows hip flexor and knee extensor strength of 4/5 bilaterally, several seborrheic keratoses on his back, and a healed appendectomy scar. His blood pressure on lisinopril is 128/76 mm Hg, and his total cholesterol is 212 mg/dL (reference range: <200 mg/dL). Which finding is a recognized shared risk factor for his falls, incontinence, and delirium?","choices":{"A":"Controlled hypertension","B":"Seborrheic keratoses","C":"Borderline cholesterol","D":"Leg muscle weakness","E":"Prior appendectomy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Different geriatric syndromes often share the same underlying risk factors: extremity muscle weakness, affective disturbances, white matter changes, cognitive deficits, and sensory loss. His reduced hip and knee strength is the finding that links his falls, incontinence, and delirium. Controlled hypertension is not among these shared risk factors. Benign seborrheic keratoses are not either. A borderline cholesterol does not link these syndromes. A prior appendectomy is unrelated to them. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 3, shared etiological pathways). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mixed incontinence combines features of stress and urgency incontinence (First Aid Renal—Pathology, urinary incontinence, p. 618). COMLEX: finding one shared risk factor, such as leg weakness, gives a single target that can lower the risk of several syndromes at once (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial contributors to fall risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the 'Geriatric Syndromes'","batch":2,"stem":"An 87-year-old woman has had three falls in 4 months. She has early cataracts with visual acuity of 20/50 in both eyes, mildly reduced vibration sense in her feet, and knee osteoarthritis that slows her on stairs. Each problem was judged too mild to need treatment on its own. She lives alone and is otherwise well. Which concept best explains her falls?","choices":{"A":"A single undiagnosed disease","B":"Normal aging of balance","C":"An unrecognized small stroke","D":"Primary psychiatric illness","E":"Accumulated small deficits"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Geriatric syndromes are not normal aging; they typically arise when cumulative, subclinical impairments across several systems use up a person's compensatory reserve. Her mild visual, sensory, and joint problems are each minor, but together they exceed her ability to compensate. Searching for a single undiagnosed disease misses the multifactorial nature of her falls. Normal aging of balance does not produce a geriatric syndrome. An unrecognized small stroke is another single-cause search that misses the pattern. Nothing suggests a primary psychiatric illness. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 3, definition and core concept); Kane Ch. 1 (Geriatric Syndromes). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aging impairs accommodation (presbyopia), hearing (presbycusis), smell, and taste (First Aid Pathology—Aging, special senses, p. 225). COMLEX: Kane cites the view that geriatric syndromes and functional dependence result when decline in several domains overwhelms compensatory capacity (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — assessment of the patient with fall risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the association between comorbidity and risk for disability and mortality","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old man has type 2 diabetes mellitus, knee osteoarthritis, COPD, and residual right-sided weakness from a stroke 3 years ago. Over 2 years he has gone from living independently to needing help with shopping, cooking, and bathing. His gait speed is 0.6 m/s (reference range for age: >0.8 m/s), and his grip strength has declined. His neurologic examination is unchanged from last year. Which factor best explains his loss of independence?","choices":{"A":"Chronic disease burden","B":"Expected normal aging","C":"Acute stroke recurrence","D":"Medication nonadherence","E":"A new depressive episode"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Multiple chronic diseases accelerate decline in activities of daily living, and illnesses such as diabetes, arthritis, stroke, and cardiopulmonary disease lead to reduced physical performance, slower gait, loss of strength, and eventually loss of independent living. His slow gait and weaker grip track this course. Loss of independence is not expected with normal aging. His unchanged neurologic examination argues against acute stroke recurrence. Nothing in his history points to medication nonadherence. Nothing suggests a new depressive episode either. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 4, risk for disability); Kane Ch. 1 (The Link Between Aging and Multimorbidity). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sorbitol accumulation causes osmotic nerve damage in diabetes, and the resulting neuropathy can be motor, sensory in a glove-and-stocking pattern, or autonomic (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, diabetes mellitus, p. 350). COMLEX: slowing gait and weakening grip in a patient with several chronic diseases are early signs of losing independence (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — major organ disease and frailty","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the association between comorbidity and risk for disability and mortality","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman with heart failure, COPD, type 2 diabetes mellitus, chronic kidney disease, atrial fibrillation, and depression is admitted with community-acquired pneumonia, and her chest radiograph shows a right lower lobe infiltrate. Compared with an 84-year-old woman admitted with the same pneumonia and no chronic conditions, she is more likely to have which outcome?","choices":{"A":"Faster functional recovery","B":"Extended hospitalization","C":"Lower hospital costs","D":"Shorter antibiotic course","E":"Earlier discharge home"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The SDL 5 notes link multimorbidity to more emergency department visits, longer hospital stays, more unplanned readmissions, and higher costs. Her six chronic conditions make an extended hospitalization likely. Faster functional recovery runs the opposite way. Lower hospital costs are also the opposite of what multimorbidity brings. The antibiotic course for pneumonia is set by the infection, not shortened by comorbidity. Earlier discharge home runs the opposite way as well. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 4, health care utilization); Kane Ch. 1 (The Link Between Aging and Multimorbidity, utilization and expenditures). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in adults older than 65, common causes of pneumonia include Streptococcus pneumoniae, influenza virus, anaerobes, Haemophilus influenzae, and gram-negative rods (First Aid Microbiology—Systems, common causes of pneumonia, p. 175). COMLEX: Kane associates comorbidity with heavy use of health care and high spending (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — transitional care in the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the association between comorbidity and risk for disability and mortality","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man is scheduled for elective total hip replacement for osteoarthritis. He also has heart failure, COPD, diabetes with nephropathy, a prior stroke, peripheral artery disease, and treated prostate cancer. His daughter asks how his many conditions affect his outlook over the next several years, apart from the surgery itself. Which outcome is most strongly associated with his degree of comorbidity?","choices":{"A":"Lower dementia incidence","B":"Reduced cancer incidence","C":"Lower readmission risk","D":"Greater muscle mass","E":"Higher mortality risk"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Across longitudinal studies, rising comorbidity markedly increases both short-term inpatient mortality and long-term all-cause mortality, and comorbidity independently predicts disability and institutionalization. More comorbidity does not lower dementia incidence. It does not reduce cancer incidence either. It raises rather than lowers readmission risk. It is associated with loss rather than gain of muscle mass. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 4, risk for mortality); Kane Ch. 1 (The Link Between Aging and Multimorbidity). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, β-blockers and the drugs that block the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system lower mortality, whereas loop and thiazide diuretics mainly relieve symptoms (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, heart failure, p. 316). COMLEX: Kane states that comorbidity raises the risk of both disability and death (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — geriatric multimorbidity and outcomes in ischemic heart disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl05_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the potential for atypical presentations of common clinical problems in the aging population","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 2 hours after the sudden onset of shortness of breath and fatigue. She also has an ache in her right shoulder, and her daughter says she is more confused than usual. She has no chest pain. Her pulse is 102/min, blood pressure 148/84 mm Hg, and oxygen saturation 92% on room air (reference range: 95–100%). Faint crackles are heard at both lung bases. Which test should be obtained first?","choices":{"A":"Shoulder radiograph","B":"Head CT without contrast","C":"12-lead electrocardiogram","D":"Thyroid function tests","E":"Pulmonary function testing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In older adults, acute coronary syndromes often present without crushing chest pain and instead with sudden shortness of breath, unexplained fatigue, new confusion, or pain referred to the shoulder or jaw. Her combination of these features calls for an immediate 12-lead ECG to look for ischemia. A shoulder radiograph chases the referred pain. A head CT without contrast chases the confusion. Thyroid function tests do not address a possible infarction. Pulmonary function testing does not address it either. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 5, acute myocardial infarction). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: within the first 6 hours of a myocardial infarction the ECG is the gold standard, and cardiac troponin I rises after about 4 hours, peaks at 24 hours, and stays elevated for 7 to 10 days (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, diagnosis of myocardial infarction, p. 310). COMLEX: sudden breathlessness, fatigue, or confusion in an older adult can be the presenting picture of a heart attack (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — stable ischemic heart disease and acute coronary syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the potential for atypical presentations of common clinical problems in the aging population","batch":2,"stem":"An 86-year-old man with hypertension lives with his wife. She calls the office because since yesterday \"he just isn't himself\": he skipped his morning walk, left his breakfast uneaten, and did not shave, which he has done every day for 60 years. He has no cough, pain, or urinary complaints, and his temperature at home is 98.4°F. What is the most appropriate response?","choices":{"A":"Prompt medical evaluation","B":"Antidepressant therapy","C":"Referral for memory testing","D":"Follow-up in 3 months","E":"Haloperidol at bedtime"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When a family member notices a subtle change, such as \"he just isn't himself today,\" or an older adult abruptly stops basic routines, the change should be evaluated promptly as a possible acute medical illness. A severe illness such as pneumonia, sepsis, or myocardial infarction often first shows up as delirium, functional decline, poor appetite, falls, or lethargy, and fever is often absent. Antidepressant therapy treats presumed depression and delays finding a remediable cause. Referral for memory testing also delays the evaluation. Follow-up in 3 months is far too late. Haloperidol at bedtime would sedate him without finding the cause. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 5, nonspecific manifestations and diagnostic vigilance); Kane Ch. 1 (Atypical Presentation of Common Clinical Problems). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-α can mediate fever and sepsis (First Aid Immunology—Immune Responses, cytokines, p. 106). COMLEX: Kane says any report of a new or sudden change in an older person's behavior or function should be handled as an acute medical problem (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — how infection symptoms differ in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the potential for atypical presentations of common clinical problems in the aging population","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis takes prednisone 10 mg daily and naproxen 500 mg twice daily, along with atorvastatin, levothyroxine, and calcium with vitamin D; she received an influenza vaccine last week. She reports 2 days of mild lower abdominal discomfort and poor appetite. Her temperature is 98.9°F, and her left lower quadrant is mildly tender without guarding. Her leukocyte count is 9.8 ×10³/μL (reference range: 4.5–11.0 ×10³/μL). CT shows sigmoid diverticulitis with a small contained perforation. Which factor most likely masked the usual signs of her illness?","choices":{"A":"Daily calcium and vitamin D","B":"Her statin therapy","C":"Recent influenza vaccine","D":"Anti-inflammatory drugs","E":"Her thyroid replacement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Medications with anti-inflammatory or antipyretic action, such as NSAIDs, acetaminophen, and corticosteroids, further suppress classic signs of illness such as pain and fever in older adults, whose responses are already blunted. Her prednisone and naproxen explain why a perforated diverticulitis produced only mild discomfort, no fever, and a normal white count. Daily calcium and vitamin D do not suppress fever or pain. Her statin therapy does not blunt these signs. A recent influenza vaccine does not mask an abdominal infection. Her thyroid replacement does not suppress fever or pain. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 5, impact of baseline comorbidities and medications). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulitis classically causes left lower quadrant pain, fever, and leukocytosis, and its complications include abscess, fistula, obstruction, and perforation (First Aid Gastrointestinal—Pathology, diverticula of the GI tract, p. 390). COMLEX: review the medication list for drugs that hide pain and fever whenever an older adult looks less ill than the findings suggest (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 7 — benefits and risks of anti-inflammatory medication","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concepts of resilience and homeostenosis","batch":3,"stem":"An 80-year-old man and his 80-year-old twin brother had similar frailty scores before each underwent the same elective hip replacement. He walked independently at 3 weeks and was back to golf by 3 months, whereas his brother still needed help at 3 months. Frailty measures lost reserve; what does the difference between their courses best reflect?","choices":{"A":"Different surgical approaches","B":"Unequal baseline frailty","C":"Realized recovery potential","D":"Higher maximum heart rate","E":"Greater lean body mass"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Frailty and homeostenosis describe the biological loss of physiological reserve, whereas resilience is the actualization of a person's recovery potential when challenged. With similar frailty and the same operation, the brothers differed in how quickly and completely they returned to function. Different surgical approaches do not apply, because the operation was the same. Unequal baseline frailty does not apply, because their frailty was similar. A higher maximum heart rate alone does not explain the difference. Greater lean body mass alone does not explain it either. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 1, relationship to frailty and reserve). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: with age, total body water falls and body fat rises, so hydrophilic drugs reach higher concentrations and lipophilic drugs have longer half-lives (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, age-related changes in pharmacokinetics, p. 246). COMLEX: two patients with the same reserve on paper can still differ in how they rebound from surgery (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — contributing factors to frailty in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the relationship between aging and multiple comorbid illnesses","batch":3,"stem":"An 82-year-old woman has hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, osteoporosis, COPD, and osteoarthritis. When the recommendations written for each disease are applied one by one, she ends up with 12 drugs and contradictory advice, including a tight blood pressure target despite recent dizzy spells on standing. Which problem does this illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Prescribing cascade","B":"Therapeutic inertia","C":"Diagnostic overshadowing","D":"Medication nonadherence","E":"Guideline conflict"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Standard single-disease practice guidelines often fail or conflict when applied to older adults with several conditions, and following each one separately fosters polypharmacy. A prescribing cascade is a new drug given for another drug's side effect. Therapeutic inertia is failure to intensify treatment. Diagnostic overshadowing attributes new symptoms to a known condition. Medication nonadherence is not taking drugs as prescribed. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 2, clinical and therapeutic complexity). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aortic stiffening widens the pulse pressure, which underlies isolated systolic hypertension in older adults (First Aid Cardiovascular—Physiology, pulse pressure, p. 290). COMLEX: in an older adult with several diseases, treatment targets are weighed against one another rather than set disease by disease (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — goals and challenges of chronic disease management in the elderly","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the 'Geriatric Syndromes'","batch":3,"stem":"An 84-year-old man has stopped walking outside his apartment over the past 3 months. He has painful hip osteoarthritis, fell in a parking lot 3 months ago and now fears falling again, has lost leg strength from inactivity, and has stable heart failure. What is the most appropriate approach to his immobility?","choices":{"A":"Address each cause","B":"Prescribe bed rest","C":"Order a brain MRI","D":"Begin a sedative","E":"Provide a wheelchair"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Immobility is often multifactorial, arising from joint pain, hip fracture, fear of falling, deconditioning, or heart failure, and a single complaint can stem from several physical, emotional, and environmental causes. He has four contributors, so each needs attention. Prescribing bed rest deepens deconditioning. A brain MRI does not address the causes already found. Beginning a sedative raises fall risk. Providing a wheelchair also deepens deconditioning. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 3, immobility and the I's of geriatric syndromes). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteoarthritis causes pain in weight-bearing joints after use that improves with rest, usually asymmetric and without systemic symptoms (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Skin, and Connective Tissue—Pathology, osteoarthritis vs rheumatoid arthritis, p. 472). COMLEX: a plan for immobility in an older adult lists every contributor and treats each one (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — interventions for patients with fall risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the association between comorbidity and risk for disability and mortality","batch":3,"stem":"An 87-year-old woman who lives alone has heart failure, COPD, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and stage 3 chronic kidney disease. Over the past year she has lost weight without trying, walks slowly, and has a weak grip. Her daughter asks what her mother is at increased risk for over the next few years. Which outcome do her comorbidity and her frailty each predict on their own?","choices":{"A":"Improved gait speed","B":"Lower risk of dementia","C":"Fewer outpatient visits","D":"Nursing home placement","E":"Briefer hospital admissions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Comorbidity, the sum of established disease, and frailty, the sum of subclinical losses of reserve, each predict, independently of one another, functional disability, institutionalization, and death in older adults, and she has both. Her gait is already slow and is not expected to improve. Neither condition lowers the risk of dementia. Multimorbidity brings more, not fewer, outpatient visits. It also brings longer, not briefer, hospital admissions. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 4, independent drivers of adverse outcomes). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medicaid is health assistance funded jointly by the federal government and the states for people with limited income or resources (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Healthcare Delivery, Medicare and Medicaid, p. 276). COMLEX: a patient who is both frail and multimorbid carries two separate predictors of losing independent living, so planning for added support starts early (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the potential for atypical presentations of common clinical problems in the aging population","batch":3,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman with mild dementia is brought in because she \"hasn't felt right\" for a day. Her vital signs are normal, a focused examination is unrevealing, and basic blood and urine tests show no clear cause. She is eating and drinking, and her family can stay with her. What should the plan include?","choices":{"A":"Routine follow-up in 6 months","B":"Short-interval reassessment","C":"Empiric antibiotic course","D":"Antidepressant therapy","E":"A nightly sleep aid"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When the clinical course is uncertain, clinicians should keep a high index of suspicion, look for remediable triggers, and schedule short-interval reassessments, because an atypical presentation may be caught early in the illness. Routine follow-up in 6 months is too late. An empiric antibiotic course without a source is not indicated. Antidepressant therapy treats a problem that has not been found. A nightly sleep aid likewise treats nothing identified. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 5, diagnostic vigilance). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: reversible causes that can mimic or worsen dementia include depression, hypothyroidism, vitamin B12 deficiency, neurosyphilis, and normal pressure hydrocephalus (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: baseline dementia makes it harder to tell a new acute change from her usual state, which is another reason to see her again soon (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — how infection symptoms differ in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the potential for atypical presentations of common clinical problems in the aging population","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old man with long-standing type 2 diabetes mellitus and peripheral neuropathy has a warm, swollen right foot with a deep plantar ulcer, and MRI shows osteomyelitis of the second metatarsal head. He reports no foot pain. Which factor best explains the absence of pain?","choices":{"A":"Lost protective sensation","B":"Normal thinning of aged skin","C":"Low severity of the infection","D":"A high pain tolerance","E":"Poor recent glucose control"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Preexisting chronic diseases such as diabetic neuropathy cloud the clinical picture, and here sensory loss removes the pain that would normally signal a deep infection. Normal thinning of aged skin does not abolish pain from bone infection. Osteomyelitis is a serious infection, so low severity does not explain the missing pain. A high pain tolerance would not abolish pain from bone infection. Poor recent glucose control does not remove pain on its own. Sources: SDL 5 lecture notes (Obj 5, impact of baseline comorbidities). Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause of osteomyelitis overall, and MRI is the best imaging test for detecting it (First Aid Microbiology—Systems, osteomyelitis, p. 176). COMLEX: in a patient with neuropathy, absent pain does not rule out a serious foot infection, so the feet are examined directly (SDL 5 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — changes in sensitivity to touch and pain","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concepts of resilience and homeostenosis","batch":5,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has a body mass index of 34 kg/m², a weak grip, and low muscle mass on body composition testing, but she remains fully independent at home. This condition is linked on its own to, and comes before, a decline in which area?","choices":{"A":"Short-term verbal memory","B":"Instrumental activities","C":"Hearing in noisy rooms","D":"Resting lung volumes","E":"Sleep continuity at night"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Kane describes sarcopenic obesity, in which obesity coexists with weak physical performance and muscle that is reduced in both amount and quality, as a group with diminished capacity to respond to stressors. It predicts, independently and in advance, a drop in instrumental activities of daily living, and these patients face a high risk of functional decline. The proposed mechanism is high IL-6 and other inflammatory markers that cause muscle wasting, then disability, less activity, and further wasting. Short-term verbal memory is not an outcome Kane links to this syndrome. Hearing in noisy rooms is not one of them. Resting lung volumes are not either. Sleep continuity at night is not among the outcomes Kane describes. Sources: Kane Ch. 1 (Resilience and Homeostenosis, sarcopenic obesity). Textbook enrichment (Kane) — detail not in SDL 5 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obesity hypoventilation syndrome, also called Pickwickian syndrome, arises with a BMI of 30 kg/m2 or more (First Aid Respiratory—Pathology, obesity hypoventilation syndrome, p. 697). COMLEX: low muscle mass can hide inside a high body weight, so strength and function are checked in older adults with obesity (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — factors leading to sarcopenia with aging","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Kane"},{"id":"sdl05_q32","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the relationship between aging and multiple comorbid illnesses","batch":5,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and early diabetic kidney disease. He starts an ACE inhibitor to lower his blood pressure. Beyond its effect on the disease it was started for, what can treating one of his diseases do?","choices":{"A":"Reverses his frailty","B":"Prevents sarcopenia","C":"Lowers his drug costs","D":"Benefits several organs","E":"Restores his lost reserve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Kane points out that illnesses in different organ systems often travel together and that treatments for several diseases can likewise improve outcomes in other organ systems. Here, a drug started for blood pressure also protects his kidneys from diabetic damage. One drug does not reverse his frailty. It does not prevent sarcopenia. Lowering his drug costs is not the benefit Kane describes. It does not restore his lost physiological reserve. Sources: Kane Ch. 1 (The Link Between Aging and Multimorbidity). Textbook enrichment (Kane) — detail not in SDL 5 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a patient with hypertension and diabetes, ACE inhibitors and ARBs are protective against diabetic nephropathy (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hypertension treatment, p. 321). COMLEX: one well-chosen treatment can improve outcomes in more than one organ system in an older adult with several diseases (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in the elderly","isHighYield":false,"enrichmentSource":"Kane"},{"id":"sdl05_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the 'Geriatric Syndromes'","batch":5,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman had surgical repair of a hip fracture 6 weeks ago. At home she declines her physical therapy exercises, saying that her hip hurts whenever she bears weight and that she is afraid of falling again. What may need to be addressed before the exercise program can begin?","choices":{"A":"Her pain and fear","B":"Bone density testing","C":"Her cholesterol level","D":"Vitamin B12 status","E":"Hearing aid fitting"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Kane uses immobility to show that one geriatric problem can have several causes, such as a hip fracture, heart failure, a recent fall with fear of falling, or pain from degenerative joint disease, and advises that it may be necessary to relieve pain and address fear of falling before starting therapy or a regular exercise program. After her fracture repair, her hip pain and her fear of another fall are what keep her from exercising. Bone density testing does not stand between her and the program. Her cholesterol level is unrelated to her immobility. Her vitamin B12 status is not a barrier here. A hearing aid fitting does not address what keeps her from exercising. Sources: Kane Ch. 1 (Geriatric Syndromes). Textbook enrichment (Kane) — detail not in SDL 5 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetaminophen reversibly inhibits cyclooxygenase mostly in the CNS and is an analgesic and antipyretic without anti-inflammatory action (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Skin, and Connective Tissue—Pharmacology, acetaminophen, p. 495). COMLEX: an exercise plan for an older adult works only when the barriers that keep the person still are treated first (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — interventions for patients with fall risk","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Kane"},{"id":"sdl05_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the association between comorbidity and risk for disability and mortality","batch":5,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman scheduled for elective colon surgery has lost 12 pounds unintentionally in the past year, feels that everything she does is an effort, has a weak grip on dynamometry, and walks slowly. Compared with a patient who is not frail, she is more likely to have which outcome after surgery?","choices":{"A":"Fewer postoperative problems","B":"Faster return home","C":"Lower anesthesia needs","D":"Quicker wound healing","E":"Skilled nursing discharge"},"correct":"E","explanation":"She meets four of Kane's five frailty phenotype criteria. Kane reports that frail patients undergoing elective surgery have more postoperative complications, stay in the hospital longer, and leave more often for a skilled nursing facility. Fewer postoperative problems run the opposite way. A faster return home also runs the opposite way. Lower anesthesia needs are not the reported difference. Quicker wound healing is the opposite of what Kane reports. Sources: Kane Ch. 1 (Frailty as the Clinical Feature of the Aging Process). Textbook enrichment (Kane) — detail not in SDL 5 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medicare Part A covers hospital admissions, including hospice and skilled nursing care, and Part B covers basic medical bills such as physician fees and diagnostic testing (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Healthcare Delivery, Medicare and Medicaid, p. 276). COMLEX: Kane notes that surgeons and oncologists combine frailty tools with functional assessment to plan care for older patients (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — prevalence of frailty in the geriatric population","isHighYield":false,"enrichmentSource":"Kane"},{"id":"sdl05_q35","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Discuss the potential for atypical presentations of common clinical problems in the aging population","batch":5,"stem":"An 80-year-old man who lives independently with his wife is seen in the emergency department for 2 days of new fatigue. His examination, electrocardiogram, and basic blood and urine tests are unrevealing, and he and his wife want to go home. Before he leaves, what should the clinician do?","choices":{"A":"Start empiric antibiotics","B":"Order a brain MRI","C":"Teach red flag signs","D":"Prescribe a sedative","E":"Arrange a 6-month visit"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Kane advises treating a new or sudden change in behavior or function as an acute medical problem, recognizing that an atypical presentation may be seen early in the illness, and, when the picture is uncertain, alerting the patient and family to red flag signs and symptoms that would signal worsening. Kane notes that independent, cognitively intact older adults may report only that they did not feel right or did not feel like making the bed and turn out to have new atrial fibrillation. Starting empiric antibiotics is not supported by the findings so far. Ordering a brain MRI is not supported either. Prescribing a sedative would mask rather than clarify the change. Arranging a 6-month visit is far too late for an unexplained new change. Sources: Kane Ch. 1 (Atypical Presentation of Common Clinical Problems). Textbook enrichment (Kane) — detail not in SDL 5 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 5 lecture notes (2026-11-17).","boardPrep":"Step 1: atrial fibrillation shows an irregularly irregular rhythm with no discrete P waves, and left atrial stasis predisposes to thromboembolism, particularly stroke (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, atrial fibrillation, p. 311). COMLEX: Kane names identifying a correctable problem as the first and principal task, because no amount of rehabilitation makes up for a missed remediable diagnosis (Kane Ch. 1).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — transitional care in the elderly","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Kane"}]},{"sdlNumber":6,"title":"SDL 6 — Neurodegenterative Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"In prion disease, the normal cellular prion protein becomes the pathogenic scrapie isoform through which change?","choices":{"A":"Proteolytic cleavage of the PrP","B":"Beta-sheet conformational shift","C":"Acquisition of a nucleic acid","D":"Loss of membrane anchoring","E":"Excess PrP gene transcription"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Normal PrPc, a 30-kDa protein rich in alpha-helix, undergoes a post-translational change in shape into PrPSc, an isoform rich in beta-pleated sheet. Proteolytic cleavage does not create the pathogenic form; PrPSc instead resists protease digestion. Prions carry no nucleic acid genome. Loss of membrane anchoring is not the converting step. Overtranscription of the PrP gene raises the amount of normal protein without changing its fold. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, prion protein isoforms); Robbins Ch. 28 (Prion Diseases). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: standard autoclaving with pressurized steam above 120°C may not reliably inactivate prions (First Aid Microbiology—Antimicrobials, disinfection and sterilization, p. 199). COMLEX: normal PrPc sits in the cytoplasm and on cell membranes, and its conversion is post-translational rather than genetic in sporadic disease (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 2 — pathophysiology and risk factors for infection in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which prion disorder affects deer and elk?","choices":{"A":"Feline spongiform encephalopathy","B":"Bovine spongiform encephalopathy","C":"Mink transmissible encephalopathy","D":"Fatal familial insomnia","E":"Chronic wasting disease"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic wasting disease is the prion disease of deer and elk. Feline spongiform encephalopathy affects cats (general knowledge). Bovine spongiform encephalopathy affects cattle. Mink transmissible encephalopathy affects mink. Fatal familial insomnia is an inherited human prion disorder. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, animal prion diseases); Robbins Ch. 28 (Prion Diseases). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bovine spongiform encephalopathy is also called mad cow disease (First Aid Microbiology—Systems, prions, p. 174). COMLEX: prion diseases, also termed transmissible spongiform encephalopathies, arise sporadically, through inheritance, or by transmission (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 2 — pathophysiology and risk factors for infection in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Kuru plaques, the extracellular aggregates of abnormal prion protein, stain positively with which pair of stains?","choices":{"A":"Prussian blue and Giemsa","B":"Gram and acid-fast","C":"Oil red O and Sudan","D":"Congo red and PAS","E":"Trichrome and reticulin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Kuru plaques are rounded extracellular PrPSc aggregates that are Congo red–positive and PAS-positive. Prussian blue marks iron, and Giemsa is a blood and parasite stain. Gram and acid-fast stains identify bacteria. Oil red O and Sudan stain neutral lipid. Trichrome and reticulin highlight collagen and reticular fibers. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, kuru plaques); Robbins Ch. 28 (Prion Diseases, Morphology). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amyloid stained with Congo red looks red-orange under ordinary light and shows apple-green birefringence under polarized light (First Aid Pathology—Cellular injury, amyloidosis, p. 208). COMLEX: in advanced prion disease, severe neuronal loss and gliosis accompany coalescing vacuoles, a pattern called status spongiosus (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 2 — pathophysiology and risk factors for infection in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme cuts amyloid precursor protein inside the amyloid-beta sequence, so that no amyloid-beta peptide forms?","choices":{"A":"Beta-secretase","B":"Alpha-secretase","C":"Neprilysin","D":"Cathepsin D","E":"Insulin-degrading enzyme"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alpha-secretase cleaves cell-surface APP within the Aβ domain, releasing a benign soluble fragment in the nonamyloidogenic pathway. Beta-secretase, BACE1, starts the amyloidogenic pathway on endocytosed APP. Neprilysin degrades Aβ peptides after they form rather than cutting APP (general knowledge). Cathepsin D is a lysosomal protease that is not one of the APP-processing secretases (general knowledge). Insulin-degrading enzyme breaks down peptides after they form rather than preventing Aβ generation (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, APP processing); Robbins Ch. 28 (Alzheimer Disease, Pathogenesis). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: senile plaques in gray matter have an extracellular Aβ core derived from cleavage of amyloid precursor protein (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: the Aβ42 peptide released by the amyloidogenic pathway is hydrophobic and aggregates readily (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":1,"stem":"In Alzheimer disease, which neuropathologic measure tracks most closely with the clinical severity of dementia?","choices":{"A":"Neuritic plaque count","B":"Diffuse plaque count","C":"Tau tangle burden","D":"Ventricular volume","E":"Vascular amyloid load"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The regional burden and spread of neurofibrillary tangles correlate with dementia severity far better than amyloid plaques do. Neuritic plaque counts correlate less closely with clinical severity. Diffuse plaques lack dystrophic neurites and also occur in the basal ganglia and cerebellum. Ventricular volume enlarges passively as parenchyma is lost. Vascular amyloid load relates to hemorrhage risk rather than to dementia severity. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, role of tau); Robbins Ch. 28 (Alzheimer Disease). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: advanced age is the strongest risk factor for Alzheimer disease, which leads all causes of late-life dementia (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: tangles are insoluble, so they persist as extracellular ghost or tombstone tangles after the neuron dies (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":1,"stem":"Which peptide makes up most of the vascular amyloid in cerebral amyloid angiopathy?","choices":{"A":"Amyloid-beta 40","B":"Amyloid-beta 42","C":"Transthyretin","D":"Serum amyloid A","E":"Beta-2 microglobulin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cerebral amyloid angiopathy deposits are predominantly Aβ40, laid down in the media and adventitia of small and medium meningeal and cortical arteries. Aβ42 is the aggregation-prone peptide released by the amyloidogenic pathway that seeds plaques. Transthyretin forms senile systemic and hereditary amyloid (general knowledge). Serum amyloid A forms AA amyloid in chronic inflammation (general knowledge). Beta-2 microglobulin forms dialysis-related amyloid (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, cerebral amyloid angiopathy); Robbins Ch. 28 (Alzheimer Disease, Morphology). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amyloid angiopathy causes recurrent lobar intraparenchymal hemorrhage in older adults (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, p. 528). COMLEX: neuritic plaques measure 20 to 200 µm and have a dense amyloid core ringed by a clear halo, dystrophic neurites, astrocytes, and microglia (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":1,"stem":"In Pick disease, which part of the temporal lobe is relatively spared by the lobar atrophy?","choices":{"A":"Anterior superior temporal gyrus","B":"Temporal pole and adjacent cortex","C":"Anterior inferior temporal gyrus","D":"Posterior superior temporal gyrus","E":"Medial temporal lobe"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pick disease produces severe, asymmetric, circumscribed frontal and temporal atrophy with relative sparing of the posterior superior temporal gyrus. The anterior superior temporal gyrus lies within the atrophic anterior temporal region. The temporal pole and the cortex next to it are part of the atrophic anterior temporal region. The anterior inferior temporal gyrus is also involved. The medial temporal lobe is not the spared region in Pick disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, Pick disease subtype); Robbins Ch. 28 (Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations, Morphology). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ex vacuo ventriculomegaly mimics hydrocephalus on imaging but reflects tissue loss, with normal intracranial pressure and no normal pressure hydrocephalus triad (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, hydrocephalus mimics, p. 536). COMLEX: FTLD is the preferred pathologic name because it describes the lobar pattern of atrophy rather than the clinical syndrome (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — features of ALS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":1,"stem":"Which dietary change is characteristic of the behavioral variant of frontotemporal dementia?","choices":{"A":"Food refusal","B":"Early satiety","C":"Binge eating","D":"Craving ice","E":"Meal skipping"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Behavioral variant FTD brings hyperorality, with dietary changes, carbohydrate cravings, and binge eating. Food refusal reflects reduced intake, as in depression or advanced dementia. Early satiety is a gastrointestinal symptom. Craving ice, called pagophagia, suggests iron deficiency (general knowledge). Meal skipping is reduced intake rather than hyperorality. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, behavioral variant FTD). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral amygdala lesions cause Klüver-Bucy syndrome, with disinhibition, hyperphagia, hypersexuality, and hyperorality, classically after HSV-1 encephalitis (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: stereotyped, compulsive, or repetitive behaviors are part of the behavioral variant as well (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — features of ALS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":1,"stem":"How does the incidence of frontotemporal lobar degeneration compare with that of Alzheimer disease in patients younger than 65 years?","choices":{"A":"About equal","B":"About one-tenth","C":"About one-half","D":"About double","E":"About fivefold"},"correct":"A","explanation":"FTLD is one of the most common causes of early-onset dementia, and in patients younger than 65 its incidence is comparable to that of Alzheimer disease. One-tenth would make it rare before 65. One-half understates its share of early-onset dementia. Double overstates it. Fivefold would make it the dominant cause before 65, which it is not. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, demographic distribution); Robbins Ch. 28 (Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: frontotemporal dementia, formerly called Pick disease, starts with personality and behavior change or with primary progressive aphasia and may bring movement disorders (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: up to 30% to 50% of patients have a positive family history, and the genetic forms are prominently autosomal dominant (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — features of ALS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her husband because of 4 months of rapidly worsening memory and confusion. Six months ago she worked full time and managed the household finances, and she has no family history of neurologic disease and no history of surgery or blood transfusion. Examination shows disorientation to date and place and a wide-based, unsteady gait. A brain biopsy specimen shows many small, round vacuoles of varying size in the neuropil and within cortical neurons, with neuronal loss and gliosis. Which immunohistochemical finding in this tissue would confirm the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Hyperphosphorylated tau tangles","B":"Alpha-synuclein inclusions","C":"Phosphorylated TDP-43 inclusions","D":"Protease-resistant prion protein","E":"Amyloid-beta plaque cores"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dementia progressing over months with spongiform vacuolation of the neuropil and perikaryon fits sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, and definitive diagnosis rests on immunohistochemical detection of proteinase K–resistant PrPSc in brain tissue. Hyperphosphorylated tau tangles are intracellular lesions of Alzheimer disease and the tauopathies. Alpha-synuclein inclusions define Lewy body disorders (general knowledge). Phosphorylated TDP-43 inclusions mark FTLD-TDP. Amyloid-beta plaque cores are the extracellular lesion of Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, characteristic findings and definitive diagnosis); Robbins Ch. 28 (Prion Diseases, Morphology). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, the EEG shows periodic sharp wave complexes and the CSF contains 14-3-3 protein (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: in sporadic CJD, PrPc converts spontaneously at a low rate, and the disease peaks in the seventh decade (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and misfolded protein aggregates","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"In 1999, a 26-year-old man who has always lived in the United Kingdom is brought to the physician by his parents because of 9 months of low mood, anxiety, and withdrawal from friends, followed by increasing unsteadiness. He has no family history of neurologic disease. Examination shows an ataxic gait, and he recalls 0 of 3 words after 5 minutes. A brain biopsy specimen shows numerous extracellular plaques of abnormal prion protein throughout the cerebral cortex, each ringed by vacuolated neuropil. Which exposure most likely caused his disease?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated beef products","B":"Cadaveric growth hormone","C":"Dural graft from a cadaver","D":"Ritual funerary cannibalism","E":"Corneal transplant tissue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A young adult with early psychiatric symptoms and abundant cortical prion plaques encircled by spongiform change has variant CJD, which is linked to bovine spongiform encephalopathy acquired by eating contaminated bovine products. Cadaveric growth hormone, dural grafts, and corneal transplants transmit iatrogenic CJD, which does not typically produce this picture of a young adult with early psychiatric symptoms and florid cortical plaques, whereas he lived in the United Kingdom during the years of bovine spongiform encephalopathy exposure. Ritual cannibalism transmitted kuru in Papua New Guinea. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, variant CJD and kuru plaques); Robbins Ch. 28 (Variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CJD is typically sporadic but can be transmitted by contaminated materials such as corneal transplants and neurosurgical equipment (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: kuru plaques cluster in the cerebellum in classic prion disease but spread widely through the cerebral cortex in vCJD (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — pathophysiology and risk factors for infection in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man comes to the physician because of 3 months of rapidly progressive forgetfulness and difficulty walking. At age 9, he received a course of growth hormone injections extracted from human cadaveric pituitary glands for short stature. Examination shows impaired short-term recall and truncal ataxia. A brain biopsy specimen shows spongiform change in the cerebral cortex and deep gray matter with neuronal loss. Which process accounts for the spread of disease within his brain after the original exposure?","choices":{"A":"Viral replication in neurons","B":"Templated refolding of host PrP","C":"Autoantibodies against neurons","D":"Integration of prion DNA into host","E":"Microglial attack on myelin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inoculated PrPSc physically binds native PrPc and catalyzes its refolding into more PrPSc in a cooperative cascade, and the resulting aggregates spread from cell to cell; contaminated cadaveric growth hormone is a recognized source of iatrogenic CJD. Viral replication is not the mechanism because prions are not viruses. Autoantibodies against neuronal antigens cause autoimmune encephalitis rather than prion spread (general knowledge). Prions carry no DNA, so nothing integrates into the host genome. Microglial attack on myelin describes demyelinating disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, self-propagating conformational cascade; iatrogenic CJD); Robbins Ch. 28 (Prion Diseases). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: kuru is an acquired prion disease described in tribal populations that practiced cannibalism (First Aid Microbiology—Systems, prions, p. 174). COMLEX: deep brain electrodes and dural or corneal grafts are other documented iatrogenic routes for CJD (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — loss of proteostasis among the hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man is brought to the physician by his wife because of 3 years of progressive forgetfulness; he now misses work meetings and cannot manage the family finances. His father and paternal grandmother developed dementia in their mid-40s, and a paternal aunt was diagnosed with the same condition at age 48. Examination shows impaired delayed recall and difficulty naming uncommon objects, with normal strength and gait. MRI of the brain shows bilateral hippocampal and entorhinal atrophy, and genetic testing identifies a missense mutation in a gene on chromosome 14. Increased activity of which enzyme complex most likely contributes to his disease?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-secretase","B":"Beta-secretase","C":"Proteasome","D":"Acetylcholinesterase","E":"Gamma-secretase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Autosomal dominant early-onset Alzheimer disease with a chromosome 14 mutation indicates PSEN1, whose gain-of-function mutations enhance gamma-secretase generation of Aβ42, since presenilin is part of that intramembranous complex. Alpha-secretase cuts within the Aβ domain and prevents Aβ formation. Beta-secretase begins amyloidogenic cleavage but is not the enzyme presenilin mutations enhance. The proteasome degrades ubiquitinated proteins and is not altered by this mutation (general knowledge). Acetylcholinesterase hydrolyzes acetylcholine and is not increased by presenilin mutations (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, early-onset familial AD; APP processing); Robbins Ch. 28 (Alzheimer Disease, Pathogenesis). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mutations in APP, presenilin-1, and presenilin-2 cause the familial forms of Alzheimer disease, about 10% of cases, which begin at a younger age (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: PSEN2 on chromosome 1 and point mutations in APP on chromosome 21 are the other early-onset familial causes (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with Down syndrome is brought to the physician by his sister because of 18 months of progressive forgetfulness; he can no longer follow his bus route to work or prepare the simple meals he once made. He had an atrioventricular septal defect repaired in infancy and takes no medications. Examination shows flat facies, upslanting palpebral fissures, and a single palmar crease, and he cannot recall any of 3 words after 5 minutes, which is below his baseline. His serum TSH is 2.1 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL), and his vitamin B12 is 520 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Which mechanism best explains the early onset of his dementia?","choices":{"A":"APOE epsilon-4 inheritance","B":"Extra APP gene dosage","C":"Presenilin-1 gain of function","D":"Prion protein misfolding","E":"Vascular amyloid deposition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chromosome 21 carries the APP gene, so trisomy 21 produces a gene-dosage excess of APP, and people with Down syndrome develop Alzheimer neuropathology by their second or third decade; his normal TSH and B12 argue against reversible causes. APOE ε4 lies on chromosome 19 and raises late-onset risk, which his karyotype does not explain. Presenilin-1 gain-of-function mutations on chromosome 14 cause autosomal dominant familial disease. Prion protein misfolding causes prion disease. Vascular amyloid deposition accompanies Alzheimer disease but is not the cause of early onset here. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, genetic determinants of Aβ accumulation); Robbins Ch. 28 (Alzheimer Disease, Pathogenesis). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 95% of trisomy 21 cases arise from meiotic nondisjunction, most often in meiosis I, and the risk rises with maternal age (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, Down syndrome, p. 61). COMLEX: the rare APP A673T variant lowers Aβ generation and protects against cognitive decline (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics vs lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman dies in a nursing home after a 10-year course of progressive memory loss that ended with mutism and inability to walk. She had hypertension treated with lisinopril and no history of stroke. Neurologic examination in her final year showed no focal motor deficits. At autopsy, the brain weighs 980 g (reference range: 1200–1400 g) with narrowed gyri, widened sulci, and enlarged lateral ventricles, and assays of cortical tissue show choline acetyltransferase activity at 35% of age-matched control values. Loss of neurons in which structure most likely accounts for the enzyme finding?","choices":{"A":"Locus ceruleus","B":"Substantia nigra pars compacta","C":"Basal nucleus of Meynert","D":"Dorsal raphe nucleus","E":"Ventral tegmental area"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Her course and atrophic brain indicate Alzheimer disease, in which marked loss of cholinergic neurons in the basal forebrain nucleus basalis of Meynert severely depletes cortical choline acetyltransferase. The locus ceruleus is the main source of norepinephrine. The substantia nigra pars compacta supplies dopamine to the striatum. The dorsal raphe nucleus supplies serotonin. The ventral tegmental area supplies mesolimbic and mesocortical dopamine. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, cholinergic deficits and synaptic loss); First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, neurotransmitter changes with disease, p. 505. Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: donepezil, rivastigmine, and galantamine are acetylcholinesterase inhibitors used first line, and memantine is an NMDA receptor antagonist for moderate to advanced disease (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, neurodegenerative disease therapy, p. 564). COMLEX: synaptic loss in Alzheimer disease shows up as reduced synaptophysin immunoreactivity (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — pathophysiology of Parkinson disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her husband because of 2 years of personality change; she makes crude jokes to strangers, has stopped bathing, and was recently caught shoplifting. Her mother had a similar illness that began at age 55. Examination shows apathy and perseveration, with intact orientation and recall of 3 of 3 words at 5 minutes. MRI of the brain shows severe asymmetric atrophy of the frontal and anterior temporal lobes; she dies 6 years later, and at autopsy, silver staining of the frontal cortex shows swollen, ballooned neurons and smooth, round cytoplasmic inclusions. A germline mutation in which gene would most likely be found in this family?","choices":{"A":"GRN","B":"TARDBP","C":"C9orf72","D":"MAPT","E":"PSEN1"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Early behavioral change with spared memory, frontotemporal atrophy, ballooned Pick cells, and silver-positive round Pick bodies indicate Pick disease, an FTLD-tau subtype. Pick disease is usually sporadic, but with an affected mother and an autosomal dominant pattern the likely cause in this family is familial FTLD-tau (MAPT), from germline mutations in MAPT on chromosome 17. GRN loss-of-function mutations produce FTLD-TDP with intranuclear TDP-43 inclusions. TARDBP mutations alter TDP-43 itself and also produce FTLD-TDP. C9orf72 expansions produce TDP-43 pathology rather than tau-positive Pick bodies. PSEN1 mutations cause early-onset familial Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, FTLD-tau and Pick disease subtype); Robbins Ch. 28 (Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations, FTLD-tau). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inclusions in frontotemporal dementia contain either hyperphosphorylated tau, as round Pick bodies, or ubiquitinated TDP-43 (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: MAPT mutations can upset the ratio of tau splice isoforms or alter phosphorylation, so tau binds microtubules less and aggregates (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — atypical parkinsonian syndromes and how they differ from PD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 18 months of increasingly effortful, halting speech; she now speaks in short phrases with frequent speech sound errors but follows complex commands and remembers recent events. Her father and a paternal uncle had a similar progressive language disorder that began in their early 60s. Examination shows agrammatic speech with intact comprehension and normal strength, tone, and reflexes. MRI of the brain shows asymmetric left frontal and anterior temporal atrophy, and her father's autopsy report described needle-shaped intranuclear neuronal inclusions that stained for phosphorylated TDP-43. Which mechanism most likely underlies the disease in this family?","choices":{"A":"Tau splice isoform imbalance","B":"Amyloid-beta 42 overproduction","C":"Progranulin haploinsufficiency","D":"Alpha-synuclein aggregation","E":"FUS protein aggregation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Familial FTLD-TDP with needle-like, cat-eye intranuclear TDP-43 inclusions points to loss-of-function GRN mutations, which leave progranulin haploinsufficient and drive neuroinflammation; her effortful agrammatic speech is the nonfluent primary progressive aphasia presentation. Tau splice isoform imbalance underlies MAPT-related FTLD-tau, which lacks TDP-43 inclusions. Amyloid-beta 42 overproduction drives Alzheimer disease. Alpha-synuclein aggregation forms Lewy bodies (general knowledge). FUS protein aggregation defines the rarer FTLD-FUS subtype, whose inclusions lack TDP-43. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, FTLD-TDP genetic etiologies, including the needle-like, cat-eye inclusions; language variants); Robbins Ch. 28 (Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations, FTLD-TDP). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Broca aphasia from an inferior frontal gyrus lesion gives nonfluent speech with intact comprehension and preserved insight (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, aphasia, p. 529). COMLEX: TDP-43 is an RNA-binding protein normally held in the nucleus that mislocalizes in FTLD-TDP (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 7 — contribution of inflammation to the aging process","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man is brought to the physician by his wife because of 2 years of disinhibition and apathy; he makes inappropriate remarks to coworkers, has lost interest in his grandchildren, and shows no concern about these changes. Over the past 4 months, he has also had trouble climbing stairs and buttoning shirts; his mother had frontotemporal dementia, and a maternal uncle died of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis at age 60. Examination shows atrophy and fasciculations of the hand muscles, spasticity and hyperreflexia in the legs, and intact sensation, and he recalls 3 of 3 words at 5 minutes. Electromyography shows active denervation and fasciculation potentials in the arms, legs, and thoracic paraspinal muscles. Which genetic abnormality is most likely present in this family?","choices":{"A":"C9orf72 repeat expansion","B":"MAPT missense mutation","C":"GRN loss of function","D":"HTT CAG repeat expansion","E":"PSEN1 missense mutation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A GGGGCC hexanucleotide expansion in the noncoding region of C9orf72 is the most common genetic cause of FTLD-TDP and joins FTLD and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis on one disease spectrum, matching his behavioral variant FTD with upper and lower motor neuron signs in a family with both disorders. MAPT missense mutations cause FTLD-tau. GRN loss of function causes FTLD-TDP with progranulin deficiency but is not the change that the notes tie to the FTLD and ALS spectrum. HTT CAG repeat expansion causes Huntington disease, with chorea rather than motor neuron disease (general knowledge). PSEN1 missense mutations cause early-onset familial Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, C9orf72 expansion; disease progression and motor overlap); Robbins Ch. 28 (Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations, FTLD-TDP). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prefrontal lesions produce a frontal lobe syndrome of disinhibition, hyperphagia, impulsivity, loss of empathy, and impaired executive function, as seen in frontotemporal dementia (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: FTLD-FUS is a rarer subtype marked by cytoplasmic aggregates of the RNA-binding protein FUS (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 7 — features of ALS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 51-year-old man has 10 months of worsening insomnia, followed by poor balance and forgetfulness. His father and a paternal aunt died of a similar illness in their 50s. Which result on genetic testing is expected?","choices":{"A":"PSEN1 missense mutation","B":"SOD1 point mutation","C":"Germline PRNP mutation","D":"HTT CAG repeat expansion","E":"APOE epsilon-4 homozygosity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Progressive insomnia, then ataxia and cognitive decline, in a family with affected relatives in two generations fits fatal familial insomnia, an inherited prion disorder caused by specific germline PRNP mutations that speed the spontaneous conversion of PrPc. PSEN1 missense mutations cause early-onset familial Alzheimer disease. SOD1 point mutations cause some cases of familial ALS. An HTT CAG repeat expansion causes Huntington disease (general knowledge). APOE ε4 homozygosity raises the risk of late-onset Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, fatal familial insomnia; self-propagating conformational cascade). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease is a rapidly progressive dementia that is usually sporadic, with some familial forms (First Aid Microbiology—Systems, prions, p. 174). COMLEX: Gerstmann-Sträussler-Scheinker syndrome is the other inherited prion disease tied to germline PRNP mutations (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman dies 5 months after the onset of rapidly progressive dementia and ataxia. At autopsy, the cerebral cortex and deep gray matter show widespread vacuoles in the neuropil with neuronal loss and reactive gliosis. Which additional histologic finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Perivascular lymphocyte cuffs","B":"Neutrophil microabscesses","C":"Viral inclusion bodies","D":"Noncaseating granulomas","E":"Absent inflammation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Spongiform neurodegeneration in prion disease proceeds with a notable lack of inflammatory infiltrates. Perivascular lymphocyte cuffs are a feature of viral encephalitis (general knowledge). Neutrophil microabscesses point to bacterial infection (general knowledge). Viral inclusion bodies mark viral infection, and prions contain no genome. Noncaseating granulomas suggest sarcoidosis (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, absence of inflammation); Robbins Ch. 28 (Prion Diseases, Morphology). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cortex in Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease is spongiform, with vacuolation but no inflammation (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: electron microscopy places the prion vacuoles inside membranes within the cytoplasm of neuronal processes (Robbins Ch. 28, Prion Diseases).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 7 — contribution of inflammation to the aging process","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man whose mother developed Alzheimer disease at age 79 learns that he carries 2 copies of a risk allele of a lipid-transport gene on chromosome 19. Besides raising his overall risk, how does this genotype alter the disease if it develops?","choices":{"A":"Earlier age of onset","B":"Slower cognitive decline","C":"Lower amyloid burden","D":"Less tau pathology","E":"Sparing of the hippocampus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The APOE ε4 allele on chromosome 19 raises Alzheimer risk and lowers the age at which the disease begins. A slower cognitive decline is not an effect of this allele. Amyloid burden rises rather than falls, because ε4 enhances Aβ deposition. Tau pathology worsens with ε4 instead of lessening. The hippocampus is among the earliest and most severely atrophied structures in Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, late-onset sporadic AD risk; gross neuropathology). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ApoE-2 lowers and ApoE-4 raises the risk of sporadic Alzheimer disease (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: the ε4 allele accounts for about one-quarter of late-onset risk and also impairs lysosomal degradation and speeds neuroinflammation (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with early Alzheimer disease enrolls in a trial of an antibody meant to clear the amyloid-beta form that most directly causes synaptic failure and impairs long-term potentiation. Which form does the antibody target?","choices":{"A":"Dense-core plaque fibrils","B":"Soluble Aβ42 oligomers","C":"Vascular amyloid deposits","D":"Diffuse plaque deposits","E":"Paired helical filaments"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Soluble Aβ42 oligomers are the primary neurotoxic species; they cause synaptic failure, disrupt long-term potentiation, activate the tyrosine kinase Fyn, and trigger downstream tau pathology. Dense-core plaque fibrils are insoluble deposits and not the leading synaptotoxic form. Vascular amyloid deposits make up cerebral amyloid angiopathy. Diffuse plaque deposits lack surrounding dystrophic neurites. Paired helical filaments are tau aggregates rather than amyloid-beta. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, toxic oligomers). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurofibrillary tangles are intracellular aggregates of hyperphosphorylated tau, and their number correlates with the degree of dementia (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: aggregated Aβ and tau activate microglia through the NLRP3 inflammasome, and microglia then reactivate complement-mediated pruning that destroys synapses (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 8 — accumulation of damaged macromolecules in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has 2 years of trouble with words. He speaks fluently but asks what \"giraffe\" and \"hammer\" mean, cannot name common objects, and misunderstands single words, while his memory for daily events is intact. Which disorder best accounts for his presentation?","choices":{"A":"Behavioral variant FTD","B":"Progressive supranuclear palsy","C":"Corticobasal degeneration","D":"Semantic dementia","E":"Nonfluent aphasia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Loss of word meaning with impaired naming and single-word comprehension, in fluent speech, is semantic dementia, a language variant of FTD. Behavioral variant FTD begins with personality and conduct changes. Progressive supranuclear palsy is a tauopathy whose overlap with FTD is extrapyramidal motor disease. Corticobasal degeneration is likewise an overlapping tauopathy with parkinsonian features. Nonfluent aphasia brings effortful, halting speech with speech sound errors rather than lost word meaning. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, language variants; disease progression and motor overlap). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Wernicke aphasia from a superior temporal gyrus lesion gives fluent speech with impaired comprehension and poor insight (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, aphasia, p. 529). COMLEX: over several years the focal deficits of FTD progress to global cognitive impairment, mutism, and severe dependence (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — atypical parkinsonian syndromes and how they differ from PD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman's husband reports 1 year of new impulsivity, loss of empathy, and neglect of hygiene. Compared with early Alzheimer disease, which cognitive domain is expected to be relatively preserved early in her illness?","choices":{"A":"Episodic memory","B":"Executive judgment","C":"Social conduct","D":"Personality","E":"Behavioral inhibition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"FTLD begins with early, striking changes in personality, social conduct, judgment, and language, while episodic memory stays relatively intact early, the reverse of Alzheimer disease. Executive judgment is impaired early in FTLD. Social conduct deteriorates early. Personality changes are a defining early feature. Behavioral inhibition is lost early, producing disinhibition. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, distinct pattern of onset). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: reversible causes of dementia include depression, hypothyroidism, vitamin B12 deficiency, neurosyphilis, and normal pressure hydrocephalus (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: visuospatial orientation is also relatively preserved early in FTLD (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":5,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department by her husband because of 2 months of rapidly worsening confusion, unsteady gait, and blurred, distorted vision. Examination shows disorientation and a wide-based ataxic gait. An EEG shows periodic sharp wave complexes. Which additional examination finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Resting tremor","B":"Startle myoclonus","C":"Fasciculations","D":"Choreiform movements","E":"Bilateral ptosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease begins with subtle memory and behavior changes and progresses to rapid dementia, often with prominent jerking contractions provoked by sudden stimuli, called startle myoclonus. A resting tremor suggests Parkinson disease (general knowledge). Fasciculations are lower motor neuron signs, as in ALS (general knowledge). Choreiform movements suggest Huntington disease (general knowledge). Bilateral ptosis points to a neuromuscular junction disorder such as myasthenia gravis (general knowledge). Sources: Robbins Ch. 28 (Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease; the motor sign is not in the SDL 6 notes and also appears in First Aid); SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, CJD); First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535 (EEG finding). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 6 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vascular dementia, the second most common dementia in older adults, follows multiple arterial infarcts or chronic ischemia and declines in a stepwise way (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: sporadic CJD has an annual incidence near 1 per million, makes up more than 90% of CJD, and kills in an average of about 7 months from symptom onset (Robbins Ch. 28, Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl06_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":5,"stem":"The family of an 81-year-old woman meets with the hospice team at her nursing home. She has had 9 years of progressive memory loss that has advanced to mutism, urinary incontinence, and inability to walk. Her daughter asks how the illness usually ends. Which event most commonly ends the course of this disease?","choices":{"A":"Intercurrent pneumonia","B":"Recurrent lobar hemorrhage","C":"Status epilepticus","D":"Pulmonary embolism","E":"Myocardial infarction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alzheimer disease runs a slow course, often longer than 10 years, ending with mutism, incontinence, and inability to walk, and an intercurrent illness, often pneumonia, is the usual terminal event. Recurrent lobar hemorrhage can complicate cerebral amyloid angiopathy but is not the usual terminal event. Status epilepticus is not the typical end of the disease (general knowledge). Pulmonary embolism is a risk of immobility but is not the usual cause of death in Alzheimer disease (general knowledge). Myocardial infarction is not the characteristic terminal event (general knowledge). Sources: Robbins Ch. 28 (Alzheimer Disease, Clinical Features; the terminal event is Robbins-only); SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 6 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lewy body dementia brings visual hallucinations, fluctuating cognition and alertness, REM sleep behavior disorder, and parkinsonism (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: Alzheimer prevalence roughly doubles every 5 years, from about 1% at ages 60 to 64 to 40% or more at ages 85 to 89 (Robbins Ch. 28, Alzheimer Disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — pathophysiology of infections in the elderly, including respiratory infection","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl06_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":5,"stem":"A 60-year-old man is brought to the physician by his daughter because of 3 years of apathy, compulsive hoarding, and loss of empathy. His mother had a similar illness, and her autopsy showed phosphorylated, ubiquitinated inclusions of an RNA-binding protein in the cytoplasm of frontal cortical neurons, with loss of its normal nuclear staining. Examination shows perseveration and impaired verbal fluency, with intact orientation and recall. MRI of the brain shows bilateral frontal and anterior temporal atrophy. Besides RNA processing, the normal form of the protein in his mother's inclusions helps assemble which structures?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear pore complexes","B":"Autophagosomes","C":"Proteasome complexes","D":"Synaptic vesicles","E":"Stress granules"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The inclusions are TDP-43, whose normal roles include RNA processing and building stress granules, RNA-protein aggregates that hold free mRNAs when translation stops during cellular stress. Nuclear pore complexes are built from nucleoporins (general knowledge). Autophagosomes are double-membrane vesicles of the autophagy pathway (general knowledge). Proteasome complexes degrade ubiquitinated proteins (general knowledge). Synaptic vesicles store neurotransmitter at nerve terminals (general knowledge). Sources: Robbins Ch. 28 (Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations, FTLD-TDP; the stress-granule role is Robbins-only); SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, FTLD-TDP). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 6 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ALS combines upper and lower motor neuron degeneration, and riluzole and edaravone slow functional decline (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, ALS, p. 544). COMLEX: unlike C9orf72 and TARDBP, GRN mutations have not been linked to ALS (Robbins Ch. 28, Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 7 — features of ALS","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"}]},{"sdlNumber":7,"title":"SDL 7 — Neurodegenerative Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of Parkinson's Disease","batch":1,"stem":"On electron microscopy, the Lewy bodies of Parkinson disease consist of densely packed filaments of which type?","choices":{"A":"Intermediate filaments","B":"Paired helical filaments","C":"Actin microfilaments","D":"Hollow microtubules","E":"Collagen fibrils"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ultrastructurally, Lewy bodies are densely packed intermediate filaments built from aggregated alpha-synuclein. Paired helical filaments make up the tau tangles of Alzheimer disease. Actin microfilaments are not the Lewy body scaffold. Hollow microtubules are cytoskeletal tubes, not Lewy body filaments. Collagen fibrils are extracellular matrix proteins. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 1, Lewy bodies). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: oligodendrocytes, the predominant glial cell of white matter, each myelinate about 30 CNS axons and look like fried eggs on histology (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, CNS glial cells, p. 503). COMLEX: on light microscopy a Lewy body is a round to elongated eosinophilic inclusion with a dense core and a pale halo (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of Parkinson's Disease","batch":1,"stem":"Epidemiologic studies link which exposures to a lower risk of Parkinson disease?","choices":{"A":"Pesticides and herbicides","B":"Solvents and jet fuel","C":"Organic vapors and MPTP","D":"Caffeine and nicotine","E":"Statins and aspirin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Caffeine and nicotine use are epidemiologically protective against Parkinson disease. Pesticides and herbicides are agricultural risk exposures. Industrial solvents and jet fuel also raise risk. Organic vapors and MPTP are neurotoxic exposures that raise risk. Statins and aspirin are not the protective exposures named for Parkinson disease. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 1, environmental risk factors). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dopamine is made in the ventral tegmentum and the substantia nigra pars compacta of the midbrain and falls in Parkinson disease (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, neurotransmitter changes with disease, p. 505). COMLEX: agricultural chemicals, industrial solvents, organic vapors, and jet fuel are the environmental exposures tied to higher risk (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of Parkinson's Disease","batch":1,"stem":"Heterozygous mutations in which gene, which encodes a lysosomal enzyme, are a major genetic risk factor for Parkinson disease?","choices":{"A":"LRRK2","B":"SNCA","C":"GBA1","D":"PINK1","E":"HEXA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Heterozygous GBA1 mutations reduce glucocerebrosidase activity, disrupt the autophagy-lysosome network, and account for about 5% of Parkinson disease. LRRK2 encodes a kinase, not a lysosomal enzyme. SNCA encodes alpha-synuclein. PINK1 encodes a mitochondrial kinase. HEXA encodes hexosaminidase A, which is deficient in Tay-Sachs disease (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 1, lysosomal and autophagic impairment). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: homozygous glucocerebrosidase deficiency causes Gaucher disease, the most common lysosomal storage disease, with hepatosplenomegaly, bone crises, and crumpled tissue paper macrophages (First Aid Biochemistry—Metabolism, lysosomal storage diseases, p. 86). COMLEX: reduced glucocerebrosidase activity impairs alpha-synuclein clearance and turns on the unfolded protein response (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":1,"stem":"The resting tremor of Parkinson disease typically has which frequency?","choices":{"A":"1–2 Hz","B":"8–12 Hz","C":"15–20 Hz","D":"4–6 Hz","E":"25–30 Hz"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The parkinsonian resting tremor is slow, 4 to 6 Hz, and starts asymmetrically in a distal arm with a pill-rolling motion. A 1 to 2 Hz rhythm is slower than this tremor. Frequencies of 8 to 12 Hz, 15 to 20 Hz, and 25 to 30 Hz are faster than the classic resting tremor. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, resting tremor). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: essential tremor is a high-frequency postural tremor that is often familial, eases with alcohol, and is treated with propranolol or primidone (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, movement disorders, p. 533). COMLEX: the parkinsonian tremor fades during purposeful movement and stops during sleep (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":1,"stem":"Parkinsonian rigidity is distinguished from spasticity by which property of the resistance to passive movement?","choices":{"A":"Velocity-dependent tone","B":"Clasp-knife spastic tone","C":"Velocity-independent tone","D":"Decreased flaccid tone","E":"Fatigable resistance to stretch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Parkinsonian rigidity is resistance to passive joint movement that does not depend on velocity, felt as lead-pipe resistance or as cogwheeling when tremor is superimposed. Velocity-dependent tone defines spasticity (general knowledge). Clasp-knife tone is the sudden give of upper motor neuron spasticity (general knowledge). Decreased flaccid tone is a lower motor neuron sign. Resistance that fatigues with stretch is not a feature of rigidity. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, rigidity). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: upper motor neuron injury raises tone and reflexes with a positive Babinski sign and clasp-knife spasticity, whereas lower motor neuron injury lowers them (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, upper vs lower motor neuron lesions, p. 543). COMLEX: bradykinesia combines slow initiation and execution of movement with less spontaneous motor activity (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":1,"stem":"The masked facies of Parkinson disease includes which eye finding?","choices":{"A":"Facial fasciculations","B":"Decreased blink rate","C":"Bilateral ptosis","D":"Corneal reflex loss","E":"Lower facial droop"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hypomimia in Parkinson disease brings less facial animation, widened palpebral fissures, and a decreased blink rate. Facial fasciculations are lower motor neuron signs. Bilateral ptosis narrows rather than widens the fissures. Corneal reflex loss reflects trigeminal or facial nerve injury (general knowledge). Lower facial droop suggests an upper motor neuron facial lesion such as stroke (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, masked facies). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbidopa blocks peripheral DOPA decarboxylase, so more levodopa reaches the brain and peripheral nausea and vomiting lessen (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563). COMLEX: hypophonic dysarthria and micrographia accompany the loss of fine motor dexterity (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":1,"stem":"Under the one-year rule, dementia with Lewy bodies is diagnosed when dementia appears at what point relative to parkinsonism?","choices":{"A":"Within 1 year","B":"2–5 years later","C":"5–10 years later","D":"Over 10 years later","E":"At levodopa initiation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In dementia with Lewy bodies, dementia comes before, with, or within 1 year of the motor signs, reflecting an early limbic and neocortical synuclein burden. Onset 2 to 5 years later falls outside the rule. Onset 5 to 10 years later also falls well outside the 1-year window. Onset more than 10 years later is later still. Levodopa initiation is not the reference point. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, the one-year rule). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: REM sleep behavior disorder, dream enactment from lost atonia, often precedes Parkinson disease and Lewy body dementia (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, sleep physiology, p. 507). COMLEX: Parkinson disease, Parkinson disease dementia, and dementia with Lewy bodies form one continuous spectrum of alpha-synucleinopathies (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":1,"stem":"Patients with dementia with Lewy bodies show profound hypersensitivity to which drug class?","choices":{"A":"Cholinesterase inhibitors","B":"Dopamine receptor agonists","C":"Benzodiazepine sedatives","D":"Beta-adrenergic blockers","E":"Typical antipsychotics"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dementia with Lewy bodies is defined in part by profound hypersensitivity to traditional antipsychotic dopamine receptor antagonists. Cholinesterase inhibitors are not the drugs the hypersensitivity involves. Dopamine receptor agonists stimulate rather than block the receptor. Benzodiazepine sedatives act on GABA-A receptors. Beta-adrenergic blockers do not act on dopamine receptors. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, shared and distinguishing clinical features). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neuroleptic malignant syndrome follows antipsychotics, typical more than atypical, with fever, encephalopathy, unstable vitals, raised CK, and lead-pipe rigidity, and it is treated with dantrolene and dopaminergic drugs (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, p. 587). COMLEX: fluctuating attention and alertness and early recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations also define the disorder (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":1,"stem":"Overall, about what share of patients with Parkinson disease develop dementia?","choices":{"A":"1–2%","B":"20–25%","C":"10–15%","D":"30–35%","E":"85–90%"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Overall, dementia develops in about 10% to 15% of patients with Parkinson disease, a figure that rises to 40% to 80% only with long-term survival. A share of 1% to 2% is too low. Shares of 20% to 25% and 30% to 35% overstate the overall estimate and still fall short of the long-survival range. A share of 85% to 90% exceeds even the long-survival estimate. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, Parkinson disease dementia); Robbins Ch. 28 (Dementia With Lewy Bodies, share of PD patients with dementia). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on-off fluctuations develop with progressive disease on levodopa, and the MAO-B inhibitors selegiline and rasagiline help smooth them (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563). COMLEX: dementia with Lewy bodies brings early, recurrent, well-formed visual hallucinations (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":1,"stem":"Which clinical hallmark defines progressive supranuclear palsy?","choices":{"A":"Alien limb phenomenon","B":"Resting pill-rolling tremor","C":"Orthostatic hypotension","D":"Macular degeneration","E":"Impaired vertical saccades"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The hallmark of progressive supranuclear palsy is a supranuclear palsy of vertical gaze, with trouble starting voluntary downward or upward saccades. The alien limb phenomenon belongs to corticobasal degeneration. A resting pill-rolling tremor is typical of Parkinson disease and is absent in the atypical syndromes. Severe orthostatic hypotension points to autonomic failure in a different synucleinopathy. Macular degeneration with visual loss belongs to one of the spinocerebellar ataxias. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, progressive supranuclear palsy). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: progressive supranuclear palsy is a Parkinson-plus syndrome with a hummingbird sign of midbrain atrophy on MRI (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, neurodegenerative movement disorders, p. 534). COMLEX: early backward falls, symmetric axial rigidity, pseudobulbar palsy, and a frontal subcortical dementia complete the picture (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — frontotemporal lobar degeneration and FTLD-tau","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":1,"stem":"Glial cytoplasmic inclusions of alpha-synuclein within oligodendrocytes are the defining finding of which disorder?","choices":{"A":"Multiple system atrophy","B":"Progressive supranuclear palsy","C":"Corticobasal degeneration","D":"Dementia with Lewy bodies","E":"Parkinson disease"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Multiple system atrophy is a synucleinopathy defined by glial cytoplasmic inclusions of alpha-synuclein and ubiquitin in oligodendrocytes, with white matter tract degeneration. Progressive supranuclear palsy is a 4-repeat tauopathy. Corticobasal degeneration is also a 4-repeat tauopathy. Dementia with Lewy bodies has neuronal Lewy bodies. Parkinson disease has neuronal Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, multiple system atrophy). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lesions of the subthalamic nucleus cause contralateral hemiballismus (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: as a group, the atypical syndromes progress quickly, begin symmetrically, impair gait early, and respond poorly or briefly to levodopa (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — frontotemporal lobar degeneration and FTLD-tau","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":1,"stem":"Progressive supranuclear palsy and corticobasal degeneration share which molecular classification?","choices":{"A":"Synucleinopathies","B":"4-repeat tauopathies","C":"TDP-43 proteinopathies","D":"Polyglutamine disorders","E":"Amyloid-beta disorders"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Progressive supranuclear palsy and corticobasal degeneration are 4-repeat tauopathies classified under FTLD-tau. Synucleinopathies include Parkinson disease, dementia with Lewy bodies, and multiple system atrophy. TDP-43 proteinopathies include sporadic ALS and FTLD-TDP. Polyglutamine disorders include Huntington disease and several spinocerebellar ataxias. Amyloid-beta deposition defines the plaques of Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, molecular pathology of PSP and CBD). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dorsal midbrain lesions cause Parinaud syndrome, often from a pineal gland tumor (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: corticobasal degeneration combines focal frontoparietal cortical atrophy with basal ganglia atrophy (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — frontotemporal lobar degeneration and FTLD-tau","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":1,"stem":"What CAG repeat count in the huntingtin gene defines a pathogenic allele?","choices":{"A":"36 or more repeats","B":"20 or more repeats","C":"50 or more repeats","D":"100 or more repeats","E":"200 or more repeats"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normal HTT alleles carry 6 to 35 CAG repeats, with 27 to 35 an intermediate range that can expand in offspring (GeneReviews, Huntington disease), and pathogenic alleles carry 36 or more. A threshold of 20 repeats falls inside the normal range. Thresholds of 50, 100, or 200 repeats are higher than the pathogenic cutoff. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, repeat thresholds). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in fragile X syndrome a CGG premutation of 50 to 200 repeats causes tremor, ataxia, and primary ovarian insufficiency, whereas more than 200 repeats is a full mutation (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, trinucleotide repeat expansion diseases, p. 60). COMLEX: the HTT gene sits on chromosome 4p16.3 and encodes a 348-kDa protein (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":1,"stem":"In Huntington disease, which striatal neurons are lost?","choices":{"A":"Noradrenergic ceruleus neurons","B":"Dopaminergic nigral neurons","C":"Glutamatergic pyramidal cells","D":"Medium spiny GABAergic neurons","E":"Serotonergic raphe neurons"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Huntington disease destroys the medium-sized spiny GABAergic projection neurons that also express substance P, dynorphin, and enkephalin. Noradrenergic neurons lie in the locus ceruleus of the pons, not the striatum. Dopaminergic nigral neurons are lost in Parkinson disease. Glutamatergic pyramidal cells are cortical neurons. Serotonergic raphe neurons lie in the brainstem. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, selective neuronal loss). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the striatum is the putamen, which is motor, plus the caudate nucleus, which is cognitive, and the lentiform nucleus is the putamen plus the globus pallidus (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, basal ganglia, p. 511). COMLEX: surviving striatal and cortical neurons hold intranuclear ubiquitinated aggregates of huntingtin (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":1,"stem":"The expanded CAG repeat in the huntingtin gene causes neuronal dysfunction through which genetic mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Haploinsufficiency","B":"Toxic gain of function","C":"Frameshift truncation","D":"Splice-site skipping","E":"Promoter hypermethylation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The elongated polyglutamine tract near the N-terminus of huntingtin confers a toxic gain of function that promotes abnormal protein conformation. Haploinsufficiency is loss of half the gene product and is not the HTT mechanism. Frameshift truncation is not produced by an in-frame CAG expansion. Splice-site skipping is not the mechanism of this exonic repeat. Promoter hypermethylation silences genes rather than adding a toxic protein. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, polyglutamine toxicity). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trinucleotide repeat expansion diseases can show anticipation, with greater severity and earlier onset in successive generations (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, trinucleotide repeat expansion diseases, p. 60). COMLEX: transmission is autosomal dominant, with complete penetrance at 40 or more repeats and reduced penetrance at 36 to 39, and new sporadic mutations are very rare (SDL 7 notes; GeneReviews, Huntington disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":1,"stem":"In Friedreich ataxia, the GAA repeat expansion lies in which part of the FXN gene?","choices":{"A":"First exon","B":"Promoter region","C":"Last exon","D":"Splice acceptor site","E":"First intron"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Friedreich ataxia is caused by an unstable GAA trinucleotide expansion within the first intron of FXN. The first exon is the location of the HTT CAG repeat, not the FXN repeat. The promoter region does not carry the expansion. The last exon does not carry it either. A splice acceptor site mutation is a different lesion type from a repeat expansion. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, Friedreich ataxia molecular genetics). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: myotonic dystrophy is an autosomal dominant CTG repeat disorder with cataracts, early balding in males, and gonadal atrophy (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, trinucleotide repeat expansion diseases, p. 60). COMLEX: Friedreich ataxia is the most common hereditary ataxia and maps to chromosome 9q13 (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — DNA damage and repair","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":1,"stem":"Which spinocerebellar ataxia is distinguished clinically by progressive macular degeneration that leads to blindness?","choices":{"A":"SCA7","B":"SCA1","C":"SCA2","D":"SCA3","E":"SCA6"},"correct":"A","explanation":"SCA7 is the polyglutamine spinocerebellar ataxia set apart by progressive macular degeneration and visual loss. SCA1 is a polyglutamine ataxia without this retinal feature. SCA2 is likewise a polyglutamine ataxia without it. SCA3, also called Machado-Joseph disease, is not defined by macular degeneration. SCA6 is a polyglutamine ataxia without retinal degeneration. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, mechanistic subclassification of SCAs). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cerebellar hemisphere lesions cause intention tremor, limb ataxia, and loss of balance on the same side, with a fall toward the lesion (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: the SCAs are a group of more than 40 autosomal dominant disorders that usually start in early to mid-adulthood (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — DNA damage and repair","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":1,"stem":"Frataxin is required to assemble which mitochondrial cofactors?","choices":{"A":"Copper-zinc centers","B":"Ubiquinone pools","C":"Iron-sulfur clusters","D":"Cristae junctions","E":"Mitochondrial ribosomes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Frataxin is essential for assembling mitochondrial iron-sulfur cluster complexes, so its loss disrupts complexes I and II, frees intramitochondrial iron, and generates reactive oxygen species. Copper-zinc centers are found in superoxide dismutase 1. Ubiquinone pools are not built by frataxin. Cristae junctions are membrane structures rather than frataxin products. Mitochondrial ribosomes are not assembled by frataxin. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, Friedreich ataxia pathophysiologic defect). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mitochondrial myopathies such as MELAS show ragged red fibers and vary in severity because of heteroplasmy (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, mitochondrial inheritance, p. 57; Biochemistry—Genetics, mitochondrial diseases, p. 60). COMLEX: Friedreich ataxia also brings a high-arched pes cavus and progressive kyphoscoliosis (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — DNA damage and repair","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":1,"stem":"In amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, Bunina bodies are found in which cells?","choices":{"A":"Cerebellar Purkinje cells","B":"Dorsal root ganglion cells","C":"Spinal oligodendrocytes","D":"Anterior horn cells","E":"Schwann cells of roots"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bunina bodies are small eosinophilic PAS-positive cytoplasmic inclusions in surviving anterior horn motor neurons and are thought to be remnants of autophagic vacuoles. Cerebellar Purkinje cells are not their site. Dorsal root ganglion cells are sensory neurons spared in ALS. Spinal oligodendrocytes are glia, not the neurons that carry Bunina bodies. Schwann cells myelinate peripheral roots and do not contain them. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, Bunina bodies). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: spinal muscular atrophy is autosomal recessive SMN1 loss with lower motor neuron signs only, giving a floppy baby with tongue fasciculations (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, spinal cord lesions, p. 544). COMLEX: anterior roots of the cord become thin and gray compared with the sensory roots, and the precentral gyrus atrophies (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — FTLD-TDP and the ALS spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":1,"stem":"Which motor function is classically preserved until late in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Tongue protrusion strength","B":"Swallowing coordination","C":"Fine hand dexterity","D":"Diaphragmatic strength","E":"Extraocular eye movements"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Extraocular eye movements, along with sensation and sphincter control, are classically spared until late in ALS. Tongue strength falls with hypoglossal degeneration. Swallowing coordination fails with bulbar involvement. Fine hand dexterity is often the first function lost. Diaphragmatic strength declines as the disease advances. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, sparing). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in an adult, a Babinski sign, an upgoing big toe when the foot is stroked, may signify an upper motor neuron lesion (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, clinical reflexes, p. 523). COMLEX: about 80% to 90% of ALS is sporadic, and familial cases are mostly autosomal dominant (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — FTLD-TDP and the ALS spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":1,"stem":"Which ALS variant shows almost exclusively upper motor neuron signs?","choices":{"A":"Progressive muscular atrophy","B":"Primary lateral sclerosis","C":"Progressive bulbar palsy","D":"Spinal muscular atrophy","E":"Multifocal motor neuropathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary lateral sclerosis is the variant marked by upper motor neuron signs with little lower motor neuron involvement. Progressive muscular atrophy is the uncommon variant in which lower motor neuron degeneration predominates. Progressive bulbar palsy begins with severe speech and swallowing impairment. Spinal muscular atrophy is an inherited lower motor neuron disease. Multifocal motor neuropathy is an immune-mediated neuropathy (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, clinical variants). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: subacute combined degeneration from vitamin B12 deficiency demyelinates the dorsal columns along with the corticospinal and spinocerebellar tracts (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, spinal cord lesions, p. 544). COMLEX: progressive bulbar palsy evolves quickly and is often fatal within 1 to 2 years (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — FTLD-TDP and the ALS spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q22","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of Parkinson's Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of 1 week of slowness, stiffness, and a shuffling gait. Two weeks ago he injected a street opioid synthesized in a home laboratory; he has no family history of movement disorders and takes no antipsychotic or antiemetic drugs. Examination shows a masked face, a 5-Hz resting tremor of both hands, cogwheel rigidity, and bradykinesia. Serum ceruloplasmin is 28 mg/dL (reference range: 20–40 mg/dL), and MRI of the brain shows no structural lesions. Damage to which neurons most likely explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Nigrostriatal dopamine neurons","B":"Striatal cholinergic interneurons","C":"Cerebellar Purkinje cells","D":"Anterior horn motor neurons","E":"Basal forebrain cholinergic neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acute parkinsonism after injecting a home-synthesized opioid points to MPTP, a mitochondrial neurotoxin that selectively damages nigrostriatal dopaminergic neurons; his normal ceruloplasmin argues against Wilson disease. Striatal cholinergic interneurons are not the MPTP target. Cerebellar Purkinje cell loss causes ataxia rather than parkinsonism (general knowledge). Anterior horn motor neuron loss causes weakness, atrophy, and fasciculations. Basal forebrain cholinergic neurons are lost in Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 1, environmental risk factors; core pathological definition). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Wilson disease, an autosomal recessive ATP7B defect with low ceruloplasmin, deposits copper in the liver, basal ganglia, and cornea (First Aid Gastrointestinal—Pathology, Wilson disease, p. 402). COMLEX: at autopsy, the substantia nigra and locus ceruleus look pale because pigmented neuromelanin-containing catecholaminergic neurons have been lost (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — proposed causes of cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q23","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of Parkinson's Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man comes to the physician because of 1 year of a left-hand tremor at rest and slowness when shaving. His father and an older sister were both diagnosed with Parkinson disease in their 60s. Examination shows a 4-Hz pill-rolling tremor of the left hand, cogwheel rigidity of the left wrist, and reduced arm swing on the left. Genetic testing identifies a heterozygous gain-of-function missense mutation in the kinase domain of a large leucine-rich repeat protein. The mutation most likely disrupts vesicular trafficking by hyperphosphorylating which proteins?","choices":{"A":"Tau proteins","B":"Histone proteins","C":"Neurofilaments","D":"Synaptotagmins","E":"Rab GTPases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Autosomal dominant Parkinson disease with a kinase-domain mutation fits LRRK2, whose hyperactivated kinase hyperphosphorylates Rab GTPases and disturbs vesicular trafficking. Tau hyperphosphorylation drives the tauopathies. Histone phosphorylation is a chromatin modification and not the LRRK2 mechanism. Neurofilaments are cytoskeletal proteins not named as LRRK2 targets. Synaptotagmins are calcium sensors for vesicle release (general knowledge) rather than the LRRK2 substrates. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 1, LRRK2 kinase mutations). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: deep brain stimulation of the subthalamic nucleus or internal globus pallidus helps advanced Parkinson disease, and non-ergot dopamine agonists are favored in younger patients (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563); the AAN 2021 early Parkinson disease guideline instead makes levodopa first-line and reserves dopamine agonists for select patients younger than 60 (Pringsheim et al., Neurology 2021). COMLEX: SNCA point mutations, duplications, or triplications promote toxic alpha-synuclein oligomers in other inherited forms (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q24","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of Parkinson's Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 2 years of slowly worsening stiffness and a right-foot tremor. Her parents are first cousins and are unaffected, and her younger brother has a similar disorder. Examination shows rigidity and bradykinesia that are worse on the right, and her symptoms improve markedly with levodopa. Genetic testing shows biallelic loss-of-function mutations in PRKN, which encodes the E3 ubiquitin ligase Parkin. Accumulation of which of the following most directly drives neuronal injury in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Hyperphosphorylated tau","B":"Amyloid-beta oligomers","C":"Defective mitochondria","D":"Polyglutamine huntingtin","E":"Unrepaired DNA breaks"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PINK1 normally recruits and activates Parkin to send damaged mitochondria for autophagic degradation, so recessive loss of Parkin lets dysfunctional mitochondria build up, generating reactive oxygen species and lowering complex I activity. Hyperphosphorylated tau accumulates in Alzheimer disease and the tauopathies. Amyloid-beta oligomers drive Alzheimer disease. Polyglutamine huntingtin accumulates in Huntington disease. Unrepaired DNA breaks accumulate in ataxia-telangiectasia. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 1, mitochondrial dysfunction and mitophagy). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mitochondrial disorders follow maternal inheritance, with all children of an affected mother at risk and none of the children of an affected father (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, modes of inheritance, p. 57). COMLEX: PARK7, which encodes DJ-1, is the third recessive gene that points to mitochondrial quality control (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q25","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 73-year-old man with a 6-year history of Parkinson disease comes to the physician because of constipation with 2 bowel movements a week despite adequate fiber and fluid intake. He takes carbidopa-levodopa and no opioids or anticholinergic drugs. Examination shows a resting tremor, rigidity, and a soft, nontender abdomen with normal bowel sounds. His serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL), and his TSH is 1.8 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL). Lewy body pathology in which structure most likely explains his constipation?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal vagal motor nucleus","B":"Intermediolateral cell column","C":"Nucleus tractus solitarius","D":"Edinger-Westphal nucleus","E":"Inferior salivatory nucleus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autonomic dysfunction in Parkinson disease, including constipation, arises from Lewy body pathology in the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus and in sympathetic ganglia; his normal calcium and TSH exclude metabolic causes. The intermediolateral cell column is a spinal cord column rather than the vagal parasympathetic output to the gut (general knowledge). The nucleus tractus solitarius is a visceral sensory nucleus (general knowledge). The Edinger-Westphal nucleus controls pupillary constriction and accommodation (general knowledge). The inferior salivatory nucleus supplies the parotid gland (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, autonomic dysfunction). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the antimuscarinics benztropine and trihexyphenidyl improve tremor and rigidity but do little for bradykinesia (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563). COMLEX: orthostatic hypotension, bladder urgency, incontinence, and poor temperature regulation are the other autonomic features (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — gastrointestinal disease in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q26","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 1 year of a right-hand tremor at rest and slowness when dressing. She has no history of antipsychotic or antiemetic use, and her family history is negative for tremor. Examination shows a 5-Hz pill-rolling tremor of the right hand that disappears when she reaches for a cup, cogwheel rigidity at the right wrist, reduced right arm swing, and micrographia. Laboratory studies show a TSH of 2.0 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL) and a vitamin B12 of 610 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Which response to a drug trial would most strongly support idiopathic Parkinson disease over an atypical parkinsonian syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Tremor relief with propranolol","B":"Sustained levodopa benefit","C":"Tremor relief with alcohol","D":"Improvement with donepezil","E":"Improvement with haloperidol"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A dramatic, sustained initial improvement after dopamine replacement with levodopa or a dopamine agonist supports idiopathic Parkinson disease, whereas the atypical syndromes respond poorly or briefly. Tremor relief with propranolol points to essential tremor. Tremor relief with alcohol is also typical of essential tremor. Donepezil is a cholinesterase inhibitor for dementia and does not test dopaminergic responsiveness (general knowledge). Haloperidol blocks dopamine receptors and worsens parkinsonism (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, therapeutic responsiveness; Obj 4, general distinctions). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: resting tremor, chorea, and athetosis localize to basal ganglia lesions such as those of Parkinson and Huntington disease (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: hypophonia makes the voice soft and monotonous in Parkinson disease (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q27","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man comes to the physician because of 18 months of slowness in walking and difficulty getting out of chairs. He has a mild, intermittent tremor of the left hand at rest and takes no dopamine-blocking drugs. Examination shows reduced facial expression, slow, small finger taps that decrement in amplitude, and mild rigidity of the left arm. Dopamine transporter SPECT shows striatal tracer uptake at 40% of age-matched normal values, worse on the right. Which cardinal feature of his disease tracks most closely with the degree of striatal dopamine depletion?","choices":{"A":"Resting tremor","B":"Postural instability","C":"Hyposmia","D":"Bradykinesia","E":"Constipation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bradykinesia, the slowness in starting and carrying out movement, is typically proportional to the degree of striatal dopamine depletion. Resting tremor is mild in this patient and does not track depletion as closely. Postural instability emerges later with disease progression. Hyposmia is a nonmotor feature rather than a cardinal motor sign (general knowledge). Constipation is an autonomic nonmotor feature. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, bradykinesia and akinesia). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nigrostriatal dopamine stimulates the direct pathway through D1 receptors and inhibits the indirect pathway through D2 receptors, both of which favor movement (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, basal ganglia, p. 511). COMLEX: postural instability brings backward falls and an abnormal response to the pull test as the disease advances (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q28","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man is brought to the physician by his wife because of 8 months of episodes of confusion that alternate with near-normal days; he often says he sees children playing in the living room. Over the past 4 months, he has developed a shuffling gait and stiffness. Examination shows inattention on serial sevens, mild symmetric rigidity, and bradykinesia without tremor. His serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and MRI of the brain shows mild generalized atrophy without infarcts. At autopsy, Lewy bodies are most likely to be found early in which distribution?","choices":{"A":"Lower brainstem nuclei","B":"Widespread cerebral cortex","C":"Spinal anterior horns","D":"Cerebellar Purkinje cell layer","E":"Striatal oligodendroglia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cognitive decline that comes before parkinsonism, with fluctuating attention and well-formed visual hallucinations, fits dementia with Lewy bodies, in which Lewy bodies appear early throughout the frontal, temporal, and parietal cortices and cingulate gyrus. Lower brainstem nuclei are the early site in idiopathic Parkinson disease. Spinal anterior horns are the site of motor neuron loss in ALS. The cerebellar Purkinje cell layer is not the early site. Striatal oligodendroglial inclusions suggest multiple system atrophy. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, neuropathological concordance; the one-year rule). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lewy body dementia is called so when cognitive and motor symptoms begin less than 1 year apart, and otherwise the dementia is attributed to Parkinson disease (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: both disorders share neuronal loss and depigmentation in the substantia nigra and locus ceruleus (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q29","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man with a 14-year history of asymmetric Parkinson disease is brought to the physician because of 1 year of slowed thinking and difficulty managing his medications. His motor symptoms responded well to levodopa for many years. Examination shows bradykinesia, rigidity worse on the left, and slowed, inattentive responses with intact orientation. MRI of the brain shows mild generalized atrophy, and his serum vitamin B12 is 540 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Which biological feature links his disease with dementia with Lewy bodies?","choices":{"A":"Glial synuclein inclusions","B":"Tau-positive tufted astrocytes","C":"Identical synuclein fold","D":"Polyglutamine aggregates","E":"TDP-43 inclusions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Parkinson disease dementia and dementia with Lewy bodies share genetic susceptibility loci and the identical alpha-synuclein molecular fold on structural analysis, making them phenotypes on one spectrum. Glial synuclein inclusions, with a distinct oligodendroglial fold, define multiple system atrophy. Tau-positive tufted astrocytes are not a synuclein fold. Polyglutamine aggregates occur in Huntington disease and several SCAs. TDP-43 inclusions characterize sporadic ALS and FTLD-TDP. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, unified clinicopathological spectrum). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: entacapone and tolcapone inhibit COMT to prevent peripheral breakdown of levodopa to 3-O-methyldopa, and tolcapone also acts centrally (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563). COMLEX: in idiopathic Parkinson disease, Lewy pathology climbs from the medulla through the pons and midbrain before reaching limbic cortex and neocortex (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q30","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman dies in a nursing home after 5 years of dementia with marked day-to-day swings in alertness and recurrent visual hallucinations. Mild parkinsonism appeared 6 months after her memory problems began. Her final examination showed symmetric rigidity and bradykinesia without tremor. At autopsy, immunostaining shows numerous neuronal inclusions of phosphorylated alpha-synuclein. Which additional pathology is commonly present in varying degrees in this disorder?","choices":{"A":"Amyloid plaques and tangles","B":"Glial cytoplasmic inclusions","C":"Spongiform vacuolation","D":"Tufted tau astrocytes","E":"Intranuclear huntingtin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dementia with Lewy bodies is accompanied by varying degrees of coexisting Alzheimer pathology, with amyloid-beta plaques and tau tangles. Glial cytoplasmic inclusions define multiple system atrophy. Spongiform vacuolation is the lesion of prion disease. Tufted tau astrocytes are a tauopathy lesion rather than coexisting Alzheimer pathology. Intranuclear huntingtin aggregates occur in Huntington disease. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, neuropathological concordance). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the non-ergot dopamine agonists pramipexole and ropinirole can cause impulse control disorders such as gambling, along with postural hypotension and hallucinations (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563). COMLEX: SNCA, GBA1, and APOE epsilon-4 are shared susceptibility loci across the Lewy body spectrum (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q31","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man comes to the physician because of 1 year of frequent backward falls and trouble reading and walking downstairs. A 6-week trial of levodopa produced no improvement, and he has no tremor. Examination shows marked symmetric rigidity of the neck and trunk, slowed vertical saccades with intact oculocephalic movements, and dysarthria with emotional lability. MRI of the brain shows midbrain atrophy with a preserved pons. Autopsy would most likely show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Round Pick bodies","B":"Tufted astrocytes","C":"Glial cytoplasmic inclusions","D":"Cortical Lewy bodies","E":"Bunina bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Early backward falls, axial rigidity, a supranuclear vertical gaze palsy, and levodopa resistance indicate progressive supranuclear palsy, a 4-repeat tauopathy with globular tau-positive tangles and tufted astrocytes in the subthalamic nucleus, globus pallidus, substantia nigra, and midbrain. Round Pick bodies are the tau inclusions of Pick disease. Glial cytoplasmic inclusions define multiple system atrophy. Cortical Lewy bodies characterize dementia with Lewy bodies. Bunina bodies occur in ALS. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, progressive supranuclear palsy). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amantadine increases dopamine release and blocks its reuptake, is used mainly against levodopa-induced dyskinesias, and can cause livedo reticularis and peripheral edema (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563). COMLEX: the absence of a pill-rolling resting tremor and early prominent falls help separate the atypical syndromes from Parkinson disease (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — negative outcomes of impaired mobility and falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q32","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 2 years of progressive stiffness and clumsiness of her right arm; she says the arm sometimes rises and grasps objects on its own. Levodopa produced no benefit. Examination shows severe rigidity and dystonic posturing of the right arm with myoclonic jerks, inability to mime using a comb with that hand, and impaired two-point discrimination on the right with normal primary sensation. MRI of the brain shows asymmetric left frontoparietal cortical atrophy. Which finding is expected in this patient's brain tissue?","choices":{"A":"Glial cytoplasmic inclusions","B":"Brainstem Lewy bodies","C":"Intranuclear huntingtin","D":"Astrocytic tau plaques","E":"Spongiform vacuolation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Strikingly asymmetric rigidity and dystonia with myoclonus, ideomotor apraxia, cortical sensory loss, and an alien limb indicate corticobasal degeneration, a 4-repeat tauopathy with tau-positive astrocytic plaques, coiled bodies in oligodendrocytes, and ballooned achromatic neurons. Glial cytoplasmic inclusions define multiple system atrophy. Brainstem Lewy bodies are the early lesion of Parkinson disease. Intranuclear huntingtin aggregates occur in Huntington disease. Spongiform vacuolation is the lesion of prion disease. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, corticobasal degeneration). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dystonia is a sustained involuntary muscle contraction, as in writer's cramp, blepharospasm, and torticollis, and it is treated with botulinum toxin injection (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, movement disorders, p. 533). COMLEX: the alien limb makes involuntary movements that look purposeful but that the patient feels are beyond voluntary control (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — FTLD-tau pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q33","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man comes to the physician because of 18 months of progressive unsteadiness, light-headedness on standing, erectile dysfunction, and urinary incontinence. Levodopa has not helped. His blood pressure is 138/84 mm Hg supine and 96/60 mm Hg after 3 minutes standing, with no compensatory rise in heart rate, and examination shows a wide-based ataxic gait, dysmetria, and reduced sweating. MRI of the brain shows atrophy of the pons, middle cerebellar peduncles, and cerebellum. Degeneration of which structure most directly accounts for his orthostatic hypotension?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons","B":"Anterior horn motor neurons","C":"Intermediolateral cell column","D":"Nucleus ambiguus neurons","E":"Lateral corticospinal tracts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cerebellar ataxia with pontocerebellar atrophy, levodopa-refractory course, and severe autonomic failure fit multiple system atrophy, in which degeneration of the catecholaminergic medullary centers and the spinal intermediolateral cell columns causes orthostatic hypotension, anhidrosis, erectile dysfunction, and incontinence. Dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons carry afferent input rather than sympathetic output. Anterior horn motor neurons control skeletal muscle. Nucleus ambiguus neurons supply the pharynx, larynx, and cardiac vagal output rather than sympathetic vasoconstriction (general knowledge). Lateral corticospinal tracts are descending motor pathways rather than autonomic ones. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, multiple system atrophy). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intention tremor is a slow zigzag movement toward a target caused by cerebellar dysfunction (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, movement disorders, p. 533). COMLEX: atypical parkinsonian syndromes progress rapidly from a symmetric onset and lack the classic pill-rolling resting tremor (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man is brought to the physician because of 1 year of irritability, impulsive outbursts, and brief involuntary jerking movements of his face and arms. His father developed similar symptoms at age 45, and his paternal grandfather at age 60. Examination shows irregular, dancelike movements of the face, trunk, and limbs and impaired rapid alternating movements. Genetic testing shows 58 CAG repeats in one HTT allele, compared with 44 repeats in his father. Which process best explains his earlier onset?","choices":{"A":"Maternal mitochondrial inheritance","B":"Germline mosaicism in mother","C":"Somatic repeat contraction","D":"Uniparental disomy of chromosome 4","E":"Expansion during spermatogenesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CAG tracts are meiotically unstable and tend to expand during paternal spermatogenesis, so paternal transmission yields longer repeats and earlier, more severe disease, and repeat length correlates inversely with age of onset. Maternal mitochondrial inheritance does not apply to a nuclear gene transmitted from the father. Germline mosaicism in his mother does not explain a paternally inherited expansion. Somatic contraction would shorten rather than lengthen the repeat. Uniparental disomy is not the mechanism of anticipation (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, genetic anticipation). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chorea is sudden, jerky, purposeless movement from basal ganglia lesions, seen in Huntington disease and in Sydenham chorea of acute rheumatic fever (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, movement disorders, p. 533). COMLEX: as striatal degeneration advances, chorea gives way to dystonia, bradykinesia, rigidity, and dysarthria (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q35","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her husband because of 2 years of depression, poor planning, and fidgety movements she cannot control. Her mother died in her 50s of a similar illness. Examination shows continuous irregular movements of the face and fingers, motor impersistence of tongue protrusion, and slowed saccades. MRI of the brain shows atrophy of the caudate nuclei and putamen with box-shaped enlargement of the frontal horns of the lateral ventricles. Loss of the inhibitory output of her damaged striatal neurons most directly disinhibits which pathway, producing her movements?","choices":{"A":"Spinocerebellar pathway","B":"Dorsal column pathway","C":"Mesolimbic dopamine pathway","D":"Thalamocortical pathway","E":"Rubrospinal pathway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Loss of striatal projection neurons in Huntington disease disinhibits the thalamocortical motor pathway, producing hyperkinetic chorea. The spinocerebellar pathway carries proprioceptive input to the cerebellum. The dorsal column pathway carries vibration and position sense. The mesolimbic dopamine pathway is involved in reward rather than chorea (general knowledge). The rubrospinal pathway is a brainstem motor tract not disinhibited by striatal loss (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, macroscopic striatal atrophy; selective neuronal loss). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the indirect pathway, striatal GABA inhibits the external globus pallidus, which disinhibits the subthalamic nucleus, which then drives the internal globus pallidus to inhibit the thalamus (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, basal ganglia, p. 511). COMLEX: emotional blunting, irritability, and anxiety are early personality changes in Huntington disease (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of FTLD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with a 15-year history of a hereditary movement disorder dies of aspiration pneumonia in a nursing home. In his final years he had severe dementia, rigidity, and dysarthria, after earlier years of chorea. His father and a paternal aunt had the same illness. At autopsy, the brain weighs 1050 g (reference range: 1200–1400 g) with marked caudate and putamen atrophy, and surviving striatal neurons contain ubiquitinated intranuclear aggregates. The aggregated protein contains an abnormally long tract of which amino acid?","choices":{"A":"Glutamine","B":"Glycine","C":"Alanine","D":"Glutamate","E":"Asparagine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The CAG expansion in exon 1 of HTT is translated into an elongated polyglutamine tract near the N-terminus of huntingtin. Glycine is not encoded by CAG. Alanine is not encoded by CAG either. Glutamate differs from glutamine and is not the expanded residue. Asparagine is not the amino acid encoded by the repeat. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, polyglutamine toxicity; disease course). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Huntington disease, dopamine rises while GABA and acetylcholine fall in the brain, and neurons die through NMDA receptor binding and glutamate excitotoxicity (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, neurodegenerative movement disorders, p. 534). COMLEX: average survival is about 15 years after symptom onset, and aspiration pneumonia is the most common terminal event (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q37","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of 3 years of progressive clumsiness and frequent falls. His parents are unaffected, and an older sister has the same problem. Examination shows a wide-based ataxic gait, absent ankle reflexes with bilateral extensor plantar responses, loss of vibration and position sense in the feet, high-arched feet, and kyphoscoliosis. Echocardiography shows an interventricular septal thickness of 16 mm (reference range: 6–11 mm), and his fasting plasma glucose is 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). The mutation responsible for his disorder reduces gene expression by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Polyglutamine protein toxicity","B":"Nonsense-mediated mRNA decay","C":"Heterochromatin gene silencing","D":"Dominant-negative frataxin","E":"Toxic nuclear RNA foci"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Autosomal recessive childhood ataxia with areflexia, extensor plantars, posterior column loss, pes cavus, scoliosis, and septal hypertrophy indicates Friedreich ataxia, in which the intronic GAA expansion promotes heterochromatin formation and silences FXN. Polyglutamine protein toxicity is the mechanism of Huntington disease and the polyglutamine SCAs. Nonsense-mediated decay follows premature stop codons rather than an intronic repeat (general knowledge). A dominant-negative protein does not fit recessive frataxin deficiency. Toxic RNA foci are a mechanism of noncoding expansions such as C9orf72. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, Friedreich ataxia). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Friedreich ataxia degenerates the corticospinal tracts, spinocerebellar tracts, dorsal columns, and dorsal root ganglia, and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is the cause of death (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, Friedreich ataxia, p. 545). COMLEX: Friedreich ataxia begins in the first or second decade with progressive gait ataxia and clumsiness (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — changes in the cardiovascular system with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q38","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of an unsteady gait since she began walking and three episodes of pneumonia in the past 2 years. Her parents are unaffected first cousins. Examination shows truncal ataxia, writhing movements of the hands, and dilated tortuous vessels on the bulbar conjunctivae and ears. Her serum IgA is 10 mg/dL (reference range for age: 33–200 mg/dL). For which malignancy is she at greatest risk?","choices":{"A":"Medulloblastoma","B":"Neuroblastoma","C":"Hepatoblastoma","D":"Retinoblastoma","E":"T-cell lymphoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Childhood cerebellar ataxia with choreoathetosis, oculocutaneous telangiectasias, recurrent sinopulmonary infections, and low IgA indicate ataxia-telangiectasia, which carries a marked susceptibility to lymphoid neoplasms, particularly T-cell leukemias and lymphomas. Medulloblastoma is not the characteristic malignancy (general knowledge). Neuroblastoma is not linked to this syndrome (general knowledge). Hepatoblastoma is not linked to it either (general knowledge). Retinoblastoma arises from RB1 mutations (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, ataxia-telangiectasia). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in ataxia-telangiectasia, defective ATM fails to detect DNA damage and halt the cell cycle, giving the triad of cerebellar defects, spider angiomas, and IgA deficiency (First Aid Immunology—Immune Responses, immunodeficiencies, p. 115). COMLEX: patients are hypersensitive to ionizing radiation, and the gene maps to chromosome 11q22-q23 (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — pathophysiology and risk factors for infection in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q39","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man comes to the physician because of 3 years of worsening unsteadiness and slurred speech. His father and paternal grandfather developed the same problem in their 40s, and his two younger siblings are unaffected so far. Examination shows a wide-based gait, dysmetria, dysdiadochokinesia, gaze-evoked nystagmus, and scanning dysarthria. Genetic testing shows an expanded CAG repeat in a spinocerebellar ataxia gene, and MRI of the brain shows cerebellar and pontine atrophy. What is the risk that each of his children will inherit the disorder?","choices":{"A":"25%","B":"75%","C":"67%","D":"50%","E":"10%"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The spinocerebellar ataxias are inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, so each child of an affected parent has a 50% risk. A 25% risk applies to children of two carriers of a recessive disorder. A 75% risk would require both parents to be affected heterozygotes, which his history does not suggest (general knowledge). A 67% figure is the carrier probability for an unaffected sibling in a recessive disorder (general knowledge). A 10% risk does not match any Mendelian pattern here. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, spinocerebellar ataxias). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cerebellum receives contralateral cortical input through the middle cerebellar peduncle and sends output through the deep nuclei and superior peduncle (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, cerebellum, p. 510). COMLEX: noncoding repeat expansions such as SCA10, SCA12, and SCA31 form a second mechanistic group of SCAs (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q40","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man dies 3 years after the onset of asymmetric weakness of his left hand. During his illness he developed atrophy and fasciculations of the hand and tongue muscles, brisk reflexes, and bilateral extensor plantar responses, with normal sensation and bladder control. Electromyography had shown active denervation in all four limbs, and nerve conduction studies showed normal sensory responses. At autopsy, the anterior spinal roots are thin and gray compared with the posterior roots. A myelin stain of the spinal cord would most likely show pallor in which region?","choices":{"A":"Lateral corticospinal tracts","B":"Posterior dorsal columns","C":"Spinocerebellar tracts","D":"Anterior spinothalamic tracts","E":"Medial longitudinal fasciculi"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Combined upper and lower motor neuron signs indicate ALS, in which loss of large myelinated axons in the lateral corticospinal tracts leaves them pale on myelin stains. The posterior dorsal columns carry sensory fibers spared in ALS. The spinocerebellar tracts degenerate in Friedreich ataxia. The anterior spinothalamic tracts carry crude touch and are not the pale region. The medial longitudinal fasciculi coordinate eye movements, which stay intact. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, microscopic hallmarks). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ALS gives lower motor neuron signs such as fasciculations and bulbar palsy with upper motor neuron signs such as spasticity, hyperreflexia, and pseudobulbar palsy, without sensory or bowel and bladder deficits (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, p. 544). COMLEX: anterior horn motor neurons are depleted from cervical to lumbosacral levels with extensive fibrillary astrogliosis (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — negative outcomes of impaired mobility and falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q41","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman with a 1-year history of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis is referred for cognitive testing because her husband says she has trouble planning meals and paying bills. She has progressive weakness of both legs and fasciculations but no hallucinations or personal history of stroke. Examination shows spastic paraparesis, hyperreflexia, and atrophy of the thigh muscles, and she recalls 3 of 3 words at 5 minutes. Her serum vitamin B12 is 480 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL), and her TSH is 2.2 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL). Testing is most likely to show deficits in which cognitive domain?","choices":{"A":"Visuospatial function","B":"Episodic memory","C":"Sensory perception","D":"Remote memory","E":"Executive function"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Up to half of patients with ALS have measurable frontotemporal cognitive or behavioral deficits, most often executive dysfunction, and about 15% meet criteria for frontotemporal dementia. Visuospatial function is not the domain usually affected. Episodic memory is intact on her recall testing. Sensory perception is spared in ALS. Remote memory is not the characteristic deficit. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, the ALS–frontotemporal spectrum). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prefrontal lesions give disinhibition, impulsivity, and impaired executive function as part of a frontal lobe syndrome (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: TDP-43 inclusions are present in sporadic, C9orf72, and TARDBP cases of ALS but absent in SOD1 and FUS cases (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — FTLD-TDP and the ALS spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q42","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 6 months of slurred speech, choking on liquids, and a hoarse voice. She has lost 5 kg, and her limbs feel normal. Examination shows a wasted tongue with visible fasciculations, weak palatal elevation, a brisk jaw jerk, and normal limb strength and sensation. Electromyography of the tongue shows active denervation, and MRI of the brain and cervical spine shows no structural lesion. Degeneration of which nucleus explains her tongue findings?","choices":{"A":"Nucleus ambiguus","B":"Hypoglossal nucleus","C":"Facial motor nucleus","D":"Motor trigeminal nucleus","E":"Abducens nucleus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bulbar ALS presents with dysarthria, dysphagia, hoarseness, and tongue fasciculations and atrophy from degeneration of motor cranial nerve nuclei; the tongue findings localize to the hypoglossal nucleus. The nucleus ambiguus explains her hoarseness and palatal weakness rather than the tongue. The facial motor nucleus supplies the muscles of facial expression (general knowledge). The motor trigeminal nucleus supplies the muscles of mastication. The abducens nucleus controls lateral gaze, which is spared. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, bulbar involvement). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a lower motor neuron lesion of CN XII makes the tongue deviate toward the side of the lesion (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, cranial nerve lesions, p. 546). COMLEX: autophagy receptor genes such as SQSTM1 and OPTN, and cytoskeletal genes such as TUBA4A and PFN1, also drive ALS (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — gastrointestinal disease in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":3,"stem":"A 76-year-old man with Parkinson disease walks stooped with short steps; once moving, his steps involuntarily speed up and he has trouble stopping. Which term describes this finding?","choices":{"A":"Circumduction","B":"Steppage gait","C":"Festination","D":"Waddling gait","E":"Scissor gait"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Festination is an involuntary, uncontrollable forward acceleration of steps in the parkinsonian gait. Circumduction is the outward swing of a spastic leg after stroke (general knowledge). Steppage gait is high stepping from foot drop (general knowledge). Waddling gait reflects proximal hip weakness (general knowledge). Scissor gait is crossing of spastic legs (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, parkinsonian festinating gait). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: athetosis is a slow, snakelike writhing movement, especially of the fingers, caused by basal ganglia lesions and seen in Huntington disease (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, movement disorders, p. 533). COMLEX: reduced or absent arm swing and freezing at thresholds also mark the parkinsonian gait (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q02","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old man has had asymmetric parkinsonism treated with levodopa for 9 years. Over the past year he has developed progressive cognitive decline. How is his dementia classified?","choices":{"A":"Corticobasal degeneration","B":"Frontotemporal dementia","C":"Dementia with Lewy bodies","D":"Normal pressure hydrocephalus","E":"Parkinson disease dementia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dementia that appears years after established asymmetric parkinsonism is Parkinson disease dementia, a late secondary complication on the Lewy body spectrum. Corticobasal degeneration is a levodopa-unresponsive tauopathy with strikingly asymmetric cortical signs. Frontotemporal dementia begins with behavior or language change rather than after years of parkinsonism (general knowledge). Dementia with Lewy bodies requires dementia before, with, or within 1 year of motor onset. Normal pressure hydrocephalus brings gait apraxia, incontinence, and cognitive decline without a long levodopa-responsive course (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, the one-year rule). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal pressure hydrocephalus in older adults gives the triad of gait apraxia, cognitive dysfunction, and urinary incontinence and is treated with CSF drainage or shunting (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, hydrocephalus, p. 536). COMLEX: dementia complicates long-term survival in Parkinson disease in up to 40% to 80% of patients (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has symmetric rigidity and bradykinesia that did not improve with levodopa, along with fainting on standing and urinary incontinence. MRI shows putaminal atrophy. Damage to which circuit best explains her parkinsonism?","choices":{"A":"Olivopontocerebellar atrophy","B":"Nigrostriatal Lewy pathology","C":"Subthalamic tau tangles","D":"Striatonigral degeneration","E":"Caudate GABAergic loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In multiple system atrophy, striatonigral degeneration produces prominent symmetric parkinsonism that does not respond to levodopa, and autonomic failure explains her fainting and incontinence. Olivopontocerebellar atrophy produces the cerebellar form with ataxia. Nigrostriatal Lewy pathology is the levodopa-responsive lesion of Parkinson disease. Subthalamic tau tangles occur in progressive supranuclear palsy, which has vertical gaze palsy rather than autonomic failure. Caudate GABAergic loss occurs in Huntington disease. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, triad of affected circuits in MSA). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the nigrostriatal pathway is one of the dopaminergic pathways blocked by antipsychotics, which is why they cause extrapyramidal symptoms (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, dopaminergic pathways, p. 509). COMLEX: olivopontocerebellar atrophy in multiple system atrophy brings ataxia, dysmetria, and kinetic tremor (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — negative outcomes of impaired mobility and falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q04","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with Huntington disease is brought in by his sister, who worries about his severe depression. Compared with the general population, his risk of suicide is about what?","choices":{"A":"Twice the rate","B":"Half the rate","C":"The same rate","D":"Ten times the rate","E":"Fifty times the rate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Suicide in Huntington disease occurs at about twice the rate of the general population, alongside early personality change, irritability, depression, and impulsive aggression. Half the rate understates the risk. The same rate ignores the excess risk. Ten times and fifty times the rate overstate it. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, neuropsychiatric and behavioral disturbances). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tetrabenazine and deutetrabenazine are used to treat the chorea of Huntington disease (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, neurodegenerative disease therapy, p. 564). COMLEX: higher executive function, planning, and memory decline insidiously and progress to severe global dementia (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl with Friedreich ataxia has increased thirst and urination. Which metabolic disorder develops in up to one-quarter of patients with her disease?","choices":{"A":"Hypothyroidism","B":"Diabetes mellitus","C":"Hyperparathyroidism","D":"Adrenal insufficiency","E":"Hemochromatosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diabetes mellitus accompanies Friedreich ataxia in up to 25% of patients. Hypothyroidism is not a recognized systemic complication of the disease (general knowledge). Hyperparathyroidism is not part of it either (general knowledge). Adrenal insufficiency is not associated with it (general knowledge). Hemochromatosis is systemic iron overload, unlike the intramitochondrial iron build-up of frataxin deficiency (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, systemic complications of Friedreich ataxia). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Friedreich ataxia is an autosomal recessive GAA repeat disorder on chromosome 9 that impairs the iron-binding mitochondrial protein frataxin and presents in childhood with kyphoscoliosis (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, Friedreich ataxia, p. 545). COMLEX: hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with arrhythmias and heart failure accounts for most deaths before age 40 to 50 (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — changes in the cardiovascular system with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q06","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":3,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis now has weak intercostal and diaphragmatic muscles. Which complication most often ends the course of his disease?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac arrhythmia","B":"Hepatic encephalopathy","C":"Respiratory failure","D":"Uremic renal failure","E":"Pressure ulcer sepsis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paralysis of the intercostal and diaphragmatic muscles in ALS leads to respiratory failure, often with recurrent aspiration pneumonia. Cardiac arrhythmia is not the usual terminal event in ALS (general knowledge). Hepatic encephalopathy is unrelated to motor neuron disease (general knowledge). Uremic renal failure is not a feature of ALS (general knowledge). Pressure ulcer sepsis is uncommon because sensation is preserved (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, terminal complications). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lower motor neuron injury lowers muscle mass, tone, and reflexes and causes fasciculations, whereas upper motor neuron injury raises tone and reflexes (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, effects of motor neuron injury, p. 543). COMLEX: SOD1 mutations, in copper-zinc superoxide dismutase on chromosome 21, account for about 20% of familial ALS (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — pulmonary conditions affecting elderly individuals","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":8,"title":"SDL 8 — Hypertension in the Elderly","questions":[{"id":"sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"A formal diagnosis of hypertension requires averaging properly taken readings from at least how many clinical encounters?","choices":{"A":"One clinic visit","B":"Three separate visits","C":"Two separate visits","D":"Four separate visits","E":"Six separate visits"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diagnosis rests on the average of two or more properly taken readings obtained on two or more separate visits. One clinic visit leaves a transient rise indistinguishable from a sustained one. Three separate visits exceed the stated minimum. Four separate visits also exceed it. Six separate visits exceed it further. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, confirmation and measurement requirements). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: thoracic aortic aneurysm is linked to cystic medial degeneration, and its risk factors include hypertension, a bicuspid aortic valve, and connective tissue disease (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, aortic aneurysm, p. 306). COMLEX: hypertension is defined by sustained arterial pressure elevations measured across multiple clinical encounters (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"Heavily calcified, rigid peripheral arteries that resist compression by a standard cuff produce falsely high readings. What is this phenomenon called?","choices":{"A":"Pseudohypertension","B":"White-coat hypertension","C":"Labile hypertension","D":"Auscultatory gap","E":"Resistant hypertension"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pseudohypertension is an artificially raised cuff reading caused by calcified, rigid arteries that resist compression by the cuff. White-coat hypertension is an anxiety-driven rise in clinic readings. Labile hypertension describes wide swings in pressure over time (general knowledge). An auscultatory gap leads to systolic pressure being underestimated rather than overestimated. Resistant hypertension is a true elevation that persists despite multidrug therapy. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, diagnostic pitfalls in older adults). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abdominal or back pain from an aortic aneurysm can signal leaking, dissection, or impending rupture (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, aortic aneurysm, p. 306). COMLEX: the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline sorts adult blood pressure into four categories, which the 2025 AHA/ACC guideline that replaced it kept unchanged (SDL 8 notes; AHA newsroom, Aug 14 2025).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"During a manual blood pressure measurement, at what rate should the cuff be deflated?","choices":{"A":"5 mm Hg per second","B":"10 mm Hg per second","C":"2 mm Hg per minute","D":"2 mm Hg per second","E":"20 mm Hg per second"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Proper technique deflates the cuff at 2 mm Hg per second. A rate of 5 mm Hg per second is too fast to time the first Korotkoff sound precisely (general knowledge). A rate of 10 mm Hg per second overshoots the systolic point further (general knowledge). A rate of 2 mm Hg per minute prolongs arterial occlusion and congests the arm (general knowledge). A rate of 20 mm Hg per second makes both readings unreliable (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, proper measurement technique). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: raised intracranial pressure sets off the Cushing reflex, combining hypertension with bradycardia and depressed breathing (First Aid Cardiovascular—Physiology, baroreceptors and chemoreceptors, p. 299). COMLEX: the patient should rest quietly for at least 5 minutes with the arm supported at heart level before measurement (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":1,"stem":"Long-standing hypertension narrows the deep penetrating arteries that supply the basal ganglia, hemispheric white matter, and brainstem through which vessel-wall change?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrosis","B":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","C":"Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","D":"Monckeberg medial calcification","E":"Amyloid angiopathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Long-standing hypertension causes hyaline arteriolosclerosis of these deep penetrating arteries, and thrombotic occlusion of the narrowed vessels produces lacunar infarcts. Fibrinoid necrosis marks acute arteriolar injury in a hypertensive emergency (general knowledge). Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis is onion-skin thickening seen in severe hypertension (general knowledge). Monckeberg medial calcification affects the media of medium-sized arteries without narrowing the lumen (general knowledge). Amyloid angiopathy deposits amyloid in cortical vessel walls (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, ischemic stroke and lacunar infarctions). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in hypertension or diabetes, plasma proteins leak into the subendothelium and thicken small-vessel walls, producing hyaline arteriolosclerosis (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, arteriolosclerosis, p. 306). COMLEX: the deep penetrating arteries affected by small-vessel disease measure about 40 to 900 µm in diameter (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 1 — stable ischemic heart disease and acute coronary syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":1,"stem":"Hypertension accounts for what share of deep intraparenchymal brain hemorrhages?","choices":{"A":"Over half","B":"One-tenth","C":"One-fifth","D":"One-quarter","E":"One-third"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypertension causes over half of deep parenchymal hemorrhages, which arise when rigid arteriolosclerotic vessels rupture. One-tenth understates the share. One-fifth also understates it. One-quarter understates it as well. One-third remains too low. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, intraparenchymal brain hemorrhage). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: systolic dysfunction, or heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, usually follows ischemia, myocardial infarction, or dilated cardiomyopathy (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, heart failure, p. 316). COMLEX: midlife hypertension is a documented risk factor for Alzheimer disease (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 1 — stable ischemic heart disease and acute coronary syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":1,"stem":"Which funduscopic finding reflects damage to the retinal microcirculation from long-standing hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Cherry-red spot","B":"Drusen deposits","C":"Arteriovenous nicking","D":"Bone spicule pigment","E":"Retinal neovascularization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Arteriovenous nicking is a hallmark of hypertensive retinopathy, along with arteriolar narrowing, microaneurysms, flame hemorrhages, and exudates. A cherry-red spot appears with central retinal artery occlusion (general knowledge). Drusen deposits signal age-related macular degeneration (general knowledge). Bone spicule pigment marks retinitis pigmentosa (general knowledge). Retinal neovascularization defines proliferative diabetic retinopathy (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, hypertensive retinopathy). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: papilledema in a patient with hypertensive retinopathy marks a hypertensive emergency that calls for immediate lowering of pressure (First Aid Neurology—Ophthalmology, hypertensive retinopathy, p. 552). COMLEX: high intravascular pressure damages the retinal microcirculation and leads to progressive loss of vision (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 1 — stable ischemic heart disease and acute coronary syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"Primary (essential) hypertension accounts for about what share of all cases of hypertension?","choices":{"A":"50% to 55%","B":"60% to 65%","C":"70% to 75%","D":"80% to 85%","E":"90% to 95%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Primary hypertension makes up about 90% to 95% of cases and has no single identifiable cause. A share of 50% to 55% is far too low. A share of 60% to 65% is also too low. A share of 70% to 75% still understates it. A share of 80% to 85% remains below the true figure. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, primary hypertension). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 90% of hypertension is primary and tied to cardiac output or total peripheral resistance, and most of the remainder is secondary to renal or renovascular disease, primary hyperaldosteronism, or obstructive sleep apnea (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, hypertension, p. 304). COMLEX: primary hypertension arises from polygenic susceptibility interacting with age, dietary sodium, inactivity, obesity, and insulin resistance (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — obstacles to successful hypertension treatment in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"The label essential hypertension arose from which earlier belief about raised blood pressure?","choices":{"A":"Passed down in families","B":"Needed to perfuse organs","C":"Caused by excess sodium","D":"Benign in older adults","E":"Driven by kidney disease"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Clinicians once believed that raised pressure was a required compensation to guarantee adequate perfusion of vital organs, hence the term essential. Passed down in families describes heritability rather than the origin of the term (general knowledge). Caused by excess sodium names one lifestyle risk factor. Benign in older adults reflects a later, discarded idea of benign hypertension (general knowledge). Driven by kidney disease describes a secondary form. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, historical derivation). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cushing syndrome pairs hypertension with hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, truncal obesity, osteoporosis, and easy bruising (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, Cushing syndrome, p. 352). COMLEX: secondary hypertension is directly attributable to an identifiable disorder or an exogenous chemical substance (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — obstacles to successful hypertension treatment in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"Resistant hypertension is failure to reach control despite optimal doses of three antihypertensive agents that must include which drug class?","choices":{"A":"Diuretic","B":"Nitrate","C":"Vasodilator","D":"Beta-blocker","E":"Alpha-blocker"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The definition of resistant hypertension requires that one of the three optimally dosed agents be a diuretic. A nitrate is not part of the definition (general knowledge). A direct vasodilator is not required (general knowledge). A beta-blocker is not a required component (general knowledge). An alpha-blocker is not required either (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, clinical suspicion of secondary hypertension). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism from bilateral adrenal hyperplasia or an aldosterone-secreting adenoma (Conn syndrome) raises aldosterone, suppresses renin, and leaves hypertension resistant to treatment (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, hyperaldosteronism, p. 354). COMLEX: endocrine causes of secondary hypertension are screened with TSH for thyroid disease and with serum calcium and PTH for primary hyperparathyroidism (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — obstacles to successful hypertension treatment in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":1,"stem":"In stiff, aging central arteries, reflected pressure waves return to the aorta at what point in the cardiac cycle?","choices":{"A":"During diastole","B":"At end-diastole","C":"During atrial kick","D":"Between heartbeats","E":"During systole"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In rigid aging arteries, reflected waves travel fast and return prematurely during systole, augmenting systolic pressure while diastolic pressure falls. Return during diastole is the pattern of compliant young arteries, where it boosts coronary perfusion. At end-diastole, no newly ejected wave has yet been reflected (general knowledge). Atrial contraction does not time the return of arterial reflected waves (general knowledge). Reflected waves come from each ejection, so they do not arrive between heartbeats (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, premature reflected waves). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac tamponade, aortic stenosis, cardiogenic shock, and advanced heart failure narrow the pulse pressure (First Aid Cardiovascular—Physiology, pulse pressure, p. 290). COMLEX: with loss of the Windkessel effect, stiff central arteries no longer distend in systole and recoil in diastole (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — central elastic artery changes and systolic hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":1,"stem":"Age-related nephrosclerosis lowers plasma renin activity. The resulting low-renin hypertension responds well to thiazides and to which other drug class?","choices":{"A":"Angiotensin receptor blockers","B":"Direct renin inhibitors","C":"Alpha-1 receptor blockers","D":"Calcium channel blockers","E":"Beta-adrenergic blockers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Low-renin hypertension in older adults responds well to calcium channel blockers along with thiazides. Angiotensin receptor blockers used alone act on a renin-angiotensin system that is less active here. Direct renin inhibitors target renin, which is already low (general knowledge). Alpha-1 receptor blockers are not favored for low-renin hypertension (general knowledge). Beta-adrenergic blockers lower renin release, which adds little when renin is already low (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, altered neurohormonal regulation). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazide diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, and calcium channel blockers are the listed agents for primary hypertension (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hypertension treatment, p. 321). COMLEX: aging arteries are less sensitive to beta-adrenergic stimulation, which limits vasodilatory responsiveness (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — central elastic artery changes and systolic hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":1,"stem":"Hypertension affects more than 70% to 80% of people older than what age?","choices":{"A":"45 years","B":"75 years","C":"55 years","D":"65 years","E":"95 years"},"correct":"B","explanation":"More than 70% to 80% of people older than 75 years have hypertension. An age of 45 years is far too young for that prevalence. An age of 55 years is also too young. An age of 65 years still sets the cutoff too low. An age of 95 years sets it too high. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, advancing chronological age). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertension risk rises with age, obesity, diabetes, inactivity, high sodium intake, heavy alcohol use, smoking, and family history, and incidence is highest in Black populations (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, hypertension, p. 304). COMLEX: smoking acts synergistically with high blood pressure to multiply cardiovascular, stroke, and mortality risk (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — central elastic artery changes and systolic hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman comes to the physician for a routine visit. She has had treated hypertension for 20 years and has no symptoms. The nurse inflated the cuff to 150 mm Hg and recorded 138/72 mm Hg; when the physician inflates it to 200 mm Hg, faint sounds begin at 172 mm Hg, vanish at 154 mm Hg, and return at 138 mm Hg. Carotid ultrasonography last year showed calcified plaque with 40% narrowing of the left internal carotid artery (reference: <50% is not hemodynamically significant). Which step would have prevented the nurse's error?","choices":{"A":"Deflating the cuff faster","B":"Using a narrower cuff","C":"Palpating the pulse first","D":"Measuring while standing","E":"Holding the arm lower"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Her readings show an auscultatory gap from arterial stiffness, and checking the pulse obliteration pressure by palpation before auscultating sets the inflation high enough to avoid underestimating systolic pressure. Deflating the cuff faster makes the sounds easier to miss (general knowledge). Using a narrower cuff falsely raises readings and does not expose the gap (general knowledge). Measuring while standing tests for orthostatic change rather than the gap (general knowledge). Holding the arm below heart level falsely raises readings (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, auscultatory gap). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carotid sinus hypersensitivity, set off by carotid massage, shaving, or a tight collar, slows AV conduction and can cause syncope, and it is exaggerated by atherosclerosis and older age (First Aid Cardiovascular—Physiology, baroreceptors and chemoreceptors, p. 299). COMLEX: an appropriately sized bladder cuff should cover 80% of the upper arm (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — central elastic artery changes and systolic hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old man comes for a scheduled visit after running out of his antihypertensive medications 10 days ago. He has no headache, chest pain, dyspnea, vision change, or weakness. His blood pressure is 192/112 mm Hg in both arms on repeated readings, and neurologic and funduscopic examinations are normal. Serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL), troponin I is <0.01 ng/mL (reference: <0.04 ng/mL), and the ECG shows left ventricular hypertrophy without acute changes. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Oral titration over weeks","B":"Intravenous labetalol drip","C":"Inpatient admission today","D":"Same-day aortic imaging","E":"Emergency lumbar puncture"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe asymptomatic hypertension, a systolic pressure of 180 mm Hg or more or a diastolic pressure of 110 mm Hg or more without acute target-organ injury, can be managed safely as an outpatient with gradual oral adjustments over days to weeks. An intravenous labetalol drip is parenteral therapy reserved for a hypertensive emergency (general knowledge). Inpatient admission today is needed when acute target-organ injury is present, which he lacks. Same-day aortic imaging is unwarranted without chest or back pain and with equal arm pressures (general knowledge). Emergency lumbar puncture evaluates subarachnoid hemorrhage, which his normal examination and absent headache do not suggest (general knowledge). The notes and Ham's set this cutoff at 180/110 mm Hg; the ACC/AHA cutoff of more than 180 and/or more than 120 mm Hg is general knowledge, and First Aid sets hypertensive urgency at ≥ 180/≥ 120 mm Hg (First Aid p. 304). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, severe elevations); Ham's Ch. 36 (severe asymptomatic hypertension). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertensive urgency is severe hypertension, ≥ 180/≥ 120 mm Hg, with no acute end-organ damage (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, hypertension, p. 304). COMLEX: a hypertensive emergency mandates emergent inpatient parenteral therapy (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman comes for a wellness visit. Properly taken readings at her last two visits average 134/78 mm Hg and 136/76 mm Hg. She has no diabetes, kidney disease, or cardiovascular disease, and her serum creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL). Under the 2017 ACC/AHA categories, how is her blood pressure classified?","choices":{"A":"Normal blood pressure","B":"Elevated blood pressure","C":"Stage 2 hypertension","D":"Severe hypertension","E":"Stage 1 hypertension"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Under the 2017 ACC/AHA categories, a systolic pressure of 130 to 139 mm Hg or a diastolic pressure of 80 to 89 mm Hg is stage 1 hypertension, and the 2025 AHA/ACC guideline that replaced the 2017 one kept the same categories. Normal blood pressure requires a systolic pressure below 120 mm Hg and a diastolic pressure below 80 mm Hg. Elevated blood pressure is a systolic pressure of 120 to 129 mm Hg with a diastolic pressure below 80 mm Hg. Stage 2 hypertension starts at 140 mm Hg systolic or 90 mm Hg diastolic. Severe hypertension is a systolic pressure of 180 mm Hg or more or a diastolic pressure of 110 mm Hg or more in the notes and Ham's; the ACC/AHA cutoff of more than 180 and/or more than 120 mm Hg is general knowledge, and First Aid sets hypertensive urgency at ≥ 180/≥ 120 mm Hg (First Aid p. 304). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, diagnostic criteria and staging; severe elevations); Ham's Ch. 36 (Table 36.2, BP categories); 2025 AHA/ACC HBP guideline (AHA newsroom, Aug 14 2025; stage categories unchanged; the severe cutoff is not in the release). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertension is a persistent systolic pressure of 130 mm Hg or more and/or a diastolic pressure of 80 mm Hg or more (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, hypertension, p. 304). COMLEX: JNC 8 advised starting drug therapy at 140/90 mm Hg or higher before age 60 years but at a systolic pressure of 150 mm Hg or higher from age 60 years on, and ambulatory readings predict cardiovascular mortality more strongly than office readings (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 3 hours after the sudden onset of weakness of his right face, arm, and leg. He has had poorly controlled hypertension for 25 years. Examination shows right hemiparesis with no aphasia, visual field loss, neglect, or sensory deficit. His blood pressure is 178/96 mm Hg, serum glucose is 96 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and MRI shows an 8-mm infarct in the posterior limb of the left internal capsule. Rupture of the same diseased vessels would most likely cause bleeding in which location?","choices":{"A":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage","B":"Putaminal hemorrhage","C":"Epidural hematoma","D":"Subdural hematoma","E":"Lobar amyloid hemorrhage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"His pure motor lacunar infarct reflects hypertensive disease of deep penetrating arteries, and rupture of these rigid vessels causes deep parenchymal bleeding, most often in the putamen. Subarachnoid hemorrhage usually follows a ruptured saccular aneurysm (general knowledge). Epidural hematoma results from a torn middle meningeal artery after trauma (general knowledge). Subdural hematoma results from torn bridging veins (general knowledge). Lobar amyloid hemorrhage arises from amyloid in cortical vessels rather than deep penetrating arteries (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, ischemic stroke and intraparenchymal hemorrhage). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertensive bleeds from Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms of the lenticulostriate vessels favor the putamen and globus pallidus and next affect the internal capsule, thalamus, pons, and cerebellum (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, intracranial hemorrhage, p. 528). COMLEX: thrombotic occlusion of narrowed deep arterioles leaves small cavitary lacunar infarcts (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 4 months of exertional dyspnea and ankle swelling. She has had hypertension for 30 years, often poorly controlled. Examination shows bibasilar crackles, an S4 gallop, and 1+ pitting ankle edema. Echocardiography shows concentric left ventricular hypertrophy with an ejection fraction of 62% (reference: 52–72%), and serum BNP is 420 pg/mL (reference: <100 pg/mL). Which abnormality most directly explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Loss of myofibrillar elements","B":"Dilated ventricular chamber","C":"Mitral regurgitant volume","D":"Impaired diastolic filling","E":"Pericardial constriction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hypertension produces heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, in which a stiff, thickened ventricle impairs diastolic filling. Loss of myofibrillar elements follows prolonged overload and leads to a reduced ejection fraction, which she does not have. A dilated ventricular chamber accompanies systolic failure rather than concentric hypertrophy (general knowledge). Mitral regurgitant volume would appear on echocardiography as a regurgitant jet (general knowledge). Pericardial constriction does not thicken the ventricular wall (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, concentric LVH and heart failure). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diastolic dysfunction, or heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, lowers ventricular compliance and often stems from myocardial hypertrophy (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, heart failure, p. 316). COMLEX: sustained afterload makes myocardial cells enlarge, thickening the left ventricular wall as a compensatory adaptation (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — effects of diastolic dysfunction in the aging heart","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man comes for follow-up of chronic kidney disease. He has had hypertension for 30 years and does not have diabetes. His estimated GFR has fallen from 62 to 38 mL/min/1.73 m² over 6 years (reference: >60 mL/min/1.73 m²), and the urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio is 140 mg/g (reference: <30 mg/g). Ultrasonography shows small, symmetric kidneys with finely granular cortical surfaces, and biopsy shows hyaline thickening of the afferent arterioles. Through which mechanism does this arteriolar lesion lower his GFR?","choices":{"A":"Immune complex deposition","B":"Podocyte foot effacement","C":"Tubular crystal obstruction","D":"Glomerular basement splitting","E":"Reduced glomerular perfusion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyaline thickening of afferent arterioles and interlobular arteries compromises glomerular perfusion, driving loss of functioning nephrons and a falling GFR. Immune complex deposition defines immune-mediated glomerulonephritis (general knowledge). Podocyte foot effacement underlies nephrotic syndromes such as minimal change disease (general knowledge). Tubular crystal obstruction follows crystal-forming drugs or urate (general knowledge). Glomerular basement splitting characterizes Alport syndrome (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, nephrosclerosis). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertension is the strongest risk factor for aortic dissection, a longitudinal intimal tear that creates a false lumen (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, aortic dissection, p. 307). COMLEX: nephrosclerosis speeds chronic kidney disease and its progression toward end-stage renal disease (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — factors influencing organ functional performance with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man comes for follow-up 1 week after lisinopril was added for blood pressure above goal on amlodipine and chlorthalidone. He has a 50-pack-year smoking history and claudication of both calves. His blood pressure is 176/88 mm Hg, there is a periumbilical bruit, and pedal pulses are diminished. Serum creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 1.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL), and potassium is 4.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). If the suspected lesion is confirmed, how does medical therapy compare with revascularization?","choices":{"A":"Worse BP control","B":"More cardiac deaths","C":"Faster renal decline","D":"Comparable outcomes","E":"More hospitalizations"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A creatinine rise after starting an ACE inhibitor in an older smoker with vascular disease suggests atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis, since a rise of 0.5 to 1.0 mg/dL or more raises that suspicion, and medical management is at least as effective as surgical revascularization. Worse BP control is not seen with medical therapy in this comparison. More cardiac deaths do not follow medical management. Faster renal decline is not expected with medical therapy. More hospitalizations are not a recognized drawback of medical therapy (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, renovascular disease and the creatinine rise after ACE inhibitor or ARB); Ham's Ch. 36 (renal artery stenosis, creatinine rise of 0.5 to 1 mg/dL or more). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in renovascular disease, renal ischemia increases renin release, and the angiotensin II and aldosterone that follow raise blood pressure (First Aid Renal—Pathology, renovascular disease, p. 623). COMLEX: glomerulonephritis and advanced chronic kidney disease are the renal parenchymal causes of secondary hypertension (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — stable ischemic heart disease and acute coronary syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man comes to the physician because his blood pressure remains high despite full doses of amlodipine, lisinopril, and chlorthalidone. His wife reports loud snoring with pauses in his breathing, and he falls asleep while reading. His BMI is 36 kg/m², neck circumference is 46 cm, and blood pressure is 162/94 mm Hg. Serum potassium is 4.3 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), TSH is 2.1 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL), and calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL). Which mechanism most likely sustains his daytime hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Mineralocorticoid excess","B":"Sympathetic overactivation","C":"Catecholamine-secreting tumor","D":"Renal artery narrowing","E":"Glucocorticoid excess"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Obstructive sleep apnea causes chronic nocturnal hypoxemia and sympathetic overactivation that sustain daytime hypertension. Mineralocorticoid excess often lowers serum potassium, which is normal here (general knowledge). A catecholamine-secreting tumor causes episodic headaches, sweating, and palpitations (general knowledge). Renal artery narrowing would be suggested by a creatinine rise after an ACE inhibitor. Glucocorticoid excess brings truncal obesity with striae and easy bruising (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, obstructive sleep apnea). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstructive sleep apnea is respiratory effort against a blocked airway, linked to obesity, loud snoring, and daytime sleepiness, and treated with weight loss and CPAP (First Aid Respiratory—Pathology, obstructive sleep apnea, p. 697). COMLEX: secondary hypertension is unlikely to appear as a new problem in older adults (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — pulmonary conditions affecting elderly individuals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman comes for a blood pressure check. Her pressure had been 128/76 mm Hg on one antihypertensive drug for 5 years, but it is now 156/88 mm Hg on two visits. Six weeks ago she began taking naproxen twice daily for knee osteoarthritis, and she drinks one glass of wine weekly. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL), potassium is 4.4 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and TSH is 1.8 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL). Which step is most likely to restore her previous control?","choices":{"A":"Stopping the NSAID","B":"Adding a thiazide","C":"Adding a beta-blocker","D":"Reducing salt intake","E":"Ordering renal imaging"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs raise blood pressure, so stopping naproxen removes the cause of her loss of control. Adding a thiazide treats the rise but leaves its cause in place (general knowledge). Adding a beta-blocker also adds a drug without removing the cause (general knowledge). Reducing salt intake helps but does not address the new drug (general knowledge). Ordering renal imaging is unwarranted with a normal creatinine and an evident drug cause (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, medication-induced and exogenous substances). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperparathyroidism, usually from an adenoma or hyperplasia, causes hypercalcemia, kidney stones, and hypophosphatemia and is often asymptomatic (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, hyperparathyroidism, p. 349). COMLEX: systemic corticosteroids, sympathomimetic decongestants, and excess alcohol also raise blood pressure (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — risk factors for adverse drug reactions in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman comes for a routine visit. She has no symptoms, does not have diabetes, and takes no medications. Her blood pressure is 172/68 mm Hg in both arms, a pulse pressure of 104 mm Hg. Carotid-femoral pulse wave velocity is 14 m/s (reference: <10 m/s), and echocardiography shows normal valves. Which structural change in her aortic wall best explains this pressure pattern?","choices":{"A":"Smooth muscle hyperplasia","B":"Fragmented elastin fibers","C":"Intimal lipid streaks","D":"Intimal fibrin thrombi","E":"Adventitial lymphocytes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Isolated systolic hypertension, a systolic pressure of 140 mm Hg or more with a diastolic pressure below 90 mm Hg, with a wide pulse pressure reflects a stiffer aorta, in which elastin degenerates, frays, and fragments while collagen accumulates and cross-links. The ACC/AHA guideline instead defines isolated systolic hypertension as a systolic pressure of 130 mm Hg or more with a diastolic pressure below 80 mm Hg (general knowledge); the notes' definition is used here. Smooth muscle hyperplasia narrows small arterioles in severe hypertension rather than stiffening the aorta (general knowledge). Intimal lipid streaks are early atherosclerosis and do not raise pulse wave velocity (general knowledge). Intimal fibrin thrombi form on disrupted plaques and occlude vessels (general knowledge). Adventitial lymphocytes suggest aortitis (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, ISH definition; vascular remodeling and arterial stiffening). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: elastin gets its stretch from extracellular cross-linking by lysyl oxidase and is broken down by elastase, which α1-antitrypsin normally inhibits (First Aid Biochemistry—Cellular, elastin, p. 50). COMLEX: a widened pulse pressure predicts coronary disease and stroke better than mean arterial or diastolic pressure (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — central elastic artery changes and systolic hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man is enrolled in a vascular aging study. He has hypertension, does not have diabetes, and does not smoke. Brachial ultrasonography shows flow-mediated dilation of 2% (reference: >6%), while dilation after sublingual nitroglycerin is normal. Fasting glucose is 88 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and LDL cholesterol is 96 mg/dL (reference: <100 mg/dL). Reduced activity of which enzyme most likely accounts for his impaired flow-mediated response?","choices":{"A":"Endothelial NO synthase","B":"Cyclooxygenase-1","C":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme","D":"Soluble guanylyl cyclase","E":"Prostacyclin synthase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aging arteries have diminished endothelial nitric oxide synthase activity and less bioavailable nitric oxide, impairing flow-mediated vasodilation. Cyclooxygenase-1 makes thromboxane and prostaglandins rather than the flow-mediated nitric oxide signal (general knowledge). Angiotensin-converting enzyme produces a vasoconstrictor and does not mediate this dilation (general knowledge). Soluble guanylyl cyclase must be intact because nitroglycerin still dilates the artery (general knowledge). Prostacyclin synthase does not explain a selective loss of flow-mediated dilation (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, endothelial dysfunction). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nitric oxide relaxes smooth muscle, including the lower esophageal sphincter (First Aid Gastrointestinal—Physiology, gastrointestinal regulatory substances, p. 378). COMLEX: reactive oxygen species add oxidative injury that further impairs endothelium-dependent vasodilation (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — proposed causes of cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 2 months of light-headedness when she gets out of bed. She has hypertension treated with amlodipine and takes no other medications. Supine blood pressure is 158/80 mm Hg with a pulse of 72/min; after 3 minutes of standing, it is 128/72 mm Hg with a pulse of 76/min. Hemoglobin is 13.2 g/dL (reference range: 12–16 g/dL), and serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L). Which age-related change most likely explains her findings?","choices":{"A":"Plasma volume expansion","B":"Excess renin secretion","C":"Blunted baroreflex","D":"Increased beta sensitivity","E":"Heightened alpha-1 tone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Reduced baroreceptor reflex sensitivity blunts the heart-rate and vascular response to standing, causing orthostatic hypotension and labile pressures. Plasma volume expansion would protect against an orthostatic fall (general knowledge). Excess renin secretion runs counter to the age-related decline in renin. Increased beta sensitivity runs counter to the age-related loss of beta-adrenergic responsiveness. Heightened alpha-1 tone would support standing pressure (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, altered neurohormonal regulation). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pheochromocytoma, the most common adrenal medullary tumor in adults, arises from neural crest chromaffin cells and causes episodic hypertension (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, pheochromocytoma, p. 355). COMLEX: chronic emotional stress significantly increases systemic vascular resistance (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has clinic readings near 126/78 mm Hg, but his validated home monitor averages 148/92 mm Hg over 2 weeks. What is this pattern called?","choices":{"A":"White-coat hypertension","B":"Orthostatic hypotension","C":"Labile hypertension","D":"Masked hypertension","E":"Resistant hypertension"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Masked hypertension is a nonhypertensive clinic reading (<130/80 mm Hg) that hides a higher usual pressure, which out-of-office monitoring reveals. White-coat hypertension is the reverse, with raised clinic readings and normal home readings. Orthostatic hypotension is a fall in pressure on standing rather than a clinic-home mismatch (general knowledge). Labile hypertension describes wide swings over time (general knowledge). Resistant hypertension is uncontrolled pressure despite multidrug therapy. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, out-of-office confirmation; BP categories). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tobacco smoking is the strongest risk factor for abdominal aortic aneurysm, which involves transmural inflammation and breakdown of extracellular matrix (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, aortic aneurysm, p. 306). COMLEX: ambulatory and home monitoring are the out-of-office methods used to confirm the diagnosis (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has an irregularly irregular pulse, and her ECG shows no P waves. Which hypertension-driven change most promotes this rhythm?","choices":{"A":"Aortic root dilation","B":"Mitral valve prolapse","C":"Left atrial dilation","D":"Sinus node fibrosis","E":"Pericardial effusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"High ventricular filling pressures dilate the left atrium and promote atrial hypertrophy and fibrosis, raising the incidence of atrial fibrillation. Aortic root dilation affects the aortic valve rather than atrial rhythm (general knowledge). Mitral valve prolapse is a degenerative valve disorder unrelated to hypertension (general knowledge). Sinus node fibrosis causes bradyarrhythmias such as sick sinus syndrome (general knowledge). Pericardial effusion lowers QRS voltage rather than driving atrial fibrillation (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, atrial remodeling and arrhythmias). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lacunar infarcts of the lenticulostriate arteries cause pure motor strokes without cortical signs and stem from lipohyalinosis in unmanaged hypertension (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, effects of strokes, p. 526). COMLEX: hypertension accelerates coronary atherosclerosis, predisposing to stable angina and acute coronary syndromes (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — risk factors for atrial fibrillation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with long-standing hypertension reports gradually worsening difficulty maintaining erections, which his physician attributes to his hypertension. Which mechanism links the two?","choices":{"A":"Low testosterone","B":"Endothelial injury","C":"Prolactin excess","D":"Pelvic nerve injury","E":"Psychogenic anxiety"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Endothelial injury and arterial insufficiency in the penile vascular bed explain the high prevalence of erectile dysfunction with hypertension. Low testosterone is a hormonal cause unrelated to his hypertension (general knowledge). Prolactin excess suppresses gonadotropins and is not a vascular effect (general knowledge). Pelvic nerve injury follows pelvic surgery or radiation (general knowledge). Psychogenic anxiety is not a vascular consequence of hypertension (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, peripheral vasculature and sexual function). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: β-blocker adverse effects include erectile dysfunction, bradycardia, AV block, and masked hypoglycemia (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, β-blockers, p. 244). COMLEX: systemic arterial remodeling predisposes to peripheral arterial disease, claudication, and diminished distal pulses (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — antihypertensive classes, mechanisms, and adverse effects","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":3,"stem":"An 80-year-old man whose blood pressure was controlled on one drug for 15 years has a sharp rise to 184/96 mm Hg over 2 months despite confirmed adherence. What does this change most suggest?","choices":{"A":"Secondary cause","B":"White-coat effect","C":"Normal aging","D":"Salt sensitivity","E":"Cuff artifact"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A sudden, acute rise in pressure after years of stable control should prompt a search for a secondary cause. A white-coat effect raises clinic readings from the start rather than producing a new sustained rise (general knowledge). Normal aging raises pressure gradually over years. Salt sensitivity does not explain an abrupt change after years of control (general knowledge). Cuff artifact such as pseudohypertension is a long-standing measurement problem rather than a new rise. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, clinical suspicion of secondary hypertension). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, with onion-skin proliferation of smooth muscle cells, accompanies severe hypertension (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, arteriolosclerosis, p. 306). COMLEX: atherosclerotic narrowing of the renal arteries is the common renovascular cause in older adults (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — obstacles to successful hypertension treatment in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":3,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman notes that her home blood pressure rises by about 15 mm Hg after salty holiday meals. Which age-related change best explains this response?","choices":{"A":"Increased aldosterone secretion","B":"Enhanced intestinal sodium uptake","C":"Elevated plasma renin activity","D":"Reduced renal sodium handling","E":"Heightened thirst and intake"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Aging kidneys have a diminished capacity to handle sodium loads, so older adults show pronounced salt-sensitive rises in pressure. Increased aldosterone secretion is not an age-related change (general knowledge). Enhanced intestinal sodium uptake does not develop with age (general knowledge). Elevated plasma renin activity runs counter to the age-related fall in renin. Heightened thirst and intake runs counter to the blunted thirst of older adults (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, dietary sodium sensitivity). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: fibromuscular dysplasia gives the renal artery a string-of-beads look and usually affects adult women (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, hypertension, p. 304). COMLEX: adiposity expands intravascular volume, increases sympathetic outflow, and drives hyperinsulinemia (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — nonpharmacologic treatment of hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old Black man asks how the stroke risk of Black adults in the United States compares with that of White adults. About how much higher is it for Black adults as a population?","choices":{"A":"10% higher","B":"20% higher","C":"50% higher","D":"30% higher","E":"75% higher"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Black individuals in the United States have roughly a 50% higher stroke risk than White individuals, along with earlier onset and more severe hypertension. A figure of 10% higher understates it. A figure of 20% higher also understates it. A figure of 30% higher still falls short. A figure of 75% higher overstates it. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, racial disparities). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in older adults, cerebral amyloid angiopathy produces repeated lobar hemorrhagic strokes (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, intracranial hemorrhage, p. 528). COMLEX: regular aerobic and resistance exercise lowers systolic pressure, whereas inactivity speeds arterial stiffening (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":5,"stem":"A 77-year-old man comes for follow-up of hypertension. His blood pressure has stayed near 158/84 mm Hg over three visits despite prescriptions for full doses of amlodipine, lisinopril, and chlorthalidone. He does not snore, his BMI is 25 kg/m², and serum potassium is 4.2 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) with a creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL). Before further testing, which cause should be addressed first because it is the most common cause of this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Primary aldosteronism","B":"Renal artery stenosis","C":"Obstructive sleep apnea","D":"Pheochromocytoma","E":"Medication nonadherence"},"correct":"E","explanation":"His 158/84 mm Hg is stage 2 hypertension on three optimally dosed drugs including a diuretic, which is resistant hypertension, and resistant hypertension is most often caused by nonadherence to the prescribed medications. Primary aldosteronism often lowers potassium, which is normal here (general knowledge). Renal artery stenosis is suggested by a creatinine rise after ACE inhibition, which he has not had. Obstructive sleep apnea is less likely without snoring or obesity (general knowledge). Pheochromocytoma causes episodic spells and is rare (general knowledge). Sources: Ham's Ch. 36 (Resistant Hypertension; the leading cause is not in the SDL 8 notes); SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, BP categories; Obj 3, resistant hypertension). Textbook enrichment (Ham's) — detail not in SDL 8 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Liddle syndrome, an autosomal dominant gain-of-function defect that slows breakdown of the epithelial Na+ channel, causes hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and low aldosterone (First Aid Renal—Physiology, renal tubular defects, p. 604). COMLEX: patients who need four or more antihypertensive drugs for control also meet the definition of resistant hypertension (Ham's Ch. 36, Resistant Hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — obstacles to successful hypertension treatment in older adults","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Hams"},{"id":"sdl08_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":5,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman with hypertension comes for follow-up. Her estimated GFR is 48 mL/min/1.73 m² (reference: >60 mL/min/1.73 m²), and her urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio is 22 mg/g (reference: <30 mg/g). Age-related hormonal changes, together with her reduced kidney function, raise her risk of which electrolyte disturbance?","choices":{"A":"Hypernatremia","B":"Hyperkalemia","C":"Hypokalemia","D":"Hypercalcemia","E":"Hypomagnesemia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Declining aldosterone with age, especially alongside reduced renal function, raises the risk of hyperkalemia. Hypernatremia results from water loss with impaired thirst (general knowledge). Hypokalemia follows aldosterone excess or diuretic use, the opposite situation (general knowledge). Hypercalcemia points to hyperparathyroidism or malignancy (general knowledge). Hypomagnesemia follows diuretics, alcohol use, or gastrointestinal loss (general knowledge). Sources: Ham's Ch. 36 (Risk Factors and Pathophysiology; the aldosterone decline and potassium risk are not in the SDL 8 notes); SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, altered neurohormonal regulation). Textbook enrichment (Ham's) — detail not in SDL 8 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: type 4 renal tubular acidosis follows hypoaldosteronism or aldosterone resistance, and the hyperkalemia it causes lowers ammonia excretion and produces metabolic acidosis (First Aid Renal—Physiology, renal tubular acidosis, p. 611). COMLEX: the vascular changes of aging contribute more to hypertension than the age-related fall in renin activity (Ham's Ch. 36, Risk Factors and Pathophysiology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — antihypertensive classes, mechanisms, and adverse effects","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Hams"}]},{"sdlNumber":9,"title":"SDL 9 — Pharmacology of anti hypertensive medications in the elderly","questions":[{"id":"sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":1,"stem":"Among hypertensive adults aged 60 years and older, what share achieve adequate blood pressure control?","choices":{"A":"30%","B":"50%","C":"60%","D":"71%","E":"77%"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Only about 30% of hypertensive adults aged 60 years and older reach adequate control. A share of 50% overstates control. A share of 60% overstates it further. A share of 71% is the proportion receiving drug treatment. A share of 77% is the proportion aware of their hypertension. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, epidemiology of control rates). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: β-blockers lower blood pressure by reducing cardiac output and by blocking β1 receptors on juxtaglomerular cells, which cuts renin secretion (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, β-blockers, p. 244). COMLEX: control rates are lower among Black, Asian American, and Hispanic adults than among White adults (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — adherence and safety of medication use in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician hesitates to intensify antihypertensive therapy in an older adult for fear of adverse drug events, falls, or acute kidney injury. What is this obstacle called?","choices":{"A":"Clinical equipoise","B":"Diagnostic momentum","C":"Defensive medicine","D":"Prescribing cascade","E":"Therapeutic inertia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Therapeutic inertia is a clinician's reluctance to intensify therapy despite uncontrolled blood pressure. Clinical equipoise is genuine uncertainty about which treatment is better, the ethical basis for a trial (general knowledge). Diagnostic momentum is the carrying forward of an early diagnosis without reconsidering it (general knowledge). Defensive medicine is testing or treatment ordered to avoid liability (general knowledge). A prescribing cascade is treating a drug's adverse effect with another drug (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, clinician and health system obstacles). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in hypertension with diabetes, β-blockers can mask the symptoms of hypoglycemia (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hypertension treatment, p. 321). COMLEX: guideline discrepancies, such as the JNC 8 systolic goal below 150 mm Hg at age 60 years or older versus the ACC/AHA goal below 130 mm Hg, leave clinicians unsure how hard to titrate (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — adherence and safety of medication use in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":1,"stem":"Which physiologic change makes systolic blood pressure in older adults intrinsically resistant to antihypertensive therapy?","choices":{"A":"Heightened renin activity","B":"Excess sympathetic tone","C":"Expanded plasma volume","D":"Central arterial stiffening","E":"Rapid hepatic drug clearance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Age-associated loss of central arterial compliance and marked vascular stiffening make systolic pressure intrinsically hard to lower. Heightened renin activity is the opposite of the age-related fall in renin (general knowledge). Excess sympathetic tone is not the stated cause of treatment resistance in older adults (general knowledge). Expanded plasma volume is not an age-related cause of resistance (general knowledge). Rapid hepatic drug clearance runs counter to the slower drug clearance of older adults (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, pharmacokinetic and physiological obstacles). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: fenoldopam is a dopamine D1 receptor agonist that dilates coronary, peripheral, renal, and splanchnic vessels and promotes natriuresis (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, fenoldopam, p. 323). COMLEX: older adults are more susceptible to drug accumulation, volume depletion, and severe orthostatic hypotension (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — adherence and safety of medication use in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":1,"stem":"Large clinical trials show that drug treatment of hypertension in older adults reduces the incidence of stroke and cardiovascular disease by about how much?","choices":{"A":"5% to 10%","B":"30% to 40%","C":"10% to 15%","D":"50% to 60%","E":"70% to 80%"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pharmacologic lowering of blood pressure cuts stroke and cardiovascular disease by 30% to 40% in older adults. A reduction of 5% to 10% understates the benefit. A reduction of 10% to 15% also understates it. A reduction of 50% to 60% overstates it. A reduction of 70% to 80% overstates it further. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, cardiovascular and cerebrovascular morbidity). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nitroprusside is a short-acting vasodilator of arteries and veins that raises cGMP by releasing nitric oxide and can cause cyanide toxicity (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, nitroprusside, p. 323). COMLEX: because baseline risk rises with age, older adults gain large absolute risk reductions from lowering blood pressure (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — effects of hypertension on other organ systems","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":1,"stem":"In the trial of intensive systolic control below 120 mm Hg, how many patients in the ≥75-year subgroup needed treatment over 3 years to prevent one composite cardiovascular event?","choices":{"A":"27","B":"7","C":"12","D":"54","E":"81"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intensive control in SPRINT had a number needed to treat of 27 over 3 years in the ≥75-year subgroup for the composite of myocardial infarction, stroke, heart failure, or cardiovascular death. A value of 7 overstates the benefit. A value of 12 also overstates it. A value of 54 understates it. A value of 81 understates it further. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, SPRINT). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the dihydropyridines nicardipine and clevidipine are used for hypertensive urgency or emergency (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, calcium channel blockers, p. 323). COMLEX: SPRINT compared a systolic goal below 120 mm Hg with standard control below 140 mm Hg and included a prespecified subgroup aged 75 years and older (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — effects of hypertension on other organ systems","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":1,"stem":"Thiazide diuretics lower the risk of hip fracture by about one-third in older adults. Which mechanism explains this benefit?","choices":{"A":"Increased vitamin D activation","B":"Lower parathyroid hormone","C":"Reduced urinary calcium loss","D":"Enhanced intestinal calcium uptake","E":"Fewer orthostatic falls"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiazides reduce urinary calcium excretion, which protects against osteoporosis and lowers hip fracture risk. Increased vitamin D activation is not how thiazides act (general knowledge). Lower parathyroid hormone is not the stated mechanism (general knowledge). Enhanced intestinal calcium uptake is a vitamin D effect rather than a thiazide effect (general knowledge). Fewer orthostatic falls are unlikely, because thiazides can cause volume depletion and hypotension. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, skeletal and renal protection). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides inhibit NaCl reabsorption in the early distal convoluted tubule and lower calcium excretion, so they are also used for idiopathic hypercalciuria and osteoporosis (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, thiazide diuretics, p. 627). COMLEX: a heart-healthy DASH-style eating pattern lowers systolic pressure by about 11 mm Hg (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — effects of hypertension on other organ systems","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"Limiting dietary sodium to no more than 2.3 to 2.4 g per day lowers systolic blood pressure by about how much?","choices":{"A":"1 mm Hg","B":"5 mm Hg","C":"11 mm Hg","D":"15 mm Hg","E":"20 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sodium restriction to 2.3 to 2.4 g per day lowers systolic pressure by about 5 mm Hg. A fall of 1 mm Hg understates the effect. A fall of 11 mm Hg is the effect of a heart-healthy DASH-type diet. A fall of 15 mm Hg overstates the effect. A fall of 20 mm Hg overstates it further. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, dietary sodium restriction). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the cortical collecting tubule, spironolactone and eplerenone competitively block the aldosterone receptor, while triamterene and amiloride block Na+ channels there (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, potassium-sparing diuretics, p. 627). COMLEX: older adults, Black individuals, and patients with chronic kidney disease or diabetes show the largest pressure falls with sodium restriction (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — domains of management in disease prevention in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"The DASH eating plan is not recommended for patients whose chronic kidney disease has reached stage 3 or 4 because it is rich in which minerals?","choices":{"A":"Calcium and magnesium","B":"Bicarbonate and chloride","C":"Iron and vitamin B12","D":"Phosphorus and potassium","E":"Magnesium and sulfate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The DASH diet is rich in potassium and phosphorus, which accumulate in advanced chronic kidney disease. Calcium and magnesium content is not the stated concern (general knowledge). Bicarbonate and chloride are not the minerals that limit its use (general knowledge). Iron and vitamin B12 are not the concern (general knowledge). Magnesium and sulfate are not the concern either (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, DASH eating plan clinical caveat). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: potassium-sparing diuretics can cause hyperkalemia that leads to arrhythmias, and spironolactone adds endocrine effects such as gynecomastia (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, potassium-sparing diuretics, p. 627). COMLEX: the DASH plan emphasizes fruits, vegetables, whole grains, poultry, fish, nuts, and low-fat dairy while restricting saturated fats, red meat, and sweets (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — domains of management in disease prevention in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"To lower systolic pressure by about 5 mm Hg, older adults should do at least 30 minutes of moderate-intensity activity such as brisk walking on at least how many days per week?","choices":{"A":"1 day","B":"2 days","C":"3 days","D":"4 days","E":"5 days"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At least 30 minutes of moderate-intensity activity on at least 5 days per week, about 150 minutes per week, lowers systolic pressure by about 5 mm Hg; Ham's says only at least 30 minutes per day. One day per week is far below the recommendation. Two days per week also falls short. Three days per week still falls short. Four days per week remains one day short. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, aerobic physical activity); 2017 ACC/AHA guideline (90 to 150 minutes per week, general knowledge); Ham's Ch. 36 (Table 36.4). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: β-blockers lower mortality after myocardial infarction, slow AV conduction in supraventricular tachycardia, and help prevent variceal bleeding (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, β-blockers, p. 244). COMLEX: exercise also improves cardiovascular conditioning, preserves muscle mass, and reduces fall risk (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — domains of management in disease prevention in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":1,"stem":"Chlorthalidone and hydrochlorothiazide lower blood pressure by inhibiting which transporter in the distal convoluted tubule?","choices":{"A":"Epithelial sodium channel","B":"Sodium-hydrogen exchanger","C":"Sodium-chloride symporter","D":"Sodium-glucose cotransporter","E":"Sodium-potassium ATPase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiazide and thiazide-like diuretics inhibit the sodium-chloride symporter in the distal convoluted tubule, reducing intravascular volume and then peripheral resistance. The epithelial sodium channel is blocked by amiloride and triamterene in the collecting tubule (general knowledge). The sodium-hydrogen exchanger of the proximal tubule is not their target (general knowledge). The sodium-glucose cotransporter is blocked by SGLT2 inhibitors (general knowledge). Sodium-potassium ATPase is inhibited by digoxin (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, thiazide and thiazide-like diuretics). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics block the Na+/K+/2Cl− cotransporter of the thick ascending limb and can cause ototoxicity, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, dehydration, and gout (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, loop diuretics, p. 626). COMLEX: older adults should receive low thiazide doses rather than maximal diuretic doses (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":1,"stem":"The dry cough caused by ACE inhibitors results from accumulation of which mediator?","choices":{"A":"Bradykinin","B":"Histamine","C":"Angiotensin II","D":"Leukotriene C4","E":"Serotonin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ACE inhibitors block bradykinin breakdown, and the accumulated bradykinin causes a dry cough. Histamine release drives allergic reactions rather than this cough (general knowledge). Angiotensin II falls rather than accumulates with ACE inhibition. Leukotriene C4 mediates bronchoconstriction in asthma (general knowledge). Serotonin is not the mediator of this cough (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, ACE inhibitors). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARBs block angiotensin II binding at the AT1 receptor and do not raise bradykinin, so they suit patients intolerant of ACE inhibitors (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, angiotensin II receptor blockers, p. 628). COMLEX: ACE inhibitors also cause angioedema, rash, an acute fall in GFR, and altered taste (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":1,"stem":"Diltiazem and verapamil are contraindicated in which condition?","choices":{"A":"Atrial fibrillation","B":"Systolic heart failure","C":"Vasospastic angina","D":"Essential tremor","E":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers have negative inotropic effects and are contraindicated in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. Atrial fibrillation is an indication for these drugs as rate control (general knowledge). Vasospastic angina responds to calcium channel blockers (general knowledge). Essential tremor is not a contraindication (general knowledge). Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy can be treated with verapamil (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, nondihydropyridines). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nondihydropyridines can cause cardiac depression, AV block, and constipation, and verapamil can raise prolactin (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, calcium channel blockers, p. 323). COMLEX: diltiazem and verapamil slow SA node automaticity and prolong the AV node refractory period (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman comes for follow-up of hypertension with her son. She takes four antihypertensive drugs, one each morning, two at morning and bedtime, and one three times a day, and pill counts show many missed doses. She lives alone and has mild cognitive impairment with a Montreal Cognitive Assessment score of 22/30 (normal: ≥26/30). Her blood pressure is 162/84 mm Hg, and serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL). Which change is most likely to improve her blood pressure control?","choices":{"A":"Adding a fifth antihypertensive","B":"Switching to brand-name drugs","C":"Increasing visit frequency","D":"Childproof pill containers","E":"Once-daily consolidated regimen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Multiple prescriptions with differing dosing intervals foster confusion and forgetfulness, especially with mild cognitive decline, so consolidating to once-daily dosing targets her barrier. Adding a fifth antihypertensive worsens the complexity that drives her missed doses. Switching to brand-name drugs adds cost without simplifying dosing (general knowledge). Increasing visit frequency does not fix the daily dosing confusion (general knowledge). Childproof pill containers add a physical barrier for older adults. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, complex regimens and cognitive impairment). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: α-methyldopa is used for hypertension in pregnancy and can cause a positive direct Coombs test with hemolysis, drug-induced lupus, and hyperprolactinemia (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, α2-agonists, p. 243). COMLEX: most older hypertensive adults need two or more medications to reach goal (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 5 — adherence and safety of medication use in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":2,"stem":"An 81-year-old man comes for follow-up because his blood pressure has risen from 132/78 to 158/86 mm Hg over 3 months. He has severe hand osteoarthritis with Heberden nodes, and his grip strength is markedly reduced. He says two of his newly refilled pill bottles are still full because the lids hurt his hands to open, although he reads the labels without difficulty. His serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL) and potassium is 4.3 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which intervention best addresses the barrier to his adherence?","choices":{"A":"Large-print bottle labels","B":"Easy-open container caps","C":"Liquid drug formulations","D":"Pill-crushing device","E":"Generic substitution"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Arthritis hinders opening childproof containers, so non-childproof, easy-open caps remove his barrier. Large-print bottle labels help visual impairment, which he does not have. Liquid drug formulations help patients who have trouble swallowing large tablets. A pill-crushing device also targets swallowing rather than opening (general knowledge). Generic substitution targets cost rather than access to the pills (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, physical disabilities and formulations). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hydralazine, methyldopa, labetalol, and nifedipine are the antihypertensives listed for hypertension in pregnancy (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hypertension treatment, p. 321). COMLEX: reduced salivation makes large tablets difficult for older adults to swallow (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 5 — adherence and safety of medication use in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man comes for a routine visit 4 months after hypertension was diagnosed during an evaluation for morning headaches. His headaches resolved within weeks of starting treatment, and he stopped his medications soon after because he felt well. His blood pressure is now 168/92 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL), and an ECG shows left ventricular hypertrophy. Which factor most likely explains his nonadherence?","choices":{"A":"Resolution of symptoms","B":"Fear of drug interactions","C":"Distrust of generic drugs","D":"Belief in herbal remedies","E":"Concern about addiction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because hypertension is usually silent, patients often stop treatment when they feel well or when their initial symptoms resolve. Fear of drug interactions is not suggested by his history (general knowledge). Distrust of generic drugs is not suggested either (general knowledge). Belief in herbal remedies is not described (general knowledge). Concern about addiction does not fit antihypertensive drugs or his account (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, asymptomatic nature of disease). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aliskiren is a direct renin inhibitor that blocks conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, aliskiren, p. 628). COMLEX: office readings can exceed ambulatory or home readings by up to 19/11 mm Hg because of anxiety (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — effects of hypertension on other organ systems","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman comes for follow-up of untreated hypertension. Her blood pressure has averaged 172/80 mm Hg over three visits, she lives independently, and she has no history of stroke or coronary disease. Her estimated GFR is 58 mL/min/1.73 m² (reference: >60 mL/min/1.73 m²), and serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L). Her physician cites a placebo-controlled trial of adults aged 80 years or older, mean age 84, treated with indapamide with or without perindopril to a target of 150/80 mm Hg. In that trial, which outcome showed the largest relative reduction at 2 years?","choices":{"A":"Fatal stroke","B":"Overall mortality","C":"Heart failure","D":"Myocardial infarction","E":"Hip fracture"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In HYVET, active treatment cut congestive heart failure by 64%, the largest reduction among its outcomes. Fatal stroke fell by 39%. Overall mortality fell by 21%. Myocardial infarction was not among the reported reductions. Hip fracture was not a reported HYVET outcome. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, HYVET). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in hypertension with heart failure, ACE inhibitors or ARBs, β-blockers for compensated failure, aldosterone antagonists for mortality benefit, and diuretics for symptoms are used (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hypertension treatment, p. 321). COMLEX: in HYVET, fatal or nonfatal stroke fell by 30% and cardiovascular death by 23% (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — effects of diastolic dysfunction in the aging heart","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":2,"stem":"A 77-year-old man comes for follow-up of hypertension. His systolic pressure is 146 mm Hg on two drugs, and he has no diabetes or prior stroke. He walks daily, and his estimated GFR is 64 mL/min/1.73 m² (reference: >60 mL/min/1.73 m²) with a potassium of 4.2 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). His physician proposes adding a third drug to aim for a systolic pressure below 120 mm Hg rather than below 140 mm Hg. In the trial that tested this target, what relative reduction in composite cardiovascular outcomes did it achieve in the ≥75-year subgroup?","choices":{"A":"10%","B":"21%","C":"39%","D":"64%","E":"33%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Intensive control in SPRINT produced a 33% relative risk reduction in the ≥75-year subgroup in fatal and nonfatal myocardial infarction, stroke, heart failure, or cardiovascular death. A reduction of 10% understates the benefit. A reduction of 21% is the HYVET fall in all-cause mortality. A reduction of 39% is the HYVET fall in stroke mortality. A reduction of 64% is the HYVET fall in heart failure. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, SPRINT). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nebivolol combines β1-selective blockade with β3 stimulation that activates nitric oxide synthase in the vasculature (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, β-blockers, p. 244). COMLEX: SPRINT's composite outcome counted myocardial infarction, stroke, heart failure, and cardiovascular death (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — impact of age and multimorbidity on coronary disease outcomes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man comes to the physician because his wife has noticed 1 year of slowed thinking and trouble managing their finances. He has had poorly controlled hypertension since his 40s. His MoCA screening score is 23/30 (normal: ≥26/30), vitamin B12 is 480 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL), and TSH is 1.9 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL). MRI shows confluent periventricular white matter hyperintensities and two small lacunes in the basal ganglia. Lowering his blood pressure is most likely to reduce the risk of which outcome?","choices":{"A":"Progressive dementia","B":"Frontotemporal degeneration","C":"Prion-mediated dementia","D":"Lewy body pathology","E":"Hepatic encephalopathy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypertension drives microvascular damage, ischemic white matter injury, and lacunar strokes that underlie vascular dementia and contribute to Alzheimer disease. In treatment trials, Syst-Eur (with SHEP) showed only a modest trend toward fewer dementia cases, PROGRESS reduced dementia in patients with prior cerebrovascular disease, and HYVET-COG found no significant effect (trial details: general knowledge). Frontotemporal degeneration is a proteinopathy not driven by blood pressure (general knowledge). Prion-mediated dementia is unrelated to vascular risk (general knowledge). Lewy body pathology is an alpha-synucleinopathy not prevented by blood pressure control (general knowledge). Hepatic encephalopathy results from liver failure (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, prevention of cognitive decline and dementia). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nimodipine is the calcium channel blocker used after subarachnoid hemorrhage to prevent delayed ischemia, while other dihydropyridines treat hypertension, angina, and Raynaud phenomenon (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, calcium channel blockers, p. 323). COMLEX: both midlife and late-life hypertension act as a shared driver of vascular dementia and Alzheimer disease (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man comes for follow-up of stage 2 hypertension. He weighs 104 kg with a BMI of 33 kg/m², and he takes no medications. His blood pressure is 152/88 mm Hg, hemoglobin A1c is 6.1% (reference: <5.7%), and fasting LDL cholesterol is 128 mg/dL (reference: <100 mg/dL). He agrees to a supervised program to lose 10 kg over the next year. If he succeeds, about how much would his systolic pressure be expected to fall?","choices":{"A":"1 to 2 mm Hg","B":"5 to 20 mm Hg","C":"2 to 4 mm Hg","D":"25 to 35 mm Hg","E":"40 to 50 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Weight reduction lowers systolic pressure by 5 to 20 mm Hg per 10 kg lost. A fall of 1 to 2 mm Hg understates the effect. A fall of 2 to 4 mm Hg also understates it. A fall of 25 to 35 mm Hg overstates it. A fall of 40 to 50 mm Hg overstates it further. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, weight reduction). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in hypertension with asthma, ARBs, calcium channel blockers, thiazides, and cardioselective β-blockers are preferred, while nonselective β-blockers and ACE inhibitors are avoided (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hypertension treatment, p. 321). COMLEX: a first goal of losing 5% to 10% of baseline weight improves blood pressure and reduces insulin resistance (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — sarcopenia, frailty, and malnutrition","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman comes for follow-up of hypertension treated with lisinopril. She has read that potassium lowers blood pressure and asks how to increase her intake. Her blood pressure is 146/82 mm Hg, estimated GFR is 66 mL/min/1.73 m² (reference: >60 mL/min/1.73 m²), and serum potassium is 4.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). An ECG shows normal T waves. Which way of raising her potassium intake is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Potassium chloride tablets","B":"Potassium-sparing diuretic","C":"Potassium salt substitute","D":"Diet-based potassium","E":"Effervescent potassium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Potassium should come from dietary change rather than supplements to prevent hyperkalemia in patients with renal decline or those taking renin-angiotensin blockers such as lisinopril. Potassium chloride tablets are the supplement route the notes advise against. A potassium-sparing diuretic adds a second potassium-raising drug to her ACE inhibitor (general knowledge). A potassium salt substitute is a concentrated potassium chloride source (general knowledge). Effervescent potassium is another supplement form (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, potassium supplementation via diet). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral renal artery stenosis calls for caution with ACE inhibitors because they further lower GFR and can precipitate renal failure (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, ACE inhibitors, p. 628). COMLEX: raising dietary potassium to at least 4.7 g daily lowers systolic pressure by about 4 mm Hg (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man comes for follow-up of hypertension. He drinks four beers every evening and has no withdrawal symptoms. His blood pressure is 154/90 mm Hg, gamma-glutamyl transferase is 88 U/L (reference range: 8–61 U/L), and mean corpuscular volume is 101 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). He agrees to cut back to the level recommended for blood pressure control. What daily limit should he be advised to observe?","choices":{"A":"One drink per day","B":"Three drinks per day","C":"Two drinks per day","D":"Four drinks per day","E":"Five drinks per day"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Men should limit alcohol to no more than 2 drinks per day and women to no more than 1, which lowers systolic pressure by about 4 mm Hg; pressure rises directly at intakes of 2 or more drinks per day, so his four beers sit well above that level. One drink per day is the limit for women. Three drinks per day exceeds the limit for men. Four drinks per day is his current excess intake. Five drinks per day exceeds it further. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, moderation of alcohol consumption); SDL 8 notes (alcohol ≥ 2 drinks/day raises blood pressure). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: verapamil acts most on the heart and amlodipine and nifedipine most on vascular smooth muscle, with diltiazem in between (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, calcium channel blockers, p. 323). COMLEX: smoking cessation lowers blood pressure by 1 to 6 mm Hg (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — domains of management in disease prevention in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia was started on doxazosin 2 weeks ago for both his hypertension and urinary symptoms. Last night he fainted on his way to the bathroom. His supine blood pressure is 148/82 mm Hg and his standing pressure is 116/70 mm Hg, and serum sodium is 140 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L). Postvoid residual volume is 60 mL (reference: <50 mL). In a large trial that compared this drug class with chlorthalidone, what additional problem led to advice against its use as primary monotherapy for hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Excess cardiac events","B":"Worse urinary retention","C":"Severe hyperkalemia","D":"Persistent dry cough","E":"Rebound hypertension"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In ALLHAT, the alpha-1 blocker arm had more cardiovascular events than the chlorthalidone arm, so these drugs should not be used as primary monotherapy for hypertension. Worse urinary retention is not expected, because alpha-1 blockade relaxes the bladder neck and prostate. Severe hyperkalemia is an adverse effect of renin-angiotensin blockers. Persistent dry cough is an ACE inhibitor effect. Rebound hypertension follows abrupt withdrawal of central alpha-2 agonists. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, peripherally acting alpha-1 receptor antagonists). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: α1-selective blockers such as prazosin, terazosin, and doxazosin treat urinary symptoms of BPH and hypertension, and prazosin is used for PTSD, with first-dose orthostatic hypotension, dizziness, and headache as adverse effects (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, α-blockers, p. 243). COMLEX: alpha-1 blockers relax smooth muscle in the bladder neck and prostate (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 6 — medication classes associated with increased fall risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman is admitted for a hip fracture repair, and all her home medications are held while she is kept without oral intake. Her home list is amlodipine, lisinopril, chlorthalidone, doxazosin, and clonidine 0.1 mg three times daily. Thirty hours later she is anxious and tremulous, her blood pressure is 214/112 mm Hg, and her pulse is 108/min. Troponin I is <0.01 ng/mL (reference: <0.04 ng/mL), and CT of the head shows no hemorrhage. Abrupt omission of which drug most likely explains this?","choices":{"A":"Amlodipine","B":"Lisinopril","C":"Chlorthalidone","D":"Clonidine","E":"Doxazosin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Centrally acting alpha-2 agonists such as clonidine cause severe rebound hypertension when abruptly omitted. Amlodipine has a long half-life and does not cause abrupt rebound (general knowledge). Lisinopril withdrawal does not produce sympathetic rebound (general knowledge). Chlorthalidone withdrawal causes a gradual rise rather than a crisis (general knowledge). Doxazosin withdrawal does not cause this sympathetic surge (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, centrally acting alpha-2 adrenergic agonists). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: clonidine and guanfacine can cause CNS depression, bradycardia, hypotension, respiratory depression, and miosis (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, α2-agonists, p. 243). COMLEX: central alpha-2 agonists are generally avoided in older adults because of their high risk of central nervous system toxicity (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and albuminuria comes for follow-up 3 weeks after losartan was added to his lisinopril to reduce proteinuria. He feels weak. His blood pressure is 118/64 mm Hg, serum potassium is 6.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and creatinine has risen from 1.3 to 1.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL). An ECG shows peaked T waves without arrhythmia. Besides treating the hyperkalemia, which medication change is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Adding spironolactone","B":"End dual RAS blockade","C":"Doubling the losartan","D":"Adding potassium citrate","E":"Starting a DASH diet"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Combining an ACE inhibitor with an ARB is contraindicated, so one of the two renin-angiotensin blockers must be stopped. Adding spironolactone would raise potassium further (general knowledge). Doubling the losartan intensifies the cause of his hyperkalemia. Adding potassium citrate adds potassium (general knowledge). Starting a DASH diet adds potassium-rich foods. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, angiotensin II receptor blockers). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitor adverse effects include cough, angioedema, teratogenicity, a rise in creatinine, hyperkalemia, and hypotension (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, ACE inhibitors, p. 628). COMLEX: ARBs block angiotensin II at the AT1 receptor on vascular smooth muscle and the adrenal cortex, preventing vasoconstriction and aldosterone release (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — structural and functional changes of the urinary system with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":3,"stem":"A 78-year-old retired widow on a fixed pension takes three antihypertensives and says she splits pills and lets refills lapse near the end of each month. She reads labels easily and has no side effects. What barrier most likely explains her poor control?","choices":{"A":"Cognitive impairment","B":"Adverse drug effects","C":"Health literacy gaps","D":"Out-of-pocket cost","E":"Visual impairment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The out-of-pocket expense of multidrug regimens on fixed retirement incomes leads patients to skip doses or leave prescriptions unfilled. Cognitive impairment causes confusion with complex dosing, which she does not describe. Adverse drug effects are absent in her account. Health literacy gaps are not suggested by her clear understanding (general knowledge). Visual impairment is excluded by her easy label reading. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, financial disincentives). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: phenoxybenzamine is an irreversible α-blocker given before surgery for pheochromocytoma to prevent catecholamine-driven hypertensive crisis (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, α-blockers, p. 243). COMLEX: heavy calcification of peripheral arteries can keep the cuff from compressing them and falsely raise readings in older adults (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — challenges in providing preventive care to older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":3,"stem":"An 81-year-old man has blood pressure of 172/58 mm Hg, a wide gap between his systolic and diastolic readings, and an estimated GFR that fell from 62 to 54 mL/min/1.73 m² over 2 years. Treating this pattern slows his kidney decline by limiting which process?","choices":{"A":"Glomerular hyperfiltration","B":"Immune complex formation","C":"Pulsatile vessel injury","D":"Urinary crystal deposition","E":"Tubular sodium wasting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Treating isolated systolic hypertension and wide pulse pressure blunts microvascular pulsatile injury to the kidneys and slows chronic kidney disease. Glomerular hyperfiltration drives diabetic and obesity-related nephropathy (general knowledge). Immune complex formation causes glomerulonephritis unrelated to blood pressure (general knowledge). Urinary crystal deposition causes stone or crystal nephropathy (general knowledge). Tubular sodium wasting is not a consequence of systolic hypertension (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, skeletal and renal protection). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitors dilate the efferent arteriole, lowering GFR and filtration fraction, and loss of angiotensin II feedback raises renin (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, ACE inhibitors, p. 628). COMLEX: the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline sets a systolic target below 130 mm Hg for community-dwelling, ambulatory older adults (SDL 9 notes; Ham's Ch. 36, Table 36.3).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — structural and functional changes of the urinary system with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with hypertension has smoked a pack a day for 45 years and agrees to quit today. How soon after quitting does most of his excess cardiovascular risk fall away?","choices":{"A":"Within 5 years","B":"Within 1 month","C":"After 10 years","D":"After 15 years","E":"After 25 years"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Smoking cessation cuts excess cardiovascular risk substantially within 5 years. A drop within 1 month overstates how fast the risk falls. A drop after 10 years understates the speed of benefit. A drop after 15 years understates it more. A drop after 25 years understates it further. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, smoking cessation). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: phentolamine is a reversible α-blocker used for hypertensive episodes in patients on MAO inhibitors who eat tyramine-rich foods and for cocaine-induced hypertension (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, α-blockers, p. 243). COMLEX: smoking acts synergistically with hypertension to raise coronary artery disease and stroke risk (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":3,"stem":"A 75-year-old man develops a hot, swollen first metatarsophalangeal joint 6 weeks after starting hydrochlorothiazide for hypertension. Joint fluid shows negatively birefringent needle-shaped crystals. Which drug effect most likely precipitated this?","choices":{"A":"Hypokalemia","B":"Hypercalcemia","C":"Hyperuricemia","D":"Hyponatremia","E":"Hypomagnesemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiazides can cause hyperuricemia that precipitates acute gout. Hypokalemia causes weakness and arrhythmias rather than crystal arthritis. Hypercalcemia from thiazides does not cause urate crystals (general knowledge). Hyponatremia causes confusion and falls rather than joint inflammation (general knowledge). Hypomagnesemia causes cramps and arrhythmias (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, thiazide diuretic adverse effects). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazide adverse effects include hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, hyponatremia, hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, hypercalcemia, and sulfa allergy (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, thiazide diuretics, p. 627). COMLEX: low thiazide doses rather than maximal diuretic doses are advised in older adults (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman has bilateral ankle swelling 3 weeks after amlodipine was started. Her jugular venous pressure is normal, lungs are clear, and serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). What causes the swelling?","choices":{"A":"Renal sodium retention","B":"Lymphatic obstruction","C":"Bilateral venous thrombosis","D":"Reduced plasma albumin","E":"Arteriolar vasodilation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers cause pedal edema through precapillary dilation rather than volume overload. Renal sodium retention would raise the jugular venous pressure. Lymphatic obstruction has no cause in her history (general knowledge). Bilateral venous thrombosis is unlikely with symmetric drug-timed swelling (general knowledge). Reduced plasma albumin is excluded by her normal level. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, dihydropyridine adverse effects). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hydralazine is given with a β-blocker to blunt reflex tachycardia and is safe to use in pregnancy (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hydralazine, p. 323). COMLEX: pedal edema from calcium channel blockers is a common reason older adults stop treatment (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 5 — adherence and safety of medication use in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with uncomplicated hypertension and no heart disease asks why atenolol was not chosen as his first drug. Which finding in older adults argues against it?","choices":{"A":"Worse renal outcomes","B":"Excess stroke risk","C":"Frequent dry cough","D":"Peripheral edema","E":"Higher drug cost"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Beta-blockers are not advised as initial monotherapy for uncomplicated hypertension at age 60 or older because of an excess risk of stroke compared with other agents. Worse renal outcomes are not the reason (general knowledge). Frequent dry cough belongs to ACE inhibitors. Peripheral edema belongs to dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers. Higher drug cost does not apply to generic atenolol (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, beta-blockers in older adults). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: β1-selective blockers mostly have names starting A through M, nonselective ones N through Z, and carvedilol and labetalol block both α1 and β receptors (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, β-blockers, p. 244). COMLEX: abrupt β-blocker cessation can trigger rebound tachycardia or acute myocardial ischemia (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — impact of age and multimorbidity on coronary disease outcomes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q25","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":5,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman comes for follow-up of untreated hypertension. Her readings at three visits average 168/62 mm Hg, and she has no heart failure, diabetes, or kidney disease. Her serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL), and potassium is 4.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Her physician notes that the wide gap between her readings independently predicts stroke and heart failure. Large trials suggest which drug class narrows this gap most effectively?","choices":{"A":"ACE inhibitors","B":"Thiazide diuretics","C":"Calcium channel blockers","D":"Beta-blockers","E":"Alpha-1 blockers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Data from large trials suggest that thiazides reduce pulse pressure more effectively than ACE inhibitors or calcium channel blockers. ACE inhibitors were less effective at this in those trials. Calcium channel blockers were also less effective. Beta-blockers are not first-line drugs for uncomplicated hypertension in older adults. Alpha-1 blockers are not advised as primary monotherapy. Sources: Ham's Ch. 36 (Wide Pulse Pressure; the comparison is not in the SDL 9 notes); SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, skeletal and renal protection). Textbook enrichment (Ham's) — detail not in SDL 9 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: drugs that cause hyperuricemia include pyrazinamide, thiazides, furosemide, niacin, and cyclosporine (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, drug reactions, p. 249). COMLEX: coffee raises blood pressure within hours of drinking, but its long-term significance is unclear (Ham's Ch. 36, Caffeine).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — central elastic artery changes and systolic hypertension with aging","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Hams"},{"id":"sdl09_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":5,"stem":"A 73-year-old man with newly diagnosed hypertension starts chlorthalidone 12.5 mg daily. His starting blood pressure is 158/86 mm Hg, and his target is below 130/80 mm Hg. At a recheck his pressure is 150/84 mm Hg, potassium is 3.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL). He reports no side effects. If the target is not reached, how soon should the dose be increased or a second drug added?","choices":{"A":"After 1 month","B":"After 1 week","C":"After 3 months","D":"After 6 months","E":"After 1 year"},"correct":"A","explanation":"If the target is not reached within 1 month of starting therapy, the initial dose should be raised or a second medication added. Waiting only 1 week acts before the drug's full effect is seen (general knowledge). Waiting 3 months delays intensification. Waiting 6 months delays it longer. Waiting 1 year prolongs exposure to uncontrolled pressure. Sources: Ham's Ch. 36 (Overview of Medications; the 1-month interval is not in the SDL 9 notes); SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, antihypertensive classes). Textbook enrichment (Ham's) — detail not in SDL 9 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: drugs that cause pulmonary fibrosis include methotrexate, nitrofurantoin, carmustine, bleomycin, busulfan, and amiodarone, whereas ACE inhibitors cause a dry cough (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, drug reactions, p. 250). COMLEX: in SHEP, chlorthalidone for systolic pressure of 160 mm Hg or more with diastolic pressure under 90 mm Hg reduced stroke and heart disease (Ham's Ch. 36, Isolated Systolic Hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — factors affecting drug prescribing in the elderly","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Hams"},{"id":"sdl09_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":5,"stem":"A 75-year-old man comes to the physician because his gums have become swollen and bleed when he brushes. Over the past year his gum tissue has grown over the lower edges of his teeth. He takes diltiazem, lisinopril, atorvastatin, metformin, and chlorthalidone, and he has no seizure disorder or transplant. His hemoglobin A1c is 6.9% (reference: <5.7%), and platelet count is 240,000/mm³ (reference range: 150,000–400,000/mm³). Which drug class in his regimen most likely explains his oral finding?","choices":{"A":"ACE inhibitor","B":"HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor","C":"Nondihydropyridine CCB","D":"Biguanide antidiabetic","E":"Thiazide-like diuretic"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nondihydropyridine agents such as diltiazem can cause gingival hyperplasia. An ACE inhibitor such as lisinopril causes cough and angioedema rather than gum overgrowth. An HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor such as atorvastatin causes myalgias and liver enzyme rise (general knowledge). A biguanide antidiabetic such as metformin causes gastrointestinal upset and B12 deficiency (general knowledge). A thiazide-like diuretic such as chlorthalidone causes electrolyte and metabolic effects. Sources: Ham's Ch. 36 (Antihypertensive Drugs, nondihydropyridine adverse effects; gum overgrowth is not in the SDL 9 notes); SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers). Textbook enrichment (Ham's) — detail not in SDL 9 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gingival hyperplasia is a known reaction to cyclosporine, calcium channel blockers, and phenytoin (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, drug reactions, p. 249). COMLEX: no J-curve effect is seen for stroke, possibly because the heart is perfused mostly during diastole (Ham's Ch. 36, Ischemic Heart Disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Hams"}]},{"sdlNumber":10,"title":"SDL 10 — Elder Mistreatment","questions":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":1,"stem":"About how many US adults aged 60 years and older experience some form of elder mistreatment?","choices":{"A":"1 in 100","B":"1 in 33","C":"1 in 4","D":"1 in 10","E":"2 in 5"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Approximately 10% of US adults aged 60 years and older experience some form of mistreatment. A rate of 1 in 100 understates it. A rate of 1 in 33 also understates it. A rate of 1 in 4 overstates it. A rate of 2 in 5 is far higher than the national estimate. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, United States estimates). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abuse of children, older adults, or prisoners is one of the listed exceptions to confidentiality, along with suicidal or homicidal ideation, duty to protect, impaired drivers, and reportable diseases (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, confidentiality, p. 269). COMLEX: 1-year prevalence estimates of elder mistreatment worldwide average 14.3% (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":1,"stem":"Among community-dwelling older adults in the United States, which subtype of elder mistreatment has the highest reported prevalence?","choices":{"A":"Financial exploitation","B":"Caregiver neglect","C":"Emotional mistreatment","D":"Physical mistreatment","E":"Sexual mistreatment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Financial exploitation has the highest US prevalence at 5.2%. Caregiver neglect follows closely at 5.1%. Emotional mistreatment is reported at 4.6%. Physical mistreatment is reported at 1.6%. Sexual mistreatment is a recognized subtype without a higher reported rate in the notes. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, subtype breakdown in the US). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in factitious disorder imposed on another, a caregiver causes or fabricates illness in a dependent person, most often a child but also a disabled or older adult, and it counts as child or elder abuse (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, factitious disorders, p. 583). COMLEX: in a population-based study of older Chinese Americans, about 15% reported mistreatment; only the 40.4% Hispanic figure comes from convenience samples (Ham's Ch. 33, Prevalence).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":1,"stem":"Under the US National Research Council definition, intentional harm to a vulnerable elder counts as mistreatment when the person causing it holds which kind of bond with the elder?","choices":{"A":"Blood relationship","B":"Trust relationship","C":"Legal guardianship","D":"Shared household","E":"Paid employment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The definition covers harm by a caregiver or other person who stands in a trust relationship with the elder. A blood relationship is not required. Legal guardianship is not required (general knowledge). A shared household is not part of the definition. Paid employment is not required. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, definition of elder mistreatment). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a physician determines capacity for a specific healthcare decision, whereas a judge determines competency, which covers broader legal decision-making (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, decision-making capacity, p. 267). COMLEX: a busy primary care clinician likely sees a mistreated older adult every week (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":1,"stem":"In the United States, which form of elder mistreatment is reported most often to the community agency that investigates elder abuse, neglect, and exploitation?","choices":{"A":"Financial exploitation","B":"Caregiver neglect","C":"Physical abuse","D":"Emotional abuse","E":"Self-neglect"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Self-neglect is the type reported most often to Adult Protective Services in the United States. Financial exploitation leads community prevalence surveys rather than agency reports. Caregiver neglect is reported less often. Physical abuse is reported less often. Emotional abuse is reported less often. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, epidemiological significance to APS). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: informed consent requires disclosure, understanding, capacity, and voluntariness, and patients may withdraw written consent whenever they wish, even verbally (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, informed consent, p. 268). COMLEX: recent literature increasingly treats self-neglect as a distinct geriatric syndrome (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":1,"stem":"Why is self-neglect considered a separate entity from the other forms of elder mistreatment?","choices":{"A":"No third-party perpetrator","B":"Lower associated mortality","C":"Rarely reported to agencies","D":"Absent legal definition","E":"Younger victim profile"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Self-neglect stands apart because it is not perpetrated by a third party. A lower associated mortality is not the basis for the distinction (general knowledge). Rarely reported to agencies is wrong, since it is the most common form reported to APS. The NCEA does define it; how it is defined is not what separates it from other mistreatment. A younger victim profile is not described (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, definition and conceptualization). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neglect, the most common form of child maltreatment, presents with poor hygiene, malnutrition, withdrawn affect, and failure to thrive (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, child abuse, p. 573). COMLEX: in-home assessments are among the most effective approaches for detecting self-neglect (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following is one of the five observable phenotypes of self-neglect?","choices":{"A":"Wandering","B":"Insomnia","C":"Hoarding","D":"Kleptomania","E":"Sundowning"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hoarding is one of the five phenotypes of self-neglect. Wandering is a behavior seen in dementia (general knowledge). Insomnia is a sleep disorder (general knowledge). Kleptomania is an impulse-control disorder (general knowledge). Sundowning is evening agitation in dementia (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, five clinical phenotypes). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: scurvy brings swollen gums, easy bruising, petechiae, hemarthrosis, poor wound healing, and corkscrew hair, and a tea-and-toast diet can precipitate it (First Aid Biochemistry—Nutrition, vitamin C, p. 67). COMLEX: Black older adults experience higher rates of self-neglect and psychological mistreatment (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":1,"stem":"Which relationship factor is a strong independent risk factor for physical abuse and financial exploitation of an older adult?","choices":{"A":"Long-distance caregiving","B":"Scheduled home aide visits","C":"Weekly visits from family","D":"Shared living arrangement","E":"Adult day program attendance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cohabiting with the abuser is a strong independent risk factor for physical abuse and financial exploitation. Long-distance caregiving does not carry this risk (general knowledge). Scheduled home aide visits are not listed as a relationship factor. Weekly visits from family add social contact rather than risk (general knowledge). Adult day program attendance expands social contact (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, relationship and relational factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: alcohol use disorder causes thiamine deficiency, cirrhosis, hepatitis, pancreatitis, peripheral neuropathy, and testicular atrophy, and it is treated with naltrexone, acamprosate, or disulfiram plus support groups (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, alcohol use disorder, p. 590). COMLEX: active drug or alcohol addiction in the caregiver is a well-established perpetrator risk factor (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician weighing an older woman's risk of mistreatment looks in turn at the woman herself, at the person caring for her, at their relationship, and at her wider community and society. Organizing risk across these nested levels applies which framework?","choices":{"A":"Biomedical model","B":"Stages-of-change model","C":"Health belief model","D":"Biopsychosocial model","E":"Ecological model"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Risk factors are structured with an ecological framework spanning victim, perpetrator, relationship, community, and societal levels. The biomedical model explains illness through biological causes (general knowledge). The stages-of-change model describes readiness for behavior change (general knowledge). The health belief model explains health behavior through perceived threat and benefit (general knowledge). The biopsychosocial model combines biological, psychological, and social influences on illness (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, risk factor framework). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: physicians are ethically, and usually legally, obliged to report an impaired colleague so the colleague stops patient care and gets help (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Communication Skills, challenging scenarios, p. 273). COMLEX: poverty and low socioeconomic status are community and societal risk factors for mistreatment (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":1,"stem":"Which victim characteristic, whether present at baseline or newly developing, is a potent predictor of elder mistreatment?","choices":{"A":"Rising household income","B":"Cognitive impairment","C":"Long marital duration","D":"Regular church attendance","E":"Prior military service"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Both baseline dementia and new cognitive deficits are potent predictors of elder mistreatment. Rising household income is not a risk factor, since poverty raises risk. Long marital duration is not a listed risk factor (general knowledge). Regular church attendance adds social ties, which buffer against mistreatment. Prior military service is not a listed risk factor (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, victim-specific risk factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aging skin loses dermal collagen and elastin, producing wrinkles and senile purpura, and loses sweat glands, raising the risk of heat stroke (First Aid Pathology—Aging, skin, p. 225). COMLEX: severe multimorbidity, frailty, and sensory deficits increase reliance on others and limit the ability to seek help (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":1,"stem":"An 81-year-old man with intact decision-making capacity tells his physician in Ohio that his grandson has been taking money from his bank account. He asks the physician not to tell anyone because he fears losing contact with his grandson. What is the physician's legal obligation?","choices":{"A":"Honor his request","B":"Confront the grandson","C":"File a report","D":"Defer to his family","E":"Chart it and wait"},"correct":"C","explanation":"With the exception of New York, every US state has a mandatory reporting law, so a physician in Ohio must report suspected mistreatment even when the patient asks otherwise. Honoring his request leaves a mandated report unmade. Confronting the grandson may raise the danger to the patient and is not the legal duty (general knowledge). Deferring to his family does not satisfy the physician's own duty. Charting it and waiting does not meet the reporting requirement. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, mandatory reporting). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: when a patient without capacity has no advance directive, surrogates decide in the order spouse, adult children, parents, adult siblings, and other relatives (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, surrogate decision-maker, p. 269). COMLEX: reporting laws cover healthcare providers, behavioral health specialists, and social service providers (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — challenges in preventive care in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":1,"stem":"During a routine 15-minute visit, a primary care physician wants a validated screen for elder mistreatment that takes under 2 minutes. It should ask the patient directly about emotional, physical, and financial safety. Which tool best fits?","choices":{"A":"VASS","B":"EASI","C":"H-S/EAST","D":"MoCA","E":"PHQ-9"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Elder Abuse Suspicion Index is a validated screen that takes under 2 minutes and asks the patient directly about safety, which fits a busy visit. The VASS is a longer 12-item scale of vulnerability, dependence, dejection, and coercion. The H-S/EAST measures violation of personal rights, direct abuse, and potentially abusive situations. The MoCA screens cognition rather than mistreatment (general knowledge). The PHQ-9 screens for depression (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, screening and detection tools). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: exceptions to informed consent are waiver, legal incompetence, therapeutic privilege, and emergency situations with implied consent (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, informed consent, p. 268). COMLEX: Adult Protective Services receives and investigates reports, assesses capacity, and arranges emergency shelter, medical care, and legal support (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — challenges in preventive care in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":1,"stem":"A daughter tells her mother's physician that staff at her mother's nursing home ignore call lights, restrict visits, and dismiss her complaints about her mother's rights. No crime or Medicaid fraud is suspected, and she wants someone to advocate for her mother within the facility. Which program should the physician direct her to?","choices":{"A":"Long-Term Care Ombudsman","B":"Eldercare Locator","C":"Medicaid Fraud Control Unit","D":"Area Agency on Aging","E":"Adult day health center"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Long-Term Care Ombudsman Program, established by the Older Americans Act, advocates for the health, safety, welfare, and legal rights of residents of nursing homes and assisted living facilities. The Eldercare Locator is a clearinghouse that directs callers to local agencies. A Medicaid Fraud Control Unit investigates and prosecutes Medicaid fraud and patient abuse in facilities. An Area Agency on Aging plans community services for older adults (general knowledge). An adult day health center provides daytime care (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, key reporting and support agencies). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a medical power of attorney names an agent to make decisions if the patient loses capacity, can be revoked while capacity is intact, and is more flexible than a living will (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, advance directives, p. 268). COMLEX: the Eldercare Locator (1-800-677-1116) directs clinicians and families to local adult protection agencies (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — challenges in preventive care in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":2,"stem":"An 86-year-old bedbound woman with advanced Parkinson disease is brought to the emergency department by a neighbor. Her son moved in 2 years ago and agreed to manage her meals, medications, and turning, but he has been away for days at a time. She is cachectic, her bedding is soiled, and she has a deep sacral wound exposing muscle with no bruises or fractures. Serum sodium is 152 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L), BUN is 48 mg/dL (reference range: 7–20 mg/dL), and albumin is 2.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Her bank records show no unusual withdrawals. Which form of elder mistreatment does this most likely represent?","choices":{"A":"Caregiver neglect","B":"Self-neglect","C":"Physical mistreatment","D":"Psychological mistreatment","E":"Financial exploitation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Failure of a designated provider of care to supply food, medical necessities, and basic care needed for health and safety defines caregiver neglect. Self-neglect requires the older adult's own failure to perform self-care, not a failure by her son. Physical mistreatment requires acts intended to cause injury or pain, and she has no bruises or fractures. Psychological mistreatment involves acts causing anguish or fear, which are not described. Financial exploitation is not supported by her normal bank records. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, recognized subtypes). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pressure injuries are staged from non-blanchable erythema to full-thickness skin loss (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Skin, and Connective Tissue—Dermatology, cutaneous ulcers, p. 491). COMLEX: documentation should note unexplained black eyes, abrasions, burns, fractures, pressure sores, and poor hygiene (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — contributing factors that lead to frailty in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old widow comes to her primary care physician with insomnia and constant worry for 3 months. Her adult daughter, who lives with her, calls her a burden, threatens to put her in a nursing home, and has stopped letting friends visit. She has no bruises, her finances are her own, and she eats well. Her PHQ-9 score is 12 (normal: <5), and TSH is 2.1 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL). Which subtype of elder mistreatment best describes her daughter's behavior?","choices":{"A":"Physical mistreatment","B":"Caregiver neglect","C":"Financial exploitation","D":"Emotional mistreatment","E":"Sexual mistreatment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Verbal or nonverbal acts that produce anguish, fear, or mental distress define psychological (emotional) mistreatment. Physical mistreatment requires acts intended to cause injury or pain. Caregiver neglect requires failure to provide food, shelter, or medical necessities, and she eats well. Financial exploitation is excluded because she controls her own money. Sexual mistreatment involves unwanted sexual contact, which is not described. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, recognized subtypes). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 80% of young adults who suffered emotional abuse as children meet criteria for at least one psychiatric illness by age 21 (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, child abuse, p. 573). COMLEX: sexual mistreatment is nonconsensual or unwanted sexual violation or contact (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman who emigrated from Guatemala 5 years ago comes to a community health center for diabetes follow-up. She lives with her nephew and speaks little English, and a staff member asks how common mistreatment is among older clients like her. Her hemoglobin A1c is 7.8% (reference: <5.7%), and serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL). Her blood pressure is 134/80 mm Hg, and she has no injuries on examination. In convenience samples of older Hispanic immigrants, about what share report elder mistreatment?","choices":{"A":"About 5%","B":"About 15%","C":"About 40%","D":"About 25%","E":"About 60%"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Convenience samples of older Hispanic immigrants show markedly high mistreatment rates, estimated at 40.4%. A share of about 5% understates it. A share of about 15% matches a population-based study of Chinese American older adults instead. A share of about 25% still understates it. A share of about 60% overstates it. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, disproportionate impact in racial and ethnic minorities). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an incapacitated patient's prior oral statements carry more weight when the patient was informed, the directive was specific, and the choice was repeated over time to several people (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, advance directives, p. 268). COMLEX: US community rates by subtype are 5.2% financial, 5.1% potential neglect, 4.6% emotional, and 1.6% physical (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — challenges in preventive care in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man is seen at home after a neighbor reports a foul smell from his house. He lives alone, stacks of newspapers block most rooms, the kitchen has rotting food, and his water was shut off months ago. He is unkempt but oriented and denies that anyone harms him. His hemoglobin is 11.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and vitamin C is 0.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.4–2.0 mg/dL). Before deciding how to intervene, what is the most important next step?","choices":{"A":"Capacity evaluation","B":"Involuntary admission","C":"Nursing home placement","D":"Police welfare check","E":"Psychiatric commitment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Evaluating his decision-making ability is essential, because it determines whether interventions stay voluntary or require coordination with APS. Involuntary admission overrides his choices before his capacity is known. Nursing home placement is premature without knowing whether he can decide for himself. A police welfare check adds nothing, since he has already been seen at home (general knowledge). Psychiatric commitment requires danger from a mental illness, which has not been established (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, clinical assessment and management). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbon monoxide from furnaces, space heaters, and motor exhaust is odorless and causes headache, vomiting, confusion, and visual disturbances, often in several household members at once (First Aid Respiratory—Physiology, cyanide vs carbon monoxide poisoning, p. 689). COMLEX: other phenotypes include severe impairment in personal hygiene and a house in need of critical repair (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — risk factors for poor nutritional status in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman is visited at home after her pharmacist notices she has not refilled her medications. Her home is cluttered and dirty, her refrigerator is empty, and she has lost 6 kg over 4 months. She understands her situation, weighs the risks, and clearly declines to leave her home, and her MoCA score is 27/30 (normal: ≥26/30). Her albumin is 3.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Court-ordered guardianship","B":"Voluntary support services","C":"Involuntary hospitalization","D":"Mandatory day program","E":"Mandatory home removal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With intact decision-making capacity, interventions focus on voluntary concrete services such as referrals, meal delivery, case management, counseling, and home health assistance. Court-ordered guardianship is reserved for patients who have lost capacity. Involuntary hospitalization overrides a capable patient's choice (general knowledge). A mandatory day program overrides a capable adult's choice (general knowledge). Mandatory home removal also overrides her capable refusal (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, clinical assessment and management). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intellectual disability or mental illness does not exclude capacity unless the condition currently impairs the ability to make the healthcare decision (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, decision-making capacity, p. 267). COMLEX: APS receives more reports of self-neglect than of any other mistreatment type in the United States (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — benefits of shared decision making with the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":2,"stem":"An 88-year-old man with moderate Alzheimer disease lives alone and has stopped paying his bills, and his electricity and heat have been shut off. He cannot describe his situation or the risks of staying, and his MoCA score is 11/30 (normal: ≥26/30). He has no family, no advance directive, and no one holding power of attorney. His core temperature is 35.4 °C (reference range: 36.5–37.5 °C), and serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L). Besides immediate medical care, which measure should the clinician pursue with the community protective agency?","choices":{"A":"Hospice enrollment","B":"Driving evaluation","C":"Respite care","D":"Hearing screening","E":"Conservatorship"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When decision-making capacity is lost, clinicians coordinate with APS on conservatorship, guardianship, or financial management assistance to preserve safety. Hospice enrollment does not address his loss of capacity or safety (general knowledge). A driving evaluation does not address the unpaid bills and unsafe home (general knowledge). Respite care supports a caregiver, and he has none (general knowledge). Hearing screening does not address his safety (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, clinical assessment and management). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: decisions a patient made while capacity was intact stay valid even if the patient later loses capacity (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, decision-making capacity, p. 267). COMLEX: the definition of elder mistreatment also includes a caregiver's failure to meet an elder's basic needs or to protect the elder from harm (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — benefits of shared decision making with the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman comes to the clinic with bruises on both upper arms. Her 52-year-old son lost his job a year ago and now relies on her pension and savings to pay his rent and bills. He comes to every visit and becomes angry when she mentions cutting off the money. Hemoglobin is 12.8 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL), and platelet count is 230,000/mm³ (reference range: 150,000–400,000/mm³). Which perpetrator-specific risk factor does the son's situation illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Neighborhood poverty","B":"Psychiatric illness history","C":"Dependency on the victim","D":"Severe victim frailty","E":"Active alcohol misuse"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The caregiver's financial, housing, or emotional reliance on the older victim is a perpetrator-specific risk factor. Neighborhood poverty is a community-level factor rather than a perpetrator-specific one. A psychiatric illness history is not suggested by the vignette. Severe victim frailty is a victim-level factor. Active alcohol misuse is not suggested by the vignette. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, perpetrator-specific risk factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nonaccidental injuries often show differing stages of healing or shapes resembling the implement used (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, child abuse, p. 573). COMLEX: shared living arrangements are a strong independent risk factor for physical abuse and financial exploitation (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old widow moved across the country a year ago to be near her nephew, who now handles her money. She has no friends in the new city, attends no community groups, and talks only to her nephew. She has lost interest in leaving her apartment. Her examination is unremarkable, her BMI is 23 kg/m², and her TSH is 1.8 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL). Which community-level factor most increases her risk of mistreatment?","choices":{"A":"Low social support","B":"Functional dependence","C":"Abuser dependency","D":"Shared living arrangement","E":"Cognitive impairment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Low levels of support and poor embeddedness in community networks significantly raise mistreatment risk. Functional dependence is a victim-level factor. Abuser dependency is a perpetrator-level factor. A shared living arrangement is a relationship-level factor. Cognitive impairment is a victim-level factor. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, community and societal factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: confidentiality may be broken when potential physical harm to the patient or others is serious and imminent, alternative ways to protect those at risk are not possible, and steps can be taken to prevent the harm (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, confidentiality, p. 269). COMLEX: minority status raises vulnerability to financial exploitation through socioeconomic inequities and systemic barriers (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — domains of management in disease prevention in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman brings her 81-year-old father with advanced dementia to the clinic and says she looks after him about 70 hours a week while working part time. She has had no break since taking on his care a year ago, sleeps about 4 hours a night, and says the workload has left her overwhelmed and at the end of her rope. She admits she recently shouted at him and shoved him into a chair. His examination shows a faint bruise on the forearm, and his hemoglobin is 13.4 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Which perpetrator risk factor best explains her behavior?","choices":{"A":"Abuser dependency","B":"Caregiver burnout","C":"Substance use","D":"Psychiatric illness","E":"Prior family conflict"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Overwhelmed, highly stressed providers of care are at substantial risk of becoming abusive or neglectful. Abuser dependency is not suggested, since nothing indicates she relies on him for money, housing, or emotional support. Substance use is not suggested by the vignette. Psychiatric illness is not suggested by the vignette. Prior family conflict is not described. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, perpetrator-specific risk factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: burnout is prolonged, excessive stress that leads to emotional exhaustion and depersonalization and reduces professional efficacy (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Healthcare Delivery, burnout and fatigue, p. 277). COMLEX: caregiver neglect is the failure of a designated caregiver to provide essential care, food, shelter, clothing, or medical necessities (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department by her son after a fall at home. She has bruises of different colors on both upper arms, and her son answers every question for her and insists on staying in the room. Radiographs show a healing rib fracture that was never reported. Her hemoglobin is 12.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL), and INR is 1.0 (reference range: 0.8–1.2). What is the most appropriate first step?","choices":{"A":"Confront the son","B":"Await proof of abuse","C":"Discharge with the son","D":"Notify her pastor","E":"Private interview"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The immediate priority is her physical safety, which begins with speaking to the older adult privately without the caregiver present. Confronting the son may escalate the danger to her (general knowledge). Awaiting proof of abuse delays protection, and proof is not required. Discharge with the son returns her to a possibly unsafe home. Notifying her pastor breaches confidentiality without improving her safety (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, prioritize victim safety). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: for a victim of intimate partner violence, ask whether the patient is safe and help make an emergency plan, without necessarily pressing the patient to leave or to report unless state law requires it (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Communication Skills, challenging scenarios, p. 273). COMLEX: the H-S/EAST measures violation of personal rights, direct abuse, and potentially abusive situations (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — complete assessment of the patient with fall risk or falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":2,"stem":"A 77-year-old man with mild dementia has an unexplained burn on his forearm, and his daughter's account of it changes between visits. He is calm and cannot recall how it happened. The physician is unsure whether harm occurred and asks whether she must first be certain before contacting the state agency. His albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and his hemoglobin is 13.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). What standard triggers the duty to report?","choices":{"A":"Confirmed injury pattern","B":"Reasonable suspicion","C":"Patient's written consent","D":"Police confirmation","E":"Witnessed assault"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A reasonable, good-faith suspicion is legally sufficient, and clinicians need not prove mistreatment before reporting. A confirmed injury pattern is not required. The patient's written consent is not required for a mandated report. Police confirmation is not required. A witnessed assault is not required. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, no requirement for definitive proof). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: when a dependent patient's injuries do not match the caretaker's story, document a detailed history and examination, interview the patient alone if possible, give needed care, and inform the caretaker of the duty to report (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Communication Skills, challenging scenarios, p. 273). COMLEX: state agencies conduct on-site investigations to substantiate reported findings (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman is admitted with a hip fracture, facial bruises, and dehydration, and her bank reports that a caregiver forged checks on her account. The hospital convenes geriatrics, social work, nursing, psychiatry, a lawyer, and a forensic specialist to plan her care. Her serum sodium is 149 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L), and BUN is 38 mg/dL (reference range: 7–20 mg/dL). She is alert and asks what will happen next. Which resource is this group, which coordinates emergency intervention and possible prosecution?","choices":{"A":"Hospital ethics committee","B":"Ombudsman program","C":"Risk management office","D":"Multidisciplinary team","E":"Nurse Aide Registry"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Specialized hospital and community groups that combine medicine, social work, nursing, psychiatry, law, and forensic specialists coordinate emergency interventions and prosecutions. A hospital ethics committee advises on ethical dilemmas rather than prosecution (general knowledge). The ombudsman program advocates for residents of long-term care facilities. A risk management office protects the institution from liability (general knowledge). The Nurse Aide Registry keeps abusive aides from being rehired. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, law enforcement and multidisciplinary teams). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a do-not-resuscitate order prohibits CPR, but the patient may still choose other life-sustaining measures such as intubation, feeding tubes, or chemotherapy (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, advance directives, p. 268). COMLEX: State survey agencies investigate aides and report substantiated abuse to the MFCU; the state keeps the Nurse Aide Registry (Ham's Ch. 33, Table 33.3).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old Black man asks whether older adults in his community face a higher risk of having their money misused by others. Compared with other groups, how much higher can that risk be?","choices":{"A":"Up to 1.2-fold","B":"Up to 3.8-fold","C":"Up to 1.9-fold","D":"Up to 6.5-fold","E":"Up to 10-fold"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Black older adults experience up to a 3.8-fold increased risk of financial exploitation relative to other groups. A figure of up to 1.2-fold understates it. A figure of up to 1.9-fold also understates it. A figure of up to 6.5-fold overstates it. A figure of up to 10-fold overstates it further. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, disproportionate impact in racial and ethnic minorities). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbon monoxide poisoning is treated with 100% oxygen or hyperbaric oxygen (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, specific toxicity treatments, p. 247). COMLEX: the global community prevalence of elder mistreatment is about 15.7%, roughly 141 million older adults (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — challenges in preventive care in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":3,"stem":"An 80-year-old man's home is tidy and structurally sound, and he is well groomed, but his electricity, heat, and water were shut off for nonpayment. Which self-neglect phenotype does he show?","choices":{"A":"Poor personal hygiene","B":"Unsanitary living conditions","C":"Critical home disrepair","D":"Inadequate utilities","E":"Hoarding behavior"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lack of running water, heating, or electricity is the inadequate utilities phenotype of self-neglect. Poor personal hygiene is excluded, since he is well groomed. Unsanitary living conditions are excluded by his tidy home. Critical home disrepair is excluded, since the house is structurally sound. Hoarding behavior is excluded by the lack of clutter. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, five clinical phenotypes). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbon monoxide raises carboxyhemoglobin, which co-oximetry detects but pulse oximetry does not, and it is classically linked to bilateral globus pallidus lesions on MRI (First Aid Respiratory—Physiology, cyanide vs carbon monoxide poisoning, p. 689). COMLEX: when capacity is lost, clinicians work with APS on guardianship or financial management assistance (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old woman with long-standing major depression lives with her grandson. Which category of risk factor for elder mistreatment does her depression represent?","choices":{"A":"Victim factor","B":"Perpetrator factor","C":"Relationship factor","D":"Community factor","E":"Societal factor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Baseline depression, mental illness, or substance abuse history in the older adult is a victim-specific risk factor. A perpetrator factor would describe the grandson, not her. A relationship factor describes interactions such as cohabitation or past conflict. A community factor describes social support and networks. A societal factor describes poverty or systemic inequities. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, victim-specific risk factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: geriatric patients lose phase I drug metabolism first, while phase II is relatively preserved (First Aid Pharmacology—Pharmacokinetics and Pharmacodynamics, drug metabolism, p. 230). COMLEX: inability to perform activities of daily living raises the risk of physical, psychological, and financial mistreatment (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman's husband, who hit her repeatedly during their 50-year marriage, has become her sole provider of care. Which relationship-level risk factor does this history represent?","choices":{"A":"Caregiver burnout","B":"Past spousal violence","C":"Financial dependency on her","D":"Severe victim frailty","E":"Low social support"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A history of domestic violence or dysfunctional family dynamics is a relationship-level risk factor. Caregiver burnout is a perpetrator factor, and no overload is described. Financial dependency on her is a perpetrator factor not described here. Severe victim frailty is a victim-level factor. Low social support is a community-level factor. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, relationship and relational factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sexual abuse of children most often leaves no physical signs and peaks at ages 9 to 12 years (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, child abuse, p. 573). COMLEX: strong social ties act as a protective buffer against mistreatment (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":3,"stem":"A clinician screens an 80-year-old woman with the Elder Abuse Suspicion Index, and she answers no to all five patient questions. The clinician notices she is underweight, unwashed, and has unexplained bruises on her arms. How should these findings be handled within the screen?","choices":{"A":"Discard as nonspecific","B":"Repeat the questions later","C":"Score the observation item","D":"Ask her caregiver","E":"Accept her negative answers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The EASI includes one clinician-observation question about signs such as malnutrition, poor hygiene, or bruises, so these findings count even when the patient denies problems. Discarding them as nonspecific ignores the item built to capture them. Repeating the questions later delays action on signs already seen (general knowledge). Asking her caregiver can expose the patient to a possible abuser (general knowledge). Accepting her negative answers overlooks the observed signs. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, screening and detection tools). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: if surrogates indicate the patient would not have wanted ventilator support, it is withheld or withdrawn regardless of the surrogate's own preference (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, ventilator-assisted life support, p. 269). COMLEX: APS assesses capacity and arranges emergency shelter, medical care, and legal support (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — goals and challenges of chronic disease management","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":3,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman has a black eye and a wrist fracture, and her caregiver's account of the fall differs from hers. Besides the physical findings, what should the physician document?","choices":{"A":"Clinician's opinion of guilt","B":"Family's insurance details","C":"Caregiver's criminal record","D":"Discrepancies in the history","E":"Abuser's employment history"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Records should capture physical findings and discrepancies in the history. The clinician's opinion of guilt is a legal judgment outside the medical record (general knowledge). The family's insurance details do not document mistreatment (general knowledge). The caregiver's criminal record is not the clinician's to establish (general knowledge). The abuser's employment history is not part of the clinical record (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, documentation). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: when an invasive test is done on the wrong patient, the physician must tell the patient about the mistake regardless of the outcome (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Communication Skills, challenging scenarios, p. 273). COMLEX: the immediate priority when mistreatment is suspected is the patient's physical safety (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — complete assessment of the patient with fall risk or falls","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":11,"title":"SDL 11 — Sarcopenia, frailty, and malnutrition","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":12,"title":"SDL 12 — Changes in the respiratory system with aging","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":13,"title":"SDL 13 — Changes in the respiratory system with aging","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":14,"title":"SDL 14 — Changes in the Cardiovascular System with aging","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":15,"title":"SDL 15 — Coronary Artery Disease and Atrial Fibrillation","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":16,"title":"SDL 16 — Infectious Diseases and Changes in the Immune System with Aging","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":17,"title":"SDL 17 — Gastrointestinal disease in the elderly","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":18,"title":"SDL 18 — Aging and the Urinary System","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":19,"title":"SDL 19 — Aging and musculoskeletal changes","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":20,"title":"SDL 20 — Aging and the Sensory System","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":21,"title":"SDL 21 — Gait, Balance, and Falls","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":22,"title":"SDL 22 — Metabolic bone disease","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":23,"title":"SDL 23 — Medication Prescribing in the Elderly - Pharmacokinetic Changes","questions":[]},{"sdlNumber":24,"title":"SDL 24 — Chronic Disease Mangement","questions":[]}]}]};